694
00:54:34.900 --> 00:54:39.637
The main reason is memory and processing power, but mostly memory. When big data

695
00:54:39.637 --> 00:54:44.433
processing tools perform a processing task, they split it up into several smaller

696
00:54:44.433 --> 00:54:48.815
subtasks. The processing tools then distribute these subtasks over several

697
00:54:48.815 --> 00:54:53.671
computers. These are usually commodity computers, which means that they are widely

698
00:54:53.671 --> 00:54:58.408
available and relatively inexpensive. Individually, all of these computers would

699
00:54:58.408 --> 00:55:03.263
take a long time to process the complete task. However, since all of the computers

700
00:55:03.263 --> 00:55:03.500
were

701
00:55:03.500 --> 00:55:08.423
working parallel on smaller subtasks, the task in its whole is done faster. Let's

702
00:55:08.423 --> 00:55:13.406
look at an analogy. Let's say you're running a tailor shop and need to get a batch

703
00:55:13.406 --> 00:55:18.451
of 100 shirts finished. Your very best tailor finishes a shirt in 20 minutes. Other

704
00:55:18.451 --> 00:55:23.374
tailors typically take one hour per shirt. If just one tailor can work at a time,

705
00:55:23.374 --> 00:55:28.479
it's obvious you'd have to choose the quickest tailor to finish the job. However, if

706
00:55:28.479 --> 00:55:30.910
you can split a batch in 25 shirts each,

707
00:55:30.910 --> 00:55:35.678
Having four mediocre tailors working in parallel is faster. A similar thing happens

708
00:55:35.678 --> 00:55:40.102
for big data processing tasks. As you'd expect, the obvious benefit of having

709
00:55:40.102 --> 00:55:44.755
multiple processing units is the extra processing power itself. However, there is

710
00:55:44.755 --> 00:55:49.465
another and potentially more impactful benefit of parallel computing for big data.

711
00:55:49.465 --> 00:55:53.831
Instead of needing to load all of the data in one computer's memory, you can

712
00:55:53.831 --> 00:55:58.140
partition the data and load the subsets into memory of different computers.

713
00:55:58.140 --> 00:56:02.902
That means the memory footprint per computer is relatively small, and the data can

714
00:56:02.902 --> 00:56:07.723
fit in the memory closest to the processor, the RAM. Before you start rewriting all

715
00:56:07.723 --> 00:56:12.543
