types. Here, we create the array nums npfloats using the integers 1 and 3 and a

1910
02:35:27.388 --> 02:35:33.463
float 2.5. Can you spot the difference in the output? The integers now have a

1911
02:35:33.463 --> 02:35:37.250
preceding dot in the array. That's because NumPy

1912
02:35:37.250 --> 02:35:43.644
converted the integers to floats to retain that array's homogeneous nature. Using

1913
02:35:43.644 --> 02:35:50.196
.dtype, we can verify that the elements in the array are floats. Homogeneity allows

1914
02:35:50.196 --> 02:35:56.275
NumPy arrays to be more efficient and faster than Python lists. Requiring all

1915
02:35:56.275 --> 02:36:02.590
elements be the same type eliminates the overhead needed for data type checking.

1916
02:36:02.590 --> 02:36:08.186
When analyzing data, you'll often want to perform operations over entire collections

1917
02:36:08.186 --> 02:36:13.649
of values quickly. Say for example, you'd like to square each number within a list

1918
02:36:13.649 --> 02:36:18.779
of numbers. It'd be nice if we could simply square the list and get a list of

1919
02:36:18.779 --> 02:36:24.175
squared values returned. Unfortunately, Python lists don't support these types of

1920
02:36:24.175 --> 02:36:29.771
calculations. We could square the values using a list by writing a for loop or using

1921
02:36:29.771 --> 02:36:31.170
a list comprehension.

1922
02:36:31.170 --> 02:36:36.838
But neither of these approaches is the most efficient way of doing this. Here lies

1923
02:36:36.838 --> 02:36:42.644
the second advantage of NumPy arrays, their broadcasting functionality. NumPy arrays

1924
02:36:42.644 --> 02:36:48.104
vectorize operations so that they are performed on all elements of an object at

1925
02:36:48.104 --> 02:36:53.841
once. This allows us to efficiently perform calculations over entire arrays. Notice

1926
02:36:53.841 --> 02:36:58.610
that by squaring the array nums np, all elements are squared at once.

1927
02:36:58.610 --> 02:37:05.457
Another advantage of NumPy arrays is their indexing capabilities. When comparing

1928
02:37:05.457 --> 02:37:12.047
basic indexing between a one-dimensional array and list, the capabilities are

1929
02:37:12.047 --> 02:37:19.237
identical. When using two-dimensional arrays and lists, the advantages of arrays are

1930
02:37:19.237 --> 02:37:26.426
clear. To return the second item of the first row in our two-dimensional object, the

1931
02:37:26.426 --> 02:37:27.710
array syntax is

1932
02:37:27.710 --> 02:37:34.232
one, square bracket. The analogous list syntax is a bit more verbose as you have to

1933
02:37:34.232 --> 02:37:40.754
surround both the zero and the one with square brackets. To return the first column

1934
02:37:40.754 --> 02:37:47.275
of values in our 2D object, the array syntax is square bracket, colon, comma, zero,

1935
02:37:47.275 --> 02:37:53.326
square bracket. Lists don't support this type of syntax so we must use a list

1936
02:37:53.326 --> 02:37:55.840
comprehension to return columns.

1937
02:37:55.840 --> 02:38:01.249
NumPy arrays also have a special technique called Boolean indexing. Suppose we

1938
02:38:01.249 --> 02:38:07.074
wanted to gather only positive numbers from the sequence listed here. With an array,

1939
02:38:07.074 --> 02:38:12.622
we can create a Boolean mask using a simple inequality. Indexing the array is as

1940
02:38:12.622 --> 02:38:18.378
simple as enclosing this inequality in square brackets. To do this using a list, we

1941
02:38:18.378 --> 02:38:24.133
need to write a for loop to filter the list, or use a list comprehension. In either

1942
02:38:24.133 --> 02:38:24.480
case,

1943
02:38:24.480 --> 02:38:29.546
Using a NumPy array to index is less verbose and has a faster runtime. Now that

1944
02:38:29.546 --> 02:38:34.739
we've covered powerful and efficient NumPy arrays, let's start putting what we've

1945
02:38:34.739 --> 02:38:40.125
learned into practice. In this lesson, we'll learn how to examine the runtime of our

1946
02:38:40.125 --> 02:38:45.319
code. As mentioned in the previous chapter, runtime is an important consideration

1947
02:38:45.319 --> 02:38:48.555
when thinking about efficiency. Comparing runtimes between two code bases

1948
02:38:48.555 --> 02:38:54.384
that effectively do the same thing allows us to pick the code with the optimal

1949
02:38:54.384 --> 02:39:00.437
performance. By gathering and analyzing runtimes, we can be sure to implement the

1950
02:39:00.437 --> 02:39:06.415
code that is fastest and thus more efficient. To compare runtimes, we need to be

1951
02:39:06.415 --> 02:39:09.330
able to compute the runtime for a line,

1952
02:39:09.330 --> 02:39:14.792
or multiple lines of code. IPython comes with some handy built-in magic commands we

1953
02:39:14.792 --> 02:39:20.123
can use to time our code. Magic commands are enhancements that have been added on

1954
02:39:20.123 --> 02:39:25.388
top of the normal Python syntax. These commands are prefixed with the percentage

1955
02:39:25.388 --> 02:39:30.587
sign. If you aren't familiar with magic commands, don't worry. Take a moment to

1956
02:39:30.587 --> 02:39:35.917
review the documentation using the provided link. We won't be using all the magic

1957
02:39:35.917 --> 02:39:37.760
commands listed in the docs.

1958
02:39:37.760 --> 02:39:43.455
But it's helpful to know what magic commands are at your disposal. Consider this

1959
02:39:43.455 --> 02:39:49.434
example. We want to inspect the runtime for selecting 1,000 random numbers between 0

1960
02:39:49.434 --> 02:39:55.058
and 1 using NumPy's random.rand function. Using timeit just requires adding the

1961
02:39:55.058 --> 02:40:00.681
magic command before the line of code we want to analyze. That's it. One simple

1962
02:40:00.681 --> 02:40:03.600
command to gather runtimes. Magic indeed.

1963
02:40:03.600 --> 02:40:09.164
One advantage to using TimeIt is the fact that it provides an average of timing

1964
02:40:09.164 --> 02:40:14.940
statistics. Notice that the output provides a mean and standard deviation of time.

1965
02:40:14.940 --> 02:40:20.574
We also see that multiple runs and loops were generated. TimeIt runs through the

1966
02:40:20.574 --> 02:40:26.420
provided code multiple times to estimate the code's execution time. This provides a

1967
02:40:26.420 --> 02:40:32.196
more accurate representation of the actual runtime rather than relying on just one

1968
02:40:32.196 --> 02:40:32.900
iteration.

1969
02:40:32.900 --> 02:40:38.666
to calculate the runtime. The mean and standard deviation displayed in the output is

1970
02:40:38.666 --> 02:40:44.296
a summary of the runtime considering each of the multiple runs. The number of runs

1971
02:40:44.296 --> 02:40:50.062
represents how many iterations you'd like to use to estimate the runtime. The number

1972
02:40:50.062 --> 02:40:55.622
of loops represents how many times you'd like the code to be executed per run. We

1973
02:40:55.622 --> 02:41:01.252
can specify the number of runs using the dash r flag and the number of loops using

1974
02:41:01.252 --> 02:41:02.350
the dash n flag.

1975
02:41:02.350 --> 02:41:08.977
Here, we use "-r2", to set the number of runs to 2, and "-n10", to set the number of

1976
02:41:08.977 --> 02:41:15.367
loops to 10. In this example, TimeIt would execute our random number selection 20

1977
02:41:15.367 --> 02:41:21.915
times in order to estimate runtime. Two runs, each with 10 executions. Another cool

1978
02:41:21.915 --> 02:41:28.305
feature of TimeIt is its ability to run on single or multiple lines of code. When

1979
02:41:28.305 --> 02:41:30.830
using TimeIt in line magic mode,

1980
02:41:30.830 --> 02:41:36.627
or with a single line of code, one percentage sign is used. Similarly, we can run

1981
02:41:36.627 --> 02:41:42.567
timeit in cell magic mode or provide multiple lines of code by using two percentage

1982
02:41:42.567 --> 02:41:48.507
signs. We can save the output of timeit into a variable using the dash o flag. This

1983
02:41:48.507 --> 02:41:54.376
allows us to dig deeper into the output and see things like the time for each run,

1984
02:41:54.376 --> 02:41:58.670
the best time for all runs, and the worst time for all runs.

1985
02:41:58.670 --> 02:42:04.548
Let's try using TimeIt with some of Python's built-in data structures. In a previous

1986
02:42:04.548 --> 02:42:09.936
chapter, we mentioned some of Python's built-in data structures called lists,

1987
02:42:09.936 --> 02:42:15.534
dictionaries, and tuples. Python allows us to create these data structures using

1988
02:42:15.534 --> 02:42:21.341
either a formal name, like list, dict, and tuple spelled out, or a shorthand called

1989
02:42:21.341 --> 02:42:24.980
literal syntax. If we wanted to compare the runtime,

1990
02:42:24.980 --> 02:42:30.503
between creating a dictionary using the formal name and creating a dictionary using

1991
02:42:30.503 --> 02:42:35.892
the literal syntax, we could save the output of the individual timeit commands as

1992
02:42:35.892 --> 02:42:41.282
shown here. Then, we could compare the two outputs. For simplicity, let's look at

1993
02:42:41.282 --> 02:42:46.671
just the output of both the timeit commands to see which was faster. Here, we can

1994
02:42:46.671 --> 02:42:52.194
see using the literal syntax to create a dictionary is faster than using the formal

1995
02:42:52.194 --> 02:42:52.460
name

1996
02:42:52.460 --> 02:42:57.440
Without writing code to do the analysis for us. Now that we've learned how to use

1997
02:42:57.440 --> 02:43:02.604
TimeIt, we can compare different coding approaches to select the most efficient one.

1998
02:43:02.604 --> 02:43:07.522
It's off to the rate. We've covered how to time our code using the magic command

1999
02:43:07.522 --> 02:43:12.687
TimeIt, which works well with bite-sized code. But what if we wanted to time a large

2000
02:43:12.687 --> 02:43:17.789
code base or see the line-by-line runtimes within a function? In this lesson, we'll

2001
02:43:17.789 --> 02:43:21.970
cover a concept called Code Profiling that allows us to analyze code

2002
02:43:21.970 --> 02:43:28.345
code profiling is a technique used to describe how long and how often various parts

2003
02:43:28.345 --> 02:43:34.567
of a program are executed. The beauty of a code profiler is its ability to gather

2004
02:43:34.567 --> 02:43:40.712
summary statistics on individual pieces of our code without using magic commands

2005
02:43:40.712 --> 02:43:46.627
like timeit. We'll focus on the line profiler package to profile a function's

2006
02:43:46.627 --> 02:43:48.240
runtime line by line.

2007
02:43:48.240 --> 02:43:54.157
Since this package isn't a part of Python's standard library, we need to install it

2008
02:43:54.157 --> 02:44:00.074
separately. This can easily be done with a pip install command. Let's explore using

2009
02:44:00.074 --> 02:44:06.062
line profiler with an example. Suppose we have a list of superheroes along with each

2010
02:44:06.062 --> 02:44:11.908
hero's height in centimeters and weight in kilograms loaded as NumPy arrays. We've

2011
02:44:11.908 --> 02:44:17.540
also developed a function called convert units that converts each hero's height

2012
02:44:17.540 --> 02:44:23.554
from centimeters to inches, and weight from kilograms to pounds. If we wanted to get

2013
02:44:23.554 --> 02:44:29.496
an estimated runtime of this function, we could use TimeIt, but this will only give

2014
02:44:29.496 --> 02:44:35.438
us the total execution time. What if we wanted to see how long each line within the

2015
02:44:35.438 --> 02:44:41.166
function took to run? We would have to use TimeIt on each individual line of our

2016
02:44:41.166 --> 02:44:46.750
ConvertUnits function, but that's a lot of manual work and not very efficient.

2017
02:44:46.750 --> 02:44:53.272
Instead, we can profile our function with the line profiler package. To use this

2018
02:44:53.272 --> 02:45:00.119
package, we first need to load it into our session. We can do this using the command

2019
02:45:00.119 --> 02:45:06.967
percent sign load underscore ext followed by line profiler. Now we can use the magic

2020
02:45:06.967 --> 02:45:13.488
command lprun from line profiler to gather runtimes for individual lines of code

2021
02:45:13.488 --> 02:45:16.260
within the convert units function.

2022
02:45:16.260 --> 02:45:22.329
uses a special syntax. First, we use the dash f flag to indicate we'd like to

2023
02:45:22.329 --> 02:45:28.872
profile a function. Next, we specify the name of the function we'd like to profile.

2024
02:45:28.872 --> 02:45:35.493
Note the name of the function is passed without any parentheses. Finally, we provide

2025
02:45:35.493 --> 02:45:41.799
the exact function call we'd like to profile by including any arguments that are

2026
02:45:41.799 --> 02:45:45.740
needed. The output of lprun provides a nice table.

2027
02:45:45.740 --> 02:45:51.347
that summarizes the profiling statistics. First, a column specifying the line

2028
02:45:51.347 --> 02:45:57.390
number, followed by a column displaying the number of times that line was executed,

2029
02:45:57.390 --> 02:46:03.288
called the Hits column. Next, the Time column shows the total amount of time each

2030
02:46:03.288 --> 02:46:09.186
line took to execute. This column uses a specific timer unit that can be found in

2031
02:46:09.186 --> 02:46:14.720
the first line of the output. Here, the timer unit is listed in microseconds

2032
02:46:14.720 --> 02:46:21.091
using scientific notation. We see that line three took 13 timer units, or roughly 13

2033
02:46:21.091 --> 02:46:27.008
microseconds to run. The per hit column gives the average amount of time spent

2034
02:46:27.008 --> 02:46:33.303
executing a single line. This is calculated by dividing the time column by the hits

2035
02:46:33.303 --> 02:46:39.523
column. Notice that line nine was executed three times and had a total run time of

2036
02:46:39.523 --> 02:46:42.860
three microseconds, one microsecond per hit.

2037
02:46:42.860 --> 02:46:48.187
The percent time column shows the percent of time spent on a line relative to the

2038
02:46:48.187 --> 02:46:53.383
total amount of time spent in the function. This can be a nice way to see which

2039
02:46:53.383 --> 02:46:58.644
lines of code are taking up the most time within a function. Finally, the source

2040
02:46:58.644 --> 02:47:03.971
code is displayed for each line in the line contents column. You may have noticed

2041
02:47:03.971 --> 02:47:09.101
that the total time reported when using lprun and the time reported from using

2042
02:47:09.101 --> 02:47:11.140
timeit did not match. Remember,

2043
02:47:11.140 --> 02:47:16.692
TIMET uses multiple loops in order to calculate an average and standard deviation of

2044
02:47:16.692 --> 02:47:21.914
time, so the time reported from each of these magic commands aren't expected to

2045
02:47:21.914 --> 02:47:27.070
match exactly. With great power comes great responsibility. Let's put your new

2046
02:47:27.070 --> 02:47:32.556
profiling power to good use by profiling. We've defined efficient code as code that

2047
02:47:32.556 --> 02:47:37.977
has a minimal runtime and a small memory footprint. So far, we've only covered how

2048
02:47:37.977 --> 02:47:40.290
to inspect the runtime of our code.

2049
02:47:40.290 --> 02:47:46.034
In this lesson, we'll cover a few techniques on how to evaluate our code's memory

2050
02:47:46.034 --> 02:47:51.778
footprint. One basic approach for inspecting memory consumption is using Python's

2051
02:47:51.778 --> 02:47:57.521
built-in module, Sys. This module contains system-specific functions and contains

2052
02:47:57.521 --> 02:48:02.698
one nice method, .getSizeOf, that returns the size of an object in bytes.

2053
02:48:02.698 --> 02:48:07.520
Sys.getSizeOf is a quick and dirty way to see the size of an object.

2054
02:48:07.520 --> 02:48:12.671
But this only gives us the size of an individual object. What if we wanted to

2055
02:48:12.671 --> 02:48:18.290
inspect the line-by-line memory footprint of our code? You guessed it, we'll have to

2056
02:48:18.290 --> 02:48:23.908
use a code profiler. Just like we've used code profiling to gather detailed stats on

2057
02:48:23.908 --> 02:48:29.394
runtimes, we can also use code profiling to analyze the memory allocation for each

2058
02:48:29.394 --> 02:48:34.812
line of code in our code base. We'll use the memory profiler package that is very

2059
02:48:34.812 --> 02:48:35.280
similar

2060
02:48:35.280 --> 02:48:42.107
to the line profiler package. It can be downloaded via pip and comes with a handy

2061
02:48:42.107 --> 02:48:48.766
magic command, mprun, that uses the same syntax as lprun. One drawback to using

2062
02:48:48.766 --> 02:48:55.846
mprun is that any function profiled for memory consumption must be defined in a file

2063
02:48:55.846 --> 02:49:02.841
and imported. mprun can only be used on functions defined in physical files and not

2064
02:49:02.841 --> 02:49:04.780
in the IPython session.

2065
02:49:04.780 --> 02:49:12.031
In this example, the convertUnits function was placed in a file named herofunks.py.

2066
02:49:12.031 --> 02:49:19.282
Now we simply import our function from the herofunks file and use the magic command

2067
02:49:19.282 --> 02:49:26.620
mprun to gather statistics on its memory footprint. mprun output is similar to lprun

2068
02:49:26.620 --> 02:49:32.910
output. Here we see a line-by-line description of the memory consumption

2069
02:49:32.910 --> 02:49:39.035
for the function in question. The first column represents the line number of the

2070
02:49:39.035 --> 02:49:45.313
code that has been profiled. The second column, memusage, is the memory used after

2071
02:49:45.313 --> 02:49:51.361
that line has been executed. Next, the increment column shows the difference in

2072
02:49:51.361 --> 02:49:57.332
memory of the current line with respect to the previous one. This shows us the

2073
02:49:57.332 --> 02:50:01.390
impact of the current line on the total memory usage.

2074
02:50:01.390 --> 02:50:06.864
line contents shows the source code that has been profiled. Profiling a function

2075
02:50:06.864 --> 02:50:12.407
with MPRun allows us to see what lines are taking up a large amount of memory and

2076
02:50:12.407 --> 02:50:17.950
possibly develop a more efficient solution. Before moving on, I want to draw your

2077
02:50:17.950 --> 02:50:23.630
attention to a few things. First, the memory is reported in mebibytes. Although one

2078
02:50:23.630 --> 02:50:29.173
mebibyte is not exactly the same as one megabyte, for our purposes, we can assume

2079
02:50:29.173 --> 02:50:30.610
they are close enough

2080
02:50:30.610 --> 02:50:36.524
to mean the same thing. Second, the heroes list, heights array, and weights array

2081
02:50:36.524 --> 02:50:42.511
used to generate this output are not the original datasets we've used in the past.

2082
02:50:42.511 --> 02:50:48.644
Instead, a random sample of 35,000 heroes was used to create these datasets in order

2083
02:50:48.644 --> 02:50:54.703
to clearly show the power of using the memory profiler. If we had used the original

2084
02:50:54.703 --> 02:50:59.230
datasets, the memory used would have been too small to report.

2085
02:50:59.230 --> 02:51:05.343
Don't worry too much about this. The main takeaway is that small memory allocation

2086
02:51:05.343 --> 02:51:11.381
may not show up when using MPRun, and that is a perfectly fine result. The random

2087
02:51:11.381 --> 02:51:17.642
sample of 35,000 heroes was used solely to show typical MPRun output when the memory

2088
02:51:17.642 --> 02:51:23.606
was large enough to report. Finally, the memory profiler package inspects memory

2089
02:51:23.606 --> 02:51:26.960
consumption by querying the operating system.

2090
02:51:26.960 --> 02:51:32.257
This might be slightly different from the amount of memory that is actually used by

2091
02:51:32.257 --> 02:51:37.553
the Python interpreter. Thus, results may differ between platforms and even between

2092
02:51:37.553 --> 02:51:42.786
runs. Regardless, the important information can still be observed. Now that you've

2093
02:51:42.786 --> 02:51:47.891
got the tools to evaluate memory consumption, put your skills to the test. Let's

2094
02:51:47.891 --> 02:51:52.996
start using the Memory Pro. In this lesson, we'll cover combining, counting, and

