03:29:46.744 --> 03:29:51.072
become more flexible. Imagine that later on in your script, you just want to load

2542
03:29:51.072 --> 03:29:55.507
the data and not plot it. That's easy now with the load data function. Likewise, if

2543
03:29:55.507 --> 03:29:59.782
you wanted to do some transformation to the data before plotting, you can do the

2544
03:29:59.782 --> 03:30:02.560
transformation and then call the plot data function.

2545
03:30:02.560 --> 03:30:06.987
We have decoupled the loading functionality from the plotting functionality. The

2546
03:30:06.987 --> 03:30:11.414
code will also be easier for other developers to understand, and it will be more

2547
03:30:11.414 --> 03:30:16.063
pleasant to test and debug. Finally, if you ever need to update your code, functions

2548
03:30:16.063 --> 03:30:20.656
that each have a single responsibility make it easier to predict how changes in one

2549
03:30:20.656 --> 03:30:25.083
place will affect the rest of the code. Repeated code and functions that do more

2550
03:30:25.083 --> 03:30:29.676
than one thing are examples of code smells, which are indications that you may need

2551
03:30:29.676 --> 03:30:30.340
to refactor.

2552
03:30:30.340 --> 03:30:34.987
Refactoring is the process of improving code by changing it a little bit at a time.

2553
03:30:34.987 --> 03:30:39.690
This process is well described in Martin Fowler's book, Refactoring, which is a good

2554
03:30:39.690 --> 03:30:44.393
read for any aspiring software engineer. Now you can do some refactoring of your own

2555
03:30:44.393 --> 03:30:48.872
in the exercise. The way that Python passes information to function is different

2556
03:30:48.872 --> 03:30:53.239
from any other language. It is referred to as pass by assignment, which I will

2557
03:30:53.239 --> 03:30:56.430
explain in this lesson. Let's say we have a function foo.

2558
03:30:56.430 --> 03:31:02.627
That takes a list and sets the first value of the list to 99. Then we set myList to

2559
03:31:02.627 --> 03:31:08.900
the value 123 and pass it to foo. What do you expect the value of myList to be after

2560
03:31:08.900 --> 03:31:15.022
calling foo? If you said 99, 2, 3, then you are right. Lists in Python are mutable

2561
03:31:15.022 --> 03:31:21.145
objects, meaning that they can be changed. Now let's say we have another function,

2562
03:31:21.145 --> 03:31:24.580
bar, that takes an argument and adds 90 to it.

2563
03:31:24.580 --> 03:31:29.874
Then we assign the value 3 to the variable MYVAR and call BAR with MYVAR as the

2564
03:31:29.874 --> 03:31:35.502
argument. What do you expect the value of MYVAR to be after we've called BAR? If you

2565
03:31:35.502 --> 03:31:40.729
said 3, you're right. In Python, integers are immutable, meaning they can't be

2566
03:31:40.729 --> 03:31:46.223
changed. Let's look at another example to understand what's going on. Imagine that

2567
03:31:46.223 --> 03:31:51.584
this gray bar is your computer's memory. When we set the variable A equal to the

2568
03:31:51.584 --> 03:31:54.130
list 123, the Python interpreter says,

2569
03:31:54.130 --> 03:31:59.995
Okay, now A points to this location in memory. Then, if we type B equals A, the

2570
03:31:59.995 --> 03:32:06.231
interpreter says, okay, now B points to whatever A is pointing to. So, if we were to

2571
03:32:06.231 --> 03:32:12.170
append 4 to the end of A, both variables get it, because there is only one list.

2572
03:32:12.170 --> 03:32:18.258
Likewise, if we append 5 to B, both variables get it. However, if we assign A to a

2573
03:32:18.258 --> 03:32:23.380
different object in memory, that does not change where B is pointing.

2574
03:32:23.380 --> 03:32:28.553
Now, things that happen to A are no longer happening to B, and vice versa. How does

2575
03:32:28.553 --> 03:32:33.601
this relate to the example functions we saw earlier? When we assign a list to the

2576
03:32:33.601 --> 03:32:38.711
variable myList, it sets up a location in memory for it. Then, when we pass myList

2577
03:32:38.711 --> 03:32:43.822
to the function foo, the parameter x gets assigned to that same location. So, when

2578
03:32:43.822 --> 03:32:48.808
the function modifies the thing that x points to, it is also modifying the thing

2579
03:32:48.808 --> 03:32:51.550
that myList points to. In the other example,

2580
03:32:51.550 --> 03:32:57.085
We created a variable, myvar, and assigned it the value 3. Then we passed it to the

2581
03:32:57.085 --> 03:33:02.220
function bar, which caused the argument x to point to the same place myvar is

2582
03:33:02.220 --> 03:33:07.821
pointing. But the bar function assigns x to a new value, so the myvar variable isn't

2583
03:33:07.821 --> 03:33:13.156
touched. In fact, there is no way in Python to have changed x or myvar directly,

2584
03:33:13.156 --> 03:33:15.890
because integers are immutable variables.

2585
03:33:15.890 --> 03:33:20.225
There are only a few immutable data types in Python because almost everything is

2586
03:33:20.225 --> 03:33:24.722
represented as an object. The only way to tell if something is mutable is to see if

2587
03:33:24.722 --> 03:33:29.219
there is a function or method that will change the object without assigning it to a

2588
03:33:29.219 --> 03:33:33.337
new variable. Finally, here is a thing that can get you in trouble. Foo is a

2589
03:33:33.337 --> 03:33:37.563
function that appends the value 1 to the end of a list. But whoever wrote this

2590
03:33:37.563 --> 03:33:41.952
function gave the argument an empty list as a default value. When we call foo the

2591
03:33:41.952 --> 03:33:45.420
first time, we get what you would expect, a list with one entry.

2592
03:33:45.420 --> 03:33:49.910
But, when we call foo again, the default value has already been modified. If you

2593
03:33:49.910 --> 03:33:54.512
really want a mutable variable as a default value, consider defaulting to none and

2594
03:33:54.512 --> 03:33:58.945
setting the argument in the function. You can check your understanding with the

2595
03:33:58.945 --> 03:34:03.603
following exercises. In this lesson, I'll introduce the concept of context managers

2596
03:34:03.603 --> 03:34:08.093
and show you how to use these special kinds of functions. A context manager is a

2597
03:34:08.093 --> 03:34:12.807
type of function that sets up a context for your code to run in, runs your code, and

2598
03:34:12.807 --> 03:34:14.210
then removes the context.

2599
03:34:14.210 --> 03:34:18.440
That's not a very helpful definition though, so let me explain with an analogy.

2600
03:34:18.440 --> 03:34:22.884
Imagine that you are throwing a fancy party and have hired some caterers to provide

2601
03:34:22.884 --> 03:34:27.221
refreshments for your guests. Before the party starts, the caterers set up tables

2602
03:34:27.221 --> 03:34:31.611
with food and drinks. Then, you and your friends dance, eat, and have a good time.

2603
03:34:31.611 --> 03:34:35.894
When the party is done, the caterers clean up the food and remove the tables. In

2604
03:34:35.894 --> 03:34:40.338
this analogy, the caterers are like a context manager. First, they set up a context

2605
03:34:40.338 --> 03:34:43.390
for your party, which was a room full of food and drinks.

2606
03:34:43.390 --> 03:34:47.942
Then, they let you and your friends do whatever you want. This is like you being

2607
03:34:47.942 --> 03:34:52.665
able to run your code inside the context manager's context. Finally, when the party

2608
03:34:52.665 --> 03:34:57.274
is over, the caterers clean up and remove the context that the party happened in.

2609
03:34:57.274 --> 03:35:01.827
You may have used code like this before. The open function is a context manager.

2610
03:35:01.827 --> 03:35:06.493
When you write with open, it opens a file that you can read from or write to. Then

2611
03:35:06.493 --> 03:35:11.102
it gives control back to your code so that you can perform operations on the file

2612
03:35:11.102 --> 03:35:11.500
object.

2613
03:35:11.500 --> 03:35:16.060
In this example, we read the text of the file, store the contents of the file in the

2614
03:35:16.060 --> 03:35:20.620
variable text, and store the length of the contents in the variable length. When the

2615
03:35:20.620 --> 03:35:25.180
code inside the indented block is done, the opened function makes sure that the file

2616
03:35:25.180 --> 03:35:29.686
is closed before continuing on in the script. The print statement is outside of the

2617
03:35:29.686 --> 03:35:33.920
context, so by the time it runs, the file is closed. Anytime you use a context

2618
03:35:33.920 --> 03:35:38.209
manager, it will look like this. The keyword with lets Python know that you are

2619
03:35:38.209 --> 03:35:39.620
trying to enter a context.

2620
03:35:39.620 --> 03:35:43.748
Then you call a function. You can call any function that is built to work as a

2621
03:35:43.748 --> 03:35:48.087
context manager. In the next lesson, I'll show you how to write your own functions

2622
03:35:48.087 --> 03:35:52.427
that work this way. A context manager can take arguments like any normal function.

2623
03:35:52.427 --> 03:35:56.819
You end the with statement with a colon, as if you were writing a for loop or an if

2624
03:35:56.819 --> 03:36:01.052
statement. Statements in Python that have an indented block after them, like for

2625
03:36:01.052 --> 03:36:04.969
loops, if else statements, function definitions, etc., are called compound

2626
03:36:04.969 --> 03:36:08.620
statements. The with statement is another type of compound statement.

2627
03:36:08.620 --> 03:36:13.037
Any code that you want to run inside the context that the context manager created

2628
03:36:13.037 --> 03:36:17.454
needs to be indented. When the indented block is done, the context manager gets a

2629
03:36:17.454 --> 03:36:21.817
chance to clean up anything that it needs to, like when the open context manager

2630
03:36:21.817 --> 03:36:26.071
closed the file. Some context managers want to return a value that you can use

2631
03:36:26.071 --> 03:36:30.161
inside the context. By adding as and a variable name at the end of the with

2632
03:36:30.161 --> 03:36:33.760
statement, you can assign the returned value to the variable name.

2633
03:36:33.760 --> 03:36:38.168
We used this ability when calling the open context manager, which returns a file

2634
03:36:38.168 --> 03:36:42.631
that we can read from or write to. By adding as my file to the with statement, we

2635
03:36:42.631 --> 03:36:47.204
assign the file to the variable my file. You'll learn how to write your own context

2636
03:36:47.204 --> 03:36:51.446
managers in the next lesson. For now, you can practice using them in order to

2637
03:36:51.446 --> 03:36:56.075
understand. Now that you know how to use context managers, I want to show you how to

2638
03:36:56.075 --> 03:37:00.427
write a context manager for other people to use. There are two ways to define a

2639
03:37:00.427 --> 03:37:01.860
context manager in Python.

2640
03:37:01.860 --> 03:37:06.447
By using a class that has special double underscore enter and double underscore exit

2641
03:37:06.447 --> 03:37:10.760
methods, or by decorating a certain kind of function. This course is focused on

2642
03:37:10.760 --> 03:37:15.183
writing functions, and some of you may not have been introduced to the concept of

2643
03:37:15.183 --> 03:37:19.660
classes yet, so I will only present the function-based method here. There are five

2644
03:37:19.660 --> 03:37:24.247
parts to creating a context manager. First, you need to define a function. Next, you

2645
03:37:24.247 --> 03:37:28.560
can add any setup code your context needs. This is not required, though. Third,

2646
03:37:28.560 --> 03:37:32.799
You must use the yield keyword to signal to Python that this is a special kind of

2647
03:37:32.799 --> 03:37:36.829
function. I will explain what this keyword means in a moment. After the yield

2648
03:37:36.829 --> 03:37:40.964
statement, you can add any teardown code that you need to clean up the context.

2649
03:37:40.964 --> 03:37:45.308
Finally, you must decorate the function with the context manager decorator from the

2650
03:37:45.308 --> 03:37:49.704
context lib module. You might not know what a decorator is, and that's okay. We will

2651
03:37:49.704 --> 03:37:53.681
discuss decorators in depth in the next chapter of this course. For now, the

2652
03:37:53.681 --> 03:37:56.560
important thing to know is that you write the at symbol

2653
03:37:56.560 --> 03:38:00.911
followed by contextlib.contextManager on the line immediately above your context

2654
03:38:00.911 --> 03:38:05.207
manager function. The yield keyword may also be new to you. When you write this

2655
03:38:05.207 --> 03:38:09.612
word, it means that you are going to return a value, but you expect to finish the

2656
03:38:09.612 --> 03:38:13.800
rest of the function at some point in the future. The value that your context

2657
03:38:13.800 --> 03:38:18.097
manager yields can be assigned to a variable in the with statement by adding as

2658
03:38:18.097 --> 03:38:18.851
variable name. Here, we've assigned

2659
03:38:18.851 --> 03:38:24.166
the value 42 that my context yields to the variable foo. By running this code, you

2660
03:38:24.166 --> 03:38:29.480
can see that after the context block is done executing, the rest of the my context

2661
03:38:29.480 --> 03:38:34.859
function gets run, printing goodbye. Some of you may recognize the yield keyword as

2662
03:38:34.859 --> 03:38:37.970
a thing that gets used when creating generators.

2663
03:38:37.970 --> 03:38:42.595
In fact, a context manager function is technically a generator that yields a single

2664
03:38:42.595 --> 03:38:46.997
value. The ability for a function to yield control and know that it will get to

2665
03:38:46.997 --> 03:38:51.622
finish running later is what makes context managers so useful. This context manager

2666
03:38:51.622 --> 03:38:56.192
is an example of code that accesses a database. Like most context managers, it has

2667
03:38:56.192 --> 03:39:00.872
some setup code that runs before the function yields. This context manager uses that

2668
03:39:00.872 --> 03:39:02.990
setup code to connect to the database.

2669
03:39:02.990 --> 03:39:07.490
Most context managers also have some teardown or cleanup code when they get control

2670
03:39:07.490 --> 03:39:11.719
back after yielding. This one uses the teardown section to disconnect from the

2671
03:39:11.719 --> 03:39:16.218
database. This setup teardown behavior allows a context manager to hide things like

2672
03:39:16.218 --> 03:39:20.718
connecting and disconnecting from a database so that a programmer using the context

2673
03:39:20.718 --> 03:39:24.947
manager can just perform operations on the database without worrying about the

2674
03:39:24.947 --> 03:39:29.447
underlying detail. The database context manager that we've been looking at yields a

2675
03:39:29.447 --> 03:39:30.260
specific value.

2676
03:39:30.260 --> 03:39:34.729
The database connection that can be used in the context block. Some context managers

2677
03:39:34.729 --> 03:39:39.093
don't yield an explicit value. Indir is a context manager that changes the current

2678
03:39:39.093 --> 03:39:43.296
working directory to a specific path and then changes it back after the context

2679
03:39:43.296 --> 03:39:47.553
block is done. It does not need to return anything with its yield statement. Now

2680
03:39:47.553 --> 03:39:51.809
it's your turn to practice writing. You've learned a lot about using and writing

2681
03:39:51.809 --> 03:39:56.226
context managers. In this lesson, we'll cover nested contexts, handling errors, and

2682
03:39:56.226 --> 03:39:58.620
how to know when to create a context manager.

2683
03:39:58.620 --> 03:40:03.228
Imagine you are implementing this copy function that copies the contents of one file

2684
03:40:03.228 --> 03:40:07.725
to another file. One way you could write this function would be to open the source

2685
03:40:07.725 --> 03:40:11.894
file, store the contents of the file in the contents variable, then open the

2686
03:40:11.894 --> 03:40:16.337
destination file and write the contents to it. This approach works fine until you

2687
03:40:16.337 --> 03:40:20.890
try to copy a file that is too large to fit in memory. What would be ideal is if we

2688
03:40:20.890 --> 03:40:25.443
could open both files at once and copy over one line at a time. Fortunately for us,

2689
03:40:25.443 --> 03:40:28.240
the file object that the OpenContextManager returns

2690
03:40:28.240 --> 03:40:33.287
can be iterated over in a for loop. The statement for line in my file here will read

2691
03:40:33.287 --> 03:40:38.154
in the contents of my file one line at a time until the end of the file. So going

2692
03:40:38.154 --> 03:40:43.201
back to our copy function, if we could open both files at once, we could read in the

2693
03:40:43.201 --> 03:40:48.127
source file line by line and write each line out to the destination as we go. This

2694
03:40:48.127 --> 03:40:53.054
would let us copy the file without worrying about how big it is. In Python, nested

2695
03:40:53.054 --> 03:40:57.200
with statements are perfectly legal. This code opens the source file,

2696
03:40:57.200 --> 03:41:02.077
and then opens the destination file inside the source file's context. That means

2697
03:41:02.077 --> 03:41:07.197
code that runs inside the context created by opening the destination file has access

2698
03:41:07.197 --> 03:41:12.135
to both the f underscore source and the f underscore dest file objects. So we are

2699
03:41:12.135 --> 03:41:17.195
able to copy the file over one line at a time like we wanted to. One thing you will

2700
03:41:17.195 --> 03:41:21.889
want to think about when writing your context managers is what happens if the

2701
03:41:21.889 --> 03:41:26.400
programmer who uses your context manager writes code that causes an error?

2702
03:41:26.400 --> 03:41:31.234
Imagine you've written this function that lets someone connect to the printer. The

2703
03:41:31.234 --> 03:41:36.126
printer only allows one connection at a time, so it is imperative that p.disconnect

2704
03:41:36.126 --> 03:41:41.019
gets called, or else no one else will be able to print. Someone decides to use your

2705
03:41:41.019 --> 03:41:45.676
git printer function to print the text of their document. However, they weren't

2706
03:41:45.676 --> 03:41:50.509
paying attention and accidentally typed txt instead of text. This will raise a key

2707
03:41:50.509 --> 03:41:53.280
error because txt is not in the doc dictionary.

2708
03:41:53.280 --> 03:41:57.715
And that means p disconnect doesn't get called. So what can we do? You may be

2709
03:41:57.715 --> 03:42:02.324
familiar with the try statement. It allows you to write code that might raise an

2710
03:42:02.324 --> 03:42:06.874
error inside the try block and catch the error inside the accept block. You can

2711
03:42:06.874 --> 03:42:11.655
choose to ignore the error or re-raise it. The try statement also allows you to add

2712
03:42:11.655 --> 03:42:16.263
a finally block. This is code that gets run no matter what, whether an exception

2713
03:42:16.263 --> 03:42:20.699
occurred or not. The solution then is to put a try statement before the yield

2714
03:42:20.699 --> 03:42:22.830
statement in our getPrinter function.

2715
03:42:22.830 --> 03:42:27.774
and a finally statement before p.disconnect. When the sloppy programmer runs their

2716
03:42:27.774 --> 03:42:32.778
code, they still get the key error, but finally ensures that p.disconnect is called

2717
03:42:32.778 --> 03:42:37.722
before the error is raised. If you notice that your code is following any of these

2718
03:42:37.722 --> 03:42:42.726
patterns, you might consider using a context manager. For instance, in this lesson,

2719
03:42:42.726 --> 03:42:47.670
we've talked about open, which uses the open-close pattern, and git-printer, which

2720
03:42:47.670 --> 03:42:49.840
uses the connect-disconnect pattern.

2721
03:42:49.840 --> 03:42:54.200
See if you can find other instances of these patterns in code you are familiar with.

2722
03:42:54.200 --> 03:42:58.456
Test your understanding with the next few examples. In this chapter, you are going

2723
03:42:58.456 --> 03:43:02.660
to learn about decorators, a powerful way of modifying the behavior of functions.

2724
03:43:02.660 --> 03:43:06.969
But first, we need to build up some foundational concepts that will make decorators

2725
03:43:06.969 --> 03:43:11.225
easier to understand. The main thing you should take away from this lesson is that

2726
03:43:11.225 --> 03:43:15.273
functions are just like any other object in Python. They are not fundamentally

2727
03:43:15.273 --> 03:43:17.090
different from lists, dictionaries,

2728
03:43:17.090 --> 03:43:22.028
Data frames, strings, integers, floats, modules, or anything else in Python. And

2729
03:43:22.028 --> 03:43:27.029
because functions are just another type of object, you can do anything to or with

2730
03:43:27.029 --> 03:43:32.029
them that you would do with any other kind of object. You can take a function and

2731
03:43:32.029 --> 03:43:37.214
assign it to a variable, like x. Then, if you wanted to, you could call x instead of

2732
03:43:37.214 --> 03:43:42.214
my function. It doesn't have to be a function you defined, either. If you felt so

2733
03:43:42.214 --> 03:43:46.350
inclined, you could assign the print function to printymcprintface,

2734
03:43:46.350 --> 03:43:51.190
and use it instead of your print function. You can also add functions to a list or

2735
03:43:51.190 --> 03:43:56.088
dictionary. Here, we've added the functions myFunction, open, and print to the list

2736
03:43:56.088 --> 03:44:00.987
listoffunction. We can call an element of the list and pass it arguments. Since the

2737
03:44:00.987 --> 03:44:05.768
third element of the list is the print function, it prints the string argument to

2738
03:44:05.768 --> 03:44:10.666
the console. Below that, we've added the same three functions to a dictionary under

2739
03:44:10.666 --> 03:44:15.506
the keys func1, func2, and func3. Since the print function is stored under the key

2740
03:44:15.506 --> 03:44:15.860
func3,

2741
03:44:15.860 --> 03:44:20.119
We can reference it and use it as if we were calling the function directly. Notice

2742
03:44:20.119 --> 03:44:24.326
that when you assign a function to a variable, you do not include the parentheses

2743
03:44:24.326 --> 03:44:28.585
after the function name. This is a subtle but very important distinction. When you

2744
03:44:28.585 --> 03:44:32.844
type my function with the parentheses, you are calling that function. It evaluates

2745
03:44:32.844 --> 03:44:37.104
to the value that the function returns. However, when you type my function without

2746
03:44:37.104 --> 03:44:41.467
the parentheses, you are referencing the function itself. It evaluates to a function

2747
03:44:41.467 --> 03:44:43.700
object. Here's where things get really fun.

2748
03:44:43.700 --> 03:44:48.758
Since a function is just an object like anything else in Python, you can pass one as

2749
03:44:48.758 --> 03:44:53.816
an argument to another function. The hasDocString function checks to see whether the

2750
03:44:53.816 --> 03:44:58.632
function that is passed to it has a doc string or not. We could define these two

2751
03:44:58.632 --> 03:45:03.389
functions, no and yes, and pass them as arguments to the hasDocString function.

2752
03:45:03.389 --> 03:45:08.326
Since the no function doesn't have a doc string, the hasDocString function returns

2753
03:45:08.326 --> 03:45:12.180
false. Likewise, hasDocString returns true for the yes function.

2754
03:45:12.180 --> 03:45:17.145
Functions can also be defined inside other functions. These kinds of functions are

2755
03:45:17.145 --> 03:45:21.990
called nested functions, although you may also hear them called inner functions,

2756
03:45:21.990 --> 03:45:27.076
helper functions, or child functions. A nested function can make your code easier to

2757
03:45:27.076 --> 03:45:32.041
read. In this example, if x and y are within some bounds, foo prints x times y. We

2758
03:45:32.041 --> 03:45:37.007
can make that if statement easier to read by defining an inrange function. There's

2759
03:45:37.007 --> 03:45:41.730
also nothing stopping us from returning a function. For instance, the function

2760
03:45:41.730 --> 03:45:46.535
git function creates a new function, print me, and then returns it. If we assign the

2761
03:45:46.535 --> 03:45:51.339
result of calling git function to the variable new func, we are assigning the return

2762
03:45:51.339 --> 03:45:56.029
value, print me, to new func. We can then call new func as if it were the print me

2763
03:45:56.029 --> 03:46:00.720
function. The way that Python treats everything as an object is one of my favorite

2764
03:46:00.720 --> 03:46:05.124
things about the language, and it gives you the ability to do a lot of really

2765
03:46:05.124 --> 03:46:09.928
complex things. In the coming lessons, we'll dig into what this ability allows us to

2766
03:46:09.928 --> 03:46:10.100
do.

2767
03:46:10.100 --> 03:46:14.503
For now, you can check your understanding. Before we can dig into decorators, we

2768
03:46:14.503 --> 03:46:19.016
need to understand how scope works in Python. Scope determines which variables can

2769
03:46:19.016 --> 03:46:23.584
be accessed at different points in your code. Names are very useful things, both in

2770
03:46:23.584 --> 03:46:28.098
Python and in the real world. For instance, this is Tom, and this is Janelle. When

2771
03:46:28.098 --> 03:46:32.501
we say Tom, we know we are talking about the person on the left, and when we say

2772
03:46:32.501 --> 03:46:37.069
Janelle, we know we are talking about the person on the right. If Janelle says, Tom

2773
03:46:37.069 --> 03:46:38.610
didn't go to work yesterday,

2774
03:46:38.610 --> 03:46:43.613
We can be fairly sure she is talking about the tom standing next to her, and not

2775
03:46:43.613 --> 03:46:48.554
some tom in a different country. This is sort of how scope works in programming

2776
03:46:48.554 --> 03:46:53.557
languages like Python. Python has names too, variable names. When we say print x

2777
03:46:53.557 --> 03:46:58.810
here, Python knows we mean the x that we just defined. What happens if we redefine x

2778
03:46:58.810 --> 03:47:03.814
inside the function foo, though? In foo's print statement, do we mean the x that

2779
03:47:03.814 --> 03:47:05.940
equals 42, or the x that equals 7?

2780
03:47:05.940 --> 03:47:11.001
Python applies the same logic we applied with Tom and Janelle and assumes we mean

2781
03:47:11.001 --> 03:47:16.124
the x that was defined right there in the function. However, there is no y defined

2782
03:47:16.124 --> 03:47:21.373
in the function foo, so it looks outside the function for a definition when asked to

2783
03:47:21.373 --> 03:47:26.559
print y. Note that setting x equal to 42 inside the function foo doesn't change the

2784
03:47:26.559 --> 03:47:29.870
value of x that we said earlier outside the function.

2785
03:47:29.870 --> 03:47:34.545
Python has to have strict rules about which variable you are referring to when using

2786
03:47:34.545 --> 03:47:38.776
a particular variable name. So when we typed printx in the function foo, the

2787
03:47:38.776 --> 03:47:43.061
interpreter had to follow those rules to determine which x we met. First, the

2788
03:47:43.061 --> 03:47:47.459
interpreter looks in the local scope. When you are inside a function, the local

2789
03:47:47.459 --> 03:47:52.078
scope is made up of the arguments and any variables defined inside the function. If

2790
03:47:52.078 --> 03:47:56.754
the interpreter can't find the variable in the local scope, it expands its search to

2791
03:47:56.754 --> 03:47:57.700
the global scope.

2792
03:47:57.700 --> 03:48:01.804
These are the things to find outside the function. Finally, if it can't find the

2793
03:48:01.804 --> 03:48:05.907
thing it is looking for in the global scope, the interpreter checks the built-in

2794
03:48:05.907 --> 03:48:10.216
scope. These are things that are always available in Python. For instance, the print

2795
03:48:10.216 --> 03:48:14.320
function is in the built-in scope, which is why we are able to use it in our foo

2796
03:48:14.320 --> 03:48:18.167
function. I actually skipped a level in that diagram. In the case of nested

2797
03:48:18.167 --> 03:48:22.424
functions, where one function is defined inside another function, Python will check

2798
03:48:22.424 --> 03:48:25.810
the scope of the parent function before checking the global scope.

2799
03:48:25.810 --> 03:48:30.531
This is called the non-local scope to show that it is not the local scope of the

2800
03:48:30.531 --> 03:48:35.488
child function and not the global scope. Note that Python only gives you read access

2801
03:48:35.488 --> 03:48:40.326
to variables defined outside of your current scope. In foo, when we set x equal to

2802
03:48:40.326 --> 03:48:45.047
42, Python assumed we wanted a new variable in the local scope, not the x in the

2803
03:48:45.047 --> 03:48:49.650
global scope. If what we had really wanted was to change the value of x in the

2804
03:48:49.650 --> 03:48:54.548
global scope, then we have to declare that we mean the global x by using the global

2805
03:48:54.548 --> 03:48:55.020
keyword.

2806
03:48:55.020 --> 03:48:59.802
Notice that when we print x after calling foo now, it prints 42 instead of 7 like it

2807
03:48:59.802 --> 03:49:04.185
used to. However, you should try to avoid using global variables like this if

2808
03:49:04.185 --> 03:49:08.852
possible, because it can make testing and debugging harder. And if we ever want to

2809
03:49:08.852 --> 03:49:13.178
modify a variable that is defined in the non-local scope, we have to use the

2810
03:49:13.178 --> 03:49:17.789
non-local keyword. It works exactly the same way as the global keyword, but it is

2811
03:49:17.789 --> 03:49:22.571
used when you are inside a nested function and you want to update a variable that is

2812
03:49:22.571 --> 03:49:24.620
defined inside your parent function.

2813
03:49:24.620 --> 03:49:29.561
Try a few exercises to make sure you understand how scope works before moving on.

2814
03:49:29.561 --> 03:49:34.563
The last topic I need to explain before discussing decorators is how closures work

2815
03:49:34.563 --> 03:49:39.564
in Python. A closure in Python is a tuple of variables that are no longer in scope

2816
03:49:39.564 --> 03:49:44.566
but that a function needs in order to run. Let's explain this with an example. The

2817
03:49:44.566 --> 03:49:49.690
function foo defines a nested function, bar, that prints the value of a. Foo returns

2818
03:49:49.690 --> 03:49:52.740
this new function. So when we say func equals foo,

2819
03:49:52.740 --> 03:49:57.868
We are assigning the bar function to the variable func. Now, what happens when we

2820
03:49:57.868 --> 03:50:02.996
call func? As expected, it prints the value of variable a, which is 5. But wait a

2821
03:50:02.996 --> 03:50:08.314
minute. How does function func know anything about variable a? a is defined in foo's

2822
03:50:08.314 --> 03:50:13.505
scope, not bar's. You would think that a would not be observable outside the scope

2823
03:50:13.505 --> 03:50:18.380
of foo. That's where closures come in. When foo returns the new bar function,

2824
03:50:18.380 --> 03:50:23.430
Python helpfully attached any non-local variable that bar was going to need to the

2825
03:50:23.430 --> 03:50:28.295
function object. Those variables get stored in a tuple in the double underscore

2826
03:50:28.295 --> 03:50:33.283
closure attribute of the function. The closure for func has one variable, and you

2827
03:50:33.283 --> 03:50:38.149
can view the value of that variable by accessing the cell contents of the item.

2828
03:50:38.149 --> 03:50:43.260
Let's examine this bit of code. Here, x is defined in the global scope. foo creates

2829
03:50:43.260 --> 03:50:47.140
a function bar that prints whatever argument was passed to foo.

2830
03:50:47.140 --> 03:50:52.461
When we call foo and assign the result to myFunk, we pass in x. So, as expected,

2831
03:50:52.461 --> 03:50:57.781
calling myFunk prints the value of x. Now, let's delete x and call myFunk again.

2832
03:50:57.781 --> 03:51:03.168
What do you think will happen this time? If you guessed that we would still print

2833
03:51:03.168 --> 03:51:08.422
25, then you are correct. That's because foo's value argument gets added to the

2834
03:51:08.422 --> 03:51:14.009
closure attached to the new myFunk function. So even though x doesn't exist anymore,

2835
03:51:14.009 --> 03:51:16.270
the value persists in its closure.

2836
03:51:16.270 --> 03:51:20.927
Notice that nothing changes if we overwrite x instead of deleting it. Here we've

2837
03:51:20.927 --> 03:51:25.525
passed x into foo and then assigned the new function to the variable x. The old

2838
03:51:25.525 --> 03:51:30.298
value of x, 25, is still stored in the new function's closure, even though the new

2839
03:51:30.298 --> 03:51:35.130
function is now stored in the x variable. This is going to be important to remember

2840
03:51:35.130 --> 03:51:39.728
when we talk about decorators in the next lesson. Let's go over some of the key

2841
03:51:39.728 --> 03:51:44.443
concepts again to be sure you understand. A nested function is a function defined

2842
03:51:44.443 --> 03:51:45.840
inside another function.

2843
03:51:45.840 --> 03:51:50.566
We'll sometimes refer to the outer function as the parent and the nested function as

2844
03:51:50.566 --> 03:51:55.012
the child. A non-local variable is any variable that gets defined in the parent

2845
03:51:55.012 --> 03:51:59.513
function's scope that gets used by the child function. And finally, a closure is

2846
03:51:59.513 --> 03:52:04.014
Python's way of attaching non-local variables to a returned function so that the

2847
03:52:04.014 --> 03:52:08.572
function can operate, even when it is called outside of its parent's scope. We've

2848
03:52:08.572 --> 03:52:12.736
gotten pretty deep into the internals of how Python works, and you must be

2849
03:52:12.736 --> 03:52:14.930
wondering, why does all of this matter?

2850
03:52:14.930 --> 03:52:19.322
Well, in the next lesson, we'll finally get to talk about decorators. In order to

2851
03:52:19.322 --> 03:52:23.714
work, decorators have to make use of all of these concepts. Functions as objects,

2852
03:52:23.714 --> 03:52:28.269
nested functions, non-local scope, and closures. Now that you have a firm foundation

2853
03:52:28.269 --> 03:52:32.607
to build on, understanding how decorators work should be easy. Before we move on

2854
03:52:32.607 --> 03:52:36.945
though, try out a few practice problems to make sure you understand how closures

2855
03:52:36.945 --> 03:52:41.066
work. Now that you know functions can be passed around as variables, and you

2856
03:52:41.066 --> 03:52:44.320
understand scope and closures, we can talk about decorators.

2857
03:52:44.320 --> 03:52:48.965
So, what is a decorator? Let's say you have a function that takes some inputs and

2858
03:52:48.965 --> 03:52:53.724
returns some outputs. A decorator is a wrapper that you can place around a function

2859
03:52:53.724 --> 03:52:58.082
that changes that function's behavior. You can modify the inputs, modify the

2860
03:52:58.082 --> 03:53:02.554
outputs, or even change the behavior of the function itself. You may have seen

2861
03:53:02.554 --> 03:53:07.256
decorators in Python before. When you use them, you type the at symbol followed by

2862
03:53:07.256 --> 03:53:11.843
the decorator's name on the line directly above the function you are decorating.

2863
03:53:11.843 --> 03:53:12.130
Here,

2864
03:53:12.130 --> 03:53:16.881
The double args decorator modifies the behavior of the multiply function. Double

2865
03:53:16.881 --> 03:53:21.752
args is a decorator that multiplies every argument by 2 before passing them to the

2866
03:53:21.752 --> 03:53:26.503
decorated function. So 1 times 5 becomes 2 times 10, which equals 20. That seems

2867
03:53:26.503 --> 03:53:31.373
kind of magical that we can alter the behavior of function. So let's peel back the

2868
03:53:31.373 --> 03:53:36.125
layers and see how it works. We will build the double args decorator together in

2869
03:53:36.125 --> 03:53:40.817
this lesson. Let's continue to use the multiply function as the function we are

2870
03:53:40.817 --> 03:53:41.470
decorating.

2871
03:53:41.470 --> 03:53:46.322
Decorators are just functions that take a function as an argument and return a

2872
03:53:46.322 --> 03:53:51.423
modified version of that function. To start off, let's not have double args modify

2873
03:53:51.423 --> 03:53:56.275
anything. It just takes a function and immediately returns it. If we call this

2874
03:53:56.275 --> 03:54:01.439
version of double args that does nothing and pass it the multiply function and then

2875
03:54:01.439 --> 03:54:06.477
assign the result to the variable new multiply, then we call new multiply and get

2876
03:54:06.477 --> 03:54:09.650
the same value we would have gotten from multiply .

2877
03:54:09.650 --> 03:54:14.427
In order for your decorator to return a modified function, it is usually helpful for

2878
03:54:14.427 --> 03:54:19.146
it to define a new function to return. We'll call that nested function wrapper. All

2879
03:54:19.146 --> 03:54:23.582
wrapper does is take two arguments and passes them on to whatever function was

2880
03:54:23.582 --> 03:54:27.960
passed to double args in the first place. If double args then returns the new

2881
03:54:27.960 --> 03:54:32.566
wrapper function, the return value acts exactly the same as whatever function was

2882
03:54:32.566 --> 03:54:37.229
passed to double args, assuming that the function passed to double args also takes

2883
03:54:37.229 --> 03:54:38.480
exactly two arguments.

2884
03:54:38.480 --> 03:54:42.735
DoubleARGS is still not doing anything to actually modify the function it is

2885
03:54:42.735 --> 03:54:47.326
decorating. Once again, we'll pass multiply to doubleARGS and assign the result to

2886
03:54:47.326 --> 03:54:51.525
newMultiply. If we then call newMultiply, which is now equal to the wrapper

2887
03:54:51.525 --> 03:54:55.892
function, wrapper calls multiply because it is the function that was passed to

2888
03:54:55.892 --> 03:55:00.483
doubleARGS. So wrapper calls multiply with the arguments 1 and 5, which returns 5.

2889
03:55:00.483 --> 03:55:04.850
Now let's actually modify the function our decorator is decorating. This time,

2890
03:55:04.850 --> 03:55:09.820
Wrapper will still call whatever function is passed to double args, but it will

2891
03:55:09.820 --> 03:55:14.854
double every argument when it calls the original function. See how it calls func

2892
03:55:14.854 --> 03:55:20.139
with the a times 2 and b times 2? As usual, we will call double args on the multiply

2893
03:55:20.139 --> 03:55:25.235
function and assign the result to new multiply. Now what happens when we call new

2894
03:55:25.235 --> 03:55:30.332
multiply with 1 and 5 as arguments? Well, new multiply is equal to wrapper, which

2895
03:55:30.332 --> 03:55:33.100
calls multiply after doubling each argument.

2896
03:55:33.100 --> 03:55:38.382
So 1 becomes 2 and 5 becomes 10, giving us 2 times 10, which equals 20. We're almost

2897
03:55:38.382 --> 03:55:43.600
there. This time, instead of assigning the new function to newMultiply, we're going

2898
03:55:43.600 --> 03:55:48.567
to overwrite the multiply variable. Now calling multiply with arguments 1 and 5

2899
03:55:48.567 --> 03:55:53.597
gives us 20 instead of 5. Remember that we can do this because Python stores the

2900
03:55:53.597 --> 03:55:58.690
original multiply function in the new function's closure. When I first showed you

2901
03:55:58.690 --> 03:56:02.400
the double args decorator at the beginning of this lecture,

2902
03:56:02.400 --> 03:56:07.082
I used at double args on the line before the definition of multiply. This is just a

2903
03:56:07.082 --> 03:56:11.707
Python convenience for saying multiply equals the value returned by calling double

2904
03:56:11.707 --> 03:56:16.388
args with multiply as the only argument. The code shown here on the left is exactly

2905
03:56:16.388 --> 03:56:21.126
equivalent to the code on the right. And that's how decorators work. Let's practice.

2906
03:56:21.126 --> 03:56:25.695
You've learned a lot about how decorators work. This lesson will walk you through

2907
03:56:25.695 --> 03:56:30.094
some real-world decorators so that you can start to recognize common decorator

2908
03:56:30.094 --> 03:56:31.730
patterns. The timer decorator

2909
03:56:31.730 --> 03:56:35.992
runs the decorated function, and then prints how long it took for the function to

2910
03:56:35.992 --> 03:56:40.359
run. I usually wind up adding some version of this to all of my projects because it

2911
03:56:40.359 --> 03:56:44.568
is a pretty easy way to figure out where your computational bottlenecks are. All

2912
03:56:44.568 --> 03:56:48.988
decorators have fairly similar looking docstrings because they all take and return a

2913
03:56:48.988 --> 03:56:53.407
single function. For brevity, I will only include the description of the function in

2914
03:56:53.407 --> 03:56:57.827
the docstrings of the examples that follow. Like most decorators, we'll start off by

2915
03:56:57.827 --> 03:56:59.300
defining a wrapper function.

2916
03:56:59.300 --> 03:57:03.450
This is the function that the decorator will return. Wrapper takes any number of

2917
03:57:03.450 --> 03:57:07.651
positional and keyword arguments so that it can be used to decorate any function.

2918
03:57:07.651 --> 03:57:11.957
The first thing the new function will do is record the time that it was called with

2919
03:57:11.957 --> 03:57:16.262
the time function. Then, wrapper gets the result of calling the decorated function.

2920
03:57:16.262 --> 03:57:20.256
We don't return that value yet, though. After calling the decorated function,

2921
03:57:20.256 --> 03:57:24.613
wrapper checks the time again and prints a message about how long it took to run the

2922
03:57:24.613 --> 03:57:26.740
decorated function. Once we've done that,

2923
03:57:26.740 --> 03:57:30.851
We need to return the value that the decorated function calculated. So, if we

2924
03:57:30.851 --> 03:57:35.336
decorate this simple sleepNseconds function, you can see that sleeping for 5 seconds

2925
03:57:35.336 --> 03:57:39.821
takes about 5 seconds, and sleeping for 10 seconds takes about 10 seconds. This is a

2926
03:57:39.821 --> 03:57:44.092
trivial use of the decorator to show how it works, but it can be very useful for

2927
03:57:44.092 --> 03:57:48.577
finding the slow parts of your code. Memoizing is the process of storing the results

2928
03:57:48.577 --> 03:57:53.062
of a function, so that the next time the function is called with the same arguments,

2929
03:57:53.062 --> 03:57:54.770
you can just look up the answer.

2930
03:57:54.770 --> 03:57:59.153
We start by setting up a dictionary that will map arguments to results. Then, as

2931
03:57:59.153 --> 03:58:03.371
usual, we create wrapper to be the new decorated function that this decorator

2932
03:58:03.371 --> 03:58:07.918
returns. When the new function gets called, we check to see whether we've ever seen

2933
03:58:07.918 --> 03:58:09.832
these arguments before. If we haven't,

2934
03:58:09.832 --> 03:58:14.281
we send them to the decorated function and store the result in the cache dictionary.

2935
03:58:14.281 --> 03:58:18.519
Now we can look up the return value quickly in a dictionary of results. The next

2936
03:58:18.519 --> 03:58:22.915
time we call this function with those same arguments, the return value will already

2937
03:58:22.915 --> 03:58:27.152
be in the dictionary. Here, we are memoizing slow function. Slow function simply

2938
03:58:27.152 --> 03:58:28.900
returns the sum of its arguments.

2939
03:58:28.900 --> 03:58:33.323
In order to simulate a slow function, we have it sleep for 5 seconds before

2940
03:58:33.323 --> 03:58:38.218
returning. If we call slow function with the arguments 3 and 4, it will sleep for 5

2941
03:58:38.218 --> 03:58:43.055
seconds and then return 7. But if we call slow function with the arguments 3 and 4

2942
03:58:43.055 --> 03:58:47.891
again, it will immediately return 7. Because we've stored the answer in the cache,

2943
03:58:47.891 --> 03:58:52.314
the decorated function doesn't even have to call the original slow function

2944
03:58:52.314 --> 03:58:57.209
function. So when is it appropriate to use a decorator? You should consider using a

2945
03:58:57.209 --> 03:58:57.740
decorator

2946
03:58:57.740 --> 03:59:01.773
when you want to add some common bit of code to multiple functions. We could have

2947
03:59:01.773 --> 03:59:05.607
added timing code in the body of all three of these functions, but that would

2948
03:59:05.607 --> 03:59:09.490
violate the principle of don't repeat yourself. Adding a decorator is a better

2949
03:59:09.490 --> 03:59:13.622
choice. Now that you've seen a few more decorators in action, you can try your hand

2950
03:59:13.622 --> 03:59:17.655
at creating some in the following example. One of the problems with decorators is

2951
03:59:17.655 --> 03:59:21.738
that they obscure the decorated function's metadata. In this lesson, I'll show you

2952
03:59:21.738 --> 03:59:25.572
why it's a problem and how to fix it. Here we have a nice function, sleep and

2953
03:59:25.572 --> 03:59:25.970
seconds.

2954
03:59:25.970 --> 03:59:30.460
with a docstring that explains exactly what it does. If we look at the docstring

2955
03:59:30.460 --> 03:59:35.063
attribute, we can see the text of the docstring. We can also access other metadata

2956
03:59:35.063 --> 03:59:39.665
for the function, like its name and default arguments. But watch what happens when

2957
03:59:39.665 --> 03:59:44.324
we decorate sleepnseconds with the timer decorator, as we've done here. When we try

2958
03:59:44.324 --> 03:59:48.927
to print the docstring, we get nothing back. Even stranger, when we try to look up

2959
03:59:48.927 --> 03:59:53.305
the function's name, Python tells us the name of the sleepnseconds function is

2960
03:59:53.305 --> 03:59:54.820
wrapper. To understand why,

2961
03:59:54.820 --> 03:59:59.270
We have to examine the timer decorator. Remember that when we write decorators, we

2962
03:59:59.270 --> 04:00:03.720
almost always define a nested function to return. Because the decorator overwrites

2963
04:00:03.720 --> 04:00:08.115
the sleepNseconds function, when you ask for the sleepNseconds docstring or name,

2964
04:00:08.115 --> 04:00:12.674
you are actually referencing the nested function that was returned by the decorator.

2965
04:00:12.674 --> 04:00:17.232
In this case, the nested function was called wrapper and it didn't have a docstring.

2966
04:00:17.232 --> 04:00:21.573
Fortunately, Python provides us with an easy way to fix this. The wraps function

2967
04:00:21.573 --> 04:00:22.930
from the functools module

2968
04:00:22.930 --> 04:00:27.333
is a decorator that you use when defining a decorator. If you use it to decorate the

2969
04:00:27.333 --> 04:00:31.632
wrapper function that your decorator returns, it will modify wrapper's metadata to

2970
04:00:31.632 --> 04:00:36.035
look like the function you are decorating. Notice that the wraps decorator takes the

2971
04:00:36.035 --> 04:00:40.385
function you are decorating as an argument. We haven't talked about decorators that

2972
04:00:40.385 --> 04:00:44.422
take arguments yet, but we will cover that in the next lesson. If we use this

2973
04:00:44.422 --> 04:00:48.668
updated version of the timer decorator to decorate sleepNseconds, and then try to

2974
04:00:48.668 --> 04:00:51.970
print the sleepNseconds docstring, we get the result we expect.

2975
04:00:51.970 --> 04:00:56.876
Likewise, printing the name or any other metadata now gives you the metadata from

2976
04:00:56.876 --> 04:01:01.963
the function being decorated rather than the metadata of the wrapper function. As an

2977
04:01:01.963 --> 04:01:06.990
added bonus, using wraps when creating your decorator also gives you easy access to

2978
04:01:06.990 --> 04:01:11.895
the original undecorated function via the double underscore wrapped attribute. Of

2979
04:01:11.895 --> 04:01:16.922
course, you always had access to this function via the closure, but this is an easy

2980
04:01:16.922 --> 04:01:18.860
way to get to it if you need it.

2981
04:01:18.860 --> 04:01:23.142
Now that you know how to preserve the metadata of the functions you are decorating,

2982
04:01:23.142 --> 04:01:27.115
take a moment to practice. Sometimes it would be nice to add arguments to our

2983
04:01:27.115 --> 04:01:31.191
decorators. To do that, we need to add another level of function nesting. Let's

2984
04:01:31.191 --> 04:01:35.525
consider this silly run three times decorator. If you use it to decorate a function,

2985
04:01:35.525 --> 04:01:39.498
it will run that function three times. If we use it to decorate the print sum

2986
04:01:39.498 --> 04:01:43.781
function, and then run print sum with arguments three and five, it will print eight

2987
04:01:43.781 --> 04:01:44.400
three times.

2988
04:01:44.400 --> 04:01:49.225
Let's think about what we would need to change if we wanted to write a run n times

2989
04:01:49.225 --> 04:01:53.696
decorator. We want to pass n as an argument instead of hard-coding it in the

2990
04:01:53.696 --> 04:01:58.580
decorator. If we had some way to pass n into the decorator, we could decorate print

2991
04:01:58.580 --> 04:02:03.463
sum so that it gets run three times and decorate print hello to run five times. But

2992
04:02:03.463 --> 04:02:08.229
a decorator is only supposed to take one argument, the function it is decorating.

2993
04:02:08.229 --> 04:02:13.113
Also, when you use decorator syntax, you're not supposed to use the parentheses. So

2994
04:02:13.113 --> 04:02:13.760
what gives?

2995
04:02:13.760 --> 04:02:18.752
To make run ntimes work, we have to turn it into a function that returns a decorator

2996
04:02:18.752 --> 04:02:23.685
rather than a function that is a decorator. So let's start by redefining run ntimes

2997
04:02:23.685 --> 04:02:28.677
so that it takes n as an argument instead of func. Then, inside of run ntimes, we'll

2998
04:02:28.677 --> 04:02:33.609
define a new decorator function. This function takes func as an argument because it

2999
04:02:33.609 --> 04:02:38.363
is the function that will be acting as our decorator. We start our new decorator

3000
04:02:38.363 --> 04:02:40.800
with a nested wrapper function, as usual.

3001
04:02:40.800 --> 04:02:45.618
Now, since we are still inside the runNTImes function, we have access to the end

3002
04:02:45.618 --> 04:02:50.677
parameter that was passed to runNTImes. We can use that to control how many times we

3003
04:02:50.677 --> 04:02:55.556
repeat the loop that calls our decorated function. As usual for any decorator, we

3004
04:02:55.556 --> 04:03:00.555
return the new wrapper function. And if runNTImes returns the decorator function we

3005
04:03:00.555 --> 04:03:05.493
just defined, then we can use that return value as a decorator. Notice how when we

3006
04:03:05.493 --> 04:03:09.890
decorate printSum with runNTImes, we use the parentheses after runNTImes.

3007
04:03:09.890 --> 04:03:15.051
This indicates that we are actually calling run ntimes and decorating print sum with

3008
04:03:15.051 --> 04:03:19.782
the result of that function call. Since the return value from run ntimes is a

3009
04:03:19.782 --> 04:03:24.514
decorator function, we can use it to decorate print sum. This is a little bit

3010
04:03:24.514 --> 04:03:29.490
confusing, so let me show you how this works without using decorator syntax. Like

3011
04:03:29.490 --> 04:03:34.406
before, we have a function run ntimes that returns a decorator function when you

3012
04:03:34.406 --> 04:03:39.260
call it. If we call run ntimes with the argument 3, it will return a decorator.

3013
04:03:39.260 --> 04:03:43.863
In fact, it returns the decorator that we defined at the beginning of this lesson,

3014
04:03:43.863 --> 04:03:48.579
run three times. We could decorate print sum with this new decorator using decorator

3015
04:03:48.579 --> 04:03:53.070
syntax. Python makes it convenient to do both of those in a single step, though.

3016
04:03:53.070 --> 04:03:57.561
When we use decorator syntax, the thing that comes after the at symbol must be a

3017
04:03:57.561 --> 04:04:02.164
reference to a decorator function. We can use the name of a specific decorator, or

3018
04:04:02.164 --> 04:04:06.767
we can call a function that returns a decorator. To prove to you that it works the

3019
04:04:06.767 --> 04:04:08.620
way we expect, here is print sum.

3020
04:04:08.620 --> 04:04:13.423
Decorated with run n times 3, when we call print sum with the arguments 3 and 5, it

3021
04:04:13.423 --> 04:04:18.052
prints 8 3 times. And we can just as easily decorate print hello, which prints a

3022
04:04:18.052 --> 04:04:22.912
hello message with run n times 5. When we call print hello, we get 5 hello messages,

3023
04:04:22.912 --> 04:04:27.773
as expected. I try to keep this example simple to explain the concept of decorators.

3024
04:04:27.773 --> 04:04:32.633
In the next lesson, I'll show you some useful things you can do with decorators that

3025
04:04:32.633 --> 04:04:37.436
take arguments like this. In the meantime, try these practice exercises to get more

3026
04:04:37.436 --> 04:04:38.130
comfortable.

3027
04:04:38.130 --> 04:04:42.138
We're going to finish up by looking at an example of a real-world decorator that

3028
04:04:42.138 --> 04:04:46.347
takes an argument to give you a better sense of how they work. Let's imagine that we

3029
04:04:46.347 --> 04:04:50.556
have some functions that occasionally either run for longer than we want them to, or

3030
04:04:50.556 --> 04:04:54.614
just hang and never return. It would be nice if we could add some kind of timeout

3031
04:04:54.614 --> 04:04:58.522
decorator to those functions that will raise an error if the function runs for

3032
04:04:58.522 --> 04:05:02.481
longer than expected. To create the timeout decorator, we are going to use some

3033
04:05:02.481 --> 04:05:06.389
functions from Python's signal module. These functions have nothing to do with

3034
04:05:06.389 --> 04:05:06.940
decorators.

3035
04:05:06.940 --> 04:05:11.799
But understanding them will help you understand the timeout decorator I am about to

3036
04:05:11.799 --> 04:05:16.717
show you. The raiseTimeout function simply raises a timeout error when it is called.

3037
04:05:16.717 --> 04:05:21.635
The signal function tells Python, when you see the signal whose number is signalNum,

3038
04:05:21.635 --> 04:05:26.084
call the handler function. In this case, we tell Python to call raiseTimeout

3039
04:05:26.084 --> 04:05:30.944
whenever it sees the alarm signal. The alarm function lets us set an alarm for some

3040
04:05:30.944 --> 04:05:35.569
number of seconds in the future. Passing zero to the alarm function cancels the

3041
04:05:35.569 --> 04:05:35.920
alarm.

3042
04:05:35.920 --> 04:05:40.292
We'll start by creating a decorator that times out in exactly 5 seconds, and then

3043
04:05:40.292 --> 04:05:44.826
build from there to create a decorator that takes the timeout length as an argument.

3044
04:05:44.826 --> 04:05:49.091
Our timeout in 5 seconds decorator starts off by defining a wrapper function to

3045
04:05:49.091 --> 04:05:53.571
return as the new decorated function. Returning this function is what makes timeout

3046
04:05:53.571 --> 04:05:58.051
in 5 seconds a decorator. First, wrapper sets an alarm for 5 seconds in the future.

3047
04:05:58.051 --> 04:06:02.369
Then it calls the function being decorated. It wraps that call in a try block so

3048
04:06:02.369 --> 04:06:04.960
that in a finally block we can cancel the alarm.

3049
04:06:04.960 --> 04:06:09.872
This ensures that the alarm either rings or gets cancelled. Remember, when the alarm

3050
04:06:09.872 --> 04:06:14.550
rings, Python calls the raiseTimeout function. Let's use timeout in 5 seconds to

3051
04:06:14.550 --> 04:06:19.462
decorate a function that will definitely timeout. foo sleeps for 10 seconds and then

3052
04:06:19.462 --> 04:06:23.848
prints foo. If we call foo, the 5 second alarm will ring before it finishes

3053
04:06:23.848 --> 04:06:28.468
sleeping, and Python will raise a timeout error. Now let's create a more useful

3054
04:06:28.468 --> 04:06:33.380
version of the timeout decorator. This decorator takes an argument. To decorate foo,

3055
04:06:33.380 --> 04:06:37.752
We'll set the timeout to 5 seconds, like we did previously. But, when decorating

3056
04:06:37.752 --> 04:06:42.233
bar, we can set the timeout to 20 seconds. This allows us to set a timeout that is

3057
04:06:42.233 --> 04:06:46.604
appropriate for each function. Timeout is a function that returns a decorator. I

3058
04:06:46.604 --> 04:06:51.085
like to think of it as a decorator factory. When you call timeout, it cranks out a

3059
04:06:51.085 --> 04:06:55.675
brand new decorator that times out in 5 seconds, or 20 seconds, or whatever value we

3060
04:06:55.675 --> 04:07:00.047
pass in as n seconds. The first thing we need to do is define this new decorator

3061
04:07:00.047 --> 04:07:01.140
that it will return.

3062
04:07:01.140 --> 04:07:05.926
That decorator begins, like all of our decorators, by defining a wrapper function to

3063
04:07:05.926 --> 04:07:10.540
return. Now, because nseconds is available to the wrapper function, we can set an

3064
04:07:10.540 --> 04:07:15.098
alarm for nseconds in the future. The rest of the wrapper function looks exactly

3065
04:07:15.098 --> 04:07:19.485
like the wrapper function from the timeout in 5 seconds function. Notice that

3066
04:07:19.485 --> 04:07:24.157
wrapper returns the result of calling func, decorator returns wrapper, and timeout

3067
04:07:24.157 --> 04:07:28.601
returns decorator. So, when we call foo, which has a 5 second timeout, it will

3068
04:07:28.601 --> 04:07:29.740
timeout like before.

3069
04:07:29.740 --> 04:07:34.034
But Bar, which has a 20 second timeout, prints its message in 10 seconds, so the

3070
04:07:34.034 --> 04:07:38.328
alarm gets cancelled. The following exercises will give you a few chances to try

3071
04:07:38.328 --> 04:07:42.729
your hand at creating decorators that take great job. You've covered a lot in this

3072
04:07:42.729 --> 04:07:46.969
course. In the first chapter, you learned how to make high quality functions by

3073
04:07:46.969 --> 04:07:51.424
giving them doc strings and by making sure that they only do one thing. Remembering

3074
04:07:51.424 --> 04:07:55.932
the acronym DRY, or don't repeat yourself, helped you notice when you needed to pull

3075
04:07:55.932 --> 04:07:58.240
part of your code into a reusable function.

3076
04:07:58.240 --> 04:08:02.783
You also learned how Python passes arguments to functions and the difference between

3077
04:08:02.783 --> 04:08:07.326
mutable and immutable variables. In the chapter on context managers, you learned how

3078
04:08:07.326 --> 04:08:11.707
to use the keyword with to enter and then exit a context. You also learned how to

3079
04:08:11.707 --> 04:08:16.034
write your own context managers by using the context manager decorator. You also

3080
04:08:16.034 --> 04:08:20.469
spent a lot of time in this course understanding decorators, how they work, how to

3081
04:08:20.469 --> 04:08:24.580
use them, and how to write decorators of your own. Finally, in chapter four,

3082
04:08:24.580 --> 04:08:28.593
You learned how to use functools.wraps to make sure your decorated functions

3083
04:08:28.593 --> 04:08:32.500
maintain their metadata. And you learned how to write decorators that take

3084
04:08:32.500 --> 04:08:36.724
arguments. It has been an honor to spend this time with you. I wanted to let you

3085
04:08:36.724 --> 04:08:41.054
know that I'll be donating a portion of the proceeds from this course to WIMLDS, a

3086
04:08:41.054 --> 04:08:45.489
nonprofit that helps support and promote women in the fields of machine learning and

3087
04:08:45.489 --> 04:08:49.924
data science. I'll be looking forward to hearing about all of the amazing things you

3088
04:08:49.924 --> 04:08:50.980
do with Py... Hello!

3089
04:08:50.980 --> 04:08:55.838
And welcome to unit testing for data science in Python. My name is Dibbo. I'm a test

3090
04:08:55.838 --> 04:09:00.523
automation engineer, and I will be your instructor for this course. Consider this

3091
04:09:00.523 --> 04:09:04.976
question. Suppose we have just implemented a Python function. How can we test

3092
04:09:04.976 --> 04:09:09.661
whether our implementation is correct? The easiest way is to open an interpreter,

3093
04:09:09.661 --> 04:09:14.519
test the function on a few arguments, and check whether the return value is correct.

3094
04:09:14.519 --> 04:09:18.220
If correct, we can accept the implementation and move on, right?

3095
04:09:18.220 --> 04:09:23.325
While testing on the interpreter is easy, it is actually very inefficient. This will

3096
04:09:23.325 --> 04:09:28.308
become clear if we think about the big picture of a function's lifecycle in a data

3097
04:09:28.308 --> 04:09:33.413
science project. The lifecycle of a function typically looks like this. We implement

3098
04:09:33.413 --> 04:09:38.396
the function and then test it. If the tests pass, we accept the implementation. If

3099
04:09:38.396 --> 04:09:43.441
the tests fail, we fix any bugs that we found and test again. Later, we might get a

3100
04:09:43.441 --> 04:09:47.330
new feature request or we may be asked to refactor the function.

3101
04:09:47.330 --> 04:09:51.883
So we implement the new feature or refactor the code and then test it again.

3102
04:09:51.883 --> 04:09:56.856
Alternatively, someone might discover a previously unseen bug. In that case, we fix

3103
04:09:56.856 --> 04:10:01.829
that bug and test again. Notice how many times a function needs to be tested during

3104
04:10:01.829 --> 04:10:06.323
the life cycle. Every time we modify the function, either to fix bugs or to

3105
04:10:06.323 --> 04:10:11.296
implement new features, we have to test it. If a project continues for a few years,

3106
04:10:11.296 --> 04:10:15.310
we might be testing the function about a hundred times, maybe more.

3107
04:10:15.310 --> 04:10:19.915
Let's look at an example function. It's called RowToList. It takes a single

3108
04:10:19.915 --> 04:10:25.072
argument, which is a Python string. The string is a single row in a data file, which

3109
04:10:25.072 --> 04:10:30.230
contains data on housing area and market price of the house. The string contains the

3110
04:10:30.230 --> 04:10:35.387
housing area in square feet, followed by a single tab, followed by the housing price

3111
04:10:35.387 --> 04:10:40.483
in dollars, and ending with a newline character. If the string follows this format,

3112
04:10:40.483 --> 04:10:44.720
the function returns a list of length 2, containing the housing area,

3113
04:10:44.720 --> 04:10:49.485
And the housing price. But this data file is not clean, and some rows in this data

3114
04:10:49.485 --> 04:10:53.726
file do not follow this format. The third row has missing area, while the

3115
04:10:53.726 --> 04:10:58.374
penultimate row is missing the tab between the area and price. For these invalid

3116
04:10:58.374 --> 04:11:03.081
rows, the function should return none. To test this function, we will have to try

3117
04:11:03.081 --> 04:11:07.613
all the arguments that we listed and check if the function returns the correct

3118
04:11:07.613 --> 04:11:12.029
value. Remembering that a function needs to be tested about 100 times in its

3119
04:11:12.029 --> 04:11:12.610
lifecycle,

3120
04:11:12.610 --> 04:11:17.342
And assuming it takes 5 minutes to test each time, we will spend 8 hours just

3121
04:11:17.342 --> 04:11:22.319
testing this function on the interpreter. Unit tests automate this repetitive and

3122
04:11:22.319 --> 04:11:27.481
tedious testing process. Unit tests will reduce the testing time over the life cycle

3123
04:11:27.481 --> 04:11:32.643
of a single function to 1 hour instead of 8. Now imagine how much time we would save

3124
04:11:32.643 --> 04:11:37.252
when we unit test all the functions in a project. This makes unit testing a

3125
04:11:37.252 --> 04:11:40.140
must-have skill for productive data scientists.

3126
04:11:40.140 --> 04:11:44.791
This course will teach you how to write unit tests. We have created an example data

3127
04:11:44.791 --> 04:11:48.882
science project, which predicts prices from the housing area using linear

3128
04:11:48.882 --> 04:11:53.589
regression, as we can see in the plot. The complete code for this project, including

3129
04:11:53.589 --> 04:11:58.015
actual implementations for functions like row to list, is available in a public

3130
04:11:58.015 --> 04:12:02.330
GitHub repository. We will share the link to the repository at the end of the

3131
04:12:02.330 --> 04:12:05.300
course. Here is the folder structure of this project.

3132
04:12:05.300 --> 04:12:10.087
During this course, we are going to write a complete unit test suite for this

3133
04:12:10.087 --> 04:12:15.185
example project. It's going to be fun and exciting. This will prepare you to write

3134
04:12:15.185 --> 04:12:20.345
unit tests for your own projects. Sounds good? Then let's get started by practicing

3135
04:12:20.345 --> 04:12:25.257
the concepts. In the last exercise, you went through a function's lifecycle. At

3136
04:12:25.257 --> 04:12:30.479
every step, you tested it by calling rowToList on different arguments and checked if

3137
04:12:30.479 --> 04:12:33.650
the return values are correct. This was repetitive,

3138
04:12:33.650 --> 04:12:39.221
tedious and time-consuming. In this lesson, we will learn to write unit tests and

3139
04:12:39.221 --> 04:12:44.998
improve this process. There are many Python libraries for writing unit tests such as

3140
04:12:44.998 --> 04:12:50.706
PyTest, UnitTest, NoseTest, and DocTest. We will use PyTest for this course because

3141
04:12:50.706 --> 04:12:56.002
PyTest has all essential features, is easiest to use, and is the most popular

3142
04:12:56.002 --> 04:12:57.790
testing library in Python.

3143
04:12:57.790 --> 04:13:02.349
To start unit testing with pytest, we will first create a file called

3144
04:13:02.349 --> 04:13:07.636
testRowToList.py. When pytest sees a file name starting with test underscore, it

3145
04:13:07.636 --> 04:13:13.186
understands that this is not an usual Python file, but a special one containing unit

3146
04:13:13.186 --> 04:13:18.671
tests. We must make sure to follow this naming convention. Files holding unit tests

3147
04:13:18.671 --> 04:13:24.222
are also called test modules, and we just created our first test module. In the test

3148
04:13:24.222 --> 04:13:26.270
module, we first import pytest.

3149
04:13:26.270 --> 04:13:31.496
Then we import the function under test. A unit test is written as a Python function

3150
04:13:31.496 --> 04:13:36.408
whose name starts with a test underscore, just like the test module. This way,

3151
04:13:36.408 --> 04:13:41.571
PyTest can tell that it is a unit test and not an ordinary function. The unit test

3152
04:13:41.571 --> 04:13:46.797
usually corresponds to exactly one entry in the argument and return value table for

3153
04:13:46.797 --> 04:13:52.086
rowToList. The unit test checks whether rowToList has the expected return value when

3154
04:13:52.086 --> 04:13:54.290
called on this particular argument.

3155
04:13:54.290 --> 04:13:59.537
This particular argument is a clean row, so we call the unit test, testForCleanRow.

3156
04:13:59.537 --> 04:14:04.722
The actual check is done via an assert statement, and every test must contain one.

3157
04:14:04.722 --> 04:14:09.527
The assert statement has a required first argument, which can be any Boolean

3158
04:14:09.527 --> 04:14:14.521
expression. If the expression is true, the assert statement passes, giving us a

3159
04:14:14.521 --> 04:14:19.010
blank output. If the expression is false, it raises an assertion error.

3160
04:14:19.010 --> 04:14:23.973
In this case, we want to check if rowToList returns the correct list when called on

3161
04:14:23.973 --> 04:14:28.636
the clean row. The expression we use is rowToList called on the argument equal

3162
04:14:28.636 --> 04:14:33.599
equals the correct list. If the function works, this will evaluate to true, and the

3163
04:14:33.599 --> 04:14:38.621
assert statement will pass. This will make the test pass. If the function has a bug,

3164
04:14:38.621 --> 04:14:43.524
it will evaluate to false, the assert statement will raise an assertion error, and

3165
04:14:43.524 --> 04:14:44.660
the test will fail.

3166
04:14:44.660 --> 04:14:49.566
For the second row in the table, we create a unit test called testOnMissingArea

3167
04:14:49.566 --> 04:14:54.782
because the argument has missing area data. Then we assert that the return value for

3168
04:14:54.782 --> 04:14:59.874
this argument is none. Note that the correct way to check if a variable's value is

3169
04:14:59.874 --> 04:15:04.966
none is to use the Boolean expression var isNone and not var equal equalsNone. For

3170
04:15:04.966 --> 04:15:10.058
the third row in the table, we create a unit test called testForMissingTab because

3171
04:15:10.058 --> 04:15:13.660
the argument is missing the tab separating area and price.

3172
04:15:13.660 --> 04:15:18.712
The assert statement is similar to the second test. To test whether RotoList is

3173
04:15:18.712 --> 04:15:23.891
working at any time in its lifecycle, we simply run the test module. The standard

3174
04:15:23.891 --> 04:15:28.879
way to run tests is to open a common line and type pytest followed by the test

3175
04:15:28.879 --> 04:15:34.186
module name. In the DataCamp exercises, we can't create files directly, so you will

3176
04:15:34.186 --> 04:15:39.493
define the unit tests in the script.py area, which is highlighted here. In the next

3177
04:15:39.493 --> 04:15:40.900
step or next exercise,

3178
04:15:40.900 --> 04:15:45.607
We will write the tests to a test module in the background and tell you its file

3179
04:15:45.607 --> 04:15:50.490
path. This is highlighted in this slide. Once you know the test module's file path,

3180
04:15:50.490 --> 04:15:55.197
you can run the tests in the IPython console at the bottom, which is highlighted

3181
04:15:55.197 --> 04:15:59.962
here. You can run any command line expression in the IPython console by adding an

3182
04:15:59.962 --> 04:16:04.845
exclamation mark before the expression. For example, to run the pytest command, you

3183
04:16:04.845 --> 04:16:08.140
have to use exclamation pytest, as shown in the picture.

3184
04:16:08.140 --> 04:16:13.242
Running this command will output the test result report, which contains information

3185
04:16:13.242 --> 04:16:18.405
about bugs in the function, if any. We will cover the test result report in the next

3186
04:16:18.405 --> 04:16:23.138
video lesson. But now, let's practice writing some simple unit tests. In this

3187
04:16:23.138 --> 04:16:28.178
lesson, we will take a deeper look at the output generated when we run tests. This

3188
04:16:28.178 --> 04:16:33.219
output is called the test result report and contains information that will help us

3189
04:16:33.219 --> 04:16:37.460
fix bugs. As an example, we will use the test module testRowToList.py

3190
04:16:37.460 --> 04:16:42.483
which we saw in the previous video lesson. It contains three unit tests for the

3191
04:16:42.483 --> 04:16:47.823
row-to-list function. The unit tests check if row-to-list returns the correct return

3192
04:16:47.823 --> 04:16:53.037
values for clean rows, rows missing area data, and rows missing the tab separator,

3193
04:16:53.037 --> 04:16:58.313
respectively. Running the tests in the IPython console produces a lot of output. So

3194
04:16:58.313 --> 04:17:03.654
much output that we had to truncate it in this slide. This is called the test result

3195
04:17:03.654 --> 04:17:06.960
report. We will break this down into smaller pieces.

3196
04:17:06.960 --> 04:17:12.122
and understand them individually. The first section provides information about the

3197
04:17:12.122 --> 04:17:17.220
operating system, Python version, PyTest package versions, the working directory,

3198
04:17:17.220 --> 04:17:22.130
and PyTest plugins. There is not much to say about this section, so let's move

3199
04:17:22.130 --> 04:17:27.418
ahead. The next bit is important. The output says collected three items, which means

3200
04:17:27.418 --> 04:17:32.013
that PyTest found three tests to run. This is accurate as the test module

3201
04:17:32.013 --> 04:17:34.720
TestRowToList.py contains three unit tests.

3202
04:17:34.720 --> 04:17:39.950
The next line contains the test module name, which is test row to list dot pi,

3203
04:17:39.950 --> 04:17:45.382
followed by the characters dot, capital F, and dot. Each character represents the

3204
04:17:45.382 --> 04:17:51.015
result of a unit test. The character capital F stands for failure. A unit test fails

3205
04:17:51.015 --> 04:17:56.513
if an exception is raised when running the unit test code. This happens most often

3206
04:17:56.513 --> 04:17:58.504
when the assert statement raises an assertion error.

3207
04:17:58.504 --> 04:18:03.786
This means that the function has a bug and we should fix it. A unit test may also

3208
04:18:03.786 --> 04:18:09.067
fail if a different exception is raised while running the unit test code. In this

3209
04:18:09.067 --> 04:18:14.283
case, execution will stop before the assert statement is run. For example, if we

3210
04:18:14.283 --> 04:18:18.130
wrote a none with a small n, this would raise a name error.

3211
04:18:18.130 --> 04:18:23.127
Since the assert statement did not run, we cannot conclude anything about the

3212
04:18:23.127 --> 04:18:28.514
function under test from the capital F result. In this case, we should fix the unit

3213
04:18:28.514 --> 04:18:33.901
test so that it can actually run the assert statement. DOT means that the unit test

3214
04:18:33.901 --> 04:18:39.157
passed. This means no exceptions were raised by the assert statement or any other

3215
04:18:39.157 --> 04:18:44.544
part of the unit test. For the test module TestRowToList.py, the first test passed,

3216
04:18:44.544 --> 04:18:47.140
the second failed, and the third passed.

3217
04:18:47.140 --> 04:18:52.370
The next section contains detailed information about failed tests. We can see that

3218
04:18:52.370 --> 04:18:57.663
the unit test test on missing area failed. The line raising the exception is marked

3219
04:18:57.663 --> 04:19:02.829
by a greater than sign on the left. In this case, it is the assert statement. The

3220
04:19:02.829 --> 04:19:08.186
following lines marked with capitally on the left contains details of the exception.

3221
04:19:08.186 --> 04:19:13.097
In this case, the assert statement raised an assertion error. The lines which

3222
04:19:13.097 --> 04:19:14.500
contain the word where

3223
04:19:14.500 --> 04:19:20.527
displays any return values that were calculated when running the assert statement.

3224
04:19:20.527 --> 04:19:25.966
In this case, it shows that the actual return value of rowToList is a list

3225
04:19:25.966 --> 04:19:32.067
containing an empty string and the string 293,410. The expected return value is, of

3226
04:19:32.067 --> 04:19:37.653
course, none. The mismatch between the expected and actual value will be our

3227
04:19:37.653 --> 04:19:43.606
starting point for debugging. We would have to figure out why rowToList returns a

3228
04:19:43.606 --> 04:19:43.900
list

3229
04:19:43.900 --> 04:19:50.068
instead of none in this case. The final line is a test result summary, saying that

3230
04:19:50.068 --> 04:19:56.162
one test failed and two passed. Additionally, we also find out that the test took

3231
04:19:56.162 --> 04:20:02.330
0.03 seconds to run. That's really fast compared to the time we would need to test

3232
04:20:02.330 --> 04:20:08.198
using the interpreter. Now, let's practice reading test results. In a previous

3233
04:20:08.198 --> 04:20:12.260
lesson, we learned that unit tests save a lot of time.

3234
04:20:12.260 --> 04:20:17.352
But the benefits of unit testing goes beyond time savings. The unit tests that we

3235
04:20:17.352 --> 04:20:22.382
wrote for row to list also serve as documentation. If a collaborator didn't know

3236
04:20:22.382 --> 04:20:27.600
this function's purpose, they could recreate the argument and return value table on

3237
04:20:27.600 --> 04:20:32.756
the right by looking at the Boolean expressions used in the assert statements. The

3238
04:20:32.756 --> 04:20:37.471
table would give them a good hint about what row to list does, helping them

3239
04:20:37.471 --> 04:20:39.860
understand the function's code faster.

3240
04:20:39.860 --> 04:20:45.757
To mimic this real-life situation, some exercises may ask you to guess a function's

3241
04:20:45.757 --> 04:20:51.725
job by looking at a test module. In this case, just type exclamation cat followed by

3242
04:20:51.725 --> 04:20:57.551
the test module name in the IPython console to see the unit tests. Unit tests also

3243
04:20:57.551 --> 04:21:03.234
increase trust in a package, as users can run the unit tests and verify that the

3244
04:21:03.234 --> 04:21:09.060
functions work. In the picture, we see NumPy's GitHub page. This highlighted badge

3245
04:21:09.060 --> 04:21:14.707
shows how much of the code base is unit tested and this other badge shows whether

3246
04:21:14.707 --> 04:21:20.284
the tests are passing. Users trust NumPy more because of these badges. We should

3247
04:21:20.284 --> 04:21:26.000
implement these badges for our projects too because user trust is important and we

3248
04:21:26.000 --> 04:21:31.716
will learn how to do that later in the course. Unit tests can also reduce downtime

3249
04:21:31.716 --> 04:21:37.572
for a productive system. Suppose we make a mistake and push bad code to a productive

3250
04:21:37.572 --> 04:21:38.060
system.

3251
04:21:38.060 --> 04:21:43.538
This will bring the system down and annoy users. We can cure this by setting up

3252
04:21:43.538 --> 04:21:49.293
continuous integration or CI. CI runs all unit tests when any code is pushed and if

3253
04:21:49.293 --> 04:21:54.978
any unit test fails, it rejects the change preventing downtime. It also informs us

3254
04:21:54.978 --> 04:22:00.387
that the code needs to be fixed. If we run productive systems that many people

3255
04:22:00.387 --> 04:22:06.003
depend on, we must write unit tests and set up CI. We will see an example of this

3256
04:22:06.003 --> 04:22:07.390
later in the course.

3257
04:22:07.390 --> 04:22:13.297
All of these benefits make the case stronger for writing unit tests. In this course,

3258
04:22:13.297 --> 04:22:19.203
we will write unit tests for all functions in the example linear regression project.

3259
04:22:19.203 --> 04:22:25.110
We already wrote tests for row to list and convert to int. They are part of the data

3260
04:22:25.110 --> 04:22:31.017
module which creates a clean data file from raw data on housing area and price. Very

3261
04:22:31.017 --> 04:22:36.220
soon, we will see functions from the feature module which compute features

3262
04:22:36.220 --> 04:22:42.439
from the clean data. Later in the course, we will meet the models module, which will

3263
04:22:42.439 --> 04:22:48.362
output a model for predicting housing price from the features. The tests that we

3264
04:22:48.362 --> 04:22:54.582
wrote are called unit tests because they test a unit, such as row to list. A unit is

3265
04:22:54.582 --> 04:23:00.431
any small independent piece of code and could be a Python function or class. In

3266
04:23:00.431 --> 04:23:02.430
contrast, integration tests

3267
04:23:02.430 --> 04:23:07.886
Check if multiple units work well together when they are connected, and not just

3268
04:23:07.886 --> 04:23:13.478
independently. For example, we could check if the data and the feature module work

3269
04:23:13.478 --> 04:23:19.069
well when connected. Here, the argument will be the raw data and the return values

3270
04:23:19.069 --> 04:23:24.661
to check would be the features. End-to-end tests check the whole software at once.

3271
04:23:24.661 --> 04:23:30.185
They start from one end, which is the unprocessed data file, goes through all the

3272
04:23:30.185 --> 04:23:31.890
units till the other end,

3273
04:23:31.890 --> 04:23:38.184
and checks whether we get the correct model. This course is focused on unit tests as

3274
04:23:38.184 --> 04:23:44.252
this is really the best way to learn about PyTest. In Chapter 2, we will dig much

3275
04:23:44.252 --> 04:23:50.321
deeper into PyTest and write more advanced unit tests for the Features and Models

3276
04:23:50.321 --> 04:23:56.090
module. Exciting! But for now, let's practice the concept. Congratulations on

3277
04:23:56.090 --> 04:24:01.260
completing the first chapter. You can already write basic unit tests,

3278
04:24:01.260 --> 04:24:06.288
And in this lesson, we will learn more about the assert statement. So far, we have

3279
04:24:06.288 --> 04:24:11.071
used only a Boolean expression as an argument of the assert statement. But the

3280
04:24:11.071 --> 04:24:15.793
assert statement can take an optional second argument called the message. The

3281
04:24:15.793 --> 04:24:20.883
message is only printed when the assert statement raises an assertion error, and it

3282
04:24:20.883 --> 04:24:25.911
should contain information about why the assertion error was raised. If the assert

3283
04:24:25.911 --> 04:24:28.180
statement passes, nothing is printed.

3284
04:24:28.180 --> 04:24:32.988
Let's look at the unit test called test for missing area for the row to list

3285
04:24:32.988 --> 04:24:38.238
function that we encountered in chapter one. We could enhance this test by adding a

3286
04:24:38.238 --> 04:24:43.236
message. We first captured the return value of row to list in a variable called

3287
04:24:43.236 --> 04:24:48.550
actual. We define a variable called expected holding the expected return value. Then

3288
04:24:48.550 --> 04:24:51.460
we define a message that contains the argument

3289
04:24:51.460 --> 04:24:57.146
The actual and expected return values. Basically everything that we need to debug in

3290
04:24:57.146 --> 04:25:02.832
case the test fails. We follow it up with the appropriate assert statement including

3291
04:25:02.832 --> 04:25:08.247
the message. Here on the top we run the unit test without the message and we get

3292
04:25:08.247 --> 04:25:13.662
PyTest's automatic output on failed tests. On the bottom we run the modified one

3293
04:25:13.662 --> 04:25:18.807
with the message. Now the automatic output is gone and we get the nice human

3294
04:25:18.807 --> 04:25:19.890
readable message

3295
04:25:19.890 --> 04:25:25.500
next to the assertion error. It is recommended to include a message with assertions

3296
04:25:25.500 --> 04:25:31.042
because it is much easier to read and understand than the automatic output. In the

3297
04:25:31.042 --> 04:25:36.584
message, print values of any variable of choice that may be relevant to debugging.

3298
04:25:36.584 --> 04:25:42.261
Next, we will learn about an especially tricky situation when the function returns a

3299
04:25:42.261 --> 04:25:47.330
float. In Python, comparisons between floats don't always work as expected.

3300
04:25:47.330 --> 04:25:52.722
As we can see in this surprising example. Because of the way Python represents

3301
04:25:52.722 --> 04:25:58.184
floats, the digits on the right might be different from what we expect, causing

3302
04:25:58.184 --> 04:26:03.991
comparisons to fail. Why this happens is out of the scope of this course. The bottom

3303
04:26:03.991 --> 04:26:09.729
line is, we should not use the usual way to compare floats in the assert statement.

3304
04:26:09.729 --> 04:26:15.467
Instead, we should use the pytest.approx function to wrap the expected value, as we

3305
04:26:15.467 --> 04:26:16.850
see in this example.

3306
04:26:16.850 --> 04:26:22.825
This ensures that the digits far on the right are ignored and we can compare floats

3307
04:26:22.825 --> 04:26:28.871
safely. We get an empty output since the assert statement passes. pytest.approx also

3308
04:26:28.871 --> 04:26:34.774
works for NumPy arrays containing floats. For example, notice how we are passing a

3309
04:26:34.774 --> 04:26:40.605
NumPy array to the pytest.approx function in the assert statements shown here. So

3310
04:26:40.605 --> 04:26:44.780
far, we have only seen one assert statement per unit test.

3311
04:26:44.780 --> 04:26:50.351
But unit tests can have more than one assert statement. Remember the convertToInt

3312
04:26:50.351 --> 04:26:55.923
function which converts an integer valued string with commas to an integer? Let's

3313
04:26:55.923 --> 04:27:01.425
look at a unit test which you wrote for convertToInt in one of the exercises. We

3314
04:27:01.425 --> 04:27:06.790
will modify this unit test on the right. We first want to test if the function

3315
04:27:06.790 --> 04:27:11.330
returns an integer at all. For this we use the isInstance function

3316
04:27:11.330 --> 04:27:16.979
which takes the return value as a first argument and the expected type of the return

3317
04:27:16.979 --> 04:27:22.158
value as a second argument, which is int in this case. We follow this up with

3318
04:27:22.158 --> 04:27:27.404
another assert statement which checks if the return value matches the expected

3319
04:27:27.404 --> 04:27:32.918
value. The modified test will pass if both assert statements pass. It will fail if

3320
04:27:32.918 --> 04:27:38.433
any of them raises an assertion error. Wow! We have just learned quite a bit about

3321
04:27:38.433 --> 04:27:39.980
assert statements. Now,

3322
04:27:39.980 --> 04:27:45.278
So far, we have used the assert statement to check if a function returns the

3323
04:27:45.278 --> 04:27:51.134
expected value. However, some functions may not return anything, but rather raise an

3324
04:27:51.134 --> 04:27:56.710
exception when called on certain arguments. Consider the split into training and

3325
04:27:56.710 --> 04:28:02.496
testing sets function that you tested in the last exercise. This function returns a

3326
04:28:02.496 --> 04:28:06.400
two tuple containing the training and the testing array.

3327
04:28:06.400 --> 04:28:12.286
It puts 75% of the rows of the argument numpy array into the training array and the

3328
04:28:12.286 --> 04:28:18.244
rest of the rows into the testing array. This function expects the argument array to

3329
04:28:18.244 --> 04:28:23.563
have rows and columns. That is, the argument array must be two-dimensional.

3330
04:28:23.563 --> 04:28:29.449
Otherwise, splitting by rows is undefined. So if we pass a one-dimensional array to

3331
04:28:29.449 --> 04:28:34.910
this function, it should not return anything, but rather raise a value error.

3332
04:28:34.910 --> 04:28:39.988
which is a specific type of exception. We will now learn how to test whether this

3333
04:28:39.988 --> 04:28:45.191
function raises value error when it gets a one-dimensional array as argument. Let's

3334
04:28:45.191 --> 04:28:49.955
call the unit test test value error on one-dimensional argument. The example

3335
04:28:49.955 --> 04:28:55.221
argument is a one-dimensional array as shown here. Then we use a with statement that

3336
04:28:55.221 --> 04:29:00.424
we will explain in detail now. First, let's understand the with statement. Any code

3337
04:29:00.424 --> 04:29:04.060
that is inside the with statement is known as the context.

3338
04:29:04.060 --> 04:29:09.135
The with statement takes a single argument, which is known as the context manager.

3339
04:29:09.135 --> 04:29:14.086
The context manager runs some code before entering and exiting the context, just

3340
04:29:14.086 --> 04:29:18.727
like a security guard who checks or does something when we enter or leave a

3341
04:29:18.727 --> 04:29:23.926
building. In this case, we are using a context manager called pytest.races. It takes

3342
04:29:23.926 --> 04:29:29.062
a single argument, which is the type of exception that we are checking for, in this

3343
04:29:29.062 --> 04:29:30.300
case, a value error.

3344
04:29:30.300 --> 04:29:35.446
This context manager does not run any code on entering the context, but it does

3345
04:29:35.446 --> 04:29:40.592
something on exit. If the code in the context raises a value error, the context

3346
04:29:40.592 --> 04:29:45.868
manager silences the error. And if the code in the context does not raise a value

3347
04:29:45.868 --> 04:29:51.145
error, the context manager raises an exception itself. Here is an example where a

3348
04:29:51.145 --> 04:29:55.900
value error is raised in the context and silenced by the context manager.

3349
04:29:55.900 --> 04:30:00.953
The second code block shows an example where no value error is raised and the

3350
04:30:00.953 --> 04:30:06.466
context manager raises an exception called failed. Getting back to the unit test, we

3351
04:30:06.466 --> 04:30:11.848
call the function on the one-dimensional array inside the context. If the function

3352
04:30:11.848 --> 04:30:17.360
raises a value error as expected, it will be silenced and the test will pass. If the

3353
04:30:17.360 --> 04:30:22.807
function is buggy and no value error is raised, the context manager raises a failed

3354
04:30:22.807 --> 04:30:25.170
exception, causing the test to fail.

3355
04:30:25.170 --> 04:30:30.564
We can unit test details of the raised exception as well. For example, we might want

3356
04:30:30.564 --> 04:30:35.894
to check if the raised value error contains the correct error message, which starts

3357
04:30:35.894 --> 04:30:41.096
with, argument data array must be two-dimensional. In order to do that, we extend

3358
04:30:41.096 --> 04:30:46.105
the with statement with the as clause. If the value error is raised within the

3359
04:30:46.105 --> 04:30:51.114
context, then exception info will contain information about the silenced value

3360
04:30:51.114 --> 04:30:53.040
error. After the context ends,

3361
04:30:53.040 --> 04:30:58.428
We can check whether ExceptionInfo has the correct message. To do this, we use a

3362
04:30:58.428 --> 04:31:03.817
simple assert statement with the MATCH method of ExceptionInfo. The MATCH method

3363
04:31:03.817 --> 04:31:09.475
takes a string as argument and checks if the string is present in the error message.

3364
04:31:09.475 --> 04:31:15.132
We have learned a lot. Now let's practice the pytest.raises. In this lesson, we will

3365
04:31:15.132 --> 04:31:19.780
explore the question, how many tests should one write for a function?

3366
04:31:19.780 --> 04:31:24.840
Consider the split into training and testing sets function that we saw in earlier

3367
04:31:24.840 --> 04:31:29.650
lessons. This function takes a two-dimensional numpy array as an argument and

3368
04:31:29.650 --> 04:31:34.647
randomly puts about 75% of its rows into a training array and the remaining rows

3369
04:31:34.647 --> 04:31:39.894
into a testing array. It returns the training and the testing arrays as a two-tuple.

3370
04:31:39.894 --> 04:31:44.954
Because this function has randomness, we test for the lengths of the training and

3371
04:31:44.954 --> 04:31:47.890
testing arrays rather than their actual values.

3372
04:31:47.890 --> 04:31:52.893
The length of the training array is given by the integer part of 0.75 times the

3373
04:31:52.893 --> 04:31:58.213
number of rows in the argument. The testing array gets the rest. For example, if the

3374
04:31:58.213 --> 04:32:03.217
argument has 8 rows, then the training array should have 6 rows and the testing

3375
04:32:03.217 --> 04:32:07.967
array should have 2 rows. In general, the more arguments we check, the more

3376
04:32:07.967 --> 04:32:12.970
confident we can be that the function is working correctly. But since we cannot

3377
04:32:12.970 --> 04:32:17.150
write tests for hundreds of arguments because of time limitations,

3378
04:32:17.150 --> 04:32:22.257
How many tests can be considered enough? The best practice is to pick a few from

3379
04:32:22.257 --> 04:32:27.236
each of the following categories of arguments, which are called bad arguments,

3380
04:32:27.236 --> 04:32:32.599
special arguments, and normal arguments. If we have tested for all of these argument

3381
04:32:32.599 --> 04:32:37.770
types, then our function can be declared well-tested. Let's define these argument

3382
04:32:37.770 --> 04:32:41.600
types for the split into training and testing sets function.

3383
04:32:41.600 --> 04:32:46.348
Bad arguments are arguments for which the function raises an exception instead of

3384
04:32:46.348 --> 04:32:50.921
returning a value. For split into training and testing sets, a one-dimensional

3385
04:32:50.921 --> 04:32:55.728
array, like the one shown, is a bad argument. It doesn't have rows and columns, so

3386
04:32:55.728 --> 04:33:00.652
splitting row-wise doesn't make any sense. The expected outcome is a value error. An

3387
04:33:00.652 --> 04:33:05.459
array with a single row is also a bad argument, because this row can be put in the

3388
04:33:05.459 --> 04:33:09.680
training array, but then the testing array will be empty, or vice versa.

3389
04:33:09.680 --> 04:33:14.512
Empty training or testing arrays are useless, so the function raises a value error.

3390
04:33:14.512 --> 04:33:19.285
Next comes special arguments. These are of two types, boundary values and argument

3391
04:33:19.285 --> 04:33:24.117
values for which the function uses a special logic to produce the written value. So

3392
04:33:24.117 --> 04:33:28.890
what are boundary values? If we look at the number of rows of the argument, we see

3393
04:33:28.890 --> 04:33:33.780
that the function raises a value error for one row, but returns training and testing

3394
04:33:33.780 --> 04:33:36.400
array for arguments having more than one row.

3395
04:33:36.400 --> 04:33:41.472
The value 2 marks the boundary for this behavior change, and therefore is a boundary

3396
04:33:41.472 --> 04:33:46.543
value. So we append the test arguments table with the boundary value. The other type

3397
04:33:46.543 --> 04:33:51.494
of special arguments are those that trigger special logic in the function. Look at

3398
04:33:51.494 --> 04:33:56.264
the last row where the argument has 4 rows. The standard logic of a 75% and 25%

3399
04:33:56.264 --> 04:34:01.336
split would produce a training array with 3 rows and a testing array with 1 row. But

3400
04:34:01.336 --> 04:34:05.260
we might want the function to return a training array with 2 rows

3401
04:34:05.260 --> 04:34:10.368
and testing array with two rows instead. Then four rows would be a special case

3402
04:34:10.368 --> 04:34:15.799
because the function isn't using the usual 75% and 25% logic. Finally, anything that

3403
04:34:15.799 --> 04:34:21.101
is not a bad or special argument is a normal argument. First notice that since the

3404
04:34:21.101 --> 04:34:26.208
function uses special logic at four, the behavior at four is different from the

3405
04:34:26.208 --> 04:34:31.446
behavior on either side of four. This turns three and five which flanks four into

3406
04:34:31.446 --> 04:34:32.480
boundary values.

3407
04:34:32.480 --> 04:34:37.754
The remaining arguments with number of rows exceeding 5 are then normal arguments.

3408
04:34:37.754 --> 04:34:42.643
It is recommended to test for 2 or 3 normal arguments. The final rows of the

3409
04:34:42.643 --> 04:34:47.917
argument table correspond to 2 normal arguments, having 6 and 8 rows respectively.

3410
04:34:47.917 --> 04:34:52.741
If we test the split into training and testing sets function with all these

3411
04:34:52.741 --> 04:34:57.887
arguments, then we can declare it well tested. When applying this logic to other

3412
04:34:57.887 --> 04:34:58.530
functions,

3413
04:34:58.530 --> 04:35:03.911
Note that not all functions have special or bad arguments. In that case, we should

3414
04:35:03.911 --> 04:35:09.293
just ignore those types of arguments. Enough theory. Let's now apply this to other

3415
04:35:09.293 --> 04:35:14.543
functions in the end. By now, we understand why it is so important to write unit

3416
04:35:14.543 --> 04:35:20.055
tests. But in the real world, it is all too common to skip writing them. Bosses want

3417
04:35:20.055 --> 04:35:25.568
to prioritize feature implementation because they want fast results. Unit tests only

3418
04:35:25.568 --> 04:35:26.880
get second priority.

3419
04:35:26.880 --> 04:35:32.107
So we tell ourselves that we will write the not-so-urgent unit tests tomorrow.

3420
04:35:32.107 --> 04:35:37.668
Eventually, the unit tests never get written. Of course, we pay for this mistake in

3421
04:35:37.668 --> 04:35:43.230
the long term. In this lesson, we will learn a new coding method called test-driven

3422
04:35:43.230 --> 04:35:48.791
development, or TDD in short, which tries to ensure that unit tests do get written.

3423
04:35:48.791 --> 04:35:54.286
We got introduced to the lifecycle of a function in Chapter 1. A function is first

3424
04:35:54.286 --> 04:35:55.090
implemented,

3425
04:35:55.090 --> 04:36:01.280
and then it is tested, according to this lifecycle. Test-driven development alters

3426
04:36:01.280 --> 04:36:07.018
the usual lifecycle by adding a single step before implementation. This step

3427
04:36:07.018 --> 04:36:13.359
involves writing unit tests for the function. Yes, that's right. We write tests even

3428
04:36:13.359 --> 04:36:19.624
before the function is implemented in code. By making unit tests a precondition for

3429
04:36:19.624 --> 04:36:23.550
implementation, this ensures that writing unit tests

3430
04:36:23.550 --> 04:36:29.346
cannot be postponed or de-prioritized. It also means that we, along with our bosses,

3431
04:36:29.346 --> 04:36:35.005
should factor in the time for writing unit tests as a part of implementation time.

3432
04:36:35.005 --> 04:36:40.732
Furthermore, when we write unit tests first, we have to think of possible arguments

3433
04:36:40.732 --> 04:36:46.391
and return values, which includes normal, special, and bad arguments. This type of

3434
04:36:46.391 --> 04:36:52.049
thinking before implementation actually helps in finalizing the requirements for a

3435
04:36:52.049 --> 04:36:52.670
function.

3436
04:36:52.670 --> 04:36:57.542
When the requirements for a function is clear and precise, it makes the

3437
04:36:57.542 --> 04:37:03.170
implementation much easier. That's all the theory we need. In the coding exercises

3438
04:37:03.170 --> 04:37:08.523
following this video lesson, you will apply this coding method to the function

3439
04:37:08.523 --> 04:37:13.876
convertToInt. We have seen this function before. It converts an integer-valued

3440
04:37:13.876 --> 04:37:17.650
string with comma as thousands separator to an integer.

3441
04:37:17.650 --> 04:37:22.683
In the exercises, you will start with a blank slate, which means that the function

3442
04:37:22.683 --> 04:37:27.656
is not yet implemented. Then you will go through a three-step process. First, you

3443
04:37:27.656 --> 04:37:32.628
will write the tests for this function in the test module testConvertToInt.py. As

3444
04:37:32.628 --> 04:37:37.293
you write the unit tests, you will think more about the requirements of this

3445
04:37:37.293 --> 04:37:42.388
function. Then you will execute the test module. Of course, the tests will not pass

3446
04:37:42.388 --> 04:37:45.150
because the function does not even exist yet.

3447
04:37:45.150 --> 04:37:50.000
Finally, you will implement the function and run the tests again.

3448
04:37:50.570 --> 04:37:55.650
If you implemented the function correctly, then the tests should pass this time.

3449
04:37:55.650 --> 04:38:00.920
Otherwise, you would have to fix bugs and repeat this step. Got it? Then let's give

3450
04:38:00.920 --> 04:38:06.190
it a try. Congratulations on finishing Chapter 2. You wrote about 16 unit tests for

3451
04:38:06.190 --> 04:38:10.000
the function's rowToList, convertToInt, getDataAsNumPyArray,

3452
04:38:10.000 --> 04:38:15.181
and split into training and testing sets in chapter 1 and 2. Well done! As the

3453
04:38:15.181 --> 04:38:20.693
number of unit tests keep growing, we would need a strategy to keep all these tests

3454
04:38:20.693 --> 04:38:26.206
organized. Otherwise, we risk our unit tests looking like this clothes cabinet. But

3455
04:38:26.206 --> 04:38:31.519
don't worry, this lesson is going to provide us with a strategy. Assume that the

3456
04:38:31.519 --> 04:38:36.700
four functions that you tested are present in the following project structure.

3457
04:38:36.700 --> 04:38:42.288
There's a top level directory called SRC, which holds all application code. Inside,

3458
04:38:42.288 --> 04:38:47.943
there's the data package. This package deals with functions that preprocess data. It

3459
04:38:47.943 --> 04:38:53.530
has a Python module called preprocessing helpers.py containing the functions row to

3460
04:38:53.530 --> 04:38:58.647
list and convert to int. Then there's the features package, which deals with

3461
04:38:58.647 --> 04:39:04.100
extracting features from the preprocess data. It has a module called as numpy.py,

3462
04:39:04.100 --> 04:39:09.445
containing the function getDataAsNumPyArray. Finally, there's the models package,

3463
04:39:09.445 --> 04:39:14.856
which deals with training and testing the linear regression model. It has a module

3464
04:39:14.856 --> 04:39:20.068
called train.py. So far, it only contains one function, split into training and

3465
04:39:20.068 --> 04:39:25.479
testing sets. The developers of PyTest recommend that we create a directory called

3466
04:39:25.479 --> 04:39:30.560
tests at the same level as SRC. This directory is also called the test suite.

3467
04:39:30.560 --> 04:39:36.325
Inside this folder, we simply mirror the inner structure of SRC and create empty

3468
04:39:36.325 --> 04:39:42.233
packages called data, features, and models, respectively. The general rule is that

3469
04:39:42.233 --> 04:39:47.926
for each Python module myModule.py, there should be a corresponding test module

3470
04:39:47.926 --> 04:39:53.691
called testMyModule.py. For example, for the module preprocessing helpers.py, we

3471
04:39:53.691 --> 04:39:57.870
create a test module called test preprocessing helpers.py.

3472
04:39:57.870 --> 04:40:03.579
Since preprocessing helpers.py belongs to the data package, we put the corresponding

3473
04:40:03.579 --> 04:40:09.287
test module in the mirrored package inside the tests directory. The mirroring in the

3474
04:40:09.287 --> 04:40:14.588
directory structure and test module names ensure that if we know where to find

3475
04:40:14.588 --> 04:40:20.161
application code, we can follow the same route inside the test directory to access

3476
04:40:20.161 --> 04:40:21.520
corresponding tests.

3477
04:40:21.520 --> 04:40:26.850
The test module test preprocessing helpers.py should contain tests for row to list

3478
04:40:26.850 --> 04:40:32.245
and convert to int. We could just put the tests sequentially like this. But this is

3479
04:40:32.245 --> 04:40:37.640
an organizational nightmare. Because there's no way to tell where the tests for one

3480
04:40:37.640 --> 04:40:42.645
function ends and another function begins. PyTest solves this problem using a

3481
04:40:42.645 --> 04:40:47.975
construct called the test class. A test class is just a simple container for tests

3482
04:40:47.975 --> 04:40:49.470
of a specific function.

3483
04:40:49.470 --> 04:40:54.860
To declare a test class, we start with the class keyword and follow it up with the

3484
04:40:54.860 --> 04:41:00.250
name of the class. The name of the class should be in camel case and should always

3485
04:41:00.250 --> 04:41:05.575
start with test. The best way to name a test class is to follow the test with the

3486
04:41:05.575 --> 04:41:10.899
name of the function. For example, testRowToList. A test class takes one argument

3487
04:41:10.899 --> 04:41:13.660
and this argument is always called object.

3488
04:41:13.660 --> 04:41:19.057
To know more about this argument, check out the Datacamp course on object-oriented

3489
04:41:19.057 --> 04:41:24.519
Python. But we don't really need to for testing purposes, as we will never use this

3490
04:41:24.519 --> 04:41:29.916
argument anywhere else. Now put all tests for the function under the test class as

3491
04:41:29.916 --> 04:41:35.116
follows. Note that this time, all tests should receive a single argument called

3492
04:41:35.116 --> 04:41:39.920
self. This also comes from object-oriented Python. For the other function

3493
04:41:39.920 --> 04:41:42.750
convertToInt, we create another test class,

3494
04:41:42.750 --> 04:41:48.863
testConvertToInt and put the tests for convertToInt inside that class. Then the

3495
04:41:48.863 --> 04:41:55.130
tests for the two functions are nicely separated. This procedure is then repeated

3496
04:41:55.130 --> 04:42:01.320
for testAsNumPy.py, which would hold the test class testGetDataAsNumPyArray, and

3497
04:42:01.320 --> 04:42:07.433
testTrain.py, which would hold the test class testSplitIntoTraining and testing

3498
04:42:07.433 --> 04:42:11.070
sets. Now our tests are, should we say clothes,

3499
04:42:11.070 --> 04:42:16.355
are well organized. Before we move on to the exercises, note that the IPython

3500
04:42:16.355 --> 04:42:21.982
console's working directory will be the tests directory from now on. Let's try all

3501
04:42:21.982 --> 04:42:27.679
this out in the exercise. In the last lesson, we learned how to organize tests. The

3502
04:42:27.679 --> 04:42:33.375
centerpiece was the tests folder, which holds all tests for the project. The folder

3503
04:42:33.375 --> 04:42:37.630
contains mirror packages, each of which contain a test module.

3504
04:42:37.630 --> 04:42:43.253
The test modules contain many test classes. A test class is just a container for

3505
04:42:43.253 --> 04:42:49.016
unit tests for a particular function. PyTest provides an easy way to run all tests

3506
04:42:49.016 --> 04:42:54.568
contained in the tests folder. We simply change to the tests folder and run the

3507
04:42:54.568 --> 04:43:00.191
command PyTest. This command automatically discovers tests by recursing into the

3508
04:43:00.191 --> 04:43:02.510
subtree of the working directory.

3509
04:43:02.510 --> 04:43:08.482
It identifies all files with names starting with test underscore as test modules.

3510
04:43:08.482 --> 04:43:14.307
Within test modules it identifies classes with names starting with test as test

3511
04:43:14.307 --> 04:43:20.501
classes. Within each test class it identifies all functions with names starting with

3512
04:43:20.501 --> 04:43:26.621
test underscore as unit tests. It collects these unit tests and runs them all. Here

3513
04:43:26.621 --> 04:43:30.750
is the result of the command. You wrote 16 tests so far.

3514
04:43:30.750 --> 04:43:36.600
So the command ran all these 16 tests, 15 passed and one failed. A typical scenario

3515
04:43:36.600 --> 04:43:42.449
to run this command is in a CI server after a commit is pushed to the code base. In

3516
04:43:42.449 --> 04:43:48.088
this case, we are only interested in the binary question, do all unit tests pass

3517
04:43:48.088 --> 04:43:53.656
after including the commit? In this case, adding the minus x flag to the pytest

3518
04:43:53.656 --> 04:43:59.294
command can save time and resources. This flag makes pytest stop after the first

3519
04:43:59.294 --> 04:44:00.210
failing test.

3520
04:44:00.210 --> 04:44:05.994
because a failing test already answers the binary question. In the report, we see

3521
04:44:05.994 --> 04:44:11.707
that only 9 tests ran this time, since execution stopped after the failing test,

3522
04:44:11.707 --> 04:44:17.562
test on one tab with missing value. Very often, we would only want to run a subset

3523
04:44:17.562 --> 04:44:23.275
of tests. For example, we might want to just run tests contained in a particular

3524
04:44:23.275 --> 04:44:26.560
test module, say, testpreprocessinghelpers.py.

3525
04:44:26.560 --> 04:44:31.999
You already know how to do that, since you did this several times in the exercises.

3526
04:44:31.999 --> 04:44:37.241
Just type pytest, followed by the path to the test module. This only runs the 13

3527
04:44:37.241 --> 04:44:42.483
tests contained in testpreprocessinghelpers.py, as we can see in the test result

3528
04:44:42.483 --> 04:44:47.528
report. At other times, we want to be more specific. For example, when we are

3529
04:44:47.528 --> 04:44:53.033
working on a particular function, say, rowToList, we only care about the test class,

3530
04:44:53.033 --> 04:44:53.950
testRowToList.

3531
04:44:53.950 --> 04:44:59.944
During automatic test discovery, PyTest assigns a node ID to every test class and

3532
04:44:59.944 --> 04:45:05.939
unit test that it encounters. The node ID of a test class is the path to the test

3533
04:45:05.939 --> 04:45:12.081
module followed by the name of the test class, separated by two colons. The node ID

3534
04:45:12.081 --> 04:45:18.002
of a unit test follows the same format, with the unit test name added to the end

3535
04:45:18.002 --> 04:45:20.740
using another double colon separator.

3536
04:45:20.740 --> 04:45:25.782
When we run the command pytest followed by the node ID of the test class

3537
04:45:25.782 --> 04:45:31.664
testRowToList, for example, it only runs the seven tests contained in testRowToList.

3538
04:45:31.664 --> 04:45:37.196
When we run the command with the node ID of the unit test, test on one tab with

3539
04:45:37.196 --> 04:45:42.938
missing value, it only runs a single test. A faster and flexible way to do this is

3540
04:45:42.938 --> 04:45:48.750
by using keyword expressions. To run tests using keyword expressions, use the minus

3541
04:45:48.750 --> 04:45:49.380
k option.

3542
04:45:49.380 --> 04:45:55.115
This option takes a quoted string containing a pattern as a value. For example, we

3543
04:45:55.115 --> 04:46:00.781
can specify a test class such as test split into training and testing sets as the

3544
04:46:00.781 --> 04:46:06.447
pattern. And this will run only the two tests within that test class. We can also

3545
04:46:06.447 --> 04:46:12.252
enter only part of the test class name, as long as that is unique. This saves a lot

3546
04:46:12.252 --> 04:46:14.700
of typing and has the same outcome.

3547
04:46:14.700 --> 04:46:19.755
We can even use Python logical operators in the pattern to do more complex

3548
04:46:19.755 --> 04:46:25.492
subsetting. For example, the following command will execute all tests in tests split

3549
04:46:25.492 --> 04:46:30.957
into training and testing sets except the unit test, test on one row, which only

3550
04:46:30.957 --> 04:46:36.626
leaves one test to run. Let's run some tests using these command line tricks in the

3551
04:46:36.626 --> 04:46:42.022
exercise. In the last lesson, we learned the magic command pytest that runs all

3552
04:46:42.022 --> 04:46:43.730
tests. If all tests pass,

3553
04:46:43.730 --> 04:46:49.410
Then our test suite is green. We can relax on the beach and drink a cocktail. If any

3554
04:46:49.410 --> 04:46:54.616
test fails, then our test suite is red. This means we better work and fix it.

3555
04:46:54.616 --> 04:47:00.161
Otherwise, our users will be very angry. This is good in theory, but sometimes the

3556
04:47:00.161 --> 04:47:05.706
red light can be a false alarm that will ruin our beach vacations. An example will

3557
04:47:05.706 --> 04:47:10.980
make things clear. Let's say we are implementing this new function trainModel.

3558
04:47:10.980 --> 04:47:16.475
which returns the best fit line on the training data. Since we are gonna use TDD,

3559
04:47:16.475 --> 04:47:22.107
the first step is to write tests. So we create a test class, testTrainModel and add

3560
04:47:22.107 --> 04:47:27.738
a test to it. If we run PyTest, this test will fail because the function trainModel

3561
04:47:27.738 --> 04:47:33.437
is not yet implemented. And this is just a result of using TDD. It does not indicate

3562
04:47:33.437 --> 04:47:39.068
a problem with the code base. But the CI server does not know this and will set off

3563
04:47:39.068 --> 04:47:39.950
a false alarm

3564
04:47:39.950 --> 04:47:45.536
when that test fails. It would be nice to have a way to tell pytest that we expect

3565
04:47:45.536 --> 04:47:51.053
this test to fail. We do that by using the xFail decorator. The decorator goes on

3566
04:47:51.053 --> 04:47:56.707
top of a test and it starts with the character at the rate. This is followed by the

3567
04:47:56.707 --> 04:48:02.020
name of the decorator pytest.mark.xFail. After adding the decorator, if we run

3568
04:48:02.020 --> 04:48:07.470
pytest again, we see that one test is xFailed, but there are no reported errors.

3569
04:48:07.470 --> 04:48:12.708
which means that the test suite remains green. At other times, we might know that

3570
04:48:12.708 --> 04:48:17.947
the test fails only under certain conditions and we don't want to be warned about

3571
04:48:17.947 --> 04:48:23.315
them. Common situations are when some function won't work under a particular Python

3572
04:48:23.315 --> 04:48:28.424
version or a particular platform. As an example, we have deliberately added the

3573
04:48:28.424 --> 04:48:33.662
unicode function in the failure message for the test, test with no comma, that we

3574
04:48:33.662 --> 04:48:37.090
wrote earlier. This only works on Python 2.7 or lore.

3575
04:48:37.090 --> 04:48:42.939
If we run pytest using Python 3, the test suite will go red. To tell pytest to skip

3576
04:48:42.939 --> 04:48:48.789
running this test on Python versions higher than 2.7, we need the skipif decorator.

3577
04:48:48.789 --> 04:48:54.638
The syntax is similar to xfail. The name of the decorator is pytest.mark.skipif. It

3578
04:48:54.638 --> 04:49:00.558
takes a single Boolean expression as an argument. If the Boolean expression is true,

3579
04:49:00.558 --> 04:49:05.280
then the test will be skipped. To construct the Boolean expression,

3580
04:49:05.280 --> 04:49:11.334
Import the built-in module sys and use the attribute sys.versioninfo. This attribute

3581
04:49:11.334 --> 04:49:17.171
can be compared against a tuple containing the major and minor Python version. In

3582
04:49:17.171 --> 04:49:23.080
this case, 2 and 7. We must also add the required reason argument which states why

3583
04:49:23.080 --> 04:49:28.917
the test is skipped. Running PyTest again confirms that one test was x-failed and

3584
04:49:28.917 --> 04:49:33.890
another one was skipped. The test suite remains green. Perfect again.

3585
04:49:33.890 --> 04:49:39.269
We can make the reason for skipping show in the report. For that, we can use the

3586
04:49:39.269 --> 04:49:44.783
minus R option. The minus R option can be followed by any number of characters. If

3587
04:49:44.783 --> 04:49:50.230
we add the character S, it will show us tests that were skipped in the short test

3588
04:49:50.230 --> 04:49:55.542
summary section near the end. The XFail decorator also takes an optional reason

3589
04:49:55.542 --> 04:50:01.056
argument. For the test that we marked with XFail, we will add the reason using TDD

3590
04:50:01.056 --> 04:50:03.140
train model is not implemented.

3591
04:50:03.140 --> 04:50:09.312
If we add the character X to the minus R option, it will only show us tests that are

3592
04:50:09.312 --> 04:50:15.190
X-failed along with the reason in the test summary info. We can show reasons for

3593
04:50:15.190 --> 04:50:21.215
both by using the combination SX. If we are skipping and X-failing multiple tests,

3594
04:50:21.215 --> 04:50:27.020
note that these decorators can be applied to entire test classes as well. Let's

3595
04:50:27.020 --> 04:50:30.620
practice X-failing and skipping in the exercises.

3596
04:50:30.620 --> 04:50:36.029
We saw how NumPy increases user trust by adding code coverage and build status

3597
04:50:36.029 --> 04:50:41.716
badges. In this lesson, we will learn to implement these badges for our own GitHub

3598
04:50:41.716 --> 04:50:47.195
projects. Let's start with the build status badge. This badge uses a continuous

3599
04:50:47.195 --> 04:50:52.882
integration server which runs all tests automatically whenever we push a commit to

3600
04:50:52.882 --> 04:50:58.430
GitHub. It shows whether tests are currently passing or failing. To an end user,

3601
04:50:58.430 --> 04:51:04.365
passing indicates a stable codebase, while failing indicates instability. We will

3602
04:51:04.365 --> 04:51:10.227
use Travis CI as our CI server. To integrate with Travis CI, we have to create a

3603
04:51:10.227 --> 04:51:16.382
settings file called .Travis.yml at the root of our repository. The file is arranged

3604
04:51:16.382 --> 04:51:22.098
into sections. First, there's a language setting, and we set it to Python. The

3605
04:51:22.098 --> 04:51:27.740
Python setting determines which Python version will be used to run the tests.

3606
04:51:27.740 --> 04:51:33.504
We choose Python 3.6. The install setting is a list of commands to install our

3607
04:51:33.504 --> 04:51:39.046
project and dependencies in the CI server. If we organized our tests in the

3608
04:51:39.046 --> 04:51:45.180
recommended way, then we can use a local pip install using pip install minus e dot.

3609
04:51:45.180 --> 04:51:51.313
The script section lists the commands necessary to run the tests once everything is

3610
04:51:51.313 --> 04:51:55.230
installed. We use pytest tests to run the test suite.

3611
04:51:55.230 --> 04:52:01.304
We push this settings file to GitHub. Now we go to the GitHub profile page and click

3612
04:52:01.304 --> 04:52:07.377
on Marketplace. We search for Travis CI, click on it, and install the app. It's free

3613
04:52:07.377 --> 04:52:13.234
for public repositories. We allow the app access to the necessary repositories or

3614
04:52:13.234 --> 04:52:18.657
organizations. Here we are only allowing it access to the public repository

3615
04:52:18.657 --> 04:52:24.080
univariate linear regression, which holds the example code for this course.

3616
04:52:24.080 --> 04:52:29.612
We will be redirected to Travis CI where we should log in using our GitHub account.

3617
04:52:29.612 --> 04:52:35.011
This will bring us to the Travis CI dashboard. That's all the setup we need. From

3618
04:52:35.011 --> 04:52:40.010
now on, whenever we push a commit to the GitHub repo, we should see a build

3619
04:52:40.010 --> 04:52:45.342
appearing in the Travis CI dashboard. When the build finishes, the badge appears

3620
04:52:45.342 --> 04:52:50.474
here. We click on the badge, choose markdown from the dropdown, and paste the

3621
04:52:50.474 --> 04:52:53.340
markdown code in the README file on GitHub.

3622
04:52:53.340 --> 04:52:59.108
This adds the badge to the GitHub repo. We will add the code coverage badge next.

3623
04:52:59.108 --> 04:53:04.947
The code coverage badge indicates the percentage of our application code that gets

3624
04:53:04.947 --> 04:53:10.786
run when we run the test suite. High percentages indicate a well-tested code base.

3625
04:53:10.786 --> 04:53:16.340
This badge comes from a service called CodeCov that integrates seamlessly with

3626
04:53:16.340 --> 04:53:22.250
GitHub and Travis CI. First, we will modify the .travis.yml to enable code coverage

3627
04:53:22.250 --> 04:53:22.820
reports.

3628
04:53:22.820 --> 04:53:28.708
In the install setting, pip install pytest cov and codecov, as they are necessary to

3629
04:53:28.708 --> 04:53:34.246
generate and upload coverage reports. The usual pytest command to run the tests

3630
04:53:34.246 --> 04:53:39.994
should be modified by adding a command line flag, minus minus cov, which points to

3631
04:53:39.994 --> 04:53:45.672
the application directory, src. This new command will not only run the tests, but

3632
04:53:45.672 --> 04:53:51.139
also produce a coverage report. Finally, add a setting called after underscore

3633
04:53:51.139 --> 04:53:51.700
success.

3634
04:53:51.700 --> 04:53:57.750
And add the command code curve. This makes Travis CI push the code coverage results

3635
04:53:57.750 --> 04:54:03.872
to code curve after every build. To enable code curve for our repository, we install

3636
04:54:03.872 --> 04:54:09.849
the code curve app in the GitHub marketplace in the same way we install Travis CI.

3637
04:54:09.849 --> 04:54:15.972
From now, when we push a new commit, the code coverage report should show up in code

3638
04:54:15.972 --> 04:54:21.220
curve, accessible at code curve.io, after Travis CI completes the build.

3639
04:54:21.220 --> 04:54:26.710
Go to the badge section in settings and paste the markdown code to the GitHub readme

3640
04:54:26.710 --> 04:54:32.200
file and that adds the code coverage badge. Let's practice some of these concepts in

3641
04:54:32.200 --> 04:54:37.560
the exercises. It is also recommended that you go through these steps for a GitHub

3642
04:54:37.560 --> 04:54:43.050
repository that you... Congratulations! You are now in the fourth and final chapter.

3643
04:54:43.050 --> 04:54:48.214
In this lesson, we are going to look at functions whose tests require more than

3644
04:54:48.214 --> 04:54:49.390
assert statements.

3645
04:54:49.390 --> 04:54:54.859
As an example, consider the function preprocess, which accepts paths to a raw data

3646
04:54:54.859 --> 04:55:00.128
file and a clean file as arguments. Let's say that the raw data file looks like

3647
04:55:00.128 --> 04:55:05.664
this. The function first applies row to list on the rows. The second row has no tab

3648
04:55:05.664 --> 04:55:11.133
separator, so row to list filters it out. ConvertToInt is applied next. The fourth

3649
04:55:11.133 --> 04:55:16.469
and fifth rows are dirty because the area and the price entry are missing commas

3650
04:55:16.469 --> 04:55:18.270
respectively. ConvertToInt.

3651
04:55:18.270 --> 04:55:23.606
filters them out. For the two valid rows, convertToInt converts the comma-separated

3652
04:55:23.606 --> 04:55:28.621
strings into integers. The result is written to the clean file. Pre-process is

3653
04:55:28.621 --> 04:55:33.893
different from other functions because it has a precondition to work properly. The

3654
04:55:33.893 --> 04:55:38.715
precondition is the presence of a raw data file in the environment. Another

3655
04:55:38.715 --> 04:55:43.601
difference is that when we call the function, it modifies the environment by

3656
04:55:43.601 --> 04:55:46.430
creating a clean data file. We create a test

3657
04:55:46.430 --> 04:55:51.685
call test on raw data for this function. We create the raw data file first. This

3658
04:55:51.685 --> 04:55:56.809
step is called setup, and it is used to bring the environment to a state where

3659
04:55:56.809 --> 04:56:02.261
testing can begin. Then we call the function, which creates the clean data file. We

3660
04:56:02.261 --> 04:56:07.714
open that file, read it, and assert that it contains the expected lines. Afterwards

3661
04:56:07.714 --> 04:56:13.166
we need to remove the clean and raw data file so that the next run of the test gets

3662
04:56:13.166 --> 04:56:14.480
a clean environment.

3663
04:56:14.480 --> 04:56:19.954
This step is called Teardown, and it cleans any modification to the environment and

3664
04:56:19.954 --> 04:56:25.363
brings it back to the initial state. To summarize, instead of a sequence of assert

3665
04:56:25.363 --> 04:56:30.837
statements, we have to follow the workflow, setup, assert, and teardown. In PyTest,

3666
04:56:30.837 --> 04:56:36.312
the setup and teardown is placed outside the test in a function called a fixture. A

3667
04:56:36.312 --> 04:56:41.720
fixture is a function which has the PyTest.fixture decorator. The first section is

3668
04:56:41.720 --> 04:56:42.380
the setup.

3669
04:56:42.380 --> 04:56:47.593
Then the function returns the data that the test needs. The test can access this

3670
04:56:47.593 --> 04:56:52.935
data by calling the fixture passed as an argument. But instead of using the return

3671
04:56:52.935 --> 04:56:58.083
keyword, the fixture function actually uses the yield keyword instead. The next

3672
04:56:58.083 --> 04:57:02.904
section is the teardown. This section runs only when the test has finished

3673
04:57:02.904 --> 04:57:08.312
executing. Let's see an example of how this works for the test test on raw data. We

3674
04:57:08.312 --> 04:57:11.440
create a fixture called raw and clean data file.

3675
04:57:11.440 --> 04:57:17.138
In setup, we create the paths to the raw and clean data file. Next, we write the raw

3676
04:57:17.138 --> 04:57:22.633
data to the raw data file. Finally, we yield the paths as a tuple. The test calls

3677
04:57:22.633 --> 04:57:28.060
the fixture and gets the path required to call the preprocess function. Then, we

3678
04:57:28.060 --> 04:57:33.488
proceed to the assert statement. In the teardown section, we remove both raw and

3679
04:57:33.488 --> 04:57:38.915
clean data file using the os.remove function. There is a built-in PyTest fixture

3680
04:57:38.915 --> 04:57:39.547
called tempTier.

3681
04:57:39.547 --> 04:57:45.032
which is useful when dealing with files. This fixture creates a temporary directory

3682
04:57:45.032 --> 04:57:50.450
during setup and deletes the temporary directory during teardown. We can pass this

3683
04:57:50.450 --> 04:57:55.538
fixture as an argument to our fixture. This is called fixture chaining, which

3684
04:57:55.538 --> 04:57:59.040
results in the setup of temp-tier to be called first,

3685
04:57:59.040 --> 04:58:04.875
followed by the setup of our fixture. When the test finishes, the teardown of our

3686
04:58:04.875 --> 04:58:10.782
fixture is called first, followed by the teardown of tempdir. The tempdir argument

3687
04:58:10.782 --> 04:58:16.688
supports all os.path commands such as join. We use the join function of tempdir to

3688
04:58:16.688 --> 04:58:22.595
create the raw and clean data file inside the temporary directory. The rest of the

3689
04:58:22.595 --> 04:58:24.180
setup looks identical.

3690
04:58:24.180 --> 04:58:30.090
The awesome thing is we can omit the teardown code in our fixture entirely because

3691
04:58:30.090 --> 04:58:35.928
the teardown of tempdir will delete all files in the temporary directory when the

3692
04:58:35.928 --> 04:58:41.405
test ends. Let's get some practice with using fixtures in the actual. In the

3693
04:58:41.405 --> 04:58:47.460
previous lesson, we tested the preprocess function. preprocess applies rowToList and

3694
04:58:47.460 --> 04:58:53.370
convertToInt sequentially to the raw data file to create a clean data file. If the

3695
04:58:53.370 --> 04:58:53.730
tests

3696
04:58:53.730 --> 04:58:59.021
For preprocess where to pass, row to list and convert to int must also work as

3697
04:58:59.021 --> 04:59:04.652
expected. If any of them has a bug, the tests for preprocess will not pass, even if

3698
04:59:04.652 --> 04:59:10.350
preprocess has no bugs. But the test results should be indicative of the bugs in the

3699
04:59:10.350 --> 04:59:15.777
function under test, and not bugs in any of its dependencies. In this lesson, we

3700
04:59:15.777 --> 04:59:21.068
will learn a trick which will allow us to test a function independently of its

3701
04:59:21.068 --> 04:59:21.950
dependencies.

3702
04:59:21.950 --> 04:59:27.960
This very useful trick is called mocking. To use mocking in PyTest, we will need two

3703
04:59:27.960 --> 04:59:33.826
packages. The first one is PyTestMock, and we can install it using pip. The second

3704
04:59:33.826 --> 04:59:39.836
one is a standard library package called UnitTest.Mock. The basic idea of mocking is

3705
04:59:39.836 --> 04:59:45.344
to replace potentially buggy dependencies, such as RowToList, with the object

3706
04:59:45.344 --> 04:59:48.850
UnitTest.Mock.MagicMock, but only during testing.

3707
04:59:48.850 --> 04:59:54.359
This replacement is done using a fixture called Mocker and calling its patch method

3708
04:59:54.359 --> 04:59:59.470
right at the beginning of the test, testOnRawData, which we wrote in the last

3709
04:59:59.470 --> 05:00:05.045
lesson. The first argument of the Mocker.patch method is the fully qualified name of

3710
05:00:05.045 --> 05:00:10.289
the dependency, including module name as registered by the function under test.

3711
05:00:10.289 --> 05:00:15.732
preprocess knows rowToList as data.preprocessingHelpers.rowToList, and that's what

3712
05:00:15.732 --> 05:00:16.860
we will use here.

3713
05:00:16.860 --> 05:00:22.304
The mocker.patch method returns the magicMock object which we store in the variable

3714
05:00:22.304 --> 05:00:27.552
rowToListMock. During the test, rowToListMock can be programmed to behave like a

3715
05:00:27.552 --> 05:00:32.734
bug-free replacement of rowToList. We call the bug-free version of rowToList as

3716
05:00:32.734 --> 05:00:37.982
rowToListBugFree. Note that this only needs to be bug-free in the context of the

3717
05:00:37.982 --> 05:00:43.361
test, and therefore can be much simpler than the actual rowToList function. In the

3718
05:00:43.361 --> 05:00:46.050
test, we use the following raw data file.

3719
05:00:46.050 --> 05:00:51.117
which we already saw in the last lesson. The rowToListBugFree simply needs to return

3720
05:00:51.117 --> 05:00:56.184
the correct result for these five rows. Therefore, we create a dictionary containing

3721
05:00:56.184 --> 05:01:01.191
the correct results for these five rows and return the results from the dictionary.

3722
05:01:01.191 --> 05:01:05.775
Then we set the sideEffect attribute of the MagicMock object to the bug-free

3723
05:01:05.775 --> 05:01:10.842
version. We can also set the sideEffect attribute by passing sideEffect as a keyword

3724
05:01:10.842 --> 05:01:12.350
argument to mocker.patch.

3725
05:01:12.350 --> 05:01:17.434
Mocker.patch treats any keyword argument that it does not recognize as an attribute

3726
05:01:17.434 --> 05:01:22.395
of the returned magic mock object, and sets the attribute value accordingly. From

3727
05:01:22.395 --> 05:01:27.540
this point on, when we call preprocess in the test, the bug-free mock of row-to-list

3728
05:01:27.540 --> 05:01:32.195
will be used, and the test will not encounter any bugs. We can also check if

3729
05:01:32.195 --> 05:01:35.870
preprocess is calling row-to-list with the correct argument.

3730
05:01:35.870 --> 05:01:41.328
The callArgsList attribute is a list of all the arguments that rowToListMock was

3731
05:01:41.328 --> 05:01:46.854
called with, wrapped in a convenience object called call. This convenience object

3732
05:01:46.854 --> 05:01:52.517
can be imported from unitTest.Mock, and we import it at the top of the test. In the

3733
05:01:52.517 --> 05:01:58.179
test, we can assert that the callArgsList attribute is the expected list containing

3734
05:01:58.179 --> 05:02:02.000
the five rows of the raw data file in the correct order.

3735
05:02:02.000 --> 05:02:07.740
We have prepared a scenario where rowToList contains a bug, but preprocess doesn't.

3736
05:02:07.740 --> 05:02:13.411
If we run the tests for both functions, we see that some tests for rowToList fail,

3737
05:02:13.411 --> 05:02:19.221
but the test for preprocess passes. That's exactly the behavior we wanted. Now let's

3738
05:02:19.221 --> 05:02:24.892
practice some mocking magic. We have come far in this course, and now it's time to

3739
05:02:24.892 --> 05:02:30.009
slowly put everything together. We are already familiar with the functions

3740
05:02:30.009 --> 05:02:30.770
preprocess,

3741
05:02:30.770 --> 05:02:36.433
GetDataAsNumpyArray and split into training and testing set. The raw data file

3742
05:02:36.433 --> 05:02:42.169
containing housing area and prices is in a file called housingData.txt inside a

3743
05:02:42.169 --> 05:02:48.267
subdirectory called raw of the top level directory data. We clean the raw data using

3744
05:02:48.267 --> 05:02:54.148
preprocess and put the file cleanHousingData.txt in the subdirectory clean of the

3745
05:02:54.148 --> 05:02:59.230
data folder. Next, we apply GetDataAsNumpyArray on the clean data file

3746
05:02:59.230 --> 05:03:03.952
to get a numpy array containing the clean data. Finally, we apply split into

3747
05:03:03.952 --> 05:03:09.171
training and testing set to randomly split this numpy array row-wise in the ratio 3s

3748
05:03:09.171 --> 05:03:14.390
to 1. 3 fourths of the data will be used for training a linear regression model. The

3749
05:03:14.390 --> 05:03:19.547
rest will be used to test the model. All these functions are well tested, thanks to

3750
05:03:19.547 --> 05:03:24.456
your efforts in the exercises. It's time now to train a linear regression model

3751
05:03:24.456 --> 05:03:26.320
using the function trainModel,

3752
05:03:26.320 --> 05:03:31.298
which takes the training set as the only argument. The training set has areas in the

3753
05:03:31.298 --> 05:03:35.744
first column and prices in the second column. The leanRegress function from

3754
05:03:35.744 --> 05:03:40.663
scipy.stats is used to perform linear regression on the two columns. It returns the

3755
05:03:40.663 --> 05:03:45.404
slope and intercept of the best fit line. It also returns three other quantities

3756
05:03:45.404 --> 05:03:50.323
related to linear regression, but since we don't need them, we simply use the dummy

3757
05:03:50.323 --> 05:03:52.220
variable underscore three times.

3758
05:03:52.220 --> 05:03:57.313
The train model function is different from the functions that we have tested so far.

3759
05:03:57.313 --> 05:04:01.982
Since it performs a complicated linear regression procedure, we cannot easily

3760
05:04:01.982 --> 05:04:07.076
predict the best fit line. And if we don't know the expected return value, we cannot

3761
05:04:07.076 --> 05:04:11.806
test the function. This is true for all data science models, be it regression,

3762
05:04:11.806 --> 05:04:16.656
random forest, support vector machine, or neural network. The trick is to use an

3763
05:04:16.656 --> 05:04:18.900
artificial or well-known training set

3764
05:04:18.900 --> 05:04:23.713
where it is easy to manually compute the return value. In the case of linear

3765
05:04:23.713 --> 05:04:28.652
regression, one such training dataset is a linear dataset. In the test test on

3766
05:04:28.652 --> 05:04:33.972
linear data, we use such a dataset which follows the equation price equals two times

3767
05:04:33.972 --> 05:04:39.291
area plus one. Since the data lies in a straight line, the best fit line is the same

3768
05:04:39.291 --> 05:04:44.610
straight line. Therefore, the slope returned by train model should be two, while the

3769
05:04:44.610 --> 05:04:46.700
intercept returned should be one.

3770
05:04:46.700 --> 05:04:51.279
We can write assert statements to check that. We can also use inequalities. For

3771
05:04:51.279 --> 05:04:55.974
example, in the test test on positively correlated data, we use a dataset that is

3772
05:04:55.974 --> 05:05:00.785
positively correlated. In this case, we can't predict the best fit line, but we can

3773
05:05:00.785 --> 05:05:05.539
definitely assert that the best fit line has a slope greater than zero. This is an

3774
05:05:05.539 --> 05:05:10.350
example of a sanity check. We shouldn't leave our model untested just because it is

3775
05:05:10.350 --> 05:05:14.987
complex. Perform all sorts of sanity checks. This will save us lots of debugging

3776
05:05:14.987 --> 05:05:16.320
effort in the long run.

3777
05:05:16.320 --> 05:05:21.348
Once the training function has been tested, we use it to find the best fit line for

3778
05:05:21.348 --> 05:05:26.375
the housing data. The next step is to test the model using the model test function.

3779
05:05:26.375 --> 05:05:30.918
It takes the testing set as the first argument. It also takes the slope and

3780
05:05:30.918 --> 05:05:35.643
intercept returned by the model and checks the performance of the model on the

3781
05:05:35.643 --> 05:05:40.549
testing set. It returns a quantity called the R squared, which expresses how well

3782
05:05:40.549 --> 05:05:43.820
the model fits the testing set. The value of R squared

3783
05:05:43.820 --> 05:05:49.346
usually ranges from 0 to 1. It is 1 when the fit is perfect. It is 0 if there is no

3784
05:05:49.346 --> 05:05:54.938
fit. It is hard to compute R-squared in the general case. Therefore, we will have to

3785
05:05:54.938 --> 05:06:00.531
use the recommendations of this lesson to test this function in the exercise. A data

3786
05:06:00.531 --> 05:06:06.056
science course without visualizations is like a pizza without cheese. Therefore, in

3787
05:06:06.056 --> 05:06:11.316
this lesson, we will add some cheese by testing visualizations generated by the

3788
05:06:11.316 --> 05:06:13.180
plotting library matplotlib.

3789
05:06:13.180 --> 05:06:18.553
We have a Python package called visualization under the application directory src.

3790
05:06:18.553 --> 05:06:23.730
