05:39:44.178 --> 05:39:49.654
in the SQL query. In this case, we referenced file A first in our join, then file B,

4205
05:39:49.654 --> 05:39:54.870
so the order is file underscore A dot CSV space file underscore B dot CSV. We've

4206
05:39:54.870 --> 05:39:56.500
used commands to CSV kit,

4207
05:39:56.500 --> 05:40:01.144
to do a variety of tasks related to the database. We've pulled data. We've

4208
05:40:01.144 --> 05:40:06.353
manipulated data. Now let's learn how to push data back to the database. Our friend

4209
05:40:06.353 --> 05:40:11.499
CSV SQL makes a reappearance since it pulls double duty for manipulating local CSV

4210
05:40:11.499 --> 05:40:16.269
files as well as uploading and pushing CSV data back to the database. In our

4211
05:40:16.269 --> 05:40:21.541
previous lesson, we focused on the dash dash query portion of CSV SQL. Here, we will

4212
05:40:21.541 --> 05:40:24.930
focus on some more option arguments, dash dash insert,

4213
05:40:24.930 --> 05:40:30.532
Here is a sample script that uses CSV SQL to upload a local CSV file,

4214
05:40:30.532 --> 05:40:37.353
spotify-music-attributes.ca, to an existing SQLite database called Spotify database.

4215
05:40:37.353 --> 05:40:44.092
The syntax for this task starts with CSV SQL and the dash dash DB flag, followed by

4216
05:40:44.092 --> 05:40:50.669
the full database name to establish location of it. Remember to pass this in as a

4217
05:40:50.669 --> 05:40:54.160
string inside quotation marks. Please note,

4218
05:40:54.160 --> 05:40:59.243
A quick built-in line break is used here to keep our code clean and readable. It's

4219
05:40:59.243 --> 05:41:04.449
worth noting that we use three forward slashes after naming the database type, which

4220
05:41:04.449 --> 05:41:09.532
is normal. However, because this is a SQLite database, we need to end the database

4221
05:41:09.532 --> 05:41:14.366
with the file extension .d. Still the same command, but now let's focus on the

4222
05:41:14.366 --> 05:41:19.449
second option, dash dash insert. This is new to us and is very interesting because

4223
05:41:19.449 --> 05:41:23.540
the insert option does many things all in one step. Traditionally,

4224
05:41:23.540 --> 05:41:28.772
Uploading a new file as a table in a database has multiple steps. Depending on your

4225
05:41:28.772 --> 05:41:33.942
SQL client or user interface, you might need to create an empty table, specify the

4226
05:41:33.942 --> 05:41:39.174
table schema, key the table, and then write in the data row by row. Here, dash dash

4227
05:41:39.174 --> 05:41:44.281
