4228
05:41:44.281 --> 05:41:49.387
what the schema of the table should be. Pretty neat, right? The insert option can

4229
05:41:49.387 --> 05:41:52.350
only be used in conjunction with the db option.

4230
05:41:52.350 --> 05:41:57.351
Which should make sense, because if we don't specify where the database is, we

4231
05:41:57.351 --> 05:42:02.416
cannot insert data into the database. Lastly, and unfortunately, CSV SQL is not

4232
05:42:02.416 --> 05:42:07.802
always going to be 100% failsafe when it comes to schema inference. If you know that

4233
05:42:07.802 --> 05:42:12.739
your data has a numbers column that should rather be treated as a text column

4234
05:42:12.739 --> 05:42:17.676
instead, then you can preemptively account for this by using the dash dash no

4235
05:42:17.676 --> 05:42:19.920
inference option flag with CSV SQL.

4236
05:42:19.920 --> 05:42:24.543
CSV SQL will treat each column as text, whether it looks like numbers, dates, or

4237
05:42:24.543 --> 05:42:29.398
strings. Likewise, dash dash node constraints allows the table to be created without

4238
05:42:29.398 --> 05:42:34.252
any character length limits, which is helpful for particularly large data tables and

4239
05:42:34.252 --> 05:42:38.933
considerably speeds up the insertion process. It will allow you to create columns

4240
05:42:38.933 --> 05:42:43.614
containing all node data without throwing any errors. Although data processing is

4241
05:42:43.614 --> 05:42:48.180
more productive on the command line, complex data work like predictive modeling

4242
05:42:48.180 --> 05:42:52.612
is still easier in Python. In this chapter, we will learn how to run Python on the

4243
05:42:52.612 --> 05:42:56.558
command line so that we can combine the best of both worlds. Python comes

4244
05:42:56.558 --> 05:43:01.044
pre-installed with macOS and Linux, but needs to be installed for Windows. The most

4245
05:43:01.044 --> 05:43:05.531
common way to interact with Python is via GUI interfaces like Jupyter Notebook, but

4246
05:43:05.531 --> 05:43:09.747
the more efficient way is to access Python directly on the command line. Let's

4247
05:43:09.747 --> 05:43:10.990
explore how to do that.

4248
05:43:10.990 --> 05:43:16.608
MAN Python calls up the documentation and option flags. Since Python 2 and Python 3

4249
05:43:16.608 --> 05:43:22.024
have different syntax, it's helpful to check on the Python version by using dash

4250
05:43:22.024 --> 05:43:27.574
capital V or dash dash version. Here, we have Python 3.5.2 installed. Likewise, it

4251
05:43:27.574 --> 05:43:33.260
is possible to have multiple copies of the same version of Python coexisting on your

4252
05:43:33.260 --> 05:43:38.743
machine. To see which Python version you're using, type which Python. Here, we're

4253
05:43:38.743 --> 05:43:40.300
