05:44:25.605 --> 05:44:30.116
hello world. To exit the session, type exit, open close parentheses, and press

4265
05:44:30.116 --> 05:44:34.627
enter. Notice that in the next line, the three redirects are replaced with the

4266
05:44:34.627 --> 05:44:35.090
dollars.

4267
05:44:35.090 --> 05:44:40.025
which indicates we're back in normal command. The interactive session is easy to

4268
05:44:40.025 --> 05:44:44.837
use, but it isn't helpful for code reproducibility. To execute the same Python

4269
05:44:44.837 --> 05:44:49.834
command multiple times and on demand, we need a second way to use Python. This is

4270
05:44:49.834 --> 05:44:55.016
done by saving code in the Python script and calling the script when we are ready to

4271
05:44:55.016 --> 05:44:59.952
execute. Let's dig further. We can create the Python file using any text editor,

4272
05:44:59.952 --> 05:45:04.270
like Sublime, Nano, Vim, Emacs. Make sure that the file ends in a dot.

4273
05:45:04.270 --> 05:45:08.798
py extension. If our script is very short, we can simply echo this command and

4274
05:45:08.798 --> 05:45:13.615
redirect it into the Python file. This one-liner does two things. It instantiates a

4275
05:45:13.615 --> 05:45:18.433
new file called hello underscore world dot py and populates the file with the print

4276
05:45:18.433 --> 05:45:23.251
command. The benefit of this method is that we never leave the command line causing

4277
05:45:23.251 --> 05:45:28.069
less disruption to our workflow. Let's sanity check and print our content. With the

4278
05:45:28.069 --> 05:45:31.900
Python script created, we still need to call it to execute. First,

4279
05:45:31.900 --> 05:45:36.795
We check that we are in the same file directory as the Python script. Then, we tell

4280
05:45:36.795 --> 05:45:41.571
our terminal to execute this file using Python by calling Python, followed by the

4281
05:45:41.571 --> 05:45:46.466
Python file, hello underscore world dot p. Now our terminal echoes hello world back

4282
05:45:46.466 --> 05:45:51.361
to you. In this lesson, let's learn about pip, the standard package manager. Python

4283
05:45:51.361 --> 05:45:56.256
comes by default with a collection of built-in functions such as the print function

4284
05:45:56.256 --> 05:45:59.440
and built-in packages such as the math or OS. However,

4285
05:45:59.440 --> 05:46:04.119
Because Python is a general purpose language, packages for specific use cases need

4286
05:46:04.119 --> 05:46:08.856
to be separately installed by the user. This includes data science packages such as

4287
05:46:08.856 --> 05:46:13.421
scikit-learn and statsmon. The installation is handled through pip, the standard

4288
05:46:13.421 --> 05:46:17.872
package manager for Python. First things first, read the documentation through

4289
05:46:17.872 --> 05:46:22.437
pip-8. pip has a variety of commands and option flags designed to manage Python.

4290
05:46:22.437 --> 05:46:27.173
Secondly, note that you can print the pip version the same way you print the Python

4291
05:46:27.173 --> 05:46:27.630
version.

4292
05:46:27.630 --> 05:46:33.490
It's important that the pip version is compatible with the pip. Here we see that pip

4293
05:46:33.490 --> 05:46:39.140
19.1.1 is compatible with Python 3.5. If pip is giving you a upgrade warning, you

4294
05:46:39.140 --> 05:46:45.000
can upgrade pip using itself via pip install dash dash upgrade pip. Before we do any

4295
05:46:45.000 --> 05:46:50.790
installing, it's a good idea to see what's already installed. Using pip list on the

4296
05:46:50.790 --> 05:46:53.650
command line, print an alphabetical list.

4297
05:46:53.650 --> 05:46:58.414
of all the Python packages in your current Python environment. To install a library

4298
05:46:58.414 --> 05:47:03.178
that's not currently installed, as indicated by pip list, the syntax is pip install

4299
05:47:03.178 --> 05:47:07.999
followed by the library name. In this case, we're installing scikit-learn. You might

4300
05:47:07.999 --> 05:47:12.591
notice from the logs though, it looks like more than one Python package is being

4301
05:47:12.591 --> 05:47:16.666
installed in this one single pip install call. This is because pip will

4302
05:47:16.666 --> 05:47:21.372
automatically install scikit-learn as well as any other Python packages that it is

4303
05:47:21.372 --> 05:47:22.750
dependent on to operate.

4304
05:47:22.750 --> 05:47:27.754
We call these other packages dependency. By default, pip install will always install

4305
05:47:27.754 --> 05:47:32.461
the latest version of the library. Here, we have installed scikit-learn version

4306
05:47:32.461 --> 05:47:37.286
0.21.3. If we wish to install an older version of scikit-learn, we simply have to

4307
05:47:37.286 --> 05:47:42.052
specify it during the install statement with double equal signs like so. If your

4308
05:47:42.052 --> 05:47:46.878
package is already installed and simply out of date, upgrade the package the same

4309
05:47:46.878 --> 05:47:51.644
way you upgraded pip earlier. Pip will automatically take care of any dependency

4310
05:47:51.644 --> 05:47:52.180
upgrades.

4311
05:47:52.180 --> 05:47:57.241
To pip install more than one python package, the packages can be listed in line with

4312
05:47:57.241 --> 05:48:02.121
the same pip install command as long as they are separated by spaces. Here, we're

4313
05:48:02.121 --> 05:48:06.699
installing both scikit-learn and stats models in one go. We can also upgrade

4314
05:48:06.699 --> 05:48:11.700
multiple packages in one command. If you want to install many packages at once, you

4315
05:48:11.700 --> 05:48:16.519
can save them one package per line in a text file called requirements.txt. If we

4316
05:48:16.519 --> 05:48:20.978
preview the file, it looks like this. It's conventional for python package

4317
05:48:20.978 --> 05:48:21.580
developers

4318
05:48:21.580 --> 05:48:27.205
to create a requirements file listing all dependencies for pip to find and install.

4319
05:48:27.205 --> 05:48:32.560
The "-r option flag in pip allows pip install to install packages from the file

4320
05:48:32.560 --> 05:48:38.253
specified after the option flag. Keep in mind that naming this file requirements.txt

4321
05:48:38.253 --> 05:48:43.946
is convention, but not required. In our example, pip install "-r requirements", will

4322
05:48:43.946 --> 05:48:48.690
have the same effect as pip install scikit-learn stats model. However,

4323
05:48:48.690 --> 05:48:53.520
Imagine how much messier this could be if we had, say, 10 packages to install. Using

4324
05:48:53.520 --> 05:48:58.121
the requirements file method is much cleaner. We've built out an entire pipeline

4325
05:48:58.121 --> 05:49:02.779
previously from data processing using the kit, Python installation using pip, and

4326
05:49:02.779 --> 05:49:07.609
pre-written Python code via the command line. Now we're at the last step, automating

4327
05:49:07.609 --> 05:49:12.440
the entire process so that we can set it and forget it. What we need is a scheduler.

4328
05:49:12.440 --> 05:49:16.350
Schedulers run jobs like Python models on a pre-determined schedule.

4329
05:49:16.350 --> 05:49:21.693
and allows us to automate our data pipeline. There are quite a few commercial and

4330
05:49:21.693 --> 05:49:27.035
open source solutions on the market. Some of the popular ones are Airflow, Luigi,

4331
05:49:27.035 --> 05:49:32.444
and Rundeck. However, Cron is still a very strong contender among these commercial

4332
05:49:32.444 --> 05:49:37.852
options because it is simple, free, customizable, 100% command line, and native to

4333
05:49:37.852 --> 05:49:43.129
Mac OS and Linux. Cron is a time-based job scheduler that comes pre-installed in

4334
05:49:43.129 --> 05:49:44.910
Unix-like operating system.

4335
05:49:44.910 --> 05:49:49.883
This means that Cron already exists in macOS and Linux systems, but not in Windows.

4336
05:49:49.883 --> 05:49:54.856
For Windows users, you can use Cron by installing Cygwin, or you can use the native

4337
05:49:54.856 --> 05:49:59.829
Windows task scheduler. Cron can be used to automate a variety of jobs, from system

4338
05:49:59.829 --> 05:50:04.622
maintenance tasks, to bash scripts, to Python files. This means we can use it to

4339
05:50:04.622 --> 05:50:09.595
automate our bash scripts for pooling data using CSV kit. We can also use it to run

4340
05:50:09.595 --> 05:50:13.070
our Python mod. To schedule tasks, we need a central file.

4341
05:50:13.070 --> 05:50:18.156
To keep records of all our jobs, when to run them, and other instructions specific

4342
05:50:18.156 --> 05:50:23.241
to the schedule. This file is called the crontab file. To see what is currently in

4343
05:50:23.241 --> 05:50:28.265
this central file, type crontab-l. This will display all, if any, tasks currently

4344
05:50:28.265 --> 05:50:33.351
scheduled via crontab. It looks like we have no tasks scheduled. To see what other

4345
05:50:33.351 --> 05:50:38.189
option flags are available with crontab, type man crontab. We can add a job to

4346
05:50:38.189 --> 05:50:42.220
crontab, the same way we added a print function to a Python file.

4347
05:50:42.220 --> 05:50:49.220
Once this is done, we can verify that the command is correctly saved by running

4348
05:50:49.220 --> 05:50:56.574
crontab-l again. This time, we see that the Python job is properly scheduled. Let's

4349
05:50:56.574 --> 05:51:03.928
dig deeper into finding when to schedule a job. First, keep in mind that cron has a

4350
05:51:03.928 --> 05:51:10.750
60-second granularity, which means that the most frequent job we can schedule

4351
05:51:10.750 --> 05:51:15.887
is one run every minute. Second, there are five time component locations. Each

4352
05:51:15.887 --> 05:51:21.420
asterisk is indication of a time component. Starting from left to right are minutes,

4353
05:51:21.420 --> 05:51:26.953
hours, day, month, and day of the week. Let's take a look at an example. Asterisk is

4354
05:51:26.953 --> 05:51:32.486
a wildcard that's synonymous with always on in CronScheduler. Placing five asterisks

4355
05:51:32.486 --> 05:51:37.821
before the Python file, createModel.py, means we are scheduling Python to run the

4356
05:51:37.821 --> 05:51:40.060
script every minute of every hour.

4357
05:51:40.060 --> 05:51:45.152
of every day, of every month, and every day of the week. In short, we run this model

4358
05:51:45.152 --> 05:51:50.123
every minute until forever. For scheduling jobs and other frequencies, maybe every

4359
05:51:50.123 --> 05:51:55.094
day, every month, or every three hours, check out the website crontab.guru to help

4360
05:51:55.094 --> 05:52:00.126
you write out the cron jobs. Congratulations, you've reached the end of the course.

