05:55:48.290 --> 05:55:53.087
In the command line, you likely have used pipes to push the output of one command

4411
05:55:53.087 --> 05:55:57.884
into the next. If we had a file with many lines, we may take advantage of this by

4412
05:55:57.884 --> 05:56:02.799
piping the file contents to sort and then unique with the flag C. The first command

4413
05:56:02.799 --> 05:56:07.537
sorts the data alphabetically and the next line would do a distinct count. It is

4414
05:56:07.537 --> 05:56:12.096
important to sort first since unique only considers adjacent for its distinct

4415
05:56:12.096 --> 05:56:16.952
operation. If we wanted the top N fruits, we could use WC with the L flag and then

4416
05:56:16.952 --> 05:56:17.900
head as follows.

4417
05:56:17.900 --> 05:56:22.379
The examples we did here were only a taste of basic shell commands. Let's practice

4418
05:56:22.379 --> 05:56:26.640
some more shell commands to get you ready for... In this lesson, you will move

4419
05:56:26.640 --> 05:56:31.119
beyond command line execution and create your first bash script. There are several

4420
05:56:31.119 --> 05:56:35.707
key parts to a bash script. The first line has a shebang or hash bang, then the path

4421
05:56:35.707 --> 05:56:40.187
to where bash is. Most commonly, that is slash user slash bash. Bash could be in a

4422
05:56:40.187 --> 05:56:44.775
different location, so you can check where bash is using the command which bash. The

4423
05:56:44.775 --> 05:56:46.960
middle of the script contains your code.

4424
05:56:46.960 --> 05:56:51.670
It may be simple commands or more advanced code you will learn in this course. Once

4425
05:56:51.670 --> 05:56:56.267
your script is written, you can save it and run it. The file extension is usually

4426
05:56:56.267 --> 05:57:00.977
.sh, though this is only convention if you have the first line with the shebang and

4427
05:57:00.977 --> 05:57:05.574
path to bash mentioned before. You can run using the command bash followed by the

4428
05:57:05.574 --> 05:57:10.000
script name. If the first line in your script is the shebang and path to bash,

4429
05:57:10.757 --> 05:57:15.559
also simply run as dot slash and the script name. Here is an example of a full

4430
05:57:15.559 --> 05:57:15.990
script.

4431
05:57:16.070 --> 05:57:21.373
It begins with the shebang and path to bash. There are a few simple lines of code.

4432
05:57:21.373 --> 05:57:26.611
If you saved it as eg.sh, then you could run using dot slash eg.sh. The output is

4433
05:57:26.611 --> 05:57:31.850
produced here. Each code line is run in turn. Each line of a bash script can be a

4434
05:57:31.850 --> 05:57:37.218
shell command. So, you can also use pipes to create a chain of terminal commands in

4435
05:57:37.218 --> 05:57:42.327
the shell. Something you are perhaps already familiar with. Here is a text file

4436
05:57:42.327 --> 05:57:43.620
called animals.text.

4437
05:57:43.620 --> 05:57:48.052
It contains some animals and their grouping. We want to determine how many animals

4438
05:57:48.052 --> 05:57:52.321
are in each group. You may be able to write a chain of commands piped into each

4439
05:57:52.321 --> 05:57:56.644
other in the terminal. However, let's instead put this into a script. The script

4440
05:57:56.644 --> 05:57:58.590
begins with the hashbang as we know.

4441
05:57:58.630 --> 05:58:03.327
Then instead of a simple echo command, like the last example, we add in the command

4442
05:58:03.327 --> 05:58:07.628
we would use. This command concatenates the text file, pipes that to the cut

4443
05:58:07.628 --> 05:58:12.382
command, which will split on spaces and take the second field, the animal group. The

4444
05:58:12.382 --> 05:58:17.136
chain then sorts and does a unique count. You could try this in your own terminal to

4445
05:58:17.136 --> 05:58:21.437
confirm it works there. Now, instead of copying and pasting in that chain of

4446
05:58:21.437 --> 05:58:26.021
commands each time, you can simply run bash group.sh. You will get the aggregates

4447
05:58:26.021 --> 05:58:26.530
returned.

4448
05:58:26.530 --> 05:58:31.086
Let's practice writing your first simple. Let's learn more about inputs and outputs

4449
05:58:31.086 --> 05:58:35.532
for your bash scripts. It is also useful to know about the three streams for bash

4450
05:58:35.532 --> 05:58:39.868
programs. Standard input is the stream of data going into the program. Standard

4451
05:58:39.868 --> 05:58:44.479
output is the stream going out. And standard error is where errors and exceptions in

4452
05:58:44.479 --> 05:58:48.924
the program are written to. By default, this isn't obvious as the streams tend to

4453
05:58:48.924 --> 05:58:53.480
appear in the terminal. Though, you may see scripts called with this bit of code at

4454
05:58:53.480 --> 05:58:56.060
the end. This is redirecting the standard error

4455
05:58:56.060 --> 05:59:00.554
to part of the Unix system which deletes input. You could use the same using 1 to

4456
05:59:00.554 --> 05:59:05.103
redirect standard output. This graphical example represents what we just discussed

4457
05:59:05.103 --> 05:59:09.708
conceptually. The standard output of the CAT program becomes the standard input for

4458
05:59:09.708 --> 05:59:14.202
the CAT program and so on until the pipe finishes, which prints the last standard

4459
05:59:14.202 --> 05:59:18.751
output to the terminal. This is the expected behavior. Though if an error occurred

4460
05:59:18.751 --> 05:59:23.356
at any time, the error would by default display in the terminal. Consider this text

4461
05:59:23.356 --> 05:59:25.020
file with three lines of data.

4462
05:59:25.060 --> 05:59:29.937
With this text file, running the cat command as seen, we'll take the data from the

4463
05:59:29.937 --> 05:59:34.815
file and redirect the standard output, the file contents, to a new file called new

4464
05:59:34.815 --> 05:59:39.692
sports.text. The one means standard output. You could cat the file created and see

4465
05:59:39.692 --> 05:59:44.570
that, indeed, the standard output of the first cat ended up in the new file. A key

4466
05:59:44.570 --> 05:59:49.269
concept for bash scripting is the use of arguments. Bash scripts take arguments

4467
05:59:49.269 --> 05:59:52.540
specified when making the execution call of the script.

4468
05:59:52.550 --> 05:59:57.710
argv is a term to describe all the arguments that are fed into the script. You can

4469
05:59:57.710 --> 06:00:02.933
access the arguments using numerical dollar sign notation, the first argument being

4470
06:00:02.933 --> 06:00:08.220
$1, etc. Some other special arguments are $at symbol and $star, which return all the

4471
06:00:08.220 --> 06:00:13.254
arguments together. $h gives the number of arguments. Let's make this conceptual

4472
06:00:13.254 --> 06:00:18.351
idea concrete. Consider a script with the following code inside. We hope to print

4473
06:00:18.351 --> 06:00:22.190
out the first, then second arguments, then all the arguments.

4474
06:00:22.190 --> 06:00:27.048
Then a phrase, including how many arguments. Let's see how it goes. If we execute

4475
06:00:27.048 --> 06:00:32.087
that script, as noted here, the strings one, two, three, four, five are all separate

4476
06:00:32.087 --> 06:00:36.825
arguments. Indeed, our return will be as expected. We print out the first, then

4477
06:00:36.825 --> 06:00:41.563
second, then all the arguments, and finally our phrase containing the number of

4478
06:00:41.563 --> 06:00:46.542
arguments, which is five. Let's practice using arguments. Let's learn how to create

4479
06:00:46.542 --> 06:00:51.100
some basic variables in bash script. Assigning variables is similar to other

4480
06:00:51.100 --> 06:00:51.700
languages.

4481
06:00:51.700 --> 06:00:56.494
You can assign the variable name using the equals notation. Here we make a variable

4482
06:00:56.494 --> 06:01:00.883
var1 and assign the string moon to be its value. Then you can reference that

4483
06:01:00.883 --> 06:01:05.677
variable with the dollar sign notation. You can name the variables as you like, and

4484
06:01:05.677 --> 06:01:10.528
it is good practice to name them something which makes sense. Let's create variables

4485
06:01:10.528 --> 06:01:14.860
for a person's first and last name, and then echo out using the dollar sign

4486
06:01:14.860 --> 06:01:19.481
notation. Nice. We were able to access and print both variables. The dollar sign

4487
06:01:19.481 --> 06:01:21.040
notation is very important.

4488
06:01:21.040 --> 06:01:25.899
Otherwise, Bash doesn't understand you are referencing a variable. Here, we create

4489
06:01:25.899 --> 06:01:30.639
the same variables, but don't reference with the dollar sign notation. Bash just

4490
06:01:30.639 --> 06:01:35.557
thinks we are echoing out three strings, not variables. Bash is not as forgiving as

4491
06:01:35.557 --> 06:01:39.290
other languages. You must not add spaces around the equal sign.

4492
06:01:39.330 --> 06:01:43.997
See here, we add some spaces in the variable assignment. When we run the script,

4493
06:01:43.997 --> 06:01:48.605
there is this error. In Bash, using different types of quotation marks can mean

4494
06:01:48.605 --> 06:01:53.447
different things when creating variables and printing. Single quotation marks means

4495
06:01:53.447 --> 06:01:58.172
that whatever is between them is interpreted literally. Double quotation marks is

4496
06:01:58.172 --> 06:02:02.955
similar to single quotation marks, but it will understand dollar sign notation and

4497
06:02:02.955 --> 06:02:07.855
back ticks that occur between the quotation marks. The last way is quite special and

4498
06:02:07.855 --> 06:02:08.380
powerful.

4499
06:02:08.380 --> 06:02:13.617
Backticks creates a shell within a shell. That is, it sends whatever is between the

4500
06:02:13.617 --> 06:02:18.916
backticks out to a new shell and takes the results back into the variable. Let's see

4501
06:02:18.916 --> 06:02:24.153
these methods in action. Let's see the effect of different types of quotation marks

4502
06:02:24.153 --> 06:02:29.263
for variable creation. Firstly, we create a variable called nowvar and assign the

4503
06:02:29.263 --> 06:02:34.184
string now. We then create a variable using single quotes around a dollar sign

4504
06:02:34.184 --> 06:02:36.140
notation call to this variable.

4505
06:02:36.140 --> 06:02:40.875
You can see that the use of single quotes treats what we gave it literally. It

4506
06:02:40.875 --> 06:02:45.731
doesn't understand there is text inside that variable. We also create a variable

4507
06:02:45.731 --> 06:02:50.769
with double quotes in the same way. The double quotes understands our variable call

4508
06:02:50.769 --> 06:02:55.504
and returns the now text inside that variable. We will use the date program to

4509
06:02:55.504 --> 06:03:00.481
demonstrate the use of back ticks for variable creation. The normal output of this

4510
06:03:00.481 --> 06:03:03.820
program is as follows. In terminal, type the word date.

4511
06:03:03.820 --> 06:03:08.479
You will get the current date and time returned as a string. Now, let's use the

4512
06:03:08.479 --> 06:03:13.197
powerful shell within a shell construct. We create a variable similar to before.

4513
06:03:13.197 --> 06:03:18.032
However, instead of using single quotes, we use backticks around the word date and

4514
06:03:18.032 --> 06:03:22.750
echo the resulting variable. We can see the result includes the current date and

4515
06:03:22.750 --> 06:03:27.409
time. What actually happened here is that a mini shell was opened between those

4516
06:03:27.409 --> 06:03:29.650
backticks and called the date program.

4517
06:03:29.650 --> 06:03:34.835
The result of this call was then saved back as a string in line. Congratulations,

4518
06:03:34.835 --> 06:03:40.148
the shell within a shell is a powerful and useful construct to add to your toolkit.

4519
06:03:40.148 --> 06:03:45.461
There is an alternate way to invoke a shell within a shell. This uses a dollar sign

4520
06:03:45.461 --> 06:03:50.582
around parentheses, as can be seen here. We create two variables around the date

4521
06:03:50.582 --> 06:03:52.310
command and echo them both.

4522
06:03:52.390 --> 06:03:56.923
You can see the result is exactly the same. Either method works. The backticks

4523
06:03:56.923 --> 06:04:01.805
method is more backwards compatible as it is older, though the parentheses method is

4524
06:04:01.805 --> 06:04:06.396
more modern and has some advantages, discussed in the link here. Let's practice

4525
06:04:06.396 --> 06:04:11.046
creating and using variables in Bash. As data people, I'm sure you want to learn

4526
06:04:11.046 --> 06:04:15.579
about using numeric variables in Bash. Let's get started. Working with numeric

4527
06:04:15.579 --> 06:04:18.020
variables is not natively built into Bash.

4528
06:04:18.020 --> 06:04:23.012
as it is in other languages. In R or Python REPL, you may be used to just typing

4529
06:04:23.012 --> 06:04:27.754
arithmetic like this and getting the result you want. You cannot natively do

4530
06:04:27.754 --> 06:04:32.871
arithmetic in bash. If you try the following in the bash terminal, you will get an

4531
06:04:32.871 --> 06:04:38.113
error like this. A useful command line program is expra. This is a program just like

4532
06:04:38.113 --> 06:04:43.354
cat and grep, which can be used for basic arithmetic. Now, if we do the following in

4533
06:04:43.354 --> 06:04:46.100
a terminal, we get the result we want. Nice.

4534
06:04:46.100 --> 06:04:51.466
EXPRA has a big limitation though. It cannot handle decimal places. If you do the

4535
06:04:51.466 --> 06:04:56.633
following in the terminal, this will be the error you get. However, there is a

4536
06:04:56.633 --> 06:05:01.601
solution. Another useful command line program is BC, which stands for basic

4537
06:05:01.601 --> 06:05:02.330
calculator.

4538
06:05:02.370 --> 06:05:08.079
You can enter BC just like a REPL in the command line. In this picture, you can see

4539
06:05:08.079 --> 06:05:13.857
BC being invoked, entering 5 plus 7, which returns 12, then the word quit exits back

4540
06:05:13.857 --> 06:05:19.291
to the shell. You can use BC without opening the program using piping, since BC

4541
06:05:19.291 --> 06:05:24.793
accepts arguments of numbers and operations. Here, we send the string 5 plus 7.5

4542
06:05:24.793 --> 06:05:30.021
into BC using a pipe. The return is exactly what we want, the decimal number

4543
06:05:30.021 --> 06:05:30.640
returned.

4544
06:05:30.640 --> 06:05:35.851
BC also has a useful argument, scale, which allows you to specify how many decimal

4545
06:05:35.851 --> 06:05:40.743
places for the return value. Here, we know the answer is 3.3 repeated, but by

4546
06:05:40.743 --> 06:05:46.018
default, BC will return the value 3 for this division. Let's see what happens if we

4547
06:05:46.018 --> 06:05:51.228
echo in the scale argument. Note the use of the semicolon to separate lines in the

4548
06:05:51.228 --> 06:05:51.800
terminal.

4549
06:05:51.880 --> 06:05:56.358
Now we get the result we were expecting to three decimal places. You can assign

4550
06:05:56.358 --> 06:06:00.836
numeric variables much the same way as the string variables you did previously.

4551
06:06:00.836 --> 06:06:03.670
Remember not to have spaces around the equal sign.

4552
06:06:03.750 --> 06:06:08.494
In this example, we set a string variable of a dog's name and a numeric variable of

4553
06:06:08.494 --> 06:06:13.181
the dog's age. You could have also used quotes around the six, but this would make

4554
06:06:13.181 --> 06:06:17.982
it a string variable so may limit what you could do later. Then we echo the sentence

4555
06:06:17.982 --> 06:06:22.612
noted. Since we use double quotes, we can use dollar sign notation to interpolate

4556
06:06:22.612 --> 06:06:27.413
the variable names within the echo call. This returns the correct sentence. There is

4557
06:06:27.413 --> 06:06:32.043
a special variant on the single bracket notation discussed in the previous lesson

4558
06:06:32.043 --> 06:06:33.300
for numeric variables.

4559
06:06:33.300 --> 06:06:38.584
Here we use expra to add two numbers. However, we can achieve the same result using

4560
06:06:38.584 --> 06:06:43.486
double parentheses, as seen here. We get back the same result for both calls.

4561
06:06:43.486 --> 06:06:48.834
Beware, this method uses expra, not bc, so you cannot use decimals with this method.

4562
06:06:48.834 --> 06:06:53.799
Do you remember when we use single parentheses or back ticks to create a shell

4563
06:06:53.799 --> 06:06:58.892
within a shell in the last lesson? This is very useful for numeric variables and

4564
06:06:58.892 --> 06:07:01.630
calculations. Here we create two variables,

4565
06:07:01.630 --> 06:07:06.488
some accuracy scores for two models. We can use the single bracket notation to

4566
06:07:06.488 --> 06:07:11.409
create a shell within a shell where we echo the sum of these to BC. On the next

4567
06:07:11.409 --> 06:07:16.641
line, we do something more complex, building on the first line and adding a division

4568
06:07:16.641 --> 06:07:21.810
after the sum to get the average. Again, the shell within a shell returns the value

4569
06:07:21.810 --> 06:07:27.042
inside the echo call. We see the sum and average printed out in line. Let's practice

4570
06:07:27.042 --> 06:07:30.530
using numeric variables in bash scripts. In this lesson,

4571
06:07:30.530 --> 06:07:35.766
We'll explore arrays. Let's get started. There are two types of arrays in Bash. The

4572
06:07:35.766 --> 06:07:40.749
first and most common is a numerical indexed array. These are known as lists in

4573
06:07:40.749 --> 06:07:42.200
Python or vectors in R.

4574
06:07:42.310 --> 06:07:47.809
You may be familiar with creating these in Python or R. There are two ways to create

4575
06:07:47.809 --> 06:07:52.980
a numerical indexed array in Bash. You can construct the array without any data

4576
06:07:52.980 --> 06:07:58.151
elements. You use the word declare, the A flag, and an array name. Or you could

4577
06:07:58.151 --> 06:08:03.519
create and add elements at the same time like this, similar to string or numerical

4578
06:08:03.519 --> 06:08:08.690
variable creation. Remember, don't add space around the equal sign. Unlike many

4579
06:08:08.690 --> 06:08:10.130
programming languages,

4580
06:08:10.130 --> 06:08:15.662
Bash separates array elements with spaces and not commas. This is not correct. This

4581
06:08:15.662 --> 06:08:20.927
is correct as it uses spaces between array elements. Bash has some useful array

4582
06:08:20.927 --> 06:08:26.393
properties. You can return all array elements by indexing into the array using the

4583
06:08:26.393 --> 06:08:31.925
at symbol. The length of an array is returned by adding a pound symbol to the front

4584
06:08:31.925 --> 06:08:37.057
of a call to the entire array. Accessing an array element uses square bracket

4585
06:08:37.057 --> 06:08:38.590
syntax. In our example,

4586
06:08:38.590 --> 06:08:41.500
To return the third element, we would write this.

4587
06:08:41.570 --> 06:08:47.127
The third element is returned successfully. Bash uses zero indexing for arrays. This

4588
06:08:47.127 --> 06:08:52.552
is just like Python, but different from R. You can change array values using index

4589
06:08:52.552 --> 06:08:57.845
notation. Here, we create an array, then change the first element to be 999, and

4590
06:08:57.845 --> 06:09:03.072
print to confirm. Don't use the dollar index notation when overwriting a single

4591
06:09:03.072 --> 06:09:08.365
element, as this won't work. You can also slice out parts of an array using this

4592
06:09:08.365 --> 06:09:08.960
notation.

4593
06:09:08.960 --> 06:09:14.809
The notation adds to the call to print the entire array with a colon, number, colon,

4594
06:09:14.809 --> 06:09:20.519
number. n and m mean the starting index and the number of elements to return after

4595
06:09:20.519 --> 06:09:26.020
that index. Here we start at the third index, or fourth element, and return two

4596
06:09:26.020 --> 06:09:31.451
elements, which returns as so. Appending to arrays uses the plus-equal syntax,

4597
06:09:31.451 --> 06:09:36.743
ensuring the elements you want to add are surrounded by parentheses. In this

4598
06:09:36.743 --> 06:09:37.300
example,

4599
06:09:37.300 --> 06:09:42.320
We create an array of numbers, then add 10 to the end. Then we print out to check.

4600
06:09:42.320 --> 06:09:47.462
Indeed, 10 is added to the end. What happens if you don't wrap the elements you want

4601
06:09:47.462 --> 06:09:52.237
to append in parentheses? Let's see. We repeat the same example, but don't use

4602
06:09:52.237 --> 06:09:57.256
parentheses when adding 10. The string 10 is just added to our first element. This

4603
06:09:57.256 --> 06:10:02.031
is not what we want at all. The second type of array in Bash is an associative

4604
06:10:02.031 --> 06:10:06.500
array. Its index is made of strings that are keys with associated values.

4605
06:10:06.500 --> 06:10:11.470
This is similar to a dictionary in Python, or a list in R. Note this is only

4606
06:10:11.470 --> 06:10:16.963
available in bash 4 onwards. In Python, you would create a dictionary like so, and a

4607
06:10:16.963 --> 06:10:22.391
list like this in R. Creating an associative array has slightly different syntax to

4608
06:10:22.391 --> 06:10:27.753
a normal array. You must use the declare syntax. Either declare first and then add

4609
06:10:27.753 --> 06:10:33.050
elements later, or declare an add in one line. You set keys using square brackets

4610
06:10:33.050 --> 06:10:35.470
and the values after the equals sign.

4611
06:10:35.470 --> 06:10:40.340
You may add multiple elements at once. Let's make an associative array of properties

4612
06:10:40.340 --> 06:10:44.514
about a city. We declare first using the correct flag and call the array

4613
06:10:44.514 --> 06:10:49.036
cityDetails. We set the keys to be the city name and the population with these

4614
06:10:49.036 --> 06:10:53.907
associated values. We can index into this array using any of the keys. Here we index

4615
06:10:53.907 --> 06:10:58.661
using the city name key and it returns what we expect, New York. The second method

4616
06:10:58.661 --> 06:11:03.473
is to declare and add elements all on the same line. Everything else, such as rules

4617
06:11:03.473 --> 06:11:04.980
around square parentheses,

4618
06:11:04.980 --> 06:11:09.746
Equal signs, et cetera are followed. Associative arrays have an extra property to

4619
06:11:09.746 --> 06:11:14.572
normal arrays. You can return the keys using an exclamation sign like here. We see

4620
06:11:14.572 --> 06:11:19.221
the keys returned. Now you have developed your understanding of arrays in bash.

