06:27:41.271 --> 06:27:46.066
Learning to schedule with Cron is an essential skill in modern data infrastructure.

4831
06:27:46.066 --> 06:27:46.990
The program Cron

4832
06:27:46.990 --> 06:27:51.633
has been part of Unix-like systems for a long time. Since the 70s, in fact. We

4833
06:27:51.633 --> 06:27:56.454
humans have been lazy for that long. The name comes from the Greek word for time,

4834
06:27:56.454 --> 06:28:01.216
Kronos. This program is driven by something called a crontab. This file contains

4835
06:28:01.216 --> 06:28:05.621
cronjobs, which tells crontab which code to run and when. You can see what

4836
06:28:05.621 --> 06:28:10.502
schedules, called cronjobs, you have currently programmed by running the following

4837
06:28:10.502 --> 06:28:15.442
in the terminal. We see that this user has no cronjobs. We'd better make a schedule

4838
06:28:15.442 --> 06:28:15.740
then.

4839
06:28:15.740 --> 06:28:20.635
This image from Wikipedia demonstrates how to construct a cron job. You can have as

4840
06:28:20.635 --> 06:28:25.529
many cron jobs as you like, one per line in the crontab file. There are five stars,

4841
06:28:25.529 --> 06:28:30.306
one for each time unit. There are minutes, hours, days of the month, month of the

4842
06:28:30.306 --> 06:28:34.729
year, and days of the week. The default, just a star, means to run at every

4843
06:28:34.729 --> 06:28:39.388
interval. Let's walk through some example cron jobs. In this first example, the

4844
06:28:39.388 --> 06:28:43.870
minute star is five, meaning five minutes past the hour. Which hour? Well...

4845
06:28:43.870 --> 06:28:50.350
The hours star is 1, meaning after 1AM. The last three are all stars, meaning every.

4846
06:28:50.350 --> 06:28:56.445
So overall, we run myscript.sh every day at 1.05AM. In this second example, the

4847
06:28:56.445 --> 06:29:02.848
minutes star is 15, meaning 15 minutes past the hour. The hours star is 14, meaning

4848
06:29:02.848 --> 06:29:09.251
after 2PM. The stars for day of month and month of year are every. The last star is

4849
06:29:09.251 --> 06:29:13.340
day seven, on Sundays. So overall, we run myscript.sh

4850
06:29:13.340 --> 06:29:19.712
You can also run programs multiple times per day, or other time units, or even every

4851
06:29:19.712 --> 06:29:25.781
X time increment. For specific intervals, you can use a comma. For example, this

4852
06:29:25.781 --> 06:29:32.153
cron job would run at 15, 30, and 45 minutes past the hour, for every hour specified

4853
06:29:32.153 --> 06:29:38.297
in the next star. Here, it is every hour, every day, etc. You can use a slash for

4854
06:29:38.297 --> 06:29:41.180
every X increment. In another example,

4855
06:29:41.180 --> 06:29:46.669
This cron job runs every 15 minutes. Again, for every hour, every day, etc. Let's

4856
06:29:46.669 --> 06:29:52.293
schedule a script called Extract Data to run every morning at 1.30am. The steps are

4857
06:29:52.293 --> 06:29:57.918
as follows. Firstly, type crontab with an E flag to edit your list of cron jobs. If

4858
06:29:57.918 --> 06:30:03.610
this is the first time, it may ask you which editor you want to use. Nano is an easy

4859
06:30:03.610 --> 06:30:09.235
option and has a less steep learning curve than Vi or Vim. Create the cron job on a

4860
06:30:09.235 --> 06:30:09.980
blank line.

4861
06:30:09.980 --> 06:30:15.013
We want it to run at 30 minutes past the first hour, every day, week, month, etc. So

4862
06:30:15.013 --> 06:30:19.747
we put 30 as the first star, 1 as the second star, and leave the rest as every.

4863
06:30:19.747 --> 06:30:24.780
Finally, you need to exit the editor, saving the cron tab. If you were using Nano on

4864
06:30:24.780 --> 06:30:29.693
a Mac, this is how you exit and save. You may need to Google the commands for your

4865
06:30:29.693 --> 06:30:34.427
system and editor. You will see a note in the terminal that a cron tab has been

4866
06:30:34.427 --> 06:30:39.280
installed. Let's check that the cron job was correctly programmed by running cron

4867
06:30:39.280 --> 06:30:39.520
tab,

4868
06:30:39.520 --> 06:30:44.177
with the L flag. The cron tab was returned. Great stuff. Let's practice scheduling

4869
06:30:44.177 --> 06:30:48.891
some bash scripts. Well done for reaching the end. Let's summarize our learning. In

4870
06:30:48.891 --> 06:30:53.662
chapter one, you learned the basics, such as how bash scripts work with command line

4871
06:30:53.662 --> 06:30:58.432
programs, how they are structured, and how data streams in and out with standard in,

4872
06:30:58.432 --> 06:31:03.146
standard error, and standard output. Remember, argv is the array of arguments given

4873
06:31:03.146 --> 06:31:07.860
to a bash script and is vital knowledge. The special properties you learned include

4874
06:31:07.860 --> 06:31:13.293
Accessing using $number notation and $at symbol for returning all the elements, you

4875
06:31:13.293 --> 06:31:18.464
went far beyond this simple exercise just here. In Chapter 2, you learned about

4876
06:31:18.464 --> 06:31:23.896
creating and using different bash variables, including string, numerical, and array

4877
06:31:23.896 --> 06:31:29.264
variables, as well as arithmetic using expra and bc. You learned the importance of

4878
06:31:29.264 --> 06:31:34.762
different quotation marks, including single, double, and backticks. We use the shell

4879
06:31:34.762 --> 06:31:36.660
within a shell concept often.

4880
06:31:36.660 --> 06:31:41.528
You could call a shell in place and capture the return value, just like in this

4881
06:31:41.528 --> 06:31:46.642
simple example. In chapters three and four, you learn to master the control of your

4882
06:31:46.642 --> 06:31:51.447
scripts with, for, while, case, and if statements, modularized functions, plus

4883
06:31:51.447 --> 06:31:56.315
pushing data in and out of them. Finally, you learn to schedule all the amazing

4884
06:31:56.315 --> 06:32:01.244
scripts you have and will create with cron, so you never need to remember to run

4885
06:32:01.244 --> 06:32:04.140
your scripts. Thank you for taking this course.

4886
06:32:04.140 --> 06:32:08.931
And congratulations for making it to the end. Bash is a tricky but powerful and

4887
06:32:08.931 --> 06:32:13.844
useful language. Now go automate some tedious things with your new skill. Hi, I'm

4888
06:32:13.844 --> 06:32:18.453
Alex, and I'll be teaching you to think object-oriented. Until now, you have

4889
06:32:18.453 --> 06:32:23.244
probably been coding in so-called procedural style. Your code was a sequence of

4890
06:32:23.244 --> 06:32:28.217
steps to be carried out. This is common when doing data analysis. You download the

4891
06:32:28.217 --> 06:32:30.340
data, process it, and visualize it.

4892
06:32:30.340 --> 06:32:34.676
Procedural thinking is natural. You get up, have breakfast, go to work. This

4893
06:32:34.676 --> 06:32:39.412
sequential viewpoint works great if you are trying to plan your day. But if you are

4894
06:32:39.412 --> 06:32:44.205
a city planner, you have to think about thousands of people with their own routines.

4895
06:32:44.205 --> 06:32:48.599
Trying to map out a sequence of actions for each individual would quickly get

4896
06:32:48.599 --> 06:32:53.392
unsustainable. Instead, you are likely to start thinking about patterns of behavior.

4897
06:32:53.392 --> 06:32:54.590
Same thing with code.

4898
06:32:54.590 --> 06:32:59.017
The more data it uses, the more functionality it has, the harder it is to think

4899
06:32:59.017 --> 06:33:03.555
about as just a sequence of steps. Instead, we view it as a collection of objects

4900
06:33:03.555 --> 06:33:07.870
and patterns of their interaction, like users interacting with elements of an

4901
06:33:07.870 --> 06:33:12.297
interface. This point of view becomes invaluable when designing framework, like

4902
06:33:12.297 --> 06:33:16.947
application program interfaces or graphical user interfaces, or building tools like

4903
06:33:16.947 --> 06:33:17.900
Pandas DataFrame.

4904
06:33:17.900 --> 06:33:22.274
It will help you organize your code better, making it more reusable and

4905
06:33:22.274 --> 06:33:27.388
maintainable. The fundamental concepts of OOP are objects and classes. An object is

4906
06:33:27.388 --> 06:33:32.563
a data structure incorporating information about state and behavior. For example, an

4907
06:33:32.563 --> 06:33:37.676
object representing a customer can have a certain phone number and email associated

4908
06:33:37.676 --> 06:33:42.789
with them, and behaviors like place order or cancel order. An object representing a

4909
06:33:42.789 --> 06:33:47.410
button on a website can have a label and can trigger an event when pressed.

4910
06:33:47.410 --> 06:33:52.131
The distinctive feature of OLAP is that state and behavior are bundled together.

4911
06:33:52.131 --> 06:33:56.970
Instead of thinking of customer data separately from customer actions, we think of

4912
06:33:56.970 --> 06:34:01.927
them as one unit representing a customer. This is called encapsulation and it is one

4913
06:34:01.927 --> 06:34:06.352
of the core tenets of object-oriented. The real strength of OLAP comes from

4914
06:34:06.352 --> 06:34:10.896
utilizing classes. Classes are like blueprints for objects. They describe the

4915
06:34:10.896 --> 06:34:15.440
possible states and behaviors that every object of a certain type could have.

4916
06:34:15.440 --> 06:34:20.108
For example, if you say, every customer will have a phone number and an email and

4917
06:34:20.108 --> 06:34:24.950
will be able to place and cancel orders, you just defined a class. This way, you can

4918
06:34:24.950 --> 06:34:29.560
talk about customers in a unified way. Then a specific customer object is just a

4919
06:34:29.560 --> 06:34:34.344
realization of this class with particular state values. In Python, everything is an

4920
06:34:34.344 --> 06:34:39.012
object. Numbers, strings, data frames, even functions are objects. In particular,

4921
06:34:39.012 --> 06:34:43.565
everything you deal with in Python has a class, a blueprint, associated with it

4922
06:34:43.565 --> 06:34:44.430
under the hood.

4923
06:34:44.430 --> 06:34:49.766
The existence of these unified interfaces is why you can use, for example, any data

4924
06:34:49.766 --> 06:34:55.166
frame in the same way. You can call type on any Python object to find out its class.

4925
06:34:55.166 --> 06:35:00.566
For example, the class of a numpy array is actually called ndarray for n-dimensional

4926
06:35:00.566 --> 06:35:05.837
array. Classes incorporate information about state and behavior. State information

4927
06:35:05.837 --> 06:35:10.530
in Python is contained in attributes and behavior information in methods.

4928
06:35:10.530 --> 06:35:15.802
Take a numpy array. You've already been using some of its methods and attributes.

4929
06:35:15.802 --> 06:35:20.748
For example, every numpy array has an attribute shape that you can access by

4930
06:35:20.748 --> 06:35:26.216
specifying the name of the array, then dot, then shape. It also has methods like max

4931
06:35:26.216 --> 06:35:31.357
and reshape which are also accessible via dot. Attributes in Python objects are

4932
06:35:31.357 --> 06:35:36.499
represented by variables like numbers or strings or tuples in the case of numpy

4933
06:35:36.499 --> 06:35:37.280
array shape.

4934
06:35:37.280 --> 06:35:41.841
Methods, or behaviors, are represented by functions. Both are accessible from an

4935
06:35:41.841 --> 06:35:46.459
object using the dots. You can list all the attributes and methods that an object

4936
06:35:46.459 --> 06:35:50.963
has by calling dir on it. For example, a numpy array has methods like trace and

4937
06:35:50.963 --> 06:35:55.638
transpose. Let's stop here. Don't worry if it seems confused. It's a different way

4938
06:35:55.638 --> 06:36:00.428
of thinking and it will take some time to get you. Now head over to the exercises to

4939
06:36:00.428 --> 06:36:04.818
review what you've done. Great job so far! Now that you know how to work with

4940
06:36:04.818 --> 06:36:06.300
existing objects in class.

4941
06:36:06.300 --> 06:36:11.002
You'll learn how to create your own. To start a new class definition, all you need

4942
06:36:11.002 --> 06:36:15.417
is a class statement, followed by the name of the class, followed by a colon.

4943
06:36:15.417 --> 06:36:20.118
Everything in the indented block after will be considered a part of the class. You

4944
06:36:20.118 --> 06:36:24.763
can create an empty class, like a blank template, by including the past statement

4945
06:36:24.763 --> 06:36:29.522
after the class declaration. Even though this class is empty, we can already create

4946
06:36:29.522 --> 06:36:34.223
objects of the class by specifying the name of the class, followed by parentheses.

4947
06:36:34.223 --> 06:36:34.510
Here,

4948
06:36:34.510 --> 06:36:39.433
C1 and C2 are two different objects of the empty class Customer. We want to create

4949
06:36:39.433 --> 06:36:44.416
objects that actually store data and operate in it, in other words, have attributes

4950
06:36:44.416 --> 06:36:49.340
and methods. Defining a method is simple. Methods are functions, so the definition

4951
06:36:49.340 --> 06:36:50.000
of a method

4952
06:36:50.787 --> 06:36:55.162
looks just like a regular Python function, with one exception, the special

4953
06:36:55.162 --> 06:37:00.009
self-argument that every method will have as the first argument, possibly followed

4954
06:37:00.009 --> 06:37:04.797
by other arguments. We'll get back to self in a minute. First, let's see how this

4955
06:37:04.797 --> 06:37:09.230
works. Here, we defined a method identify for the customer class that takes

4956
06:37:09.230 --> 06:37:14.572
self and a name as a parameter and prints IAMCustomer plus name when called. We

4957
06:37:14.572 --> 06:37:20.252
create a new customer object, call the method by using object.method syntax and pass

4958
06:37:20.252 --> 06:37:25.797
the desired name and get the output. Note that name was the second argument in the

4959
06:37:25.797 --> 06:37:31.139
method definition, but it is the first parameter when the method is called. The

4960
06:37:31.139 --> 06:37:36.683
mysterious self is not needed in the method called. So what was that self? Classes

4961
06:37:36.683 --> 06:37:37.630
are templates.

4962
06:37:37.630 --> 06:37:42.226
Objects of a class don't yet exist when a class is being defined. But we often need

4963
06:37:42.226 --> 06:37:46.766
a way to refer to the data of a particular object within class definition. This is

4964
06:37:46.766 --> 06:37:51.362
the purpose of self. It's a standing for the future object. That's why every method

4965
06:37:51.362 --> 06:37:55.737
should have the self argument. So we could use it to access attributes and call

4966
06:37:55.737 --> 06:38:00.166
other methods from within the class definition even when no objects were created

4967
06:38:00.166 --> 06:38:04.762
yet. Python will handle self when the method is called from an object using the dot

4968
06:38:04.762 --> 06:38:05.150
syntax.

4969
06:38:05.150 --> 06:38:09.878
In fact, using object.method is equivalent to passing that object as an argument.

4970
06:38:09.878 --> 06:38:14.664
That's why we don't specify it explicitly when calling the method from an existing

4971
06:38:14.664 --> 06:38:19.567
object. By the principles of OOP, the data describing the state of the object should

4972
06:38:19.567 --> 06:38:24.354
be bundled into the object. For example, customer name should be an attribute of a

4973
06:38:24.354 --> 06:38:29.082
customer object instead of a parameter passed to a method. In Python, attributes,

4974
06:38:29.082 --> 06:38:33.635
like variables, are created by assignment, meaning an attribute manifests into

4975
06:38:33.635 --> 06:38:34.160
existence

4976
06:38:34.160 --> 06:38:39.607
only when a value is assigned to it. Here is a method setName with arguments self,

4977
06:38:39.607 --> 06:38:45.187
every method should have a self argument, and newName. To create an attribute of the

4978
06:38:45.187 --> 06:38:50.635
customer class called name, all we need to do is to assign something to self.name.

4979
06:38:50.635 --> 06:38:56.148
Remember, self is a stand-in for object, so self.attribute should remind you of the

4980
06:38:56.148 --> 06:39:01.662
object.attribute syntax. Here we set the name attribute to the newName parameter of

4981
06:39:01.662 --> 06:39:02.260
the file.

4982
06:39:02.260 --> 06:39:07.029
When we create a customer, it does not yet have a name attribute. But after the

4983
06:39:07.029 --> 06:39:11.858
setName method was called, the attribute is created and we can access it through

4984
06:39:11.858 --> 06:39:16.928
.name. Equipped with a name attribute, now we can improve our identification method.

4985
06:39:16.928 --> 06:39:21.878
Instead of passing name as a parameter, we will use the data already stored in the

4986
06:39:21.878 --> 06:39:26.949
name attribute of the customer class. We remove the name parameter from the identify

4987
06:39:26.949 --> 06:39:30.510
method and replace it with self.name in the printout, which

4988
06:39:30.510 --> 06:39:35.314
by a self will pull the name attribute from the object that call that method. Now

4989
06:39:35.314 --> 06:39:40.000
the identify function will only use the data that is encapsulated in the object

4990
06:39:40.000 --> 06:39:44.983
instead of using whatever we pass. Now, let's start creating your own class. Welcome

4991
06:39:44.983 --> 06:39:49.787
back! In the previous lesson, you learned how to define methods and attributes in

4992
06:39:49.787 --> 06:39:54.710
classes. You learned that methods are functions within a class with a special first

4993
06:39:54.710 --> 06:39:55.600
argument, self.

4994
06:39:55.600 --> 06:39:59.966
and that attributes are created by assignment and referred to using the self

4995
06:39:59.966 --> 06:40:04.735
variable within method. In the exercise, you created an employee class and for each

4996
06:40:04.735 --> 06:40:09.216
attribute you wanted to create, you defined a new method and then called those

4997
06:40:09.216 --> 06:40:13.755
methods one after another. This could quickly get unsustainable if your classes

4998
06:40:13.755 --> 06:40:18.351
contain a lot of data. A better strategy would be to add data to the object when

4999
06:40:18.351 --> 06:40:19.040
creating it.

5000
06:40:19.040 --> 06:40:24.135
like when you do when creating an umpire array or a data frame. Python allows you to

5001
06:40:24.135 --> 06:40:29.168
add a special method called the constructor that is automatically called every time

5002
06:40:29.168 --> 06:40:33.717
an object is created. The method has to be named underscore underscore init

5003
06:40:33.717 --> 06:40:38.448
underscore underscore. The exact name and double underscores are essential for

5004
06:40:38.448 --> 06:40:43.239
Python to recognize. Here we define the init method for the customer class. The

5005
06:40:43.239 --> 06:40:45.180
method takes one argument, name,

5006
06:40:45.180 --> 06:40:50.102
Of course, in addition to the self-argument that should be there for any method. In

5007
06:40:50.102 --> 06:40:54.787
the body of the method, we create the name attribute, set its value to the name

5008
06:40:54.787 --> 06:40:59.057
parameter, and print a message. So now, we can pass the customer name in

5009
06:40:59.057 --> 06:41:03.267
parentheses. When creating the customer object, the init method will be

5010
06:41:03.267 --> 06:41:08.189
automatically called, and the name attribute created. We can add another parameter,

5011
06:41:08.189 --> 06:41:12.400
say, account balance, to the init method, and create another attribute.

5012
06:41:12.400 --> 06:41:17.145
also called balance that will also be initialized during object creation. We can now

5013
06:41:17.145 --> 06:41:21.778
create a customer by calling customer with two parameters in parentheses. The init

5014
06:41:21.778 --> 06:41:26.184
constructor is also a good place to set the default values for attributes. For

5015
06:41:26.184 --> 06:41:30.703
example, here we set the default value of the balance argument to zero so we can

5016
06:41:30.703 --> 06:41:35.053
create a customer object without specifying the value of the balance, but the

5017
06:41:35.053 --> 06:41:39.629
attribute is created anyway and it is initialized to the default values. So there

5018
06:41:39.629 --> 06:41:41.550
are two ways to define attributes.

5019
06:41:41.550 --> 06:41:45.899
We can define an attribute in any method in a class, and then calling the method

5020
06:41:45.899 --> 06:41:50.465
will add the attribute to the object. Alternatively, we can define them all together

5021
06:41:50.465 --> 06:41:54.651
in the constructor. If possible, try to avoid defining attributes outside the

5022
06:41:54.651 --> 06:41:59.054
constructor. Your class definition can be hundreds of lines of code long, and the

5023
06:41:59.054 --> 06:42:03.620
person reading it would have to comb through all of them to find all the attributes.

5024
06:42:03.620 --> 06:42:07.826
Moreover, defining all attributes in the constructor ensures that all of them are

5025
06:42:07.826 --> 06:42:11.875
created when the object is created, so you don't have to worry about trying to

5026
06:42:11.875 --> 06:42:16.081
access an attribute that doesn't yet exist. All this results in a more organized,

5027
06:42:16.081 --> 06:42:20.182
readable, and maintainable code. While we are on the subject of best practices,

5028
06:42:20.182 --> 06:42:24.025
let's quickly go over a few more conventions that will make your code more

5029
06:42:24.025 --> 06:42:28.386
reader-friendly. To name your classes, use KMLK, which means that if your class name

5030
06:42:28.386 --> 06:42:29.580
contains several words,

5031
06:42:29.580 --> 06:42:34.175
They should be written without delimiters, and each word should start with a capital

5032
06:42:34.175 --> 06:42:38.715
letter. For methods and attributes, it's the opposite. Words should be separated by

5033
06:42:38.715 --> 06:42:43.200
underscores and start with a lowercase letter. Here's a secret. The name self is a

5034
06:42:43.200 --> 06:42:47.686
convention. You could actually use any name for the first variable of a method. It

5035
06:42:47.686 --> 06:42:52.281
will always be treated as the object reference regardless. For example, if you are a

5036
06:42:52.281 --> 06:42:56.711
Java programmer, you might be tempted to use this, or if you are me, you might be

5037
06:42:56.711 --> 06:42:57.860
tempted to use kitty.

5038
06:42:57.860 --> 06:43:02.790
Don't do it, and always use self. Finally, classes, like functions, allow for dog

5039
06:43:02.790 --> 06:43:07.720
string, which are displayed when help is called on the object. Remember the first

