double underscore method is executed when we call print or str on an object, and the

5250
06:59:29.738 --> 06:59:32.190
wrapper method is executed when we call

5251
06:59:32.190 --> 06:59:37.235
or when we print it in the console without calling print explicitly. The difference

5252
06:59:37.235 --> 06:59:42.280
is that str is supposed to give an informal representation suitable for an end user

5253
06:59:42.280 --> 06:59:47.325
and wrapper is mainly used by the best practice is to use wrapper to print a string

5254
06:59:47.325 --> 06:59:52.309
that can be used to reproduce the object. For example, with NumPyArray, this shows

5255
06:59:52.309 --> 06:59:55.470
the exact method that was used to create the object.

5256
06:59:55.470 --> 07:00:00.651
If you only choose to implement one of them, choose wrapper, because it is also used

5257
07:00:00.651 --> 07:00:05.833
as a fallback for print when str is not defined. Let's start by implementing the str

5258
07:00:05.833 --> 07:00:10.644
method. It shouldn't accept any arguments besides self, and it should return a

5259
07:00:10.644 --> 07:00:15.455
string. Here, the string representation of a customer will consist of the word

5260
07:00:15.455 --> 07:00:20.575
customer, then on the next line, name, colon, followed by the customer's name, then

5261
07:00:20.575 --> 07:00:21.500
balance, colon,

5262
07:00:21.500 --> 07:00:26.687
Just a quick reminder, the triple quotes are used in Python to define multi-line

5263
07:00:26.687 --> 07:00:32.068
strings, and the format method is used on strings to substitute values inside curly

5264
07:00:32.068 --> 07:00:37.385
brackets with variables. If we create a customer object now and call print on that

5265
07:00:37.385 --> 07:00:42.831
object, we will see a user-friendly. Similarly, we can implement the wrapper method,

5266
07:00:42.831 --> 07:00:45.230
which also accepts one argument self.

5267
07:00:45.230 --> 07:00:50.160
and returns a string. Following best practice, we make sure that wrapper returns the

5268
07:00:50.160 --> 07:00:54.385
string that can be used to reproduce the object, in this case, the exact

5269
07:00:54.385 --> 07:00:59.256
initialization. Then, if we try to print the object in the console, we will see the

5270
07:00:59.256 --> 07:01:03.893
output of wrapper. Moreover, in this class, we didn't define the str method, so

5271
07:01:03.893 --> 07:01:08.646
wrapper will be used as a fallback for the actual print method. Notice the single

5272
07:01:08.646 --> 07:01:11.170
quotes around the name in the return state.

5273
07:01:11.170 --> 07:01:15.884
Without the quotes, the name of the customer will be substituted into the string as

5274
07:01:15.884 --> 07:01:20.314
is, but the point of wrapper is to give the exact call needed to reproduce the

5275
07:01:20.314 --> 07:01:24.971
object, where the name should be. Notice also that we can use single quotes inside

5276
07:01:24.971 --> 07:01:29.287
double quotes and vice versa. Now, head over to the exercises and try it out

5277
07:01:29.287 --> 07:01:32.240
yourself. Welcome back! Let's talk about exceptions.

5278
07:01:32.240 --> 07:01:36.990
We'll start with a brief recap and then learn how to define cut. Some statements in

5279
07:01:36.990 --> 07:01:41.683
Python will cause an error when you try to execute, for example, dividing by zero,

5280
07:01:41.683 --> 07:01:46.319
combining objects of incompatible types, and many others. These errors are called

5281
07:01:46.319 --> 07:01:50.840
exceptions. Many exceptions have special names like zero division error or type

5282
07:01:50.840 --> 07:01:55.647
error that you see here. If exceptions are not handled correctly, they will stop the

5283
07:01:55.647 --> 07:01:57.650
execution of your program entirely.

5284
07:01:57.650 --> 07:02:02.445
This often makes sense. For example, if I'm trying to use a variable that I never

5285
07:02:02.445 --> 07:02:07.239
defined, something must have gone very wrong with my script. But other times it's

5286
07:02:07.239 --> 07:02:12.034
undesired. For example, a division by zero error might be caused by missing data,

5287
07:02:12.034 --> 07:02:16.888
which isn't always a good reason to terminate the program. Instead, you might want

5288
07:02:16.888 --> 07:02:21.683
to execute special code to handle this case. To catch an exception and handle it,

5289
07:02:21.683 --> 07:02:26.300
use the TRY EXCEPT FINALLY, the code that you're worried about in a try block.

5290
07:02:26.300 --> 07:02:30.888
Then add an Accept block, followed by the name of the particular exception that you

5291
07:02:30.888 --> 07:02:35.311
want to handle, and the code that should be executed when the exception is done.

5292
07:02:35.311 --> 07:02:39.899
Then, if this particular exception does happen, the program will not terminate, but

5293
07:02:39.899 --> 07:02:44.432
execute the code in the Accept block. You can also have multiple. Finally, you can

5294
07:02:44.432 --> 07:02:49.020
use the optional Finally block that will contain the code that runs no matter what.

5295
07:02:49.020 --> 07:02:53.387
This block is best used for cleaning up, like, for example, closing open files.

5296
07:02:53.387 --> 07:02:53.940
Sometimes.

5297
07:02:53.940 --> 07:02:58.522
You want to raise exceptions yourself. For example, when some conditions are

5298
07:02:58.522 --> 07:03:03.525
unsatisfied, you can use the RAISE keyword, optionally followed by a specific error

5299
07:03:03.525 --> 07:03:08.589
method, in parentheses. The user of the code can then decide to handle the error you

5300
07:03:08.589 --> 07:03:13.291
try-except. In Python, exceptions are actually classes inherited from built-in

5301
07:03:13.291 --> 07:03:17.933
classes, baseException, or Exception. Here's a glimpse into the huge built-in

5302
07:03:17.933 --> 07:03:19.500
exception class hierarchy.

5303
07:03:19.500 --> 07:03:24.275
For example, there is a general class of arithmetic errors, of which zero division

5304
07:03:24.275 --> 07:03:29.167
error is a subclass. You can define your own exceptions in Python. Custom exceptions

5305
07:03:29.167 --> 07:03:33.826
are useful because they can be specific to your application and can provide more

5306
07:03:33.826 --> 07:03:38.660
granular handling of errors. To define a custom exception, just define a class that

5307
07:03:38.660 --> 07:03:42.620
inherits from the built-in exception class or one of its subclasses.

5308
07:03:42.620 --> 07:03:47.305
The class itself can be empty. Inheritance alone is enough to ensure that Python

5309
07:03:47.305 --> 07:03:51.990
will treat this class as an exception. For example, let's define a balance error

5310
07:03:51.990 --> 07:03:56.792
class that inherits from exception. Then, in our favorite customer class, we raise

5311
07:03:56.792 --> 07:04:01.711
an exception if a negative balance value is passed to the constructor. If we attempt

5312
07:04:01.711 --> 07:04:06.630
to create a customer with a negative account balance, the balance error exception is

5313
07:04:06.630 --> 07:04:07.040
raised.

5314
07:04:07.040 --> 07:04:11.674
In the first chapter, we dealt with this situation by merely printing a message and

5315
07:04:11.674 --> 07:04:16.141
then creating an object with zero balance. Handling it with exceptions is better

5316
07:04:16.141 --> 07:04:20.440
because in this case the constructor terminate and the customer object is not

5317
07:04:20.440 --> 07:04:25.074
created at all instead of being implicitly created with account balance set to zero

5318
07:04:25.074 --> 07:04:29.708
despite the error. This sends a clear signal to the user of the customer class that

5319
07:04:29.708 --> 07:04:30.880
something went wrong.

5320
07:04:30.880 --> 07:04:35.725
The user on their site can decide to handle this exception using try-accept block if

5321
07:04:35.725 --> 07:04:40.338
they want, but we, the author of the code, do not make this decision for us. For

5322
07:04:40.338 --> 07:04:45.125
example, here, the balance error is caught in the accept block, and if that occurs,

5323
07:04:45.125 --> 07:04:49.624
a customer is instead created with zero back. In the exercises, you'll explore

5324
07:04:49.624 --> 07:04:54.468
creating and handling whole hierarchies of custom exceptions. In this final chapter,

5325
07:04:54.468 --> 07:04:59.140
we'll talk about some good practice of class design. We'll cover two main topics.

5326
07:04:59.140 --> 07:05:03.668
Efficient use of inheritance and managing the levels of access to the data contained

5327
07:05:03.668 --> 07:05:08.196
in your class. Polymorphism means using a unified interface to operate on objects of

5328
07:05:08.196 --> 07:05:12.400
different classes. We've already dealt with it in Chapter 2. We defined a bank

5329
07:05:12.400 --> 07:05:16.874
account class and two classes inherited from a checking account class and a savings

5330
07:05:16.874 --> 07:05:21.025
class. All of them had a withdraw method, but the checking account method was

5331
07:05:21.025 --> 07:05:25.229
executing different code. Let's say we defined a function to withdraw the same

5332
07:05:25.229 --> 07:05:28.140
amount of money from a whole list of accounts at once.

5333
07:05:28.140 --> 07:05:32.569
The function doesn't know or care whether the objects passed to it are checking

5334
07:05:32.569 --> 07:05:37.223
accounts, savings accounts, or a mix. All that matters is that they have a withdraw

5335
07:05:37.223 --> 07:05:41.932
method that accepts one argument. That is enough to function work. It does not check

5336
07:05:41.932 --> 07:05:46.305
which withdraw it should call, the original or the modified. When the withdraw

5337
07:05:46.305 --> 07:05:50.510
method is actually called, Python will dynamically pull the correct method.

5338
07:05:50.510 --> 07:05:54.776
modified withdrawal for whenever a checking account has been processed and based

5339
07:05:54.776 --> 07:05:59.255
withdrawal for whenever a savings or generic bank account is processed. So you, as a

5340
07:05:59.255 --> 07:06:03.468
person writing this batch processing, don't need to worry about what exactly is

5341
07:06:03.468 --> 07:06:07.734
being passed to it, only what kind of interface. To really make use of this, you

5342
07:06:07.734 --> 07:06:11.040
have to design your classes with inheritance and polymorphism.

5343
07:06:11.040 --> 07:06:15.414
This uniformity of interface, there is a fundamental object-oriented design

5344
07:06:15.414 --> 07:06:20.196
principle of when and how to use inheritance properly, called Liskov substitution,

5345
07:06:20.196 --> 07:06:24.454
named after the computer scientist Barbara Liskov. A base class should be

5346
07:06:24.454 --> 07:06:29.178
interchangeable with any of its subclasses without altering any properties of the

5347
07:06:29.178 --> 07:06:32.910
surrounding program. Using the example of our account hierarchy,

5348
07:06:32.910 --> 07:06:37.353
That means that wherever in your application you can use a bank account object in,

5349
07:06:37.353 --> 07:06:41.308
substituting a checking account instead should not affect anything in the

5350
07:06:41.308 --> 07:06:45.804
surrounding. For example, the batchWithdraw function worked regardless of what kind

5351
07:06:45.804 --> 07:06:50.355
of account was used. This should be true both syntactically and semantically. On the

5352
07:06:50.355 --> 07:06:54.689
one hand, the method in the subclass should have a signature with parameters and

5353
07:06:54.689 --> 07:06:59.240
return values compatible with the method in the parent class. On the other hand, the

5354
07:06:59.240 --> 07:07:01.570
state of objects also must stay consistent.

5355
07:07:01.570 --> 07:07:06.673
The subclass method should not rely on stronger input conditions, should not provide

5356
07:07:06.673 --> 07:07:11.472
weaker output conditions, it should not throw additional exceptions, and so on.

5357
07:07:11.472 --> 07:07:16.392
Let's illustrate some possible violations of LSP on our account. For example, the

5358
07:07:16.392 --> 07:07:21.313
parentsWithdraw method could require one parameter, but the subclass method could

5359
07:07:21.313 --> 07:07:26.355
require two. Then we couldn't use the subclassWithdraw in place of the parents. But

5360
07:07:26.355 --> 07:07:30.000
if the subclass method had the default value for the second,

5361
07:07:30.000 --> 07:07:34.297
If the subclass method only accepts certain amounts, unlike the base one, then

5362
07:07:34.297 --> 07:07:38.925
sometimes the subclass method could not be used in place of the base class, if those

5363
07:07:38.925 --> 07:07:43.278
unsuitable amounts are used. If the base withdrawal had a check for whether the

5364
07:07:43.278 --> 07:07:47.741
resulting balance is positive and only performed the withdrawal in that case, but

5365
07:07:47.741 --> 07:07:52.258
the subclass did not do that, we wouldn't be able to use the subclass in the place

5366
07:07:52.258 --> 07:07:53.250
of the base class.

5367
07:07:53.250 --> 07:07:57.883
Because it's possible that the ambient program depends on the fact that the balance

5368
07:07:57.883 --> 07:08:02.238
is always positive after withdrawal. There are other ways to violate LSP, like

5369
07:08:02.238 --> 07:08:06.815
changing attributes that weren't changed in the base class, or throwing additional

5370
07:08:06.815 --> 07:08:11.058
exceptions that the base class didn't throw because those new exceptions are

5371
07:08:11.058 --> 07:08:13.570
guaranteed to be unhandled. The ultimate rule

5372
07:08:13.570 --> 07:08:18.156
is that if your class hierarchy violates the risk of substitution printing, then you

5373
07:08:18.156 --> 07:08:22.687
should not be using inheritance because it is likely to cause the code to behave in

5374
07:08:22.687 --> 07:08:26.999
unpredictable ways somewhere down the road. In the exercise, you'll explore the

5375
07:08:26.999 --> 07:08:31.258
circle ellipse, a famous software design problem that shows how our notions of

5376
07:08:31.258 --> 07:08:35.843
inheritance are. In the next two lessons, we'll talk about managing data access. All

5377
07:08:35.843 --> 07:08:40.429
class data in Python is technically public. Any attribute or method of any class can

5378
07:08:40.429 --> 07:08:41.630
be accessed by anyone.

5379
07:08:41.630 --> 07:08:46.438
If you're coming from a background in another programming language like Java, this

5380
07:08:46.438 --> 07:08:51.246
might seem unusual or an oversight, but it is by design. The fundamental principle

5381
07:08:51.246 --> 07:08:55.878
behind much of Python design is we are all adults. It is a philosophy that goes

5382
07:08:55.878 --> 07:09:00.627
beyond just code and describes how the Python community interacts with you should

5383
07:09:00.627 --> 07:09:05.201
have trust in your fellow developer. That said, there are a few ways to manage

5384
07:09:05.201 --> 07:09:06.080
access to data.

5385
07:09:06.080 --> 07:09:10.064
We can use some universal naming conventions to signal that the data is not for

5386
07:09:10.064 --> 07:09:14.198
external consumption. Then there are special kinds of attributes called properties

5387
07:09:14.198 --> 07:09:18.081
that allow you to control how which attribute is modified. Finally, there are

5388
07:09:18.081 --> 07:09:22.165
special methods that you can override to change how attributes are used entirely.

5389
07:09:22.165 --> 07:09:26.350
We'll cover the first two options in this chapter and you are unlikely to ever need

5390
07:09:26.350 --> 07:09:28.670
anything. Let's start with naming conventions.

5391
07:09:28.670 --> 07:09:33.024
The first and most important convention is using a single leading underscore to

5392
07:09:33.024 --> 07:09:37.379
indicate an attribute or method that isn't part of the public interface and can

5393
07:09:37.379 --> 07:09:41.899
change without notice. This convention is widely accepted among Python developers,

5394
07:09:41.899 --> 07:09:46.473
so you should follow it both as a class developer and as a class developer. Nothing

5395
07:09:46.473 --> 07:09:50.828
is technically preventing you from using these attributes, but a single leading

5396
07:09:50.828 --> 07:09:54.190
underscore is a developer's way of saying that you shouldn't.

5397
07:09:54.190 --> 07:09:59.377
The class developer trusts that you are an adult and will be able to use the class

5398
07:09:59.377 --> 07:10:04.628
responsibly. This convention is used for internal implementation details and helper

5399
07:10:04.628 --> 07:10:09.815
functions. For example, a pandas dataframe has an underscore isMixedType attribute

5400
07:10:09.815 --> 07:10:15.065
that indicates whether the dataframe contains data of mixed types, and the datetime

5401
07:10:15.065 --> 07:10:16.900
module contains an underscore

5402
07:10:16.900 --> 07:10:21.498
ymd2ord function that converts a date into a number containing how many days have

5403
07:10:21.498 --> 07:10:26.039
passed since January 1st of year 1. Another naming convention is using a leading

5404
07:10:26.039 --> 07:10:30.808
double underscore. Attributes and methods whose names start with a double underscore

5405
07:10:30.808 --> 07:10:35.519
are the closest thing Python has to private fields and methods of other programming

5406
07:10:35.519 --> 07:10:40.174
languages. In this case, it means that this data is not inherited, at least not in

5407
07:10:40.174 --> 07:10:43.410
the way I used to, because Python implements name angles.

5408
07:10:43.410 --> 07:10:47.827
Any name starting with a double underscore will be automatically prepended by the

5409
07:10:47.827 --> 07:10:52.299
name of the class when interpreted by Python. And that new name will be the actual

5410
07:10:52.299 --> 07:10:56.553
internal name of the attribute or method. The main use of these pseudo-private

5411
07:10:56.553 --> 07:11:00.806
attributes is to prevent name clashes in child classes. You can't control what

5412
07:11:00.806 --> 07:11:05.224
attributes or methods someone will introduce when inheriting from your class, and

5413
07:11:05.224 --> 07:11:09.750
it's possible that someone will unknowingly introduce a name that already exists in

5414
07:11:09.750 --> 07:11:10.350
your class.

5415
07:11:10.350 --> 07:11:14.654
thus overriding the parent method or attribute. You can use double leading

5416
07:11:14.654 --> 07:11:18.958
underscores to protect important attributes and methods that should not be

5417
07:11:18.958 --> 07:11:23.669
overridden. Finally, be careful. Leading AND trailing double underscores are only

5418
07:11:23.669 --> 07:11:28.497
used for built-in Python methods, like init, so your name should only start but not

5419
07:11:28.497 --> 07:11:33.383
end with double underscores. Head over to the exercises to review these conventions.

5420
07:11:33.383 --> 07:11:38.152
In the next video, you'll learn how to add a little more control to how attributes

5421
07:11:38.152 --> 07:11:39.490
are used. Welcome back!

5422
07:11:39.490 --> 07:11:43.736
In this last video, you'll learn about properties, which are a special kind of

5423
07:11:43.736 --> 07:11:48.199
attributes that allow customized access. In the beginning of chapter 1, you worked

5424
07:11:48.199 --> 07:11:52.662
with an employee class, where you defined methods like setSalary that were used to

5425
07:11:52.662 --> 07:11:56.962
set the values of attributes. Later, you learned about using the constructor to

5426
07:11:56.962 --> 07:12:01.480
initialize. You also learned that you can access and change the attributes directly

5427
07:12:01.480 --> 07:12:02.460
by assignment. But

5428
07:12:02.460 --> 07:12:07.355
This means that with a simple equality, we can assign anything to salary, a million,

5429
07:12:07.355 --> 07:12:12.016
a negative number, or even the word hello. Salary is an important attribute, and

5430
07:12:12.016 --> 07:12:16.794
that should not be allowed. So how do we control attribute access, validate it, or

5431
07:12:16.794 --> 07:12:21.572
even make the attribute read-only? We could modify the set-salary method, but that

5432
07:12:21.572 --> 07:12:26.467
wouldn't help because we could still use the dot syntax and assignment via equality.

5433
07:12:26.467 --> 07:12:31.129
There is a precedent for such attribute management with classes that you already

5434
07:12:31.129 --> 07:12:31.420
know.

5435
07:12:31.420 --> 07:12:35.511
For example, if you have a pandas dataframe with two columns, you can set the

5436
07:12:35.511 --> 07:12:39.814
columns attribute to a list of two strings, new names for the columns. But if you

5437
07:12:39.814 --> 07:12:43.959
try to set the attribute to a list of different lines, you'll get an error. Or

5438
07:12:43.959 --> 07:12:47.040
consider the shape attribute, it cannot be changed at all.

5439
07:12:47.040 --> 07:12:51.945
We can implement similar behavior using the property decorator. Start by defining an

5440
07:12:51.945 --> 07:12:56.733
internal attribute that will store the data. As we learned in a previous video, it

5441
07:12:56.733 --> 07:13:01.522
is recommended to store the name with one leading and here we define an underscore

5442
07:13:01.522 --> 07:13:05.901
salary. Next, we define a method whose name is the exact name we'd like the

5443
07:13:05.901 --> 07:13:10.748
restricted attribute to have and put a decorator property. In our case, that method

5444
07:13:10.748 --> 07:13:14.660
is called salary without underscore because that's how we'd like to

5445
07:13:14.660 --> 07:13:19.583
If we were writing a DataFrame class, this could be columns or shape. The method

5446
07:13:19.583 --> 07:13:24.690
just returns the actual internal attribute that is stored in the data. To customize

5447
07:13:24.690 --> 07:13:28.751
how the attribute is set, we implement a method with the decorator

5448
07:13:28.751 --> 07:13:33.735
attributeName.setter, salary.setter in our case. The method itself is again named

5449
07:13:33.735 --> 07:13:38.596
exactly the same as the property, salary, and it will be called when a value is

5450
07:13:38.596 --> 07:13:40.750
assigned to the property attribute.

5451
07:13:40.750 --> 07:13:45.142
It has a self-argument and an argument that represents the value to be assigned.

5452
07:13:45.142 --> 07:13:49.699
Here, we raise an exception if the value is negative, otherwise change the internal

5453
07:13:49.699 --> 07:13:54.256
attribute. So, there are two methods, called salary, the name of the property, that

5454
07:13:54.256 --> 07:13:58.813
have different decorators. The method with the property decorator returns the date,

5455
07:13:58.813 --> 07:14:03.095
and the method with salary.setter decorator implements validation and sets the

5456
07:14:03.095 --> 07:14:07.542
attribute. How does this work in practice? We can use this property just as if it

5457
07:14:07.542 --> 07:14:08.860
was a regular attribute.

5458
07:14:08.860 --> 07:14:13.613
Remember, the only real attribute we have is the internal underscore salary. Use the

5459
07:14:13.613 --> 07:14:18.365
dot syntax and equalities to assign a value to the salary property. Then, the setter

5460
07:14:18.365 --> 07:14:23.061
method will be called. If we try to assign a negative value to salary, an exception

5461
07:14:23.061 --> 07:14:27.644
will be. Properties are useful because the user of your class will not have to do

5462
07:14:27.644 --> 07:14:32.114
anything special. They won't even be able to distinguish between properties and

5463
07:14:32.114 --> 07:14:36.640
regular attributes. You, on the other hand, now have some control over the axis.

5464
07:14:36.640 --> 07:14:41.303
There are a few other things you can do with properties. If you do not define a

5465
07:14:41.303 --> 07:14:46.143
setter method, the property will be read-only, like dataframe shape. A method with

5466
07:14:46.143 --> 07:14:51.100
an attribute name .getter decorator will be called when the property's value is just

5467
07:14:51.100 --> 07:14:55.763
retrieved, and the method with the attribute name .deleter when an attribute is

5468
07:14:55.763 --> 07:14:59.836
deleted. Alright, time for you to create some properties by yourself.

5469
07:14:59.836 --> 07:15:04.322
Congratulations on completing this course on Introduction to Object-Oriented

5470
07:15:04.322 --> 07:15:05.620
Programming in Python.

5471
07:15:05.620 --> 07:15:09.781
You've done a great job. You learned how to think about your code in terms of

5472
07:15:09.781 --> 07:15:14.266
classes and objects, how to create attributes and methods. You explored inheritance

5473
07:15:14.266 --> 07:15:18.698
and polymorphism, two ideas that allow you to leverage and customize existing code

5474
07:15:18.698 --> 07:15:22.967
in powerful ways. You also learned the distinction between class-level data and

5475
07:15:22.967 --> 07:15:27.344
instance-level data. What does it mean for two objects to be equal? Turns out, it

5476
07:15:27.344 --> 07:15:30.370
can mean anything you want, as you learned in Chapter 3.

5477
07:15:30.370 --> 07:15:35.189
You defined custom equality functions, readable string representations, even built

5478
07:15:35.189 --> 07:15:40.066
your own exceptions. Finally, you learned what makes a relationship between classes

5479
07:15:40.066 --> 07:15:44.944
suitable for inheritance, how Python handles private versus public data, and how to

5480
07:15:44.944 --> 07:15:49.880
use properties to manage data access. So where can you go from here? You could start

5481
07:15:49.880 --> 07:15:53.700
by expanding your knowledge of functionality available in Python.

5482
07:15:53.700 --> 07:15:58.408
For example, learn about mixing classes and multiple inheritance, a highly debated

5483
07:15:58.408 --> 07:16:03.058
feature of Python that isn't present in many other object-oriented languages. You

5484
07:16:03.058 --> 07:16:07.766
could learn how to overwrite more built-in operators, like arithmetic operators or

5485
07:16:07.766 --> 07:16:12.245
the length operator. How to customize attribute access even more using special

5486
07:16:12.245 --> 07:16:17.010
methods. How to create your own iterator classes that you could use to index loops.

5487
07:16:17.010 --> 07:16:21.702
You could learn about abstract-based classes, used to create interfaces, or how to

5488
07:16:21.702 --> 07:16:26.223
leverage data classes, a new type of class that is especially suitable for data

5489
07:16:26.223 --> 07:16:30.000
storage. Also consider learning more about object-oriented design.

5490
07:16:30.236 --> 07:16:34.982
which is based on SOLID principles. SOLID is an acronym, and you've already learned

5491
07:16:34.982 --> 07:16:39.384
about the L in SOLID, the LISC of Substitution principles, but the other four

5492
07:16:39.384 --> 07:16:44.073
letters are just as important. Finally, I encourage you to learn more about design

5493
07:16:44.073 --> 07:16:48.590
pattern, reusable solutions addressing most common problems in software design.

5494
07:16:48.590 --> 07:16:52.230
Thank you so much for taking this course, and good luck in your future coding

5495
07:16:52.230 --> 07:16:56.107
adventures. Welcome to Introduction to Airflow. I'm Mike Metzger, a data engineer,

5496
07:16:56.107 --> 07:17:00.031
and I'll be your instructor while we learn the components of Apache Airflow and why

5497
07:17:00.031 --> 07:17:03.955
you'd want to use it. Let's get started. Before getting into workflows and Airflow,

5498
07:17:03.955 --> 07:17:07.406
let's discuss a bit about data engineering. While there are many specific

5499
07:17:07.406 --> 07:17:11.330
definitions based on context, the general meaning behind data engineering is taking

5500
07:17:11.330 --> 07:17:15.112
any action involving data and making it a reliable, repeatable, and maintainable

5501
07:17:15.112 --> 07:17:15.490
process.

5502
07:17:15.490 --> 07:17:19.840
Before we can really discuss Airflow, we need to talk about workflows. A workflow is

5503
07:17:19.840 --> 07:17:24.035
a set of steps to accomplish a given data engineering task. These can include any

5504
07:17:24.035 --> 07:17:28.386
given task, such as downloading a file, copying data, filtering information, writing

5505
07:17:28.386 --> 07:17:32.529
to a database, and so forth. A workflow is of varying levels of complexity. Some

5506
07:17:32.529 --> 07:17:36.620
workflows may only have two or three steps, while others consist of hundreds of

5507
07:17:36.620 --> 07:17:40.971
components. The complexity of a workflow is completely dependent on the needs of the

5508
07:17:40.971 --> 07:17:44.130
user. We show an example of a possible workflow to the right.

5509
07:17:44.130 --> 07:17:47.979
It's important to note that we're defining a workflow here in a general data

5510
07:17:47.979 --> 07:17:51.929
engineering sense. This is an informal definition to introduce the concept. As

5511
07:17:51.929 --> 07:17:56.133
you'll see later, workflow can have specific meaning within specific tools. Airflow

5512
07:17:56.133 --> 07:18:00.286
is a platform to program workflows in general, including the creation, scheduling,

5513
07:18:00.286 --> 07:18:04.439
and monitoring of said workflows. Airflow can use various tools and languages, but

5514
07:18:04.439 --> 07:18:08.693
the actual workflow code is written in Python. Airflow implements workflows as DAGs,

5515
07:18:08.693 --> 07:18:12.795
or Directed Acyclic Graphs. We'll discuss exactly what this means throughout this

5516
07:18:12.795 --> 07:18:13.150
course.

5517
07:18:13.150 --> 07:18:17.099
But for now, think of it as a set of tasks and the dependencies between them.

5518
07:18:17.099 --> 07:18:21.151
Airflow can be accessed and controlled via code, via the command line, or via a

5519
07:18:21.151 --> 07:18:25.459
built-in web interface. We'll look at all these options later on. Airflow is not the

5520
07:18:25.459 --> 07:18:29.665
only tool available for running data engineering workflows. Some other options are

5521
07:18:29.665 --> 07:18:33.922
Spotify's Luigi, Microsoft's SSIS, or even just Bash scripting. We'll use some Bash

5522
07:18:33.922 --> 07:18:37.974
scripting within our Airflow usage, but otherwise, we'll focus on Airflow. ADAG

5523
07:18:37.974 --> 07:18:39.820
stands for a Directed Acyclic Graph.

5524
07:18:39.820 --> 07:18:44.500
In Airflow, this represents the set of tasks that make up your workflow. It consists

5525
07:18:44.500 --> 07:18:48.845
of the tasks and the dependencies between tasks. DAGs are created with various

5526
07:18:48.845 --> 07:18:53.079
details about the DAG, including the name, start date, owner, email alerting

5527
07:18:53.079 --> 07:18:57.647
options, etc. We will go into further detail in the next lesson, but a very simple

5528
07:18:57.647 --> 07:19:01.769
DAG is defined using the following code. A new DAG is created with the DAG

5529
07:19:01.769 --> 07:19:06.282
underscore ID of ETL underscore pipeline and a default underscore args dictionary

5530
07:19:06.282 --> 07:19:08.900
containing a start underscore date for the DAG.

5531
07:19:08.900 --> 07:19:12.986
Note that within any Python code, this is referred to as the variable identifier,

5532
07:19:12.986 --> 07:19:16.921
ETL underscore DAG, but within the Airflow shell command, you must use the DAG

5533
07:19:16.921 --> 07:19:21.008
underscore ID. To get started, let's look at how to run a component of an Airflow

5534
07:19:21.008 --> 07:19:25.094
workflow. These components are called tasks and simply represent a portion of the

5535
07:19:25.094 --> 07:19:29.282
workflow. We'll go into further detail in later chapters. There are several ways to

5536
07:19:29.282 --> 07:19:33.469
run a task, but one of the simplest is using the Airflow run shell command. Airflow

5537
07:19:33.469 --> 07:19:35.840
run takes three arguments, a DAG underscore ID,

5538
07:19:35.840 --> 07:19:40.649
A task underscore ID, and a start underscore date. All of these arguments have

5539
07:19:40.649 --> 07:19:45.519
specific meaning and will make more sense later in the course. For our example,

5540
07:19:45.519 --> 07:19:50.635
we'll use a DAG underscore ID of example dash ETL, a task named download dash file,

5541
07:19:50.635 --> 07:19:55.691
and a start date of January 10th, 2020. This task would simply download a specific

5542
07:19:55.691 --> 07:20:00.684
file, perhaps a daily update from a remote source. Our command as such is airflow

5543
07:20:00.684 --> 07:20:03.150
run example dash ETL download dash file.

5544
07:20:03.150 --> 07:20:07.643
2020-01-10. This will then run the specified task within Airflow. We've looked at

5545
07:20:07.643 --> 07:20:12.247
Airflow and some of the basic aspects of why you'd use it. We've also looked at how

5546
07:20:12.247 --> 07:20:16.740
to run a task within Airflow from the command line. Let's practice. Welcome back.

5547
07:20:16.740 --> 07:20:21.177
You've successfully interacted with some basic Airflow workflows via the command

5548
07:20:21.177 --> 07:20:25.670
line. Let's now take a look at the primary building block of those workflows, the

5549
07:20:25.670 --> 07:20:30.330
DAG. Our first question is, what is a DAG? Beyond any specific mathematical meaning,

5550
07:20:30.330 --> 07:20:32.160
a DAG, or directed acyclic graph,

5551
07:20:32.160 --> 07:20:36.441
has the following attributes. It is directed, meaning there is an inherent flow

5552
07:20:36.441 --> 07:20:40.613
representing the dependencies or order between execution of components. These

5553
07:20:40.613 --> 07:20:45.165
dependencies, even implicit ones, provide a context to the tools on how to order the

5554
07:20:45.165 --> 07:20:49.663
running of components. A DAG is also acyclic. It does not loop or repeat. This does

5555
07:20:49.663 --> 07:20:54.106
not imply that the entire DAG cannot be rerun, only that the individual components

5556
07:20:54.106 --> 07:20:58.550
are executed once per run. In this case, a graph represents the components and the

5557
07:20:58.550 --> 07:21:00.880
relationships or dependencies between them.

5558
07:21:00.880 --> 07:21:05.029
The term DAG is found often in data engineering, not just in Airflow, but also

5559
07:21:05.029 --> 07:21:09.337
Apache Spark, Luigi, and others. As we're working with Airflow, let's look at its

5560
07:21:09.337 --> 07:21:13.699
implementation of the DAG concept. Within Airflow, DAGs are written in Python, but

5561
07:21:13.699 --> 07:21:17.901
can use components written in other languages or technologies. This means we'll

5562
07:21:17.901 --> 07:21:22.262
define the DAG using Python, but we could include Bash scripts, other executables,

5563
07:21:22.262 --> 07:21:26.624
Spark jobs, and so on. Airflow DAGs are made up of components to be executed, such

5564
07:21:26.624 --> 07:21:30.400
as operators, sensors, etc. Airflow typically refers to these as tasks.

5565
07:21:30.400 --> 07:21:34.450
We'll cover these in much greater depth later on, but for now think of a task as a

5566
07:21:34.450 --> 07:21:38.450
thing within the Airflow that needs to be done. Airflow DAGs contain dependencies

5567
07:21:38.450 --> 07:21:42.401
that are defined, either explicitly or implicitly. These dependencies define the

5568
07:21:42.401 --> 07:21:46.549
execution order so Airflow knows which components should be run at what point within

5569
07:21:46.549 --> 07:21:50.698
the workflow. For example, you would likely want to copy a file to a server prior to

5570
07:21:50.698 --> 07:21:54.500
trying to import it to a database. Let's look at defining a simple DAG within

5571
07:21:54.500 --> 07:21:58.599
Airflow. When defining the DAG in Python, you must first import the DAG object from

5572
07:21:58.599 --> 07:21:59.340
airflow.models.

5573
07:21:59.340 --> 07:22:03.361
Once imported, we create a default arguments dictionary consisting of attributes

5574
07:22:03.361 --> 07:22:07.433
that will be applied to the components of our DAG. These attributes are optional,

5575
07:22:07.433 --> 07:22:11.656
but provide a lot of power to define the runtime behavior of Airflow. Here we define

5576
07:22:11.656 --> 07:22:15.828
the owner name as jdo, an email address for alerting, and specify the start date of

5577
07:22:15.828 --> 07:22:19.950
the DAG. The start date represents the earliest date time that a DAG could be run.

5578
07:22:19.950 --> 07:22:24.179
Finally, we define our DAG object with the first argument using a name for the DAG,

5579
07:22:24.179 --> 07:22:28.409
ETL underscore workflow, and assign the default arguments dictionary to the default

5580
07:22:28.409 --> 07:22:32.587
underscore args argument. There are many other optional configurations we will use

5581
07:22:32.587 --> 07:22:36.766
later on. Note that the entire DAG is assigned to a variable called ETL underscore

5582
07:22:36.766 --> 07:22:40.689
DAG. This will be used later when defining the components of the DAG, but the

5583
07:22:40.689 --> 07:22:44.766
variable name ETL underscore DAG does not actually appear in Airflow interfaces.

5584
07:22:44.766 --> 07:22:48.842
Note DAG is case-sensitive in Python code. When working with DAGs and Airflow in

5585
07:22:48.842 --> 07:22:49.250
general,

5586
07:22:49.250 --> 07:22:53.281
You will often want to use the Airflow command line tool. The Airflow command line

5587
07:22:53.281 --> 07:22:57.263
program contains many subcommands that handle various aspects of running Airflow.

5588
07:22:57.263 --> 07:23:01.098
You've used a couple of these already in previous exercises. Use the Airflow-H

5589
07:23:01.098 --> 07:23:05.178
command for help and descriptions of the subcommands. Many of these subcommands are

5590
07:23:05.178 --> 07:23:09.062
related to DAGs. You can use the Airflow list underscore DAGs option to see all

5591
07:23:09.062 --> 07:23:13.094
recognized DAGs in an installation. When in doubt, try a few different commands to

5592
07:23:13.094 --> 07:23:15.060
find the information you're looking for.

5593
07:23:15.060 --> 07:23:19.021
You may be wondering when to use the Airflow command line tool versus writing

5594
07:23:19.021 --> 07:23:22.982
Python. In general, the Airflow command line program is used to start Airflow

5595
07:23:22.982 --> 07:23:27.046
processes, i.e. web server or scheduler, manually run DAGs or tasks, and review

5596
07:23:27.046 --> 07:23:31.315
logging information. Python code itself is usually used in the creation and editing

5597
07:23:31.315 --> 07:23:35.379
of a DAG, not to mention the actual data processing code itself. Now that we've

5598
07:23:35.379 --> 07:23:39.700
covered the basics of a DAG in Airflow and how to create one, let's practice working

5599
07:23:39.700 --> 07:23:40.780
with it. Hello again.

5600
07:23:40.780 --> 07:23:44.719
Now that we've looked at some basic DAG functionality and interacting with them,

5601
07:23:44.719 --> 07:23:48.805
it's time to introduce the Airflow Web UI. The Airflow Web UI is made up of several

5602
07:23:48.805 --> 07:23:52.891
primary page groups useful in developing and administering workflows on the Airflow

5603
07:23:52.891 --> 07:23:57.027
platform. Note that for this course, we'll only be focusing on a few pages, but it's

5604
07:23:57.027 --> 07:24:01.113
helpful to click around the various options and get familiar with what's available.

5605
07:24:01.113 --> 07:24:04.855
The DAGs view of the Airflow UI is the page we'll spend most of our time. It

5606
07:24:04.855 --> 07:24:08.400
provides a quick status of the number of DAGs slash workflows available.

5607
07:24:08.400 --> 07:24:12.971
It shows us the schedule for the DAG in date or cron format. We can see the owner of

5608
07:24:12.971 --> 07:24:17.543
the DAG, which of the most recent tasks have run, when the last run started, and the

5609
07:24:17.543 --> 07:24:21.951
last three DAG runs. The links area on the right gives us quick access to many of

5610
07:24:21.951 --> 07:24:26.522
the DAG-specific views. Don't worry about those for now. Instead, we'll click on the

5611
07:24:26.522 --> 07:24:30.984
example underscore DAG link, which takes us to our DAG detail page. The DAG detail

5612
07:24:30.984 --> 07:24:35.556
view gives us specific access to information about the DAG itself, including several

5613
07:24:35.556 --> 07:24:37.950
views of information, graph, tree, and code.

5614
07:24:37.950 --> 07:24:42.719
Illustrating the tasks and dependencies in the code. We also get access to the task

5615
07:24:42.719 --> 07:24:47.372
duration, task tries, timings, a Gantt chart view, and specific details about the

5616
07:24:47.372 --> 07:24:52.084
DAG. We have the ability to trigger the DAG to start, refresh our view, and delete

5617
07:24:52.084 --> 07:24:56.450
the DAG if we desire. The detail view defaults to the tree view, showing the

5618
07:24:56.450 --> 07:25:00.931
specific named tasks, which operators are in use, and any dependencies between

5619
07:25:00.931 --> 07:25:05.700
tasks. The circles in front of the words represent the state of the task slash DAG.

5620
07:25:05.700 --> 07:25:09.575
In the case of our specific DAG, we see that we have one task called generate

5621
07:25:09.575 --> 07:25:13.501
underscore random underscore number. The DAG graph view arranges the tasks and

5622
07:25:13.501 --> 07:25:17.728
dependencies in a chart format. This provides another view into the flow of the DAG.

5623
07:25:17.728 --> 07:25:21.805
You can see the operators in use and the state of the tasks at any point in time.

5624
07:25:21.805 --> 07:25:25.931
The tree and graph view provide different information depending on what you'd like

5625
07:25:25.931 --> 07:25:30.159
to know. Try moving between them when examining a DAG to obtain further details. For

5626
07:25:30.159 --> 07:25:34.034
this view, we again see that we have a task called generate underscore random

5627
07:25:34.034 --> 07:25:34.940
underscore number.

5628
07:25:34.940 --> 07:25:38.645
We can also see that it is of the type bash operator in the middle left of the

5629
07:25:38.645 --> 07:25:42.634
image. The DAG code view does exactly as it sounds. It provides a copy of the Python

5630
07:25:42.634 --> 07:25:46.339
code that makes up the DAG. The code view provides easy access to exactly what

5631
07:25:46.339 --> 07:25:50.281
defines the DAG without clicking in various portions of the UI. As you use Airflow,

5632
07:25:50.281 --> 07:25:54.081
you'll determine which tools work best for you. It is worth noting that the code

5633
07:25:54.081 --> 07:25:57.975
view is read-only. Any DAG code changes must be done via the actual DAG script. In

5634
07:25:57.975 --> 07:26:01.728
this view, we can finally see the code making up the generate underscore random

5635
07:26:01.728 --> 07:26:02.820
underscore number task.

5636
07:26:02.820 --> 07:26:06.767
And then it runs the bash command, echo, $random. The logs page under the browse

5637
07:26:06.767 --> 07:26:10.714
menu option provides troubleshooting and audit ability while using Airflow. This

5638
07:26:10.714 --> 07:26:14.710
includes items such as starting the Airflow web server, viewing the graph or tree

5639
07:26:14.710 --> 07:26:18.608
nodes, creating users, starting DAGs, etc. When using Airflow, look at the logs

5640
07:26:18.608 --> 07:26:22.703
often to become more familiar with the types of information included, and also what

5641
07:26:22.703 --> 07:26:26.847
happens behind the scenes of an Airflow install. Note that you'll often refer to the

5642
07:26:26.847 --> 07:26:30.597
event type present on the logs view when searching, such as graph, tree, CLI

5643
07:26:30.597 --> 07:26:31.090
scheduler.

5644
07:26:31.090 --> 07:26:34.804
In most circumstances, you can choose between using the Airflow web UI or the

5645
07:26:34.804 --> 07:26:38.517
command line tool based on your preference. The web UI is often easier to use

5646
07:26:38.517 --> 07:26:42.472
overall. The command line tool may be simpler to access depending on settings, via

5647
07:26:42.472 --> 07:26:46.523
SSH, etc. Now that we've covered some of the most important pages of the Airflow UI,

5648
07:26:46.523 --> 07:26:50.575
let's practice examining some workflows. Welcome back. We've discussed the basics of

5649
07:26:50.575 --> 07:26:54.530
tasks in Airflow without covering exactly what a task consists of. The most common

5650
07:26:54.530 --> 07:26:58.195
task in Airflow is the operator. Let's take a look at operators now. Airflow

5651
07:26:58.195 --> 07:27:00.510
operators represent a single task in a workflow.

5652
07:27:00.510 --> 07:27:04.399
This can be any type of task, from running a command, sending an email, running a

5653
07:27:04.399 --> 07:27:08.287
Python script, and so on. Typically, Airflow operators run independently, meaning

5654
07:27:08.287 --> 07:27:12.176
that all resources needed to complete the task are contained within the operator.

5655
07:27:12.176 --> 07:27:16.208
Generally, Airflow operators do not share information between each other. This is to

5656
07:27:16.208 --> 07:27:20.193
simplify workflows and allow Airflow to run the tasks in the most efficient manner.

5657
07:27:20.193 --> 07:27:24.081
It is possible to share information between operators, but the details of how are

5658
07:27:24.081 --> 07:27:27.922
beyond this course. Airflow contains many various operators to perform different

5659
07:27:27.922 --> 07:27:28.210
tasks.

5660
07:27:28.210 --> 07:27:31.517
For example, the domini operator can be used to represent a task for

5661
07:27:31.517 --> 07:27:35.602
troubleshooting, or a task that has not yet been implemented. We are focusing on the

5662
07:27:35.602 --> 07:27:39.493
bash operator for this lesson, but we'll look at the Python operator and several

5663
07:27:39.493 --> 07:27:43.384
others later on. The bash operator executes a given bash command or script. This

5664
07:27:43.384 --> 07:27:47.323
command can be pretty much anything bash is capable of that would make sense in a

5665
07:27:47.323 --> 07:27:51.262
given workflow. The bash operator requires three arguments, the task ID, which is

5666
07:27:51.262 --> 07:27:55.250
the name that shows up in the UI, the bash command, the raw command or script, and

5667
07:27:55.250 --> 07:27:56.320
the DAG it belongs to.

5668
07:27:56.320 --> 07:28:00.409
The bash operator runs the command in a temporary directory that gets automatically

5669
07:28:00.409 --> 07:28:04.498
cleaned up afterwards. It is possible to specify environment variables for the bash

5670
07:28:04.498 --> 07:28:08.390
command to try to replicate running the task as you would on a local system. If

5671
07:28:08.390 --> 07:28:12.528
you're unfamiliar with environment variables, these are runtime settings interpreted

5672
07:28:12.528 --> 07:28:16.469
by the shell. It provides flexibility while running the scripts in a generalized

5673
07:28:16.469 --> 07:28:20.558
way. The first example runs a bash command to echo example, exclamation mark to the

5674
07:28:20.558 --> 07:28:24.450
standard alt. The second example uses a predefined bash script for its command,

5675
07:28:24.450 --> 07:28:25.140
runcleanup.sh.

5676
07:28:25.140 --> 07:28:29.207
Before using the bash operator, it must be imported from airflow.operators.bash

5677
07:28:29.207 --> 07:28:33.326
underscore operator. The first example creates a bash operator that takes a task

5678
07:28:33.326 --> 07:28:37.445
underscore ID, runs the bash command echo1, and assigns the operator to the DAG.

5679
07:28:37.445 --> 07:28:41.410
Note that we've previously defined the DAG in an earlier exercise. The second

5680
07:28:41.410 --> 07:28:45.735
example is a bash operator to run a quick data cleaning operation using cat and awk.

5681
07:28:45.735 --> 07:28:49.854
Don't worry if you don't understand exactly what this is doing. This is a common

5682
07:28:49.854 --> 07:28:51.810
scenario when we're running workflows.

5683
07:28:51.810 --> 07:28:55.639
You may not know exactly what a command does, but you can still run it in a reliable

5684
07:28:55.639 --> 07:28:59.150
way. There are some general gotchas when using operators. The biggest is that

5685
07:28:59.150 --> 07:29:02.934
individual operators are not guaranteed to run in the same location or environment.

5686
07:29:02.934 --> 07:29:06.626
This means that just because one operator ran in a given directory with a certain

5687
07:29:06.626 --> 07:29:10.410
setup, it does not necessarily mean that the next operator will have access to that

5688
07:29:10.410 --> 07:29:14.148
same information. If this is required, you must explicitly set it up. You may need

5689
07:29:14.148 --> 07:29:17.978
to set up environment variables, especially for the bash operator. For example, it's

5690
07:29:17.978 --> 07:29:21.260
common in bash to use the tilde character to represent a home directory.

5691
07:29:21.260 --> 07:29:24.900
This is not defined by default in Airflow. Another example of an environment

5692
07:29:24.900 --> 07:29:28.444
variable could be AWS credentials, database connectivity details, or other

5693
07:29:28.444 --> 07:29:32.228
information specific to running a script. Finally, it can also be tricky to run

5694
07:29:32.228 --> 07:29:36.155
tasks with any form of elevated privilege. This means that any access to resources

5695
07:29:36.155 --> 07:29:39.987
must be set up for the specific user running the tasks. If you're uncertain what

5696
07:29:39.987 --> 07:29:43.962
elevated privileges are, think of running a command as root or the administrator on

5697
07:29:43.962 --> 07:29:47.937
a system. We've discussed the basics of Airflow Operator. Let's practice using them

5698
07:29:47.937 --> 07:29:49.470
in some workflows. Welcome back.

5699
07:29:49.470 --> 07:29:53.679
Now that you've worked with operators a bit, let's take a look at the concept of

5700
07:29:53.679 --> 07:29:58.098
tasks within Airflow. Within Airflow, tasks are instantiated operators. It basically

5701
07:29:58.098 --> 07:30:02.255
is a shortcut to refer to a given operator within a workflow. Tasks are usually

5702
07:30:02.255 --> 07:30:06.569
assigned to a variable within Python code. Using a previous example, we assign the

5703
07:30:06.569 --> 07:30:10.936
bash operator to the variable example underscore task. Note that within the Airflow

5704
07:30:10.936 --> 07:30:14.724
tools, this task is referred by its task ID, not the variable name. Task

5705
07:30:14.724 --> 07:30:18.880
dependencies in Airflow define an order of task completion. While not required,

5706
07:30:18.880 --> 07:30:23.379
Task dependencies are usually present. If task dependencies are not defined, task

5707
07:30:23.379 --> 07:30:27.434
execution is handled by Airflow itself, with no guarantees of order. Task

5708
07:30:27.434 --> 07:30:32.099
dependencies are referred to as upstream or downstream tasks. An upstream task means

5709
07:30:32.099 --> 07:30:36.321
that it must complete prior to any downstream tasks. Since Airflow 1.8, task

5710
07:30:36.321 --> 07:30:40.986
dependencies are defined using the bit shift operators. The upstream operator is two

5711
07:30:40.986 --> 07:30:44.930
greater than symbols. The downstream operator is two less than symbols.

5712
07:30:44.930 --> 07:30:49.159
It's easy to get confused on when to use an upstream or downstream operator. The

5713
07:30:49.159 --> 07:30:53.334
simplest analogy is that upstream means before and downstream means after. This

5714
07:30:53.334 --> 07:30:57.669
means that any upstream tasks would need to complete prior to any downstream ones.

5715
07:30:57.669 --> 07:31:01.897
Let's look at a simple example involving two bash operators. We define our first

5716
07:31:01.897 --> 07:31:06.284
task and assign it to the variable task1. We then create our second task and assign

5717
07:31:06.284 --> 07:31:10.671
it to the variable task2. Once each operator is defined and assigned to a variable,

5718
07:31:10.671 --> 07:31:13.790
we can define the task order using the bit shift operators.

5719
07:31:13.790 --> 07:31:18.269
In this case, we want to run task 1 before task 2. The most readable method for this

5720
07:31:18.269 --> 07:31:22.321
is using the upstream operator, two greater than symbols, as task 1 upstream

5721
07:31:22.321 --> 07:31:26.320
operator, task 2. Note that you could also define this in reverse using the

5722
07:31:26.320 --> 07:31:30.746
downstream operator to accomplish the same thing. In this case, it'd be task 2, two

5723
07:31:30.746 --> 07:31:35.172
less than symbols, task 1. Let's take a look at what the Airflow UI shows for tasks

5724
07:31:35.172 --> 07:31:39.437
and their dependencies. In this case, we're looking at the graph view within the

5725
07:31:39.437 --> 07:31:42.850
Airflow web interface. Note that in the task area are two tasks,

5726
07:31:42.850 --> 07:31:46.993
first underscore task and second underscore task are both present, but there is no

5727
07:31:46.993 --> 07:31:51.085
order to the task execution. This is the DAG prior to setting the task dependency

5728
07:31:51.085 --> 07:31:55.329
using the bit shift operator. Now, let's look again at the view with a defined order

5729
07:31:55.329 --> 07:31:59.522
via the bit shift operators. The view is similar, but we can see the order of tasks

5730
07:31:59.522 --> 07:32:03.715
indicated by the directed arrow between first underscore task and second underscore

5731
07:32:03.715 --> 07:32:07.706
task. Dependencies can be as complex as required to define the workflow to your

5732
07:32:07.706 --> 07:32:11.748
needs. We can chain a dependency, in this case setting task one upstream of task

5733
07:32:11.748 --> 07:32:11.950
two,

5734
07:32:11.950 --> 07:32:16.233
upstream of task 3, upstream of task 4. The Airflow Graph view shows a dependency

5735
07:32:16.233 --> 07:32:20.357
view indicating this order. You can also mix upstream and downstream bit shift

5736
07:32:20.357 --> 07:32:24.376
operators in the same workflow. If we define task 1 upstream of task 2, then

5737
07:32:24.376 --> 07:32:28.659
downstream of task 3, we get a configuration different than what we might expect.

5738
07:32:28.659 --> 07:32:33.100
This creates a DAG where first underscore task and third underscore task must finish

5739
07:32:33.100 --> 07:32:37.278
prior to second underscore task. This means we could define the same dependency

5740
07:32:37.278 --> 07:32:39.710
graph on two lines in a possibly clearer form.

5741
07:32:39.710 --> 07:32:43.963
Task one upstream of task two, task three upstream of task two. Note that because we

5742
07:32:43.963 --> 07:32:48.215
don't require it, either task one or task three could run first depending on Airflow

5743
07:32:48.215 --> 07:32:52.266
scheduling. There are many intricacies to defining tasks and using the bit shift

5744
07:32:52.266 --> 07:32:56.468
operators. The best way to solidify these is practicing the exercise. Welcome back.

5745
07:32:56.468 --> 07:33:00.568
Now that we've used the bash operator and worked with tasks, let's take a look at

5746
07:33:00.568 --> 07:33:04.770
some more common operators available within Airflow. The Python operator is similar

5747
07:33:04.770 --> 07:33:08.770
to the bash operator, except that it runs a Python function or callable method.

5748
07:33:08.770 --> 07:33:12.667
Much like the bash operator, it requires a task ID, a DAG entry, and most

5749
07:33:12.667 --> 07:33:17.044
importantly, a python underscore callable argument set to the name of the function

5750
07:33:17.044 --> 07:33:21.475
in question. You can also pass arguments or keyword style arguments into the python

5751
07:33:21.475 --> 07:33:25.959
callable as needed. Our first example shows a simple print me function that writes a

5752
07:33:25.959 --> 07:33:29.963
message to the task logs. We must first import the python operator from the

5753
07:33:29.963 --> 07:33:34.180
airflow.operators.python underscore operator library. Afterwards, we create our

5754
07:33:34.180 --> 07:33:37.650
function print me, which will write a quick log message when run.

5755
07:33:37.650 --> 07:33:41.801
Once defined, we create the Python operator instance called Python underscore task

5756
07:33:41.801 --> 07:33:46.003
and add the necessary arguments. The Python operator supports adding arguments to a

5757
07:33:46.003 --> 07:33:50.205
given task. This allows you to pass arguments that can then be passed to the Python

5758
07:33:50.205 --> 07:33:54.356
function assigned to Python callable. The Python operator supports both positional

5759
07:33:54.356 --> 07:33:58.558
and keyword style arguments as options to the task. For this course, we'll focus on

5760
07:33:58.558 --> 07:34:02.810
using keyword arguments only for the sake of clarity. To implement keyword arguments

5761
07:34:02.810 --> 07:34:06.860
with the Python operator, we define an argument on the task called op underscore

5762
07:34:06.860 --> 07:34:11.127
KWARGS. This is a dictionary consisting of the name arguments for the intended

5763
07:34:11.127 --> 07:34:15.612
Python function. Let's create a new function called sleep, which takes a length of

5764
07:34:15.612 --> 07:34:20.207
time argument. It uses this argument to call the time.sleep method. Once defined, we

5765
07:34:20.207 --> 07:34:24.638
create a new task called sleep underscore task, with the task ID, DAG, and Python

5766
07:34:24.638 --> 07:34:29.014
callable arguments added as before. This time we'll add our op underscore KWARGS

5767
07:34:29.014 --> 07:34:32.460
dictionary with the length of time variable and the value of 5.

5768
07:34:32.460 --> 07:34:36.624
Note that the dictionary key must match the name of the function argument. If the

5769
07:34:36.624 --> 07:34:40.891
dictionary contains an unexpected key, it will be passed to the Python function and

5770
07:34:40.891 --> 07:34:45.210
typically cause an unexpected keyword argument error. There are many other operators

5771
07:34:45.210 --> 07:34:48.912
available within the Airflow ecosystem. The primary operators are in the

5772
07:34:48.912 --> 07:34:53.230
airflow.operators or airflow.contrib.operators libraries. Another useful operator is

5773
07:34:53.230 --> 07:34:57.549
the email operator, which as expected sends an email from within an Airflow task. It

5774
07:34:57.549 --> 07:35:01.353
can contain the typical components of an email, including HTML content and

5775
07:35:01.353 --> 07:35:01.970
attachments.

5776
07:35:01.970 --> 07:35:06.381
Note that the Airflow system must be configured with the email server details to

5777
07:35:06.381 --> 07:35:11.012
successfully send a message. A quick example for sending an email would be sending a

5778
07:35:11.012 --> 07:35:15.643
generated sales report upon completion of a workflow. We must first import the email

5779
07:35:15.643 --> 07:35:20.274
operator object from airflow.operators.email underscore operator. We can then create

5780
07:35:20.274 --> 07:35:24.795
our email operator instance with the task ID, the to, subject, and content fields,

5781
07:35:24.795 --> 07:35:29.040
and a list of any files to attach. Note that in this case, we assume the file

5782
07:35:29.040 --> 07:35:33.226
latest underscore sales dot xlsx was previously generated. Later in the course,

5783
07:35:33.226 --> 07:35:37.518
we'll see how to verify that first. Finally, we add it to our DAG as usual. We've

5784
07:35:37.518 --> 07:35:41.597
looked at a couple of new Airflow operators. Let's practice using them in the

5785
07:35:41.597 --> 07:35:46.048
exercises. Welcome back. We've spent most of this chapter implementing Airflow tasks

5786
07:35:46.048 --> 07:35:50.287
within our workflows. Now let's take a look at what's required to schedule those

5787
07:35:50.287 --> 07:35:54.684
workflows and have them run automatically. When referring to scheduling an Airflow,

5788
07:35:54.684 --> 07:35:56.380
we must first discuss a DAG run.

5789
07:35:56.380 --> 07:36:00.973
This is an instance of a workflow at a given point in time. For example, it could be

5790
07:36:00.973 --> 07:36:05.456
the currently running instance or it could be one run last Tuesday at 3 p.m. A DAG

5791
07:36:05.456 --> 07:36:09.776
can be run manually or via the schedule interval parameter past when the DAG is

5792
07:36:09.776 --> 07:36:14.204
defined. Each DAG run maintains a state for itself and the tasks within. The DAGs

5793
07:36:14.204 --> 07:36:18.633
can have a running, failed, or success state. The individual tasks can have these

5794
07:36:18.633 --> 07:36:19.466
states or others as well.

5795
07:36:19.466 --> 07:36:23.713
i.e. queued or skipped. Within the Airflow UI, you can view all DAG runs under the

5796
07:36:23.713 --> 07:36:28.011
Browse DAG runs menu option. This provides the assorted details about any DAGs that

5797
07:36:28.011 --> 07:36:32.258
have run within the current Airflow instance. As mentioned, you can view the state

5798
07:36:32.258 --> 07:36:36.453
of a DAG run within this page, illustrating whether the DAG run was successful or

5799
07:36:36.453 --> 07:36:36.660
not.

5800
07:36:36.660 --> 07:36:40.609
When scheduling a DAG, there are many attributes to consider depending on your

5801
07:36:40.609 --> 07:36:44.609
scheduling needs. The StartDate value specifies the first time the DAG could be

5802
07:36:44.609 --> 07:36:48.609
scheduled. This is typically defined with a Python datetime object. The EndDate

5803
07:36:48.609 --> 07:36:52.811
represents the last possible time to schedule the DAG. MaxTries represents how many

5804
07:36:52.811 --> 07:36:57.064
times to retry before fully failing the DAG run. The ScheduleInterval represents how

5805
07:36:57.064 --> 07:37:01.266
often to schedule the DAG for execution. There are many nuances to this which we'll

5806
07:37:01.266 --> 07:37:05.317
cover in a moment. The ScheduleInterval represents how often to schedule the DAG

5807
07:37:05.317 --> 07:37:05.570
runs.

5808
07:37:05.570 --> 07:37:10.254
The scheduling occurs between the start date and the potential end date. Note that

5809
07:37:10.254 --> 07:37:14.766
this is not when the DAGs will absolutely run, but rather a minimum and maximum

5810
07:37:14.766 --> 07:37:19.335
value of when they could be scheduled. The schedule interval can be defined by a

5811
07:37:19.335 --> 07:37:24.133
couple methods, with a cron-style syntax or via built-in presets. The cron syntax is

5812
07:37:24.133 --> 07:37:28.874
the same as the format for scheduling jobs using the Unix cron tool. It consists of

5813
07:37:28.874 --> 07:37:33.615
five fields separated by a space, starting with the minute value, 0 through 59, the

5814
07:37:33.615 --> 07:37:34.700
hour, 0 through 23,

5815
07:37:34.700 --> 07:37:39.517
The day of the month, 1 through 31, the month, 1 through 12, and the day of the

5816
07:37:39.517 --> 07:37:44.396
week, 0 through 6. An asterisk in any of the fields represents running for every

5817
07:37:44.396 --> 07:37:49.274
interval. For example, an asterisk in the minute field means run every minute. A

5818
07:37:49.274 --> 07:37:54.396
list of values can be given on a field via comma-separated values. The cron entry 0,

5819
07:37:54.396 --> 07:37:59.275
12, asterisk, asterisk, asterisk, asterisk, means run daily at noon, 12 o'clock.

5820
07:37:59.275 --> 07:38:01.470
Asterisk, asterisk, 25, 2, asterisk.

5821
07:38:01.470 --> 07:38:06.645
represents running once per minute, but only on February 25th. Zero comma 15 comma

5822
07:38:06.645 --> 07:38:11.567
30 comma 45 asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk means to run every 15

5823
07:38:11.567 --> 07:38:16.742
minutes. Airflow has several presets or shortcut syntax options representing often

5824
07:38:16.742 --> 07:38:22.043
used time intervals. The at hourly preset means to run once an hour at the beginning

5825
07:38:22.043 --> 07:38:27.345
of the hour. It's equivalent to zero asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk in

5826
07:38:27.345 --> 07:38:30.500
cron. The at daily, at weekly, at monthly, and at.

5827
07:38:30.500 --> 07:38:35.085
Yearly presets behave similarly. Airflow also has two special presets for schedule

5828
07:38:35.085 --> 07:38:39.782
intervals. None means don't ever schedule the DAG and is used for manually triggered

5829
07:38:39.782 --> 07:38:44.032
workflows. At once means to only schedule a DAG once. Scheduling DAGs has an

5830
07:38:44.032 --> 07:38:48.617
important nuance to consider. When scheduling DAG runs, Airflow will use the start

5831
07:38:48.617 --> 07:38:53.091
date as the earliest possible value, but not actually schedule anything until at

5832
07:38:53.091 --> 07:38:57.732
least one schedule interval has passed beyond the start date. Given a start date of

5833
07:38:57.732 --> 07:38:58.850
February 25th, 2020,

5834
07:38:58.850 --> 07:39:02.949
And an at daily schedule interval, Airflow would then use the date of February 26th,

5835
07:39:02.949 --> 07:39:06.902
2020 for the first run of the DAG. This can be tricky to consider when adding new

5836
07:39:06.902 --> 07:39:10.855
DAG schedules, especially if they have longer schedule intervals. We've covered a

5837
07:39:10.855 --> 07:39:14.808
lot of ground in this lesson and chapter. Let's practice scheduling our workflows

5838
07:39:14.808 --> 07:39:18.810
and we'll see each other back in chapter. Welcome back. Now that we've gotten some

5839
07:39:18.810 --> 07:39:22.812
practice using operators and tasks within Airflow, let's look at a special kind of

5840
07:39:22.812 --> 07:39:26.765
operator called a sensor. A sensor is a special kind of operator that waits for a

5841
07:39:26.765 --> 07:39:28.180
certain condition to be true.

5842
07:39:28.180 --> 07:39:32.671
Some examples of conditions include waiting for the creation of a file, uploading a

5843
07:39:32.671 --> 07:39:37.055
database record, or a specific response from a web request. With sensors, you can

5844
07:39:37.055 --> 07:39:41.600
define how often to check for the conditions to be true. Since sensors are a type of

5845
07:39:41.600 --> 07:39:46.091
operator, they are assigned to tasks just like normal operators. This means you can

5846
07:39:46.091 --> 07:39:50.529
apply the bit shift dependencies to them as well. All sensors are derived from the

5847
07:39:50.529 --> 07:39:54.641
airflow.sensors.base-sensor-operator class. There are some default arguments

5848
07:39:54.641 --> 07:39:56.860
available to all sensors, including mode,

5849
07:39:56.860 --> 07:40:00.893
poke underscore interval, and timeout. The mode tells the sensor how to check for

5850
07:40:00.893 --> 07:40:05.076
the condition and has two options, poke or reschedule. The default is poke and means

5851
07:40:05.076 --> 07:40:09.010
to continue checking until complete without giving up a worker slot. Reschedule

5852
07:40:09.010 --> 07:40:12.944
means to give up the worker slot and wait for another slot to become available.

5853
07:40:12.944 --> 07:40:17.127
We'll discuss worker slots in the next lesson, but for now consider a worker slot to

5854
07:40:17.127 --> 07:40:21.060
be the capability to run a task. The poke interval is used in the poke mode and

5855
07:40:21.060 --> 07:40:23.600
tells Airflow how often to check for the condition.

5856
07:40:23.600 --> 07:40:27.514
This should be at least one minute to keep from overloading the Airflow scheduler.

5857
07:40:27.514 --> 07:40:31.380
The timeout field is how long to wait, in seconds, before marking the sensor task

5858
07:40:31.380 --> 07:40:35.247
has failed. To avoid issues, make sure your timeout is significantly shorter than

5859
07:40:35.247 --> 07:40:39.209
your schedule interval. Note that as sensors or operators, they also include normal

5860
07:40:39.209 --> 07:40:43.218
operator attributes, such as task underscore ID and DAG. A useful sensor is the file

5861
07:40:43.218 --> 07:40:47.228
sensor, found in the airflow.contrib.sensors library. The file sensor checks for the

5862
07:40:47.228 --> 07:40:51.189
existence of a file at a certain location in the file system. It can also check for

5863
07:40:51.189 --> 07:40:52.860
any files within a given directory.

5864
07:40:52.860 --> 07:40:57.050
A quick example is importing the file sensor object, then defining a task called

5865
07:40:57.050 --> 07:41:01.293
file underscore sensor underscore task. We set the task ID and the DAG entries as

5866
07:41:01.293 --> 07:41:05.641
usual. The file path argument is set to sales data dot CSV, looking for a file with

5867
07:41:05.641 --> 07:41:09.988
this file name to exist before continuing. We set the poke interval to 300 seconds,

5868
07:41:09.988 --> 07:41:14.336
or to repeat the check every five minutes until true. Finally, we use the bit shift

5869
07:41:14.336 --> 07:41:18.683
operators to define the sensor's dependencies within our DAG. In this case, we must

5870
07:41:18.683 --> 07:41:21.250
run the init underscore sales underscore cleanup,

5871
07:41:21.250 --> 07:41:25.304
Then wait for the file underscore sensor underscore task to finish. Then we run

5872
07:41:25.304 --> 07:41:29.204
generate underscore report. There are many types of sensors available within

5873
07:41:29.204 --> 07:41:33.361
Airflow. The external task sensor waits for a task in a separate DAG to complete.

5874
07:41:33.361 --> 07:41:37.313
This allows a loose connection to other workflow tasks without making any one

5875
07:41:37.313 --> 07:41:41.623
workflow too complex. The HTTP sensor will request a web URL and allow you to define

5876
07:41:41.623 --> 07:41:45.934
the content to check for. The SQL sensor runs a SQL query to check for content. Many

5877
07:41:45.934 --> 07:41:49.937
other sensors are available in the airflow.sensors and airflow.contrib.sensors

5878
07:41:49.937 --> 07:41:50.450
libraries.

5879
07:41:50.450 --> 07:41:54.159
You may be wondering when to use a sensor versus an operator. For the most part,

5880
07:41:54.159 --> 07:41:57.543
you'll want to use a normal operator unless you have any of the following

5881
07:41:57.543 --> 07:42:01.391
requirements. You're uncertain when a condition will be true. If you know something

5882
07:42:01.391 --> 07:42:05.239
will complete that day, but it might vary by an hour or so, you can use a sensor to

5883
07:42:05.239 --> 07:42:08.762
check until it is. If you want to continue to check for a condition, but not

5884
07:42:08.762 --> 07:42:12.379
necessarily fail the entire DAG immediately, this provides some flexibility in

5885
07:42:12.379 --> 07:42:16.087
defining your DAG. Finally, if you want to repeatedly run a check without adding

5886
07:42:16.087 --> 07:42:18.220
cycles to your DAG, sensors are a good choice.

5887
07:42:18.220 --> 07:42:21.956
We've looked at a lot about sensors, let's practice using them. Now that we've

5888
07:42:21.956 --> 07:42:25.931
implemented some Airflow sensors, let's discuss the execution model within Airflow.

5889
07:42:25.931 --> 07:42:29.954
In Airflow, an executor is the component that actually runs the tasks defined within

5890
07:42:29.954 --> 07:42:33.881
your workflows. Each executor has different capabilities and behaviors for running

5891
07:42:33.881 --> 07:42:37.665
the set of tasks. Some may run a single task at a time on a local system, while

5892
07:42:37.665 --> 07:42:41.688
others might split individual tasks among all the systems in a cluster. As mentioned

5893
07:42:41.688 --> 07:42:45.471
in the previous lesson, this is often referred to as the number of worker slots

5894
07:42:45.471 --> 07:42:45.950
available.

5895
07:42:45.950 --> 07:42:50.026
We'll discuss some of these in more detail soon, but a few examples of executors are

5896
07:42:50.026 --> 07:42:54.103
the sequential executor, the local executor, and the celery executor. This is not an

5897
07:42:54.103 --> 07:42:58.034
exhaustive list, and you can also create your own executor if required, though we

5898
07:42:58.034 --> 07:43:01.965
won't cover that in this course. The sequential executor is the default execution

5899
07:43:01.965 --> 07:43:06.041
engine for Airflow. It runs only a single task at a time. This means having multiple

5900
07:43:06.041 --> 07:43:10.069
workflows scheduled around the same time frame may cause things to take longer than

5901
07:43:10.069 --> 07:43:14.097
expected. The sequential executor is useful for debugging, as it's fairly simple to

5902
07:43:14.097 --> 07:43:15.310
follow the flow of tasks.

5903
07:43:15.310 --> 07:43:19.092
And it can also be used with some integrated development environments, though we

5904
07:43:19.092 --> 07:43:23.016
won't cover that here. The most important aspect of the sequential executor is that

5905
07:43:23.016 --> 07:43:26.987
while it's very functional for learning and testing, it's not really recommended for

5906
07:43:26.987 --> 07:43:30.864
production due to the limitations of task resources. The local executor is another

5907
07:43:30.864 --> 07:43:34.741
option for Airflow that runs entirely on a single system. It basically treats each

5908
07:43:34.741 --> 07:43:38.665
task as a process on the local system and is able to start as many concurrent tasks

5909
07:43:38.665 --> 07:43:42.542
as desired, requested, and permitted by the system resources, for example, the CPU

5910
07:43:42.542 --> 07:43:43.440
cores, memory, etc.

5911
07:43:43.440 --> 07:43:47.504
This concurrency is the parallelism of the system, and it is defined by the user in

5912
07:43:47.504 --> 07:43:51.567
one of two ways, either unlimited, or unlimited to a certain number of simultaneous

5913
07:43:51.567 --> 07:43:55.386
tasks. Defined intelligently, the local executor is a good choice for a single

5914
07:43:55.386 --> 07:43:59.499
production airflow system, and can utilize all the resources of a given host system.

5915
07:43:59.499 --> 07:44:03.563
The last executor we'll look at is the Celery executor. If you're not familiar with

5916
07:44:03.563 --> 07:44:07.675
Celery, it's a general queuing system written in Python that allows multiple systems

5917
07:44:07.675 --> 07:44:09.340
to communicate as a basic cluster.

5918
07:44:09.340 --> 07:44:13.533
Using a Celery Executor, multiple Airflow systems can be configured as workers for a

5919
07:44:13.533 --> 07:44:17.526
given set of workflows or tasks. You can add extra systems at any time to better

5920
07:44:17.526 --> 07:44:21.669
balance workflows. The power of the Celery Executor is significantly more difficult

5921
07:44:21.669 --> 07:44:25.462
to set up and configure. It requires a working Celery configuration prior to

5922
07:44:25.462 --> 07:44:29.505
configuring Airflow, not to mention some method to share DAGs between the system,

5923
07:44:29.505 --> 07:44:33.348
such as a Git server, network file system, etc. While it is more difficult to

5924
07:44:33.348 --> 07:44:37.491
configure, the Celery Executor is a powerful choice for anyone working with a large

5925
07:44:37.491 --> 07:44:38.190
number of DAGs

5926
07:44:38.190 --> 07:44:41.998
or expects their processing needs to grow. Sometimes when developing Airflow

5927
07:44:41.998 --> 07:44:46.106
workflows, you may want to know the executor being used. If you have access to the

5928
07:44:46.106 --> 07:44:50.165
command line, you can determine this by looking at the appropriate airflow.config

5929
07:44:50.165 --> 07:44:54.273
file, search for the executor colon line, and it will specify the executor in use.

5930
07:44:54.273 --> 07:44:58.181
Note that we haven't discussed the airflow.config file in depth as we assume a

5931
07:44:58.181 --> 07:45:02.240
configured Airflow instance in this course. The airflow.cfg file is where most of

5932
07:45:02.240 --> 07:45:06.348
the configuration and settings for the Airflow instance are defined, including the

5933
07:45:06.348 --> 07:45:07.200
type of executor.

5934
07:45:07.200 --> 07:45:11.166
You can also determine the executor by running Airflow list underscore DAGs from the

5935
07:45:11.166 --> 07:45:15.131
command line. Within the first few lines, you should see an entry for which executor

5936
07:45:15.131 --> 07:45:19.049
is in use. In this case, it's the sequential executor. We've just discussed some of

5937
07:45:19.049 --> 07:45:22.873
the various Airflow executors. Let's practice what we've learned in the exercises

5938
07:45:22.873 --> 07:45:26.697
ahead. Welcome back. Let's take a look at one of the biggest aspects of running a

5939
07:45:26.697 --> 07:45:30.332
production system with Airflow and data engineering in general, debugging and

5940
07:45:30.332 --> 07:45:34.156
troubleshooting. There are several common issues you may run across while working

5941
07:45:34.156 --> 07:45:34.770
with Airflow.

5942
07:45:34.770 --> 07:45:38.845
It helps to have an idea of what these might be and how best to handle them. The

5943
07:45:38.845 --> 07:45:42.971
first common issue is a DAG or DAGs that won't run on schedule. The next is a DAG

5944
07:45:42.971 --> 07:45:47.047
that simply won't load into the system. The last common scenario involves syntax

5945
07:45:47.047 --> 07:45:51.326
errors. Let's look at these more closely. The most common reason why a DAG won't run

5946
07:45:51.326 --> 07:45:55.401
on schedule is the scheduler is not running. Airflow contains several components

5947
07:45:55.401 --> 07:45:59.680
that accomplish various aspects of the system. The Airflow scheduler handles DAG run

5948
07:45:59.680 --> 07:46:02.940
and task scheduling. If it is not running, no new tasks can run.

5949
07:46:02.940 --> 07:46:06.819
You'll often see this error within the web UI if the scheduler component is not

5950
07:46:06.819 --> 07:46:10.944
running. You can easily fix this issue by running Airflow scheduler from the command

5951
07:46:10.944 --> 07:46:15.020
line. As we've covered before, another common issue with scheduling is the scenario

5952
07:46:15.020 --> 07:46:18.998
where at least one schedule interval period has not passed since either the start

5953
07:46:18.998 --> 07:46:23.024
date or the last DAG run. There isn't a specifics fix for this, but you might want

5954
07:46:23.024 --> 07:46:26.560
to modify the start date or schedule interval to meet your requirements.

5955
07:46:26.560 --> 07:46:30.882
The last scheduling issue you'll often see is related to what we've covered in the

5956
07:46:30.882 --> 07:46:35.152
previous lesson. The executor does not have enough free slots to run tasks. There

5957
07:46:35.152 --> 07:46:39.580
are basically three ways to alleviate this problem. By changing the executor type to

5958
07:46:39.580 --> 07:46:43.850
something capable of more tasks, the local executor or seller executor, by adding

5959
07:46:43.850 --> 07:46:48.067
systems or system resources, RAM, CPUs, or finally by changing the scheduling of

5960
07:46:48.067 --> 07:46:52.284
your DAGs. You'll often see an issue where a new DAG will not appear in your DAG

5961
07:46:52.284 --> 07:46:55.710
view of the web UI or in the Airflow list underscore DAGs output.

5962
07:46:55.710 --> 07:46:59.920
The first thing to check is that the Python file is in the expected DAGs folder or

5963
07:46:59.920 --> 07:47:03.873
directory. You can determine the current DAGs folder setting by examining the

5964
07:47:03.873 --> 07:47:07.827
airflow.cfg file. The line DAGs underscore folder will indicate where Airflow

5965
07:47:07.827 --> 07:47:12.139
expects to find your Python DAG files. Note that the folder path must be an absolute

5966
07:47:12.139 --> 07:47:16.349
path. Probably the most common reason a DAG workflow won't appear in your DAG list

5967
07:47:16.349 --> 07:47:20.559
is one or more syntax errors in your Python code. These are sometimes difficult to

5968
07:47:20.559 --> 07:47:24.769
find, especially in an editor not set up for Python or Airflow, such as a base vim

5969
07:47:24.769 --> 07:47:25.180
install.

5970
07:47:25.180 --> 07:47:29.118
I tend to prefer using Vim with some Python tools loaded, or VS Code, but it's

5971
07:47:29.118 --> 07:47:33.358
really up to your preference. There are two quick methods to check for these issues,

5972
07:47:33.358 --> 07:47:37.548
airflow list underscore DAGs, and running your DAG script with Python. The first is

5973
07:47:37.548 --> 07:47:41.788
to run airflow space list underscore DAGs. As we've seen before, Airflow will output

5974
07:47:41.788 --> 07:47:45.776
some debugging information in the list of DAGs it's processed. If there are any

5975
07:47:45.776 --> 07:47:49.663
errors, those will appear in the output, helping you to troubleshoot further.

5976
07:47:49.663 --> 07:47:53.702
Another method to verify Python syntax is to run the actual Python 3 interpreter

5977
07:47:53.702 --> 07:47:54.560
against the file.

5978
07:47:54.560 --> 07:47:58.783
You won't see any output normally, as there's nothing for the interpreter to do, but

5979
07:47:58.783 --> 07:48:02.956
it can check for any syntax errors in your code. If there are errors, you'll get an

5980
07:48:02.956 --> 07:48:06.978
appropriate error message. If there are no errors, you'll be returned to command

5981
07:48:06.978 --> 07:48:11.150
prompt. Let's practice handling some of these common issues in the exercises ahead.

5982
07:48:11.150 --> 07:48:14.971
Welcome back. Let's talk now about SLAs and reporting in Airflow. You may be

5983
07:48:14.971 --> 07:48:18.842
wondering, what is an SLA? SLA stands for Service Level Agreement. Within the

5984
07:48:18.842 --> 07:48:23.065
business world, this is often an uptime or availability guarantee. Airflow treats it

5985
07:48:23.065 --> 07:48:23.970
a bit differently.

5986
07:48:23.970 --> 07:48:28.677
It's considered the amount of time a task or a DAG should require to run. An SLA

5987
07:48:28.677 --> 07:48:33.560
miss is any situation where a task or DAG does not meet the expected timing for the

5988
07:48:33.560 --> 07:48:38.443
SLA. If an SLA is missed, an email alert is sent out per system configuration and a

5989
07:48:38.443 --> 07:48:43.208
note is made in the log. Any SLA miss can be viewed in the Browse SLA Misses menu

5990
07:48:43.208 --> 07:48:48.092
item of the web UI. To view any given SLA miss, you can access it in the web UI via

5991
07:48:48.092 --> 07:48:49.680
the Browse SLA Misses link.

5992
07:48:49.680 --> 07:48:53.858
It provides you general information about what task missed the SLA and when it

5993
07:48:53.858 --> 07:48:58.251
failed. It also indicates if an email has been sent and when the SLA failed. There

5994
07:48:58.251 --> 07:49:02.643
are several ways to define an SLA, but we'll only look at two for this course. The

5995
07:49:02.643 --> 07:49:07.143
first is via an SLA argument on the task itself. This takes a time delta object with

5996
07:49:07.143 --> 07:49:11.375
the amount of time to pass. The second way is using the default underscore args

5997
07:49:11.375 --> 07:49:13.250
dictionary and defining an SLA key.

5998
07:49:13.250 --> 07:49:17.527
The dictionary is then passed into the default underscore args argument of the DAG

5999
07:49:17.527 --> 07:49:21.803
and applies to any tasks internally. We haven't covered the time delta object yet,

6000
07:49:21.803 --> 07:49:26.132
so let's look at some of the details. It's found in the datetime library along with

6001
07:49:26.132 --> 07:49:30.460
the datetime object. Most easily accessed with an import statement of from datetime

6002
07:49:30.460 --> 07:49:34.737
import time delta. Takes arguments of days, seconds, minutes, hours, and weeks. It

6003
07:49:34.737 --> 07:49:38.753
also has milliseconds and microseconds available, but those wouldn't apply to

6004
07:49:38.753 --> 07:49:39.170
Airflow.

6005
07:49:39.170 --> 07:49:43.818
To create the object, you simply call time delta with the argument or arguments you

6006
07:49:43.818 --> 07:49:48.523
wish to reference. To create a 30 second time delta, call it with seconds equals 30,

6007
07:49:48.523 --> 07:49:53.227
or weeks equals 2, or you could combine it into a longer mix of any of the arguments

6008
07:49:53.227 --> 07:49:57.875
you wish, in this case, 4 days, 10 hours, 20 minutes, and 30 seconds. For reporting

6009
07:49:57.875 --> 07:50:02.524
purposes, you can use email alerting built into Airflow. There are a couple of ways

6010
07:50:02.524 --> 07:50:07.116
to do this. Airflow has built-in options for sending messages on success, failure,

6011
07:50:07.116 --> 07:50:08.180
or error, or retry.

6012
07:50:08.180 --> 07:50:12.538
These are handled via keys in the default args dictionary that gets passed on DAG

6013
07:50:12.538 --> 07:50:16.897
creation. The required component is the list of emails assigned to the email key.

6014
07:50:16.897 --> 07:50:21.201
Then there are boolean options for email underscore on underscore failure, email

6015
07:50:21.201 --> 07:50:25.398
underscore on underscore retry, and email underscore on underscore success. In

6016
07:50:25.398 --> 07:50:29.918
addition, we've already looked at the email operator earlier, but this is useful for

6017
07:50:29.918 --> 07:50:33.200
sending emails outside of one of the defined airflow options.

6018
07:50:33.200 --> 07:50:36.694
Note that sending an email does require a configuration within Airflow that is

6019
07:50:36.694 --> 07:50:40.411
outside the scope of this course. The Airflow documentation provides information on

6020
07:50:40.411 --> 07:50:43.860
how to set up the global email configuration. Let's finish up this chapter by

6021
07:50:43.860 --> 07:50:47.354
practicing what you do. Great work getting to the last chapter of this course.

6022
07:50:47.354 --> 07:50:51.117
You've learned a lot, but let's take a look at some of the more advanced features of

6023
07:50:51.117 --> 07:50:54.789
Airflow, starting with templates. You may be wondering what templates are and what

6024
07:50:54.789 --> 07:50:58.552
they do in the case of Airflow. Templates allow substitution of information during a

6025
07:50:58.552 --> 07:51:02.180
DAG run. In other words, every time a DAG with templated information is executed,

6026
07:51:02.180 --> 07:51:06.492
Information is interpreted and included with the DAG run. Templates provide added

6027
07:51:06.492 --> 07:51:10.857
flexibility when defining tasks. We'll see examples of this shortly. Templates are

6028
07:51:10.857 --> 07:51:15.222
created using the Jinja templating language. A full explanation of Jinja is out of

6029
07:51:15.222 --> 07:51:19.587
scope for this course, but we'll cover some basics in the coming slides. Before we

6030
07:51:19.587 --> 07:51:23.952
get specifically into a templated example, let's consider what we would do for the

6031
07:51:23.952 --> 07:51:28.264
following requirement. Your manager has asked you to simply echo the word reading

6032
07:51:28.264 --> 07:51:30.340
and a list of files to a log or output.

6033
07:51:30.340 --> 07:51:34.843
If we were to do this with what we currently know about Airflow, we would likely

6034
07:51:34.843 --> 07:51:39.515
create multiple tasks using the bash operator. Our first task would set up the task

6035
07:51:39.515 --> 07:51:44.130
with the intended bash command, in this case echo-reading-file1.txt as an argument

6036
07:51:44.130 --> 07:51:48.745
to the bash operator. If we had a second file, we would create a second task using

6037
07:51:48.745 --> 07:51:53.305
the bash command echo-reading-file2.txt. This type of code would continue for the

6038
07:51:53.305 --> 07:51:57.864
entire list of files. Consider what this would look like if we had 5, 10, or even

6039
07:51:57.864 --> 07:51:59.890
100 plus files we needed to process.

6040
07:51:59.890 --> 07:52:03.998
There would be a lot of repetitive code, not to mention what if you needed to change

6041
07:52:03.998 --> 07:52:07.763
the command being used. Let's take a look at how we would accomplish the same

6042
07:52:07.763 --> 07:52:11.430
behavior with templates. First, we need to create a variable containing our

6043
07:52:11.430 --> 07:52:15.538
template, which is really just a string with some specialized formatting. Our string

6044
07:52:15.538 --> 07:52:19.646
is the actual bash command, echo, and instead of the file name, we're using two open

6045
07:52:19.646 --> 07:52:23.753
curly braces, the term params.filename, and then two closing curly braces. The curly

6046
07:52:23.753 --> 07:52:27.861
braces, when used in this manner, represent information to be substituted. This will

6047
07:52:27.861 --> 07:52:29.230
make more sense in a moment.

6048
07:52:29.230 --> 07:52:33.497
If you've done any web development or worked with any reporting tool, you've likely

6049
07:52:33.497 --> 07:52:37.302
worked with something similar. Next, we create our Airflow task as we have

6050
07:52:37.302 --> 07:52:41.467
previously. We assign a task ID and a DAG argument as usual, but our bash command

6051
07:52:41.467 --> 07:52:45.477
looks a little different. We set the bash command to use the templated command

6052
07:52:45.477 --> 07:52:49.590
string we defined earlier. We also have an additional argument called params. In

6053
07:52:49.590 --> 07:52:53.806
this case, params is a dictionary containing a single key file name with the value

6054
07:52:53.806 --> 07:52:54.320
file1.txt.

6055
07:52:54.320 --> 07:52:58.889
Now if you look back at the templated command, you'll notice that the term in the

6056
07:52:58.889 --> 07:53:03.572
curly braces is params.filename. At runtime, Airflow will execute the bash operator

6057
07:53:03.572 --> 07:53:08.310
by reading the templated command and replacing params.filename with the value stored

6058
07:53:08.310 --> 07:53:12.823
in the params dictionary for the filename key. In other words, it would pass the

6059
07:53:12.823 --> 07:53:17.111
bash operator echo reading file1.txt, the actual log output would be reading

6060
07:53:17.111 --> 07:53:20.270
file1.txt, after the bash operator executed the command.

6061
07:53:20.270 --> 07:53:24.381
Now let's consider one way to use templates for our earlier task of outputting

6062
07:53:24.381 --> 07:53:28.702
reading file1.txt and reading file2.txt. First, we create our templated command as

6063
07:53:28.702 --> 07:53:33.129
before. Next, create the first task and pass the params dict with a filename key and

6064
07:53:33.129 --> 07:53:37.450
the value file1.txt. To pass another entry, we can create a second task and modify

6065
07:53:37.450 --> 07:53:41.719
the params dict accordingly. This time, the filename would contain file2.txt, and

6066
07:53:41.719 --> 07:53:45.776
Airflow would substitute that value instead. The resulting output would be as

6067
07:53:45.776 --> 07:53:49.360
expected. Note, you may be wondering what templates do for you here.

6068
07:53:49.360 --> 07:53:53.011
You'll see more in the coming exercises and lessons. We'll have more to discuss

6069
07:53:53.011 --> 07:53:56.801
about templates in the next lesson, but let's practice what we've learned and I'll

6070
07:53:56.801 --> 07:54:00.545
see you later. Welcome back. Hopefully you've cemented the basics of templates in

6071
07:54:00.545 --> 07:54:04.381
your mind. Now let's look at some of the more powerful options they provide. In our

6072
07:54:04.381 --> 07:54:08.033
last lesson, we were given the task of taking a list of file names and printing

6073
07:54:08.033 --> 07:54:11.591
reading filename to the log or output. In our templated version, we created a

6074
07:54:11.591 --> 07:54:14.780
templated command that substituted the filename value in place of the

6075
07:54:14.780 --> 07:54:15.520
params.filename.

6076
07:54:15.520 --> 07:54:19.822
We also use two tasks with different filename values to demonstrate one way to use

6077
07:54:19.822 --> 07:54:24.123
templates without changing the actual bash command. Now let's consider another way

6078
07:54:24.123 --> 07:54:28.372
to perform a substitution. Jinja templates can be considerably more powerful than

6079
07:54:28.372 --> 07:54:32.779
we've used so far. It is possible to use a for construct to allow us to iterate over

6080
07:54:32.779 --> 07:54:36.976
a list and output content accordingly. Let's change our templated command to the

6081
07:54:36.976 --> 07:54:41.277
following. We start with an open curly brace and the percent symbol, then we use a

6082
07:54:41.277 --> 07:54:44.320
normal Python command of for filename in params.filenames,

6083
07:54:44.320 --> 07:54:49.387
