There are several ways to do so, but it is convenient if within an organizational

6289
08:09:02.125 --> 08:09:06.993
unit the process is standardized. That is the aim of Singer as well, to be the

6290
08:09:06.993 --> 08:09:12.111
open-source standard for writing scripts that move data. At its core, Singer has a

6291
08:09:12.111 --> 08:09:17.166
specification that describes how data extraction scripts and data loading scripts

6292
08:09:17.166 --> 08:09:21.660
should communicate using a standard JSON-based format over standard out.

6293
08:09:21.660 --> 08:09:26.715
JSON is similar to Python dictionaries. And standard out is a standardized location

6294
08:09:26.715 --> 08:09:31.466
to which programs write their output. Because singer is a specification, these

6295
08:09:31.466 --> 08:09:36.583
extraction scripts, which are called tabs, and the loading scripts, which are called

6296
08:09:36.583 --> 08:09:41.516
targets, can be written in any programming language. And they can easily be mixed

6297
08:09:41.516 --> 08:09:46.633
and matched to create small data pipelines that move data from one place to another.

6298
08:09:46.633 --> 08:09:51.140
Tabs and targets communicate using three kinds of messages. Schema, state,

6299
08:09:51.140 --> 08:09:55.928
and record, which are sent to and read from specific streams. A stream is a named

6300
08:09:55.928 --> 08:10:00.717
virtual location to which you send messages that can be picked up at a downstream

6301
08:10:00.717 --> 08:10:05.446
location. We can use different streams to partition data based on the topic, for

6302
08:10:05.446 --> 08:10:09.939
example. Error messages would go to an error stream, and data from different

6303
08:10:09.939 --> 08:10:14.668
database tables could go to different streams as well. Imagine you would need to

6304
08:10:14.668 --> 08:10:19.574
pass this set of data to a process. With a SingerSpec, you would first describe the

6305
08:10:19.574 --> 08:10:19.870
data.

6306
08:10:19.870 --> 08:10:24.899
by specifying its schema. The schema should be given as a valid JSON schema, which

6307
08:10:24.899 --> 08:10:29.866
is another specification that allows you to annotate and even validate structured

6308
08:10:29.866 --> 08:10:34.772
data. You specify the data type of each property or field. You could also impose

6309
08:10:34.772 --> 08:10:39.924
constraints, like stating that the age should be an integer value between 1 and 130,

6310
08:10:39.924 --> 08:10:44.952
as we've done here, or that a phone number should be in a certain format. The last

6311
08:10:44.952 --> 08:10:48.080
two keys in this JSON object are the ID and schema.

6312
08:10:48.080 --> 08:10:52.874
They allow you to uniquely specify this schema within your organization and tells

6313
08:10:52.874 --> 08:10:57.549
others which version of JSON schema is being used. They're optional, but highly

6314
08:10:57.549 --> 08:11:02.225
recommended in production grade code. You can tell the Singer library to make a

6315
08:11:02.225 --> 08:11:06.723
schema message out of this JSON schema. You would call Singer's write schema

6316
08:11:06.723 --> 08:11:11.339
function, passing it the JSON schema we defined earlier. With stream name, you

6317
08:11:11.339 --> 08:11:16.074
specify the name of the stream this message belongs to. This can be anything you

6318
08:11:16.074 --> 08:11:16.370
want.

6319
08:11:16.370 --> 08:11:20.879
Data that belongs together should be sent to the same stream. The key properties

6320
08:11:20.879 --> 08:11:25.445
attribute should equal a list of strings that make up the primary key for records

6321
08:11:25.445 --> 08:11:30.180
from the stream. If you're processing data from motorized vehicles on a parking lot,

6322
08:11:30.180 --> 08:11:34.632
for example, this could be the license plate or a surrogate key. If there is no

6323
08:11:34.632 --> 08:11:39.029
primary key, then specify an empty list. As you can see, the right schema call

6324
08:11:39.029 --> 08:11:43.200
simply wraps the actual JSON schema into a new JSON message and adds a few

6325
08:11:43.200 --> 08:11:43.820
attributes.

6326
08:11:43.820 --> 08:11:48.921
The message would be printed on a single line, but we've added line breaks here to

6327
08:11:48.921 --> 08:11:53.773
fit the screen. JSON is a common format, not just in Singer, but in many other

6328
08:11:53.773 --> 08:11:58.812
places. Python provides the JSON module to work with JSON. To get objects in your

6329
08:11:58.812 --> 08:12:03.913
code serialized as JSON, you would call either JSON.dumps or JSON.dump. The former

6330
08:12:03.913 --> 08:12:09.138
simply transforms the object to a string, whereas the latter writes that same string

6331
08:12:09.138 --> 08:12:09.760
to a file.

6332
08:12:09.760 --> 08:12:14.071
Okay, now that we understand what JSON schema is and how Sing it wraps that into

6333
08:12:14.071 --> 08:12:15.580
messages, let's put this in.

6334
08:12:15.620 --> 08:12:20.538
Cool! Now that you know how to create schema messages with Singer, let us continue

6335
08:12:20.538 --> 08:12:25.516
with the record and state messages, so that we can create a full ingestion pipeline

6336
08:12:25.516 --> 08:12:30.375
with the Singer library. Here are the user records we saw earlier. To convert one

6337
08:12:30.375 --> 08:12:35.233
such user into a Singer record message, we'd call the write record function, like

6338
08:12:35.233 --> 08:12:39.971
this. The stream name would need to match the stream you specified earlier in a

6339
08:12:39.971 --> 08:12:43.090
schema message, otherwise these records are ignored.

6340
08:12:43.090 --> 08:12:48.218
This would be almost equivalent to nesting the actual record dictionary in another

6341
08:12:48.218 --> 08:12:53.347
dictionary that has two more keys, being the type and the stream. That can be done

6342
08:12:53.347 --> 08:12:58.601
elegantly with the unpacking operators, which are these two asterisks here preceding

6343
08:12:58.601 --> 08:13:03.854
the fixed dict. That unpacks a dictionary in another one and can be used in function

6344
08:13:03.854 --> 08:13:08.920
calls as well. Really useful. Now, I did say almost equivalent. The truth is that

