08:14:00.538 --> 08:14:05.345
running a tab by parsing the code with a Python interpreter like this. But you'll

6357
08:14:05.345 --> 08:14:10.270
typically find tabs and targets properly packaged, so you could call them directly.

6358
08:14:10.270 --> 08:14:15.486
Like this. You might wonder why we'd go through all this trouble just to produce a

6359
08:14:15.486 --> 08:14:20.575
CSV file from a few simple records. After all, Python's CSV module provides this

6360
08:14:20.575 --> 08:14:25.474
functionality out of the box. The answer is modularity. Each tab or target is

6361
08:14:25.474 --> 08:14:30.817
designed to do one thing very well. They are easily configured through config files.

6362
08:14:30.817 --> 08:14:35.970
By working with a standardized intermediate format, you could easily swap out the

6363
08:14:35.970 --> 08:14:38.260
target CSV for target Google Sheets,

6364
08:14:38.260 --> 08:14:43.041
or target PostgresQL, for example, which write their output to whole different

6365
08:14:43.041 --> 08:14:48.005
systems. This means you don't need to write a lot of code. Just pick the tabs and

6366
08:14:48.005 --> 08:14:52.786
targets that match with your intended source and destination and voila, you're

6367
08:14:52.786 --> 08:14:57.873
ready. We haven't discussed Singer's state messages yet. They are typically used to

6368
08:14:57.873 --> 08:15:02.715
keep track of state, which is the way something is at some moment in time. That

6369
08:15:02.715 --> 08:15:06.270
something is typically some form of memory of the process.

6370
08:15:06.270 --> 08:15:12.012
Imagine for example that you must extract only new records from this database daily

6371
08:15:12.012 --> 08:15:17.269
at noon local time. The easiest way to do so is to keep track of the highest

6372
08:15:17.269 --> 08:15:22.942
encountered last updated on value and emit that as a state message at the end of a

6373
08:15:22.942 --> 08:15:28.614
successful run of your tab. Then you can reuse the same message at a later time to

6374
08:15:28.614 --> 08:15:33.180
extract only those records that were updated after this old state.

6375
08:15:33.180 --> 08:15:38.130
You emit these state messages using the write state function. The only required

6376
08:15:38.130 --> 08:15:43.269
attribute is the value, which can be any JSON serializable object. The value field

6377
08:15:43.269 --> 08:15:48.345
is free form and only for use by the same tab. Let's write our own custom tab for

6378
08:15:48.345 --> 08:15:53.420
ingesting data from a recipe. Congratulations on completing the previous chapter.

6379
08:15:53.420 --> 08:15:58.684
Now that you know how to ingest data, you will learn how to clean and transform data

6380
08:15:58.684 --> 08:16:00.000
using PySpark. First,

6381
08:16:01.311 --> 08:16:06.717
what is Spark? Spark is an analytics engine for processing large quantities of data.

6382
08:16:06.717 --> 08:16:11.801
The Spark core powers four libraries that serve different but related purposes.

6383
08:16:11.801 --> 08:16:16.628
There's Spark SQL for manipulating mostly tabular data, Spark Streaming for

6384
08:16:16.628 --> 08:16:18.880
manipulating streaming data, MLlib,

6385
08:16:18.880 --> 08:16:23.811
for machine learning and graphics for graph analysis. These libraries can be

6386
08:16:23.811 --> 08:16:28.806
combined seamlessly in the same application. Spark runs everywhere, including

6387
08:16:28.806 --> 08:16:34.061
standalone on your laptop and in the cloud. It also exists in several programming

6388
08:16:34.061 --> 08:16:39.251
languages, like Python, where we often refer to it as PySpark. Spark is a useful

6389
08:16:39.251 --> 08:16:42.430
framework when you want to process data at scale.

6390
08:16:42.430 --> 08:16:47.427
It scales incredibly well to dataset sizes of billions of records by parallelizing

6391
08:16:47.427 --> 08:16:51.997
its execution over multiple machines. Spark can also be used in interactive

6392
08:16:51.997 --> 08:16:56.933
analytics, such as notebooks in which data scientists explore data interactively,

6393
08:16:56.933 --> 08:17:01.869
validate hypotheses about the data quickly, and drill deeper into the results. It

6394
08:17:01.869 --> 08:17:06.866
also offers a suite of machine learning algorithms and functions that allow you to

6395
08:17:06.866 --> 08:17:11.010
build features, train models, and score existing models on new data.

6396
08:17:11.010 --> 08:17:16.182
You shouldn't be using Spark when you have only little data. Spark adds overhead,

6397
08:17:16.182 --> 08:17:21.227
which makes it inefficient and slow for small quantities of data. Additionally,

6398
08:17:21.227 --> 08:17:26.463
Spark offers rich libraries for querying, machine learning and graph analytics. If

6399
08:17:26.463 --> 08:17:31.380
all you need to do is to apply a simple filter, then the richness of Spark is

6400
08:17:31.380 --> 08:17:36.233
probably overkill. Let's get started with Spark by working on a hypothetical

6401
08:17:36.233 --> 08:17:39.170
scenario where we want to find the best diaper

6402
08:17:39.170 --> 08:17:44.063
Based on qualitative aspects such as comfort and quantitative attributes such as

6403
08:17:44.063 --> 08:17:49.200
price. Our marketing department has already placed the following raw data sources in

6404
08:17:49.200 --> 08:17:53.970
the data lake. The prices dataset, which contains pricing details on different

6405
08:17:53.970 --> 08:17:58.801
diaper models in different stores across countries, and user ratings per diaper

6406
08:17:58.801 --> 08:18:03.572
model. These spreadsheets will serve as input for our data pipeline. To load a

6407
08:18:03.572 --> 08:18:07.180
dataset with PySpark, we must first create a Spark session.

6408
08:18:07.180 --> 08:18:12.231
At least in Spark version 2.x. Upon creation, the execution environment gets

6409
08:18:12.231 --> 08:18:17.813
configured. Spark offers a very convenient way to read all kinds of files, including

6410
08:18:17.813 --> 08:18:23.263
CSVs. To read a file that is on a local file system, we pass the path to that file

6411
08:18:23.263 --> 08:18:28.579
as the first argument to spark.read.csv, which returns a dataframe. This doesn't

6412
08:18:28.579 --> 08:18:33.962
immediately trigger the reading of the full file though, just some metadata and a

6413
08:18:33.962 --> 08:18:36.820
few bytes. So this operation is quite fast.

