08:19:36.136 --> 08:19:41.392
or non-specified data. We can now see that the data types are no longer all strings,

6427
08:19:41.392 --> 08:19:46.398
which makes later transformations easier. Alright, let's practice what... In the

6428
08:19:46.398 --> 08:19:51.404
previous lesson, you learned how to import data correctly. However, data usually

6429
08:19:51.404 --> 08:19:56.597
doesn't come clean, so in this lesson, we'll see how to clean data. Data scientists

6430
08:19:56.597 --> 08:19:59.100
spend a lot of their time cleaning data.

6431
08:19:59.100 --> 08:20:04.333
Because most data sources you work with are not ready for analytics. Here are some

6432
08:20:04.333 --> 08:20:09.247
common reasons. Incorrect data types. When you read from CSV, everything is a

6433
08:20:09.247 --> 08:20:14.608
string. We have to make the effort to turn the fields back into integers, floats, or

6434
08:20:14.608 --> 08:20:19.905
datetimes. Invalid rows. Especially when parsing manually entered data from sources

6435
08:20:19.905 --> 08:20:25.266
such as Microsoft Excel, some rows simply contain bogus information. These rows need

6436
08:20:25.266 --> 08:20:27.500
to be removed. Rows are incomplete.

6437
08:20:27.500 --> 08:20:32.801
Sometimes almost all fields in a row are valid except one or two. What do you do in

6438
08:20:32.801 --> 08:20:38.103
this case? Sometimes these fields are not critical and can be left empty. Sometimes

6439
08:20:38.103 --> 08:20:43.404
they can be given a value. Badly chosen placeholders. It happens that you encounter

6440
08:20:43.404 --> 08:20:48.642
strings such as NA or unknown. In these cases, very often, replacing the values by

6441
08:20:48.642 --> 08:20:53.943
null is the appropriate thing to do. If data scientists spend so much of their time

6442
08:20:53.943 --> 08:20:56.690
cleaning data, why can't you automate that?

6443
08:20:56.690 --> 08:21:01.722
Well, there is definitely tooling that can help you with that. For example, you can

6444
08:21:01.722 --> 08:21:06.390
ask Spark to infer the data types for you. And most of the time, that is good

6445
08:21:06.390 --> 08:21:11.421
enough. But so far, automating all data cleaning tasks remains a challenge, because

6446
08:21:11.421 --> 08:21:16.513
data cleaning often depends on the context. Ask yourself these questions. Are you in

6447
08:21:16.513 --> 08:21:21.060
a regulated or strict reporting environment where every row of data counts?

6448
08:21:21.060 --> 08:21:26.306
Or can your downstream systems cope with data that is 95% clean and 95% complete?

6449
08:21:26.306 --> 08:21:31.745
What are the implicit standards in your company? If you are based in Western Europe,

6450
08:21:31.745 --> 08:21:37.056
for example, all datetimes can perhaps implicitly be set to Central European time.

6451
08:21:37.056 --> 08:21:42.236
What are the low-level details of the systems you read data from? For example, a

6452
08:21:42.236 --> 08:21:47.547
machine can log UNKNOWN or NA when a sensor reading fails. You've already seen how

6453
08:21:47.547 --> 08:21:50.720
we can correct data types in the previous lesson.

6454
08:21:50.720 --> 08:21:55.729
In Spark, this table summarizes frequently used data types. Pause the video to

6455
08:21:55.729 --> 08:22:01.124
familiarize yourself with the data types available to you. Let's see what happens if

6456
08:22:01.124 --> 08:22:06.326
you have an invalid row. The invalid row is the one that has only dashes. You see

6457
08:22:06.326 --> 08:22:11.656
that Spark makes an effort to incorporate the invalid row. That's not what we want.

