08:25:15.111 --> 08:25:19.858
can be sorted chronologically by calling ORDERBY on the data frame and specifying

6500
08:25:19.858 --> 08:25:24.430
the columns by which you want to sort. Let's pretend we'd like to know all the

6501
08:25:24.430 --> 08:25:29.060
brands that were sold in the stores that we have data on. We can use the SELECT

6502
08:25:29.060 --> 08:25:29.470
method.

6503
08:25:29.470 --> 08:25:34.187
To select only the columns we care about. And if you want to rename a column, we

6504
08:25:34.187 --> 08:25:38.904
simply call the alias method on that column. Notice, however, that now there are

6505
08:25:38.904 --> 08:25:43.621
duplicate rows. Typically, you want to reduce these duplicates. In Spark, we can

6506
08:25:43.621 --> 08:25:48.574
call the distinct function on a data frame to return only distinct rows. Now imagine

6507
08:25:48.574 --> 08:25:53.350
that we have sales data from diapers in several stores. Let's say we want to know

6508
08:25:53.350 --> 08:25:55.650
the average price of diapers per brand.

6509
08:25:55.650 --> 08:26:00.463
It's as simple as grouping the data by the brand column and calling the average of

6510
08:26:00.463 --> 08:26:05.276
the price within each group. Note that the new column gets an auto-generated name.

6511
08:26:05.276 --> 08:26:10.089
Now let's make it a little bit more exciting. What if we want both the average and

6512
08:26:10.089 --> 08:26:14.667
the count? Then we can use the ag method in PySpark, which takes any number of

6513
08:26:14.667 --> 08:26:19.363
columns. AVG and count are two aggregation functions that produce these columns.

6514
08:26:19.363 --> 08:26:24.000
Remember the alias function? We've used it here to rename the produced columns.

6515
08:26:24.000 --> 08:26:28.632
Integrating different data sources typically happens through joins. Elaborating on

6516
08:26:28.632 --> 08:26:33.207
all the ins and outs of join behavior would need its own course. Let's just cover

6517
08:26:33.207 --> 08:26:37.783
the basics here. Imagine we want to join the prices table with the ratings table.

6518
08:26:37.783 --> 08:26:42.528
Let's print the two tables again for reference. We can see both tables can be linked

6519
08:26:42.528 --> 08:26:47.103
by brand and model. This means that two rows can be joined together if both their

6520
08:26:47.103 --> 08:26:51.170
brand and their model are the same. How would you tell Spark to do this?

6521
08:26:51.170 --> 08:26:55.751
As expected, Spark has built-in functionality specifically for this. We can simply

6522
08:26:55.751 --> 08:27:00.387
call the join method on the ratings and pass it the prices and the columns on which

6523
08:27:00.387 --> 08:27:04.912
to join in that order. As you can see, all rows are now augmented with additional

6524
08:27:04.912 --> 08:27:09.548
information from the table of prices. Now that you've seen why and how to transform

6525
08:27:09.548 --> 08:27:14.240
data, let's practice some of these common transformations. Now that you have created

6526
08:27:14.240 --> 08:27:18.541
an entire Spark transformation pipeline in Python, you want to deploy it into

6527
08:27:18.541 --> 08:27:20.720
production. Let's see how this is done.

6528
08:27:20.720 --> 08:27:25.109
You could run a PySpark data pipeline locally, like you would run any Python

6529
08:27:25.109 --> 08:27:29.902
application. That is, by invoking the Python interpreter and passing it the path to

6530
08:27:29.902 --> 08:27:34.752
the main application. And this will work, provided you have locally installed Spark,

6531
08:27:34.752 --> 08:27:39.603
access to any resources your pipeline requires, and a properly configured classpath.

6532
08:27:39.603 --> 08:27:44.281
The classpath serves a similar role as the Python path, in that it tells the Java

6533
08:27:44.281 --> 08:27:48.900
Virtual Machine, which is what Spark runs on, where to look for classes that are

6534
08:27:48.900 --> 08:27:49.420
imported.

6535
08:27:49.420 --> 08:27:54.325
In daily operations, you will be using the SparkSubmit script though. This script

6536
08:27:54.325 --> 08:27:59.109
helps setting up the launch environment in a manner appropriate for the cluster

6537
08:27:59.109 --> 08:28:04.195
manager in the selected deploy mode. Cluster managers are programs that make cluster

6538
08:28:04.195 --> 08:28:09.100
resources, like the RAM and CPUs of different nodes, available to other programs.

6539
08:28:09.100 --> 08:28:14.066
There exist a few, Yarn is a popular one, and each has different options, which is

6540
08:28:14.066 --> 08:28:17.820
why SparkSubmit tries to unify the calling parameter somewhat.

6541
08:28:17.820 --> 08:28:22.856
The deploy mode tells Spark where to run the driver of the Spark application, either

6542
08:28:22.856 --> 08:28:27.713
on a dedicated master node, or on one of the cluster worker nodes. This would get

6543
08:28:27.713 --> 08:28:32.449
deeply technical fast, as each mode has pros and cons. For the purposes of this

6544
08:28:32.449 --> 08:28:37.305
course, it's also not important, as we simply set out to understand the basics of

6545
08:28:37.305 --> 08:28:42.342
the SparkSubmit program. After the setup of the launch environment, SparkSubmit also

6546
08:28:42.342 --> 08:28:44.620
invokes the main class or main method.

6547
08:28:44.620 --> 08:28:49.790
For many PySpark applications, the following template will suffice to launch a job.

6548
08:28:49.790 --> 08:28:54.897
We invoke spark-submit and pass it the optional master argument, which is either a

6549
08:28:54.897 --> 08:28:59.942
URL specific to the cluster manager or a string like local star. This tells Spark

6550
08:28:59.942 --> 08:29:05.174
where it can get resources from, which is either a real cluster or simply your local

6551
08:29:05.174 --> 08:29:08.350
machine. The latter is often good enough for tests.

6552
08:29:08.350 --> 08:29:13.124
If our PySpark data pipeline relies on more than just one Python module, we will

6553
08:29:13.124 --> 08:29:17.839
need to copy these modules to all nodes, so that each Python interpreter on the

6554
08:29:17.839 --> 08:29:22.374
cluster's workers knows where to find the details of the functions they must

6555
08:29:22.374 --> 08:29:27.029
execute. We typically provide the modules that aren't already installed on the

6556
08:29:27.029 --> 08:29:31.922
worker nodes through zip files, and that is what the PyFiles argument does for us.

6557
08:29:31.922 --> 08:29:36.875
It copies these dependencies over to the workers. Finally, we tell Spark which file

6558
08:29:36.875 --> 08:29:37.890
is the main file.

6559
08:29:37.890 --> 08:29:42.923
the entry point to our application. It is the file that contains code to trigger the

6560
08:29:42.923 --> 08:29:47.717
creation of the Spark session. If that file, that Python module actually, parses

6561
08:29:47.717 --> 08:29:52.690
command line arguments, through the argparse module for example, then we may supply

6562
08:29:52.690 --> 08:29:57.723
these extra arguments at the end of the Spark submit command. The pyfiles option can

6563
08:29:57.723 --> 08:30:02.457
take a comma separated list of files that will be added on each worker's Python

6564
08:30:02.457 --> 08:30:07.250
path, which lists the places where the Python interpreter will look for modules.

6565
08:30:07.250 --> 08:30:12.751
ZIP files are a common way to package and distribute your code. To create a proper

6566
08:30:12.751 --> 08:30:18.318
ZIP file ready to be used with PySpark, navigate in the shell to the directory that

6567
08:30:18.318 --> 08:30:23.685
contains the root folder of your module. In the example shown, that would be the

6568
08:30:23.685 --> 08:30:29.252
folder containing the PyDiaper folder. Once there, invoke the ZIP utility, enabling

6569
08:30:29.252 --> 08:30:34.820
it to recursively add all files in all subfolders by passing the RecursePaths flag.

6570
08:30:34.820 --> 08:30:39.589
Provide a name for the resulting compressed archive, and finally, provide the name

6571
08:30:39.589 --> 08:30:44.300
of the folder you want to compress. The resulting zip file can be passed as is to

6572
08:30:44.300 --> 08:30:49.069
Spark submits, PyFiles argument. Both CleanPrices.py and CleanRatings.py are Spark

6573
08:30:49.069 --> 08:30:53.954
jobs, as they start a Spark session, so either one could be given as the main Python

6574
08:30:53.954 --> 08:30:58.723
file. Alright, that's it. Let's practice packaging and running a Spark job to make

6575
08:30:58.723 --> 08:31:03.550
sure our pipeline works as expected. Ready for the next step in building pipelines?

6576
08:31:03.550 --> 08:31:08.602
We already know how to ingest data and how to deal with big datasets. Now let's make

6577
08:31:08.602 --> 08:31:13.653
our code more robust and reusable. A software product, like a browser app, is rarely

6578
08:31:13.653 --> 08:31:18.344
ever finished. It is natural for a product to change because different or more

6579
08:31:18.344 --> 08:31:23.275
functionality is requested. Also, problems get found and need to be resolved, like

6580
08:31:23.275 --> 08:31:28.086
incorrect calculations or poor responsiveness. However, key aspects of a product

6581
08:31:28.086 --> 08:31:29.710
also need to stay constant.

6582
08:31:29.710 --> 08:31:34.586
Code that calculates people's wages based on the hours they worked had better be

6583
08:31:34.586 --> 08:31:39.646
constant and well tested. So how do you ensure core functionality remains stable in

6584
08:31:39.646 --> 08:31:44.705
the light of changes? The answer is tests. Tests are written and executed to assert

6585
08:31:44.705 --> 08:31:49.825
our expectations are matched. By committing tests to our codebase, we have a written

6586
08:31:49.825 --> 08:31:54.640
copy of our expectations as they were at some time in the past. So having tests

6587
08:31:54.640 --> 08:31:57.810
reduces the chances of introducing breaking changes.

6588
08:31:57.810 --> 08:32:02.441
If you need to change a small function somewhere so that it returns a tupon rather

6589
08:32:02.441 --> 08:32:06.902
than a single object, how sure are you that that function wasn't used elsewhere

6590
08:32:06.902 --> 08:32:11.533
where it required the return value to be a single object? Without tests, you would

6591
08:32:11.533 --> 08:32:16.277
not be informed until this function was called. This is costly. Automated tests help

6592
08:32:16.277 --> 08:32:20.682
in preventing this. Tests are rarely exhaustive, though. A function that tests

6593
08:32:20.682 --> 08:32:25.369
whether a certain day is a holiday would need to test every day of every year to be

6594
08:32:25.369 --> 08:32:26.160
sure it works.

6595
08:32:26.160 --> 08:32:30.720
You would need a lookup table of thousands of records. Clearly, this is insane.

6596
08:32:30.720 --> 08:32:35.107
Instead, we choose to test only a few samples. Therefore, tests don't assert

6597
08:32:35.107 --> 08:32:39.956
correctness in all cases, but they raise confidence that our expectations are met in

6598
08:32:39.956 --> 08:32:44.458
a few cases and that already is a very good reason to have them. Another great

6599
08:32:44.458 --> 08:32:48.787
argument for the need of tests is that they are the most up-to-date form of

6600
08:32:48.787 --> 08:32:53.463
documentation. Oftentimes, people write documents describing the functionality of

6601
08:32:53.463 --> 08:32:55.310
some piece of software manually.

6602
08:32:55.310 --> 08:32:59.503
That's fine as long as the description remains high level. Details about certain

6603
08:32:59.503 --> 08:33:03.801
functions should be avoided, because they have a tendency to grow out of sync with

6604
08:33:03.801 --> 08:33:08.204
what is actually running. A well-written test can clarify what a piece of code does,

6605
08:33:08.204 --> 08:33:12.450
even if it treats that piece as a black box. There are more reasons to test code,

6606
08:33:12.450 --> 08:33:16.853
but these three reasons alone should provide sufficient incentive to have them. Now,

6607
08:33:16.853 --> 08:33:21.099
sometimes people avoid writing tests because it takes time they believe is better

6608
08:33:21.099 --> 08:33:23.300
spent on adding more features to software.

6609
08:33:23.300 --> 08:33:28.320
While it is true that testing takes time, it is an investment that pays back as your

6610
08:33:28.320 --> 08:33:33.340
project gets bigger, especially when tests aren't duplicated at different places and

6611
08:33:33.340 --> 08:33:38.181
tests non-trivial parts. A useful concept to keep in mind when designing tests is

6612
08:33:38.181 --> 08:33:43.141
the test pyramid. It tells you where to invest your efforts. Testing pieces of code

6613
08:33:43.141 --> 08:33:48.041
that do not rely on integration with external components are unit tests. These can

6614
08:33:48.041 --> 08:33:50.850
run fast and their development effort is cheap.

6615
08:33:50.850 --> 08:33:55.888
An example of this is a data cleaning function that transforms all variations of yes

6616
08:33:55.888 --> 08:34:00.866
and no strings to proper booleans. Because these tests run so fast, you should have

6617
08:34:00.866 --> 08:34:05.845
many of them. Interactions with file systems and databases are integration tests or

6618
08:34:05.845 --> 08:34:10.583
service tests as they integrate different services or components. They run more

6619
08:34:10.583 --> 08:34:15.381
slowly and their setup also takes more effort. You can have less of them because

6620
08:34:15.381 --> 08:34:18.260
your unit tests should already cover some parts.

6621
08:34:18.260 --> 08:34:23.389
like the conversion to booleans before inserting records into a database. The same

6622
08:34:23.389 --> 08:34:28.456
goes for end-to-end tests, which often mimic the experience a user would get when

6623
08:34:28.456 --> 08:34:33.523
interacting through a user interface. They're even more costly as they run slowly

6624
08:34:33.523 --> 08:34:38.090
and are often difficult to write robustly but are close to the end-user's

6625
08:34:38.090 --> 08:34:43.094
experience. Okay, let's see. Welcome back. In the previous lesson, we've covered

6626
08:34:43.094 --> 08:34:47.410
different kinds of tests and why good code has tests. In this lesson,

6627
08:34:47.410 --> 08:34:52.566
We will learn how to write unit tests for our PySpark application. In doing so, we

6628
08:34:52.566 --> 08:34:57.660
will restructure our code and create more reusable components. In the application

6629
08:34:57.660 --> 08:35:02.627
from the previous chapter, we created an ETL pipeline. We extracted data from a

6630
08:35:02.627 --> 08:35:07.784
location, transformed it, and loaded it to another location. In the first and last

6631
08:35:07.784 --> 08:35:12.751
steps, we interact with other services, like databases, file systems, and cloud

6632
08:35:12.751 --> 08:35:13.380
providers.

6633
08:35:13.380 --> 08:35:18.123
Those interactions are costly, and, as we know from the previous lesson, should be

6634
08:35:18.123 --> 08:35:22.808
tested, but not as often as our own transformation logic. That's where we add the

6635
08:35:22.808 --> 08:35:27.435
real business value. In this lesson, we will rewrite the transformations in this

6636
08:35:27.435 --> 08:35:32.178
piece of code from our pipeline to allow testing. Now, the transformations operate

6637
08:35:32.178 --> 08:35:36.574
on data frames, which we obtain through interacting with the file system. As

6638
08:35:36.574 --> 08:35:40.855
mentioned, we would like to remove that dependency and focus solely on the

6639
08:35:40.855 --> 08:35:41.780
transformations.

6640
08:35:41.780 --> 08:35:46.878
If the transformations would no longer get data from files, how do we still feed

6641
08:35:46.878 --> 08:35:49.741
them data? Well, Spark data frames can be created

6642
08:35:49.741 --> 08:35:55.453
in memory too. In the code we had written earlier, we created a dataframe by loading

6643
08:35:55.453 --> 08:36:01.096
a CSV file. This has a number of downsides which we can solve by creating in-memory

6644
08:36:01.096 --> 08:36:06.536
dataframes like this. Create a custom Spark SQL row class and pass any iterable,

6645
08:36:06.536 --> 08:36:07.420
like a tuple,

6646
08:36:07.420 --> 08:36:12.192
of instances of this class to the createDataFrame constructor. There are clear

6647
08:36:12.192 --> 08:36:17.148
benefits here. We immediately see the data being passed to our functions. We also

6648
08:36:17.148 --> 08:36:22.287
don't have to click around to find or download a file to understand what's in there.

6649
08:36:22.287 --> 08:36:27.427
Our original code did a lot at once. It linked one data frame to another and added a

6650
08:36:27.427 --> 08:36:32.383
column based on some mathematical function. Combining steps makes it hard to test

6651
08:36:32.383 --> 08:36:35.870
the functionality. We could separate these steps like so.

6652
08:36:35.870 --> 08:36:40.677
Write out each step to its own function and apply them in sequence. While it may

6653
08:36:40.677 --> 08:36:45.063
seem silly to write a new function for only a single transformation, each

6654
08:36:45.063 --> 08:36:49.869
transformation by itself can be tested and reused. We now know enough to write a

6655
08:36:49.869 --> 08:36:54.916
test for calculating the unit price in euros. We start by creating an in-memory data

6656
08:36:54.916 --> 08:36:59.662
frame with just the comms we need. Here we see another way of instantiating row

6657
08:36:59.662 --> 08:37:04.709
classes. Again, we use the operator that unpacks a dictionary. And you'll see why in

6658
08:37:04.709 --> 08:37:05.250
a second.

6659
08:37:05.250 --> 08:37:10.430
We pass this dataframe to the function we want to test. Our expectation is that the

6660
08:37:10.430 --> 08:37:15.673
unit underscore price underscore in underscore euro field is added and that the math

6661
08:37:15.673 --> 08:37:20.916
is correct. We can calculate this value easily ourselves, which is another advantage

6662
08:37:20.916 --> 08:37:26.096
to running tests with in-memory dataframes. You now also see why we used a starting

6663
08:37:26.096 --> 08:37:31.277
dictionary. It is repeated in both the initial and expected dataframes. Finally, we

6664
08:37:31.277 --> 08:37:34.460
must compare the resulting and expected dataframes.

6665
08:37:34.460 --> 08:37:39.335
We're using a helper function here, assertDataFrameEqual, which does exactly that.

6666
08:37:39.335 --> 08:37:44.329
Explaining how this function is defined would be a bit too long for this lesson, but

6667
08:37:44.329 --> 08:37:49.145
you probably have a good idea of what it does. A test such as the one we just saw

6668
08:37:49.145 --> 08:37:54.139
runs well in a testing framework like PyTest. Your testing framework is a preference

6669
08:37:54.139 --> 08:37:58.657
though. What you should take home from this session is that interacting with

6670
08:37:58.657 --> 08:38:00.560
external data sources is costly,

6671
08:38:00.560 --> 08:38:05.235
In-memory data frames allow you to test a single function more easily by bringing

6672
08:38:05.235 --> 08:38:09.910
the data in plain sight and focusing on just a small number of examples. And that

6673
08:38:09.910 --> 08:38:14.181
your code should be refactored so that you can create small, reusable, and

6674
08:38:14.181 --> 08:38:18.971
well-named functions which are also easier to test. Now it's your turn to dive into

6675
08:38:18.971 --> 08:38:23.415
a few exercises. Then we'll move forward with automating the testing program.

6676
08:38:23.415 --> 08:38:28.205
Welcome back! Now that you're able to refactor code and write unit tests, you still

6677
08:38:28.205 --> 08:38:30.110
have to actually run those tests.

6678
08:38:30.110 --> 08:38:35.327
In this lesson, we'll see how that can be done. Many programming languages provide

6679
08:38:35.327 --> 08:38:40.545
functionality to execute tests. In Python, there are several modules that do this.

6680
08:38:40.545 --> 08:38:45.890
The best-known ones are UnitTest, Doctest, PyTest, and Nose, of which only the first

6681
08:38:45.890 --> 08:38:50.725
two are in the standard library. In all cases, these tools look for modules,

6682
08:38:50.725 --> 08:38:56.006
classes, and functions that are marked in a special way, for example, by having the

6683
08:38:56.006 --> 08:38:56.770
prefix test.

6684
08:38:56.770 --> 08:39:01.845
They drill down to find code that can be executed and then executed. Their core task

6685
08:39:01.845 --> 08:39:06.921
is to assert something, for example, that a computed value equals an expected value,

6686
08:39:06.921 --> 08:39:11.875
or that an error was erased. The great feature about these frameworks is that they

6687
08:39:11.875 --> 08:39:16.588
generate a report at the end of a run, summarizing which test passed and which

6688
08:39:16.588 --> 08:39:21.241
failed, often with some extra information to help debugging. If a command ran

6689
08:39:21.241 --> 08:39:25.410
unsuccessfully, like in this example, the exit code will be non-zero.

6690
08:39:25.410 --> 08:39:30.023
One small remark here. In the first lesson of this chapter, we said that unit tests

6691
08:39:30.023 --> 08:39:34.581
were supposed to run fast. Two seconds for a single test is a long time. Spark and

6692
08:39:34.581 --> 08:39:39.139
other distributed computing frameworks add overhead in tests. Automation is one of

6693
08:39:39.139 --> 08:39:43.753
the prime objectives of a data engineer. Running unit tests manually is tedious and

6694
08:39:43.753 --> 08:39:48.310
error-prone, as you might forget to run it after a series of changes to your code.

6695
08:39:48.310 --> 08:39:52.590
If you're working professionally with code, you're using some sort of version

6696
08:39:52.590 --> 08:39:53.980
control system, like Git.

6697
08:39:53.980 --> 08:39:58.840
In many version control systems, you can configure certain scripts to be run when

6698
08:39:58.840 --> 08:40:03.880
you change code. This is great, as it can give you rapid feedback on the changes you

6699
08:40:03.880 --> 08:40:08.620
made. You would do well to explore the options provided by your version control

6700
08:40:08.620 --> 08:40:13.600
system. The second line of defense is your project's CI-CD pipeline. Let's see what

6701
08:40:13.600 --> 08:40:17.980
that is. CI-CD stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery.

6702
08:40:17.980 --> 08:40:22.810
The first term has come to present the practice of integrating code changes as soon

6703
08:40:22.810 --> 08:40:27.292
as possible with the code that runs in production, which is called the master

6704
08:40:27.292 --> 08:40:31.889
branch. This should only be allowed if the changes didn't break anything, which

6705
08:40:31.889 --> 08:40:36.720
tests can detect to some degree. So continuous integration focuses a lot on running

6706
08:40:36.720 --> 08:40:41.375
tests and having plenty of them. The second term means that all artifacts should

6707
08:40:41.375 --> 08:40:45.100
always be in a deployable state at any time without any problem.

6708
08:40:45.100 --> 08:40:49.908
When you push your code as part of some version control system to some remote

6709
08:40:49.908 --> 08:40:54.653
server, you can also trigger running unit tests and static code checks, like

6710
08:40:54.653 --> 08:40:59.711
compliancy with pip8, which is the Python style guide. CircleCI is a service that

6711
08:40:59.711 --> 08:41:04.581
runs tests automatically for you. Like many of these tools, it's looking for a

6712
08:41:04.581 --> 08:41:09.389
specific file in your code repository. For CircleCI, it's config.yamon in the

6713
08:41:09.389 --> 08:41:13.760
.circleci folder, which should be at the root of your code repository.

6714
08:41:13.760 --> 08:41:19.028
The syntax is YAML, which is a superset of JSON, but has a different focus and adds

6715
08:41:19.028 --> 08:41:24.168
many features. In the config file, there is a section called jobs. Each job has a

6716
08:41:24.168 --> 08:41:28.928
name, like test. The job is a collection of steps that get executed in some

6717
08:41:28.928 --> 08:41:34.196
environment, in this case a Docker image designed for a specific version of Python.

6718
08:41:34.196 --> 08:41:39.400
The steps are executed in sequence and would typically be the steps that you would

6719
08:41:39.400 --> 08:41:42.510
