6727
08:42:12.030 --> 08:42:17.203
Now let's put it all together and see how these tasks get triggered. A workflow is a

6728
08:42:17.203 --> 08:42:22.377
sequence of tasks that are either scheduled to run or that could be triggered by the

6729
08:42:22.377 --> 08:42:27.303
occurrence of an event. Workflows are typically used by data scientists and data

6730
08:42:27.303 --> 08:42:32.354
engineers to orchestrate data processing pipelines. Oftentimes, we have tasks that

6731
08:42:32.354 --> 08:42:37.465
need to run on a schedule. Churn prediction algorithms might need to be run weekly,

6732
08:42:37.465 --> 08:42:41.530
for example. An often used software utility in such cases is Cron.

6733
08:42:41.540 --> 08:42:47.363
Cron reads configuration files, known as crontab files. In these files, you tabulate

6734
08:42:47.363 --> 08:42:52.771
tasks that you want to run at a specific time. Here's an example. This crontab

6735
08:42:52.771 --> 08:42:58.456
record would execute the process, log my activity, at a specific time, which, read

6736
08:42:58.456 --> 08:43:04.210
from left to right, would mean every 15 minutes, between normal office hours, every

6737
08:43:04.210 --> 08:43:09.825
day of the month, for every month, but only on Mondays, Tuesdays, Wednesdays, and

6738
08:43:09.825 --> 08:43:10.380
Fridays.

6739
08:43:10.380 --> 08:43:15.292
The fields of this time notation are delimited by whitespace. You could also add

6740
08:43:15.292 --> 08:43:20.326
comments, so this CronTab record would do the same but is easier on the eyes. Cron

6741
08:43:20.326 --> 08:43:25.360
is a very basic scheduler. It works very well, but isn't the best tool for complex

6742
08:43:25.360 --> 08:43:30.272
workflows that we want to monitor over time. Other tools have come to the scene,

6743
08:43:30.272 --> 08:43:34.140
like Luigi, Azkaban and Airflow. We'll be looking into Airflow.

6744
08:43:34.140 --> 08:43:39.395
What we expect from modern workflow management tools is that they allow us to create

6745
08:43:39.395 --> 08:43:44.087
and visualize complex workflows. Here's how Airflow visualizes the workflow

6746
08:43:44.087 --> 08:43:49.092
schematic we saw earlier. These tools should also allow us to monitor workflows.

6747
08:43:49.092 --> 08:43:54.159
Airflow can show us when certain tasks failed or how long each task took and plot

6748
08:43:54.159 --> 08:43:58.100
that in clear charts. Finally, horizontal scaling is important.

6749
08:43:58.100 --> 08:44:03.068
As we get more tasks to execute, we want our tool to work with multiple machines,

6750
08:44:03.068 --> 08:44:08.097
rather than increasing the performance of one single machine. Airflow fulfills all

6751
08:44:08.097 --> 08:44:13.249
of these needs, which is why it is a good replacement for Cron. The central piece in

6752
08:44:13.249 --> 08:44:18.216
an Airflow workflow is the DAG, which is an acronym for Directed Acyclic Graph. A

6753
08:44:18.216 --> 08:44:23.368
graph is a collection of nodes that are connected by edges. The directed part in the

6754
08:44:23.368 --> 08:44:27.600
acronym implies that there is a sense of direction between the nodes.

6755
08:44:27.600 --> 08:44:32.903
The arrows on the edges indicate the direction. The acyclic part simply means that

6756
08:44:32.903 --> 08:44:38.271
when you traverse the directed graph, there is no way for you to circle back to the

6757
08:44:38.271 --> 08:44:43.639
same node. In Airflow, the nodes are operators, each instance of which can be given

6758
08:44:43.639 --> 08:44:48.877
a unique label, the task ID. Operators do something, like run a Python script, or

6759
08:44:48.877 --> 08:44:54.310
schedule tasks to the cloud provider. They're triggered by a scheduler, but executed

6760
08:44:54.310 --> 08:44:55.280
by an executor.

6761
08:44:55.280 --> 08:45:00.361
which is typically a different process. In code, an Airflow DAC requires a DAC ID.

6762
08:45:00.361 --> 08:45:05.505
Some of the most common optional arguments are the schedule interval, where you can

6763
08:45:05.505 --> 08:45:10.400
pass a cron schedule, and the date on which the DAC should start. Operators are

6764
08:45:10.400 --> 08:45:15.544
assigned to this DAC later in the code. Tasks are essentially instances of operator

6765
08:45:15.544 --> 08:45:20.315
classes. Airflow comes with many, but you can also define your own operators.

6766
08:45:20.315 --> 08:45:22.980
They're typically named after what they do.

6767
08:45:22.980 --> 08:45:28.559
A bash operator runs a bash command, a python operator runs a python script, and so

6768
08:45:28.559 --> 08:45:34.138
on. Dependencies between operators are coded with a set upstream and set downstream

6769
08:45:34.138 --> 08:45:39.716
methods, like this. You could also define the same relationships using the bitshift

6770
08:45:39.716 --> 08:45:45.295
operator, like so. These could even be chained, like this. A simple DAG description

6771
08:45:45.295 --> 08:45:50.000
like this would result in a linear flow, as shown in this image. Note,

6772
08:45:50.000 --> 08:45:54.788
that the arguments of the operators allow you to specify non-default conditions,

6773
08:45:54.788 --> 08:45:59.816
like run if any of the tasks directly upstream fails. Alright, now that you know the

6774
