6784
08:46:48.752 --> 08:46:53.618
class. Finally, in the bash command, which is specific to the bash operator, you

6785
08:46:53.618 --> 08:46:58.727
specify the bash command to be executed as a string. You could also specify multiple

6786
08:46:58.727 --> 08:47:03.836
of these, or even a script file by name. Although many tasks could be done using the

6787
08:47:03.836 --> 08:47:08.580
bash operator, by passing the appropriate bash command, like python-my-app.py,

6788
08:47:08.580 --> 08:47:13.958
It doesn't serve all use cases. For example, if you don't have the ability to modify

6789
08:47:13.958 --> 08:47:19.080
a Python module, but it has a function you know you need, then the bash operator

6790
08:47:19.080 --> 08:47:24.266
won't be of much use. In this case, it is much more appropriate to use the Python

6791
08:47:24.266 --> 08:47:29.515
operator. The Python operator's Python callable argument accepts anything that can

6792
08:47:29.515 --> 08:47:34.701
be called, like functions, but also classes. Pay special attention that you don't

6793
08:47:34.701 --> 08:47:37.070
actually call your callable here yet.

6794
08:47:37.070 --> 08:47:41.861
You should pass a reference. The operator will then call it for you when the task

6795
08:47:41.861 --> 08:47:46.712
gets triggered. If this callable needs extra arguments, you can pass them with the

6796
08:47:46.712 --> 08:47:51.621
optional keyword arguments, opargs and opquarks. In chapter 2, we created pipelines

