08:57:36.983 --> 08:57:43.537
What are these services? I already mentioned IAM. S3 or Simple Storage Service lets

6917
08:57:43.537 --> 08:57:50.091
us store files in the cloud. SNS or Simple Notification Service lets us send emails

6918
08:57:50.091 --> 08:57:52.460
and texts to alert subscribers

6919
08:57:52.460 --> 08:57:59.203
based on events and conditions in our data pipelines. Comprehend performs sentiment

6920
08:57:59.203 --> 08:58:06.026
analysis on blocks of text, and Recognition extracts texts from images and looks for

6921
08:58:06.026 --> 08:58:12.444
cats in a picture. RDS, EC2, and Lambda are other common services, but we won't

6922
08:58:12.444 --> 08:58:19.024
cover them in this class. Lastly, let's meet Sam. She's an analyst at the City of

6923
08:58:19.024 --> 08:58:21.380
San Diego. She analyzes data,

6924
08:58:21.380 --> 08:58:26.640
and builds dashboards to improve city operations. Sam works with a variety of

6925
08:58:26.640 --> 08:58:32.378
datasets, but much of her work is centered around Get It Done. The city of San Diego

6926
08:58:32.378 --> 08:58:38.048
launched Get It Done to allow residents to report problems like potholes and broken

6927
08:58:38.048 --> 08:58:43.445
sidewalks. We will be helping Sam harness the cloud to improve the lives of San

6928
08:58:43.445 --> 08:58:48.910
Diego residents using Get It Done and other datasets. In this lesson, we learned

6929
08:58:48.910 --> 08:58:55.574
That Amazon Web Services are like home utilities. Decoupled and granular, they work

6930
08:58:55.574 --> 08:59:01.997
well together or on their own. We learned about key services we will be using in

6931
08:59:01.997 --> 08:59:08.019
this class. We learned how to create an IAM user and get a key slash secret

6932
08:59:08.019 --> 08:59:14.522
combination. Lastly, we learned to initialize an S3 client and list buckets using

6933
08:59:14.522 --> 08:59:16.770
our AWS key and secret. Now,

6934
08:59:16.770 --> 08:59:23.406
Let's help Sam start building some things. In the last lesson, we learned about

6935
08:59:23.406 --> 08:59:30.125
different AWS services and how to create our first Bato3 client. Now, let's dive

6936
08:59:30.125 --> 08:59:36.845
into cloud storage with AWS S3. S3 lets us put any file in the cloud and make it

6937
08:59:36.845 --> 08:59:43.648
accessible anywhere in the world through our URL. Managing cloud storage is a key

6938
08:59:43.648 --> 08:59:46.000
component of data pipelines.

6939
08:59:46.000 --> 08:59:51.887
Many services we will learn will depend on an object being uploaded to S3. The main

6940
08:59:51.887 --> 08:59:57.703
components of S3 are buckets and objects. Buckets are like folders on our desktop.

6941
08:59:57.703 --> 09:00:03.236
Objects are like files within those folders. But there's a lot of power hidden

6942
09:00:03.236 --> 09:00:09.052
underneath. Buckets have their own permissions policies. They can be configured to

6943
09:00:09.052 --> 09:00:15.010
act as folders for a static website. They can generate logs about their own activity

6944
09:00:15.010 --> 09:00:21.070
and write them to a different bucket. The most important thing that buckets do? They

6945
09:00:21.070 --> 09:00:26.914
contain objects. An object can be anything. An image, a video file, CSV, or a log

6946
09:00:26.914 --> 09:00:32.613
file. There are plenty of operations we can do with objects. But for now, let's

6947
09:00:32.613 --> 09:00:38.457
focus on what we can do with buckets. What can we do with buckets using Bato3? We

6948
09:00:38.457 --> 09:00:42.930
can create a bucket, list buckets that we have in our account,

6949
09:00:42.930 --> 09:00:49.328
And we can delete a bucket. We can only store objects in buckets, so knowing how to

6950
09:00:49.328 --> 09:00:55.340
work with buckets is a crucial component of S3 knowledge. Let's dive into some

6951
09:00:55.340 --> 09:01:01.814
buckets. Let's start off by making a new bucket called GIDRequest. We create a Bato3

6952
09:01:01.814 --> 09:01:07.981
client that lets us interact with AWS S3. Then we call the client's createBucket

6953
09:01:07.981 --> 09:01:12.220
method, passing the bucket name as the argument. Ta-da!

6954
09:01:12.220 --> 09:01:18.530
We have a shiny new bucket. We can see it in the console as well. Keep in mind that

6955
09:01:18.530 --> 09:01:24.611
bucket names have to be unique across all of S3. Otherwise, we will get an error

6956
09:01:24.611 --> 09:01:30.921
when trying to create one. Now that we can create a bucket, let's get a list of all

6957
09:01:30.921 --> 09:01:37.307
the buckets we have in S3. Once again, we create a Bato3 S3 client, then we call the

6958
09:01:37.307 --> 09:01:41.260
list buckets method on the client. When S3 responds,

6959
09:01:41.260 --> 09:01:46.693
It will give us some additional response metadata, but it will include a dictionary

6960
09:01:46.693 --> 09:01:51.996
under the buckets key. Let's get that dictionary and print it out. We can see our

6961
09:01:51.996 --> 09:01:57.429
new bucket name and the time that it was created. Now that we have this dictionary,

6962
09:01:57.429 --> 09:02:02.928
we can run it through a for loop and perform an operation on multiple buckets. Let's

6963
09:02:02.928 --> 09:02:08.231
say we don't need the GADRequests bucket anymore. Let's delete it. Once again, we

6964
09:02:08.231 --> 09:02:10.260
create the BATO3S3 client, then

6965
09:02:10.260 --> 09:02:15.560
We call the delete bucket method. Alas, our bucket is gone. If we tried to delete it

6966
09:02:15.560 --> 09:02:20.797
and it didn't exist, we would have gotten an error. It's nowhere to be found in the

6967
09:02:20.797 --> 09:02:25.907
console either. We will learn more operations on buckets as we get further in the

6968
09:02:25.907 --> 09:02:31.207
course, but we won't learn them all. Get in the habit of reading Bato3 documentation

6969
09:02:31.207 --> 09:02:36.381
for all the methods we can do on an Amazon web service. In this lesson, we learned

6970
09:02:36.381 --> 09:02:39.220
about how to work with a key component of S3.

6971
09:02:39.220 --> 09:02:45.331
We learned that buckets contain objects, how to create buckets, how to list buckets,

6972
09:02:45.331 --> 09:02:51.225
and how to delete buckets. We also learned that there are more operations that we

6973
09:02:51.225 --> 09:02:57.263
can read about in the Bato3 docs. Now we're ready to dive into buckets. In the last

6974
09:02:57.263 --> 09:03:03.084
lesson, we learned how to list, create, and delete buckets. Now it's time to put

6975
09:03:03.084 --> 09:03:06.940
stuff in them. Let's take a look at how objects work.

6976
09:03:06.940 --> 09:03:13.991
The files in S3 buckets are called objects. An object can be anything. An image, a

6977
09:03:13.991 --> 09:03:20.870
video file, CSV, or a log file. Managing objects is a key component of many data

6978
09:03:20.870 --> 09:03:27.749
pipelines. Objects and buckets in S3 work somewhat like files and folders on our

6979
09:03:27.749 --> 09:03:34.628
desktop. Each bucket has a name. Objects names are called keys. A bucket name is

6980
09:03:34.628 --> 09:03:35.660
just a name.

6981
09:03:35.660 --> 09:03:42.309
An object's key is the full path of the object from the bucket's root. A bucket's

6982
09:03:42.309 --> 09:03:48.712
name is unique in all of S3. An object's key is unique in the bucket. A bucket

6983
09:03:48.712 --> 09:03:55.607
contains many objects, but an object can only belong to one bucket. First, we create

6984
09:03:55.607 --> 09:04:02.338
the client and assign it to the S3 variable. Now, we can perform operations on our

6985
09:04:02.338 --> 09:04:03.980
objects and buckets.

6986
09:04:03.980 --> 09:04:09.087
Let's upload an object into a bucket. We upload the file using the client's

6987
09:04:09.087 --> 09:04:14.739
uploadFile method. The file name is the local file path. Bucket parameter takes the

6988
09:04:14.739 --> 09:04:20.255
name of the bucket we are uploading to. Key is what we want to name the object in

6989
09:04:20.255 --> 09:04:25.566
S3. We are not capturing the return from this method in a variable. The method

6990
09:04:25.566 --> 09:04:31.082
doesn't return anything. If there's an error, it will throw an exception. Woohoo!

6991
09:04:31.082 --> 09:04:32.580
Our file is now in S3.

6992
09:04:32.580 --> 09:04:38.279
I've uploaded a few more objects for us to play with. Let's list them with Bato3.

6993
09:04:38.279 --> 09:04:43.556
Call the clients list objects method, passing GID requests for bucket name.

6994
09:04:43.556 --> 09:04:49.395
Optionally, we can limit the response to two objects with the max keys argument. If

6995
09:04:49.395 --> 09:04:55.024
we omit it, S3 will return up to a thousand objects in our bucket if they exist.

6996
09:04:55.024 --> 09:05:00.160
Another way to limit the response is to use the optional prefix argument.

6997
09:05:00.160 --> 09:05:05.998
Passing it will limit the response to objects that start with the string we provide.

6998
09:05:05.998 --> 09:05:11.418
The response dictionary contains the contents key. This key contains a list of

6999
09:05:11.418 --> 09:05:17.047
objects and their info. Each object dictionary is returned with a key, a modified

7000
09:05:17.047 --> 09:05:22.815
date, and the object size and bytes. If we want to know these things about a single

7001
09:05:22.815 --> 09:05:28.306
object, we can use the client's head object method, passing the bucket name and

7002
09:05:28.306 --> 09:05:29.070
object key.

7003
09:05:29.070 --> 09:05:34.171
Notice that because we are only working with one object, there is no contents

7004
09:05:34.171 --> 09:05:39.735
dictionary. The metadata is directly in the response dictionary. To download a file,

7005
09:05:39.735 --> 09:05:45.299
we use the client's download file method. We pass the file name or the local path we

7006
09:05:45.299 --> 09:05:50.665
want the file to download to. Then we specify the bucket and key of the object we

7007
09:05:50.665 --> 09:05:56.030
want to download. Sometimes an object has outlived its usefulness and needs to be

7008
09:05:56.030 --> 09:05:56.560
deleted.

7009
09:05:56.560 --> 09:06:02.221
Use the client's delete object method, passing the bucket name and object key to

7010
09:06:02.221 --> 09:06:07.670
delete the object. In this lesson, we learn that buckets are like folders and

7011
09:06:07.670 --> 09:06:13.472
objects are like files within them. We learn to create the client before we can do

7012
09:06:13.472 --> 09:06:19.204
anything else. We learn how to upload files to a bucket, how to list objects in a

7013
09:06:19.204 --> 09:06:24.865
bucket, how to head object or get object metadata, how to download a file from a

7014
09:06:24.865 --> 09:06:25.360
bucket,

7015
09:06:25.360 --> 09:06:30.804
And finally, how to delete an object. Let's help Sam continue working on her

7016
09:06:30.804 --> 09:06:36.678
pipeline. In the previous chapter, we learned how to create and delete buckets and

7017
09:06:36.678 --> 09:06:42.695
upload objects inside of them. Often, we work with private data or data we want only

7018
09:06:42.695 --> 09:06:48.712
certain users to see. This is where the AWS permission system comes in. AWS defaults

7019
09:06:48.712 --> 09:06:53.440
to denying permission. That means if we upload a file, by default,

7020
09:06:53.440 --> 09:06:59.042
only our key and secret have access to it. This behavior may sound annoying, but

7021
09:06:59.042 --> 09:07:04.784
it's much more secure to opt in to allowing public access to our data than to have

7022
09:07:04.784 --> 09:07:10.176
it be public by default. If we or our colleague tries to download a file with

7023
09:07:10.176 --> 09:07:15.988
pandas, S3 won't let them, even if it's in the same script that uploaded. But if we

7024
09:07:15.988 --> 09:07:21.520
initialize the S3 client with our credentials, it will work. Let's learn how to

7025
09:07:21.520 --> 09:07:22.780
protect and expose

7026
09:07:22.780 --> 09:07:29.912
There are four ways we can control permissions in S3. We can use IAM to control

7027
09:07:29.912 --> 09:07:37.495
users access to AWS services, buckets, and objects. We used it in lesson one to give

7028
09:07:37.495 --> 09:07:44.266
permissions by attaching IAM policies to a user. IAM applies across all AWS

7029
09:07:44.266 --> 09:07:51.669
services. Bucket policies give us control on the bucket and the objects within it.

7030
09:07:51.669 --> 09:07:52.030
ACLs

7031
09:07:52.030 --> 09:07:58.896
or access control lists let us set permissions on specific objects within a bucket.

7032
09:07:58.896 --> 09:08:05.845
Lastly, pre-signed URLs let us provide temporary access to an object. IAM and bucket

7033
09:08:05.845 --> 09:08:12.711
policies are great in multi-user environments, but since SAM isn't managing one, we

7034
09:08:12.711 --> 09:08:19.660
will focus on ACLs and pre-signed URLs. ACLs are entities attached to objects in S3.

7035
09:08:19.660 --> 09:08:26.730
We will focus on two types of ACL, private and public read. Say we upload a file. By

7036
09:08:26.730 --> 09:08:33.548
default, its ACL is private. Let's set the ACL to public read using S3 put object

7037
09:08:33.548 --> 09:08:40.618
ACL method. Now, anyone in the world can download this file. We can also set the ACL

7038
09:08:40.618 --> 09:08:47.520
as public read on upload. We pass a dictionary with key ACL and value public read.

7039
09:08:47.520 --> 09:08:55.620
Once we have a public object, anyone in the world can access it at the URL within

7040
09:08:55.620 --> 09:09:04.020
the format of bucket.s3.amazonaws.com slash object key. A URL for an object with the

7041
09:09:04.020 --> 09:09:12.320
key 2019 slash potholes dot CSV and bucket GAD requests will look like this. We can

7042
09:09:12.320 --> 09:09:16.220
use Python's nifty string format method

7043
09:09:16.220 --> 09:09:22.047
to generate a public URL for an object in S3. This is different than a pre-signed

7044
09:09:22.047 --> 09:09:27.801
URL, which we will cover in the next lesson. We create a string where the bucket

7045
09:09:27.801 --> 09:09:33.412
name and the object key are empty brackets. We call the format method, passing

7046
09:09:33.412 --> 09:09:39.310
positional arguments of bucket, GID request, and key, 2019 slash potholes dot CSV.

7047
09:09:39.310 --> 09:09:44.130
We can now pass this URL to something like pandas with no problems.

7048
09:09:44.130 --> 09:09:50.054
So how does this all work together? When a request comes in, if it's a pre-signed

7049
09:09:50.054 --> 09:09:56.125
URL, it will allow the download. If it's not pre-signed, it will check the policies

7050
09:09:56.125 --> 09:10:02.122
to make sure they allow the download. AWS's default behavior is to deny access. In

7051
09:10:02.122 --> 09:10:08.266
this lesson, we learned about AWS permission system. IAM answers, what can this user

7052
09:10:08.266 --> 09:10:13.020
do in AWS? Bucket policies answer, who can access this S3 bucket?

7053
09:10:13.020 --> 09:10:20.508
ACLs answer who can access this object and pre-signed URLs let us grant temporary

7054
09:10:20.508 --> 09:10:28.180
access to objects. We can set ACLs to public read or private on existing objects or

7055
09:10:28.180 --> 09:10:35.852
we can upload with a specified ACL using extra args. Lastly, we can generate public

7056
09:10:35.852 --> 09:10:39.550
URLs for objects with a public read ACL.

7057
09:10:39.550 --> 09:10:45.406
Now that we have a grasp of basic AWS permissions, in the last lesson, we learned

7058
09:10:45.406 --> 09:10:51.190
how to set permissions on objects in buckets, making them private and public. We

7059
09:10:51.190 --> 09:10:56.901
also learned how to access public files. But what happens when we want to share

7060
09:10:56.901 --> 09:11:02.685
private data? Data engineers are always balancing security, sharing, and access.

7061
09:11:02.685 --> 09:11:07.890
Let's dig in. If we did not explicitly set a public read ACL for a file,

7062
09:11:07.890 --> 09:11:14.161
AWS defaults to private. If we try to read it with pandas read CSV through a public

7063
09:11:14.161 --> 09:11:20.432
object URL, we will get a 403 error, which means we are forbidden. If we don't make

7064
09:11:20.432 --> 09:11:26.628
it public to the world, what do we do? We can use the Bato3's download file method

7065
09:11:26.628 --> 09:11:32.899
to download the file and have pandas read the CSV from disk. This is a great option

7066
09:11:32.899 --> 09:11:36.450
if we expect this file not to change much. Yet,

7067
09:11:36.450 --> 09:11:42.922
There's a much simpler way by using Bato3's getObject method. To access the file's

7068
09:11:42.922 --> 09:11:49.394
contents from S3, we call the getObject method with the bucket name and object key

7069
09:11:49.394 --> 09:11:55.630
as parameters. In response, we receive very similar information we get from the

7070
09:11:55.630 --> 09:12:02.260
headObject method in Chapter 1, metadata about the file. We also get a body key with

7071
09:12:02.260 --> 09:12:05.180
a streaming body object as the value.

7072
09:12:05.180 --> 09:12:10.787
A streaming body is a special type of response that doesn't download the whole

7073
09:12:10.787 --> 09:12:16.609
object immediately. Pandas knows how to handle this type of response. We pass the

7074
09:12:16.609 --> 09:12:22.288
contents of this key to Pandas and it will read it like a CSV file. We can also

7075
09:12:22.288 --> 09:12:28.182
grant access to S3 private objects temporarily by using pre-signed URLs. These are

7076
09:12:28.182 --> 09:12:33.070
special URLs that expire within a given time period. In this lesson,

7077
09:12:33.070 --> 09:12:39.338
We will use it for accessing private files. However, we can use this mechanism to

7078
09:12:39.338 --> 09:12:45.451
give access to a myriad of S3 operations. Let's upload a file. By default, it's

7079
09:12:45.451 --> 09:12:51.952
private. We generate a pre-signed URL that grants access to the file for one hour or

7080
09:12:51.952 --> 09:12:58.452
3600 seconds. This way, our colleagues can open it in Pandas or a browser. After one

7081
09:12:58.452 --> 09:13:01.470
hour passes, the access expires. Often,

7082
09:13:01.470 --> 09:13:07.374
Data engineers have to parse multiple files on S3 that follow a pattern, load them

7083
09:13:07.374 --> 09:13:13.349
into one data frame, then do something with it. This is quite simple to do with the

7084
09:13:13.349 --> 09:13:19.037
tools we already learned. First, let's create a list to hold the data frames of

7085
09:13:19.037 --> 09:13:25.084
every file we will read from S3. Then, let's get the list of CSVs from S3 that start

7086
09:13:25.084 --> 09:13:29.620
with 2019 slash. Let's assign the contents of the contents key,

7087
09:13:29.620 --> 09:13:35.732
from the response dictionary to request files variable. We iterate over the request

7088
09:13:35.732 --> 09:13:41.918
files variable, loading each object. We read object body into a data frame. Then, we

7089
09:13:41.918 --> 09:13:48.031
append the data frame to the list of data frames we are collecting. After the loop,

7090
09:13:48.031 --> 09:13:54.143
we use panda's concat method to combine all the data frames in the list. We can now

7091
09:13:54.143 --> 09:13:58.930
see our combined data frame with the head method. In this lesson,

7092
09:13:58.930 --> 09:14:05.335
We discussed how to open and share private files in S3. We can simply download the

7093
09:14:05.335 --> 09:14:11.271
file and read the local file into Pandas. We can use get object and read the

7094
09:14:11.271 --> 09:14:17.832
responses body key into Pandas. Lastly, we can generate a short-lived pre-signed URL

7095
09:14:17.832 --> 09:14:24.237
and pass it to Pandas. We now also have two ways to share a file. Public files are

7096
09:14:24.237 --> 09:14:26.580
shared through the public URL.

7097
09:14:26.580 --> 09:14:32.749
That's generated using the strings format method. We can also share private files

7098
09:14:32.749 --> 09:14:39.070
temporarily using pre-signed URLs. We get these from running Bato3's get pre-signed

7099
09:14:39.070 --> 09:14:44.935
URL method. Now that we know how to open and share private files, in the last

7100
09:14:44.935 --> 09:14:51.256
lessons, we learned how to access public and private files in S3 for use in our own

7101
09:14:51.256 --> 09:14:55.140
scripts and for sharing with others in this lesson.

7102
09:14:55.140 --> 09:15:01.020
We will cover how to share files through a website. S3 is able to serve as HTML

7103
09:15:01.020 --> 09:15:06.677
pages. This becomes useful when we want to share results of an analysis with

7104
09:15:06.677 --> 09:15:12.856
management and update the results via a pipeline. But wait, don't run away. This is

7105
09:15:12.856 --> 09:15:19.034
not going to turn into a web dev boot camp. Many Python data science tools have the

7106
09:15:19.034 --> 09:15:22.830
ability to generate simple HTML from data. The goal

7107
09:15:22.830 --> 09:15:28.124
is not to have us making beautiful websites, but to share our analyses with the

7108
09:15:28.124 --> 09:15:30.000
world. You are familiar with

7109
09:15:30.933 --> 09:15:37.831
the pandas to CSV method. Pandas has another method to HTML. We can use it to write

7110
09:15:37.831 --> 09:15:44.812
a pandas data frame to an HTML file. The optional render links argument converts any

7111
09:15:44.812 --> 09:15:49.300
URLs to be clickable. We can use the columns parameter

7112
09:15:49.300 --> 09:15:54.918
to pass a list of only columns we want rendered. In this example, we are only

7113
09:15:54.918 --> 09:16:00.973
showing service name and info link columns. We also have some basic border controls

7114
09:16:00.973 --> 09:16:06.810
via the border argument. Zero value will remove the border, while a value of one

7115
09:16:06.810 --> 09:16:12.939
will show it. There are a few more configuration arguments, but you can look through

7116
09:16:12.939 --> 09:16:17.900
them on the documentation for pandas to HTML. Uploading an HTML file

7117
09:16:17.900 --> 09:16:24.500
is like uploading any other file, but we need to set the content type so that the

7118
09:16:24.500 --> 09:16:31.345
browser knows to treat it as HTML. We use the upload file method to upload it to S3.

7119
09:16:31.345 --> 09:16:37.864
We specify the local file name and bucket. We will upload this file with the key

7120
09:16:37.864 --> 09:16:44.627
table.html. In the extra args dictionary, we specify the content type as text slash

7121
09:16:44.627 --> 09:16:46.990
HTML. Finally, we set the ACL

7122
09:16:46.990 --> 09:16:53.502
to public read. We can use the same pattern we learned in lesson one for accessing

7123
09:16:53.502 --> 09:16:59.855
public files to access table.html. Because we set the content type as text slash

7124
09:16:59.855 --> 09:17:06.208
HTML, the browser knows to show it as HTML. And here's our table. HTML files are

7125
09:17:06.208 --> 09:17:12.641
treated just like regular files. We can also make this page private and provide a

7126
09:17:12.641 --> 09:17:15.500
pre-signed URL for temporary access.

7127
09:17:15.500 --> 09:17:21.770
Uploading images, such as the ones generated from Python's charting libraries, is

7128
09:17:21.770 --> 09:17:28.194
very similar to uploading HTML files. But if we want the browser to render them, we

7129
09:17:28.194 --> 09:17:34.310
need to set the proper content type during upload. In the case of a PNG, we use

7130
09:17:34.310 --> 09:17:40.425
image slash PNG as the content type. IANA has a list of all file types based on

7131
09:17:40.425 --> 09:17:44.140
their file extension. Some common ones are JSON,

7132
09:17:44.140 --> 09:17:50.777
PNG, PDF, and CSV. You can find more on IANA's website below. So what if we have

7133
09:17:50.777 --> 09:17:57.164
multiple HTML and image files that we want to share and we want to generate a

7134
09:17:57.164 --> 09:18:03.884
listing for them? Well, we can do that with the tools we already learned. We list

7135
09:18:03.884 --> 09:18:10.687
the objects in the bucket using ClientListObject. We convert the response contents

7136
09:18:10.687 --> 09:18:12.180
into a data frame.

7137
09:18:12.180 --> 09:18:18.729
We create a column link where we create the URL for every key in the object. Then we

7138
09:18:18.729 --> 09:18:25.044
write the data frame to HTML. We upload the index page we generated like we would

7139
09:18:25.044 --> 09:18:30.970
any other HTML file. It's now available at the public URL. We learned how to

7140
09:18:30.970 --> 09:18:37.441
generate an HTML table in Pandas, how to upload the HTML file to S3. We learned how

7141
09:18:37.441 --> 09:18:39.780
to upload an image file to S3.

7142
09:18:39.780 --> 09:18:46.085
We learn how to share the URL for our HTML page. Now that we know how to share files

7143
09:18:46.085 --> 09:18:52.166
with the world through an S3 bucket website, in this chapter, we've learned about

7144
09:18:52.166 --> 09:18:58.471
the ways S3 can host and share files publicly and privately. In this lesson, we will

7145
09:18:58.471 --> 09:19:03.200
walk through everything we learned to see how it fits together.

7146
09:19:03.200 --> 09:19:08.890
Every month, SAM is asked to compile a report on the number of requests received and

7147
09:19:08.890 --> 09:19:14.512
get it done per case type. When council asks, she emails them the report. They want

7148
09:19:14.512 --> 09:19:20.202
to see how many requests came in, which types of requests they were, and how many of

7149
09:19:20.202 --> 09:19:25.892
each type there were total. The council members should be able to access a page, see

7150
09:19:25.892 --> 09:19:31.040
the listing of all available and generated charts, click on one, and see it.

7151
09:19:31.040 --> 09:19:38.472
In order to accomplish this, we will download files for the month from the raw data

7152
09:19:38.472 --> 09:19:45.724
bucket, concatenate them into one CSV, create an aggregated data frame, write the

7153
09:19:45.724 --> 09:19:53.156
data frame to CSV and HTML, generate a bokeh plot, save as HTML, create GID reports

7154
09:19:53.156 --> 09:20:00.140
bucket, configure for website, upload all the three files for the month to S3,

7155
09:20:00.140 --> 09:20:06.924
Generate an index.html file that lists all the files. Get the website URL. We will

7156
09:20:06.924 --> 09:20:13.460
be picking up raw data from the GID requests bucket. This bucket contains daily

7157
09:20:13.460 --> 09:20:20.409
CSVs. Every CSV has rows for every request that was made in the Get It Done app that

7158
09:20:20.409 --> 09:20:27.358
day. First, let's create a list to hold our data frames. Then, let's get the listing

7159
09:20:27.358 --> 09:20:28.930
of all files in S3.

7160
09:20:28.930 --> 09:20:34.972
that start with 2019 underscore Jan. We can use the prefix argument of the list

7161
09:20:34.972 --> 09:20:41.243
objects method to filter. Don't forget that our actual dictionary of objects is in

7162
09:20:41.243 --> 09:20:47.590
the response content. Next, we will iterate over each object and load its streaming

7163
09:20:47.590 --> 09:20:53.708
body using get object. We can't use the object URL because it's private. We will

7164
09:20:53.708 --> 09:20:58.450
then pass that body to pandas read CSV, creating a data frame.

7165
09:20:58.450 --> 09:21:04.807
We append that data frame to a list, so we will have a list of 31 data frames, one

7166
09:21:04.807 --> 09:21:11.242
for each day in the dflist variable. We concatenate all the data frames in the list

7167
09:21:11.242 --> 09:21:17.366
into one using pdconcat. Then we will do a basic aggregation. We will write the

7168
09:21:17.366 --> 09:21:23.646
resulting data frame to a CSV and an HTML file. We will also make a boke plot and

7169
09:21:23.646 --> 09:21:27.600
write it to HTML. We will place aggregated reports,

7170
09:21:27.600 --> 09:21:35.613
In the GID reports bucket. We upload the aggregated CSV to S3 with the key 2019

7171
09:21:35.613 --> 09:21:44.134
slash Jan slash final report dot CSV. We also set the public read ACL. We upload the

7172
09:21:44.134 --> 09:21:52.553
table HTML next. We use the content type text slash HTML to tell S3 that this is an

7173
09:21:52.553 --> 09:21:54.480
HTML file. Finally,

7174
09:21:54.480 --> 09:22:00.821
We upload the chart.html as well. This is now what we have in our S3 console for

7175
09:22:00.821 --> 09:22:07.479
January. Next, we want to generate an index.html file that lets Sam's manager browse

7176
09:22:07.479 --> 09:22:13.820
the website and click on what she wants to see. We list the GADReport bucket for

7177
09:22:13.820 --> 09:22:20.082
objects starting with 2019 slash. Then, we convert the response contents into a

7178
09:22:20.082 --> 09:22:23.490
dataframe since it's a dictionary. Finally,

7179
09:22:23.490 --> 09:22:30.906
We add a column link that combines key with the base S3 website URL. Then we write

7180
09:22:30.906 --> 09:22:38.231
our data frame to HTML using pandas, selecting only link, last modified, and size

7181
09:22:38.231 --> 09:22:45.557
columns. Note that we use the argument render links equals true so that our links

7182
09:22:45.557 --> 09:22:50.350
are clickable in HTML. We upload the index.html file.

7183
09:22:50.350 --> 09:22:56.105
to the root of the GAD Reports bucket. Finally, let's get the website URL containing

7184
09:22:56.105 --> 09:23:01.587
bucket name and share it with the council. Sam has built a pretty cool reporting

7185
09:23:01.587 --> 09:23:07.274
system. Now that we've had a general overview, let's see. In the last two chapters,

7186
09:23:07.274 --> 09:23:13.029
we learned about cloud storage with S3 and how to share and not share files with the

7187
09:23:13.029 --> 09:23:17.620
world. But what if we send a text with a link to the latest report?

7188
09:23:17.620 --> 09:23:23.110
or send an email if certain criteria are met in the data. Alerting humans and

7189
09:23:23.110 --> 09:23:29.027
machines to take action is a key component in data engineering. That's where Amazon

7190
09:23:29.027 --> 09:23:34.731
SNS comes in. Amazon SNS stands for Simple Notification Service. With it, we can

7191
09:23:34.731 --> 09:23:40.364
send emails, text messages, and push notifications. Publishers post messages to

7192
09:23:40.364 --> 09:23:46.210
topics, and subscribers receive them. To fully understand SNS, let's talk about...

7193
09:23:46.210 --> 09:23:51.834
The Superbowl. I know, sports aren't my thing either. Just bear with me for the

7194
09:23:51.834 --> 09:23:57.528
analogy. Say the Superbowl is happening right now, and CBS is broadcasting. That

7195
09:23:57.528 --> 09:24:03.366
makes CBS a publisher. CBS is publishing to Channel 2, and others can subscribe to

7196
09:24:03.366 --> 09:24:09.203
it by selecting that channel. Channel 2 is like an SNS topic. The channel can have

7197
09:24:09.203 --> 09:24:15.040
phones, TVs, tablets, and other devices watching. They all get the same Superbowl.

7198
09:24:15.040 --> 09:24:21.254
In SNS, these are subscribers. In SNS, we publish messages to an SNS topic and

7199
09:24:21.254 --> 09:24:27.946
subscribers receive those messages via email or text. To access SNS, we open the AWS

7200
09:24:27.946 --> 09:24:34.559
Services tab, type in SNS, and click on the Simple Notification Service option that

7201
09:24:34.559 --> 09:24:41.012
pops up. Once we get to the dashboard, we can see topics and subscriptions on the

7202
09:24:41.012 --> 09:24:41.410
left.

7203
09:24:41.410 --> 09:24:46.849
Every topic has an ARN or an Amazon resource name. This is a unique ID for this

7204
09:24:46.849 --> 09:24:52.357
topic. On the bottom, we can see subscriptions. Each of those has a unique ID as

7205
09:24:52.357 --> 09:24:58.071
well. We'll explore those in the next lesson. To create an SNS topic, we initialize

7206
09:24:58.071 --> 09:25:03.579
the SNS client. This is the first time we are using a BATO3 client for something

7207
09:25:03.579 --> 09:25:09.155
other than S3. We do this by passing SNS instead of S3 as the first argument. Now

7208
09:25:09.155 --> 09:25:10.670
that we have a client,

7209
09:25:10.670 --> 09:25:16.601
We can create a topic by calling the create topic method. In return, we get an API

7210
09:25:16.601 --> 09:25:22.097
response from AWS. We grab the topic ARN key from the response to store in a

7211
09:25:22.097 --> 09:25:27.883
variable. Or we can do the whole thing with a shortcut, calling create topic and

7212
09:25:27.883 --> 09:25:33.669
grabbing topic ARN at the same time. We can see our new topic and its ARN on the

7213
09:25:33.669 --> 09:25:39.600
dashboard. Creating topics is idempotent. If we create a topic with the same name,

7214
09:25:39.600 --> 09:25:45.729
It will return the ARN of the topic that already exists, not an error. In the first

7215
09:25:45.729 --> 09:25:51.711
lesson, we used IAM to configure our AWS access key and ID to have full access to

7216
09:25:51.711 --> 09:25:57.840
SNS. This will allow us to create topics, make subscriptions, and publish messages.

7217
09:25:57.840 --> 09:26:03.452
We can list topics by calling the list topics method. We get a response that

7218
09:26:03.452 --> 09:26:08.400
contains a topics key with a list of topics our user has access to.

7219
09:26:08.400 --> 09:26:14.252
We can do this to perform an operation like delete on multiple topics. Sometimes, we

7220
09:26:14.252 --> 09:26:19.964
need to delete a topic that's outlived its usefulness. To delete a topic, pass its

7221
09:26:19.964 --> 09:26:25.746
topic ARN to the delete topic method. The topic is gone. Whew! This has been a full

7222
09:26:25.746 --> 09:26:31.109
lesson. First, we learned how SNS works. We publish messages to an SNS topic.

7223
09:26:31.109 --> 09:26:36.891
Subscribers to this topic will receive that message via email or text. To work with

7224
09:26:36.891 --> 09:26:37.170
SNS,

7225
09:26:37.170 --> 09:26:43.375
We have to create an SNS client. And now we can create a shiny new topic. To see all

7226
09:26:43.375 --> 09:26:49.285
the created topics, we can use list topics. And to delete a topic, we use delete

7227
09:26:49.285 --> 09:26:55.417
topic. Now that we have the power of topics, in the last lesson, we explored how to

7228
09:26:55.417 --> 09:27:01.474
create, list, and delete SNS topics. Now let's explore how to manage subscriptions

7229
09:27:01.474 --> 09:27:06.350
to those topics. This is how we choose who gets the notifications.

7230
09:27:06.350 --> 09:27:11.895
and how they get them. Let's dive in. In the last lesson, we looked at the topic

7231
09:27:11.895 --> 09:27:17.302
page and the subscription listing below. Every subscription has a unique ID, a

7232
09:27:17.302 --> 09:27:22.917
protocol, and a status. There are several protocols in SNS, but we will only work

7233
09:27:22.917 --> 09:27:28.601
with email and SMS, or text messages. The endpoint is the specific phone number or

7234
09:27:28.601 --> 09:27:34.146
email address where the message should be sent. Lastly, the status can either be

7235
09:27:34.146 --> 09:27:34.770
confirmed

7236
09:27:34.770 --> 09:27:41.594
or pending confirmation. Phone numbers are automatically confirmed, but for email,

7237
09:27:41.594 --> 09:27:48.502
the user has to click a unique link to authorize the subscription. To create an SMS

7238
09:27:48.502 --> 09:27:54.993
subscription, we initialize the BATO3 SNS client. Then, we call SNS subscribe,

7239
09:27:54.993 --> 09:28:01.734
passing the topic ARN of the city alerts topic we created in the last lesson. The

7240
09:28:01.734 --> 09:28:04.480
protocol is SMS, and the endpoint

7241
09:28:04.480 --> 09:28:10.555
is a phone number. In our response, we get a dictionary that contains a subscription

7242
09:28:10.555 --> 09:28:16.631
ARN key. If we want this ARN for later use, we can grab it from the dictionary using

7243
09:28:16.631 --> 09:28:22.417
that key. Creating an email subscription is very similar. We call SNS subscribe,

7244
09:28:22.417 --> 09:28:28.059
passing email as the protocol, and an email address as an endpoint. This time,

7245
09:28:28.059 --> 09:28:33.700
however, we see pending confirmation instead of a subscription ARN. Meanwhile,

7246
09:28:33.700 --> 09:28:39.296
The user has received an email with a link to click. Once the subscriber confirms,

7247
09:28:39.296 --> 09:28:44.892
he will be able to receive emails and the address will show up as confirmed in the

7248
09:28:44.892 --> 09:28:50.487
subscription list. Let's list the subscriptions we made for the City Alerts topic.

7249
09:28:50.487 --> 09:28:55.878
Listing is essential for bulk operations, like unsubscribing users. To list the

7250
09:28:55.878 --> 09:29:01.542
subscriptions for a topic ARN, we call the SNS client's List Subscriptions by Topic

7251
09:29:01.542 --> 09:29:02.020
method.

7252
09:29:02.020 --> 09:29:07.626
And we pass in the topic ARN. Our response will contain a subscriptions key with a

7253
09:29:07.626 --> 09:29:13.369
list of subscription dictionaries. These will include the ARN of the subscription or

7254
09:29:13.369 --> 09:29:18.633
pending confirmation if the subscription has not been confirmed. It will also

7255
09:29:18.633 --> 09:29:23.692
include the protocol, email or SMS, and the endpoint. We can also list all

7256
09:29:23.692 --> 09:29:29.366
subscriptions. This will give us back the same response, but for all topics instead

7257
09:29:29.366 --> 09:29:31.280
of just one that we specify.

7258
09:29:31.280 --> 09:29:37.271
Lastly, to delete a subscription, we simply call the SNS unsubscribe method. What if

7259
09:29:37.271 --> 09:29:43.048
we choose to stop sending notifications via SMS? We delete all subscriptions that

7260
09:29:43.048 --> 09:29:48.967
use the SMS protocol. We list all subscriptions and grab the subscriptions key from

7261
09:29:48.967 --> 09:29:54.459
the response. For every subscription, if SMS, we call the unsubscribe method,

7262
09:29:54.459 --> 09:30:00.450
passing it the subscription ARN. In this lesson, we learned about SNS subscriptions.

7263
09:30:00.450 --> 09:30:06.268
A subscription is when someone listens for messages on a topic. During the request

7264
09:30:06.268 --> 09:30:11.872
to create a subscription, the protocol can be SMS or email. The endpoint is the

7265
09:30:11.872 --> 09:30:17.761
phone number or email address. Once the subscription is created, it's automatically

7266
09:30:17.761 --> 09:30:23.082
confirmed for SMS. For emails, it's pending confirmation until the receiver

7267
09:30:23.082 --> 09:30:28.190
verified. We learned to create a subscription with the subscribe method.

7268
09:30:28.190 --> 09:30:33.640
We learned how to list subscriptions by topic with the list subscriptions by topic

7269
09:30:33.640 --> 09:30:39.089
method. We have learned how to list all subscriptions unfiltered by topic. Lastly,

7270
09:30:39.089 --> 09:30:44.473
we learned how to unsubscribe a user. SNS subscriptions and topics are a powerful

7271
09:30:44.473 --> 09:30:50.055
way to manage who gets what notifications when. In the next lesson, we'll get to the

7272
09:30:50.055 --> 09:30:55.505
really cool stuff, sending messages to the people that need to receive them. Okay,

7273
09:30:55.505 --> 09:30:57.100
in the last two lessons,

7274
09:30:57.100 --> 09:31:02.925
We learned about creating topics and subscriptions. Now it's time to actually start

7275
09:31:02.925 --> 09:31:08.539
publishing. In other words, let's send some texts and emails. Ready? Game on. By

7276
09:31:08.539 --> 09:31:14.363
publishing a message to a topic, all of our subscribers will receive it. To publish

7277
09:31:14.363 --> 09:31:19.627
to a topic, call the publish method with topic ARN, message, and subject as

7278
09:31:19.627 --> 09:31:24.960
arguments. This will broadcast the message to every subscriber of the topic.

7279
09:31:24.960 --> 09:31:30.094
The email subscribers will receive this message in their inbox. The SMS subscribers

7280
09:31:30.094 --> 09:31:34.857
will receive it on their phone. The message argument contains the body of the

7281
09:31:34.857 --> 09:31:39.806
message. This goes into the email body and the SMS message. The subject argument

7282
09:31:39.806 --> 09:31:44.878
gets shown in the subject line of the email and is not visible for those receiving

7283
09:31:44.878 --> 09:31:49.888
text messages. We obviously want to send custom messages. In order to do this, we

7284
09:31:49.888 --> 09:31:52.610
can use Python's nifty string format method,

7285
09:31:52.610 --> 09:31:58.498
to replace text with a variable from our data. This lets us generate custom messages

7286
09:31:58.498 --> 09:32:04.386
as part of our code. Alternatively, we can send a single SMS message. If we use this

7287
09:32:04.386 --> 09:32:10.204
method, we don't have to worry about having a topic or subscribers to it. We simply

7288
09:32:10.204 --> 09:32:15.952
send a text message to a phone number. To send a single SMS, we also use the Bato3

7289
09:32:15.952 --> 09:32:21.769
SNS Clients Publish method. Since SMS messages don't have subjects, we pass a phone

7290
09:32:21.769 --> 09:32:22.190
number

7291
09:32:22.190 --> 09:32:27.724
and the message body. We don't pass a topic ARN because we have no topic to publish

7292
09:32:27.724 --> 09:32:33.191
to. The person on the other end of the line will get an SMS on her phone. This can

7293
09:32:33.191 --> 09:32:38.792
be good for one-off text messages, but quickly gets very unwieldy for data pipeline.

7294
09:32:38.792 --> 09:32:44.393
One of the tasks of a data engineer is to balance getting stuff done with being able

7295
09:32:44.393 --> 09:32:49.660
to maintain it. Sending one-off texts lives in the realm of getting stuff done.

7296
09:32:49.660 --> 09:32:54.106
Setting up topics and publishing to them lives in the realm of being able to

7297
09:32:54.106 --> 09:32:58.902
maintain it. There are two ways to send a message. We could publish a message to a

7298
09:32:58.902 --> 09:33:03.289
topic. This assumes we have created a topic and have subscriptions that are

7299
09:33:03.289 --> 09:33:08.086
listening to it. This is better when there are multiple subscribers and we need to

7300
09:33:08.086 --> 09:33:12.941
manage a list of them. Alternatively, we could send a one-off SMS. It's great if we

7301
09:33:12.941 --> 09:33:16.860
just need to send one message to a phone number, keeping it simple.

7302
09:33:16.860 --> 09:33:21.789
This is great if we don't have to manage a list, don't need to send via email, and

7303
09:33:21.789 --> 09:33:26.477
don't want to have a topic or subscriptions. In this lesson, we learned how to

7304
09:33:26.477 --> 09:33:31.225
actually publish our message. We learned the difference between publishing to a

7305
09:33:31.225 --> 09:33:35.853
topic so that all of our subscribers get it, as opposed to sending single SMS

7306
09:33:35.853 --> 09:33:40.842
messages. And we learned how to make custom messages. Now that we have the power to

7307
09:33:40.842 --> 09:33:45.710
alert, in the last three lessons, Sam has been prototyping topics, subscriptions,

7308
09:33:45.710 --> 09:33:51.594
and publishing notifications on SNS. Now it's time to put it all together. Let's get

7309
09:33:51.594 --> 09:33:57.408
started. Potholes and illegal dumping backlogs are growing out of control. The city

7310
09:33:57.408 --> 09:34:03.222
council asked Sam to help directors know when they're running behind on their work.

7311
09:34:03.222 --> 09:34:08.476
Sam is going to maintain a CSV of directors with their emails, numbers, and

7312
09:34:08.476 --> 09:34:13.449
department. She will use that list to create subscriptions to the trash

7313
09:34:13.449 --> 09:34:14.780
notifications topic

7314
09:34:14.780 --> 09:34:20.403
she creates. Then, she will check the final report CSV created in the previous

7315
09:34:20.403 --> 09:34:26.169
chapter. If a legal dumping case count exceeds 30, she will publish to the trash

7316
09:34:26.169 --> 09:34:32.225
notifications topic. All the subscribers from contacts.csv will receive it. She will

7317
09:34:32.225 --> 09:34:38.136
do the same for potholes with a different topic. Let's get started. First, we will

7318
09:34:38.136 --> 09:34:43.470
create topics and subscribe users to them based on the contact list. Next,

7319
09:34:43.470 --> 09:34:49.224
We will get the pothole count and illegal dumping count from the CSV that Sam

7320
09:34:49.224 --> 09:34:55.277
created in the previous chapter. Finally, if the pothole count exceeds 100 and if

7321
09:34:55.277 --> 09:35:01.180
illegal dumping exceeds 30, we will send out alerts to the people subscribed to

7322
09:35:01.180 --> 09:35:07.009
those topics. Let's get started. First, we initialize the Bato3 SNS client. We

7323
09:35:07.009 --> 09:35:10.820
create a topic for trash and streets notifications.

7324
09:35:10.820 --> 09:35:16.798
We store their ARNs in a variable for future use. When we rerun the script, we don't

7325
09:35:16.798 --> 09:35:20.000
have to worry about new topics being created.

7326
09:35:20.562 --> 09:35:26.908
with the same name since the create topic command is idempotent. Excellent! We have

7327
09:35:26.908 --> 09:35:33.102
two shiny new topics. Next, we need to subscribe users to the topics. We load the

7328
09:35:33.102 --> 09:35:37.460
contacts.csv into a dataframe. This file is stored on S3.

7329
09:35:37.460 --> 09:35:42.961
as we will likely update it often due to requests from directors. Let's subscribe

7330
09:35:42.961 --> 09:35:48.394
each user's email and phone to their respective department topics. We want bells

7331
09:35:48.394 --> 09:35:54.099
going off when there's a backlog. We are going to make a function SubscribeUser that

7332
09:35:54.099 --> 09:35:59.735
we will use for every row in the context data frame. If the department column value

7333
09:35:59.735 --> 09:36:05.236
for that row is trash, we will subscribe the user's phone number and email to the

7334
09:36:05.236 --> 09:36:07.070
trash topic ARN. Otherwise,

7335
09:36:07.070 --> 09:36:13.107
We will subscribe them to the streets topic ARN. We will use the topic ARN variable

7336
09:36:13.107 --> 09:36:18.998
from the previous create topic call. Woo! The users are subscribed. For the email

7337
09:36:18.998 --> 09:36:25.107
subscriptions to be confirmed, they still have to click the link in the confirmation

7338
09:36:25.107 --> 09:36:31.071
email. Now, we need to get last month's aggregated report. Because it's public, we

7339
09:36:31.071 --> 09:36:36.380
can simply use pdreadcsv to load the report into a data frame. The report

7340
09:36:36.380 --> 09:36:42.351
contains a count of requests for every service name. Let's pull out the aggregated

7341
09:36:42.351 --> 09:36:48.104
numbers. We set the index on service name for easier access. Then we use pandas

7342
09:36:48.104 --> 09:36:53.493
df.at to get the counts and store them in a variable. We compare the trash

7343
09:36:53.493 --> 09:36:59.464
violations count value to the threshold we have set. If the threshold is exceeded,

7344
09:36:59.464 --> 09:37:04.780
we make a message string with the current count of trash violations. Then

7345
09:37:04.780 --> 09:37:09.882
We publish this message to the trash notifications topic. We also include the

7346
09:37:09.882 --> 09:37:15.316
subject so that the email subscribers will see trash alert as their email subject.

7347
09:37:15.316 --> 09:37:20.815
We repeat with the streets violations which have a different threshold. We send the

7348
09:37:20.815 --> 09:37:26.249
message to the streets notifications topic if the threshold is exceeded. The final

7349
09:37:26.249 --> 09:37:31.483
result is a notification system that is able to analyze data and send alerts to

7350
09:37:31.483 --> 09:37:33.140
users that can act on it.

7351
09:37:33.140 --> 09:37:38.973
If Sam sets this job to run on a schedule, along with her scheduled reports, she can

7352
09:37:38.973 --> 09:37:44.042
trigger alerts at different thresholds for different people to act on the

7353
09:37:44.042 --> 09:37:49.527
information as it becomes available. Sam is becoming a data engineer. Combining

7354
09:37:49.527 --> 09:37:54.735
disparate pieces of data movement to serve a purpose and converting data to

7355
09:37:54.735 --> 09:38:00.429
actionable information is the core of data engineering. Let's see if we can expand

