09:44:36.040 --> 09:44:41.440
have already initialized the Bato3 Comprehend client. From the documentation, we

7434
09:44:41.440 --> 09:44:46.907
know that we can use the client's detect sentiment method to analyze the text. We

7435
09:44:46.907 --> 09:44:47.920
call the method

7436
09:44:47.920 --> 09:44:53.487
storing the API response in the response variable. The response will contain a

7437
09:44:53.487 --> 09:44:59.198
sentiment key and a dictionary with the sentiment score, which means, again, our

7438
09:44:59.198 --> 09:45:05.193
one-liner could access the sentiment key directly from the response. In this lesson,

7439
09:45:05.193 --> 09:45:10.475
we went on a tour of natural language processing in AWS. AWS Translate and

7440
09:45:10.475 --> 09:45:15.900
Comprehend make it really easy and smooth to perform basic NLP on free text,

7441
09:45:15.900 --> 09:45:20.858
allowing data engineers to enrich their data with sentiment and language

7442
09:45:20.858 --> 09:45:26.436
information. To perform a translation, we initialize the Batra 3 translate client

7443
09:45:26.436 --> 09:45:32.220
and call translate text. If we don't know the source language, that's okay. AWS will

7444
09:45:32.220 --> 09:45:38.005
figure that out for us if we pass auto for source language code. AWS figures out the

