7532
09:52:48.254 --> 09:52:53.555
work for different universities, or that a third-party company can have multiple

7533
09:52:53.555 --> 09:52:55.410
professors working for them.

7534
09:52:55.410 --> 09:53:00.950
In order to analyze the data, I needed to make sure its quality was good and stayed

7535
09:53:00.950 --> 09:53:06.490
good throughout the process. That's why I stored my data in a database, whose quite

7536
09:53:06.490 --> 09:53:11.896
complex design you can see in the right graphic. All these rectangles were turned

7537
09:53:11.896 --> 09:53:15.300
into database tables. But why did I use a database?

7538
09:53:15.300 --> 09:53:20.954
A database models real-life entities like professors and universities by storing

7539
09:53:20.954 --> 09:53:26.395
them in tables. Each table only contains data from a single entity type. This

7540
09:53:26.395 --> 09:53:32.190
reduces redundancy by storing entities only once. For example, there only needs to

7541
09:53:32.190 --> 09:53:37.985
be one row of data containing the details of a certain company. Lastly, a database

7542
09:53:37.985 --> 09:53:41.660
can be used to model relationships between entities.

7543
09:53:41.660 --> 09:53:47.202
You can define exactly how entities relate to each other. For instance, a professor

7544
09:53:47.202 --> 09:53:52.544
can work at multiple universities and companies, while a company can employ more

7545
09:53:52.544 --> 09:53:57.886
than one professor. Throughout this course, you will actually work with the same

7546
09:53:57.886 --> 09:54:03.227
real-life data used during my investigation. You'll start from a single table of

7547
09:54:03.227 --> 09:54:08.769
data and build a full-blown relational database from it, column by column, table by

7548
09:54:08.769 --> 09:54:09.170
table.

7549
09:54:09.170 --> 09:54:14.585
By doing so, you'll get to know constraints, keys, and referential integrity. These

7550
09:54:14.585 --> 09:54:19.870
three concepts help preserve data quality in databases. By the end of the course,

7551
09:54:19.870 --> 09:54:24.829
you'll know how to use them. In order to get going, you'll just need a basic

7552
09:54:24.829 --> 09:54:30.114
understanding of SQL, which can also be used to build and maintain databases, not

7553
09:54:30.114 --> 09:54:35.399
just for querying data. I've already created a single Postgres SQL database table

7554
09:54:35.399 --> 09:54:38.270
containing all the raw data for this course.

7555
09:54:38.270 --> 09:54:43.986
In the next few exercises, I want you to have a look at that table. For that, you'll

7556
09:54:43.986 --> 09:54:49.701
need to retrieve your SQL knowledge and query the Information Schema database, which

7557
09:54:49.701 --> 09:54:55.281
is available in PostgreSQL by default. Information Schema is actually some sort of

7558
09:54:55.281 --> 09:55:00.316
meta database that holds information about your current database. It's not

7559
09:55:00.316 --> 09:55:05.759
PostgreSQL specific and also available in other database management systems like

7560
09:55:05.759 --> 09:55:07.120
MySQL or SQL Server.

7561
09:55:07.120 --> 09:55:08.764
This information schema database holds various

7562
09:55:08.764 --> 09:55:14.607
information in different tables, for example in the tables table. Information schema

7563
09:55:14.607 --> 09:55:20.449
also holds information about columns in the columns table. Once you know the name of

7564
09:55:20.449 --> 09:55:25.040
a table, you can query its columns by accessing the columns table.

7565
09:55:25.040 --> 09:55:30.259
Here, for example, you see that the system table pgconfig has only two columns,

7566
09:55:30.259 --> 09:55:35.809
supposedly for storing name value pairs. Okay, let's have a look at your... Now that

7567
09:55:35.809 --> 09:55:41.358
you've had a first look at your database, let's delve into one of the most important

7568
09:55:41.358 --> 09:55:46.313
concepts behind databases, tables. You might have noticed that there's some

7569
09:55:46.313 --> 09:55:51.863
redundancy in the university professor's table. Let's have a look at the first three

7570
09:55:51.863 --> 09:55:53.250
records, for example.

7571
09:55:53.250 --> 09:55:59.017
As you can see, this professor is repeated in the first three records. Also, his

7572
09:55:59.017 --> 09:56:04.928
university, the ETH Lausanne, is repeated a couple of times, because he only works

7573
09:56:04.928 --> 09:56:06.370
for this university.

7574
09:56:06.370 --> 09:56:11.623
However, he seems to have affiliations with at least three different organizations.

7575
09:56:11.623 --> 09:56:16.938
So there's a certain redundancy in that table. The reason for this is that the table

7576
09:56:16.938 --> 09:56:22.128
actually contains entities of at least three different types. Let's have a look at

7577
09:56:22.128 --> 09:56:23.330
these entity types.

7578
09:56:23.330 --> 09:56:27.922
Actually, the table stores professors, highlighted in blue, universities,

7579
09:56:27.922 --> 09:56:32.955
highlighted in green, and organizations, highlighted in brown. There's also this

7580
09:56:32.955 --> 09:56:37.924
column called function, which denotes the role the professor plays at a certain

7581
09:56:37.924 --> 09:56:40.000
organization. More on that later.

7582
09:56:40.000 --> 09:56:45.491
Let's look at the current database once again. The graphic used here is called an

7583
09:56:45.491 --> 09:56:51.051
entity relationship diagram. Squares denotes so-called entity types, while circles

7584
09:56:51.051 --> 09:56:56.610
connected to these denote attributes, or columns. So far, we have only modeled one

7585
09:56:56.610 --> 09:57:02.305
so-called entity type, university professors. However, we discovered that this table

7586
09:57:02.305 --> 09:57:05.220
actually holds many different entity types.

7587
09:57:05.220 --> 09:57:10.610
This updated entity relationship model on the right side would be better suited. It

7588
09:57:10.610 --> 09:57:16.065
represents three entity types, professors, universities, and organizations, in their

7589
09:57:16.065 --> 09:57:21.195
own tables, with respective attributes. This reduces redundancy, as professors,

7590
09:57:21.195 --> 09:57:26.195
unlike now, need to be stored only once. Note that, for each professor, their

7591
09:57:26.195 --> 09:57:31.520
respective university is also denoted through the university short name attribute.

7592
09:57:31.520 --> 09:57:37.013
However, one original attribute, the function, is still missing. As you know, this

7593
09:57:37.013 --> 09:57:42.373
database contains affiliations of professors with third party organizations. The

7594
09:57:42.373 --> 09:57:47.866
attribute function gives some extra information to that affiliation. For instance,

7595
09:57:47.866 --> 09:57:52.690
somebody might act as a chairman for a certain third party organization.

7596
09:57:52.690 --> 09:57:58.049
So the best idea at the moment is to store these affiliations in their own table. It

7597
09:57:58.049 --> 09:58:03.216
connects professors with their respective organizations where they have a certain

7598
09:58:03.216 --> 09:58:03.790
function.

7599
09:58:03.790 --> 09:58:09.072
The first thing you need to do now is to create four empty tables for professors,

7600
09:58:09.072 --> 09:58:14.419
universities, organizations, and affiliations. This is quite easy with SQL. You'll

7601
09:58:14.419 --> 09:58:19.896
use the create table command for that. At the minimum, this command requires a table

7602
09:58:19.896 --> 09:58:25.309
name and one or more columns with the respective data types. For example, you could

7603
09:58:25.309 --> 09:58:28.830
create a weather table with three aptly named columns.

7604
09:58:28.830 --> 09:58:33.892
After each column name, you must specify the data type. There are many different

7605
09:58:33.892 --> 09:58:39.144
types, and you will discover some in the remainder of this course. For example, you

7606
09:58:39.144 --> 09:58:43.763
could specify a text column, a numeric column, and a column that requires

7607
09:58:43.763 --> 09:58:48.952
fixed-length character strings with five characters each. These data types will be

7608
09:58:48.952 --> 09:58:54.014
explained in more detail in the next chapter. For now, you will first create the

7609
09:58:54.014 --> 09:58:58.190
four tables and then migrate data from the original table to them.

7610
09:58:58.190 --> 09:59:03.659
Well done so far. You now have a database consisting of five different tables. Now

7611
09:59:03.659 --> 09:59:08.794
it's time to migrate the data. Here's the current entity relationship diagram

7612
09:59:08.794 --> 09:59:14.396
showing the five tables. At this moment, only the university professor's table holds

7613
09:59:14.396 --> 09:59:17.730
data. The other four shown in red are still empty.

7614
09:59:17.730 --> 09:59:22.767
In the remainder of this chapter, you'll migrate data from the green part of this

7615
09:59:22.767 --> 09:59:27.742
diagram to the red part, moving the respective entity types to their appropriate

7616
09:59:27.742 --> 09:59:32.530
tables. In the end, you'll be able to delete the university professors table.

7617
09:59:32.530 --> 09:59:39.059
One advantage of splitting up university professors into several tables is the

7618
09:59:39.059 --> 09:59:45.922
reduced redundancy. As of now, university professors holds 1,377 entries. However,

7619
09:59:45.922 --> 09:59:52.618
there are only 1,287 distinct organizations, as this query shows. Therefore, you

7620
09:59:52.618 --> 09:59:59.230
only need to store 1,287 distinct organizations in the new organizations table.

7621
09:59:59.230 --> 10:00:04.700
In order to copy data from an existing table to a new one, you can use the insert

7622
10:00:04.700 --> 10:00:10.305
into select distinct pattern. After insert into, you specify the name of the target

7623
10:00:10.305 --> 10:00:15.978
table, organizations in this case. Then you select the columns that should be copied

7624
10:00:15.978 --> 10:00:21.651
over from the source table, university professors in this case. You use the distinct

7625
10:00:21.651 --> 10:00:24.960
keyword to only copy over distinct organizations.

7626
10:00:24.960 --> 10:00:30.718
As the output shows, only 1,287 records are inserted into the organizations table.

7627
10:00:30.718 --> 10:00:36.547
If you just used insert into select without the distinct keyword, duplicate records

7628
10:00:36.547 --> 10:00:42.305
would be copied over as well. In the following exercises, you'll migrate your data

7629
10:00:42.305 --> 10:00:43.920
to the four new tables.

7630
10:00:43.920 --> 10:00:48.998
By the way, this is the normal use case for insert into, where you insert values

7631
10:00:48.998 --> 10:00:54.266
manually. Insert into is followed by the table name and an optional list of columns

7632
10:00:54.266 --> 10:00:59.343
which should be filled with data. Then follows the values keyword and the actual

7633
10:00:59.343 --> 10:01:04.421
values you want to insert. Before you start migrating the table, you need to fix

7634
10:01:04.421 --> 10:01:09.054
some stuff. In the last lesson, I created the affiliations table for you.

7635
10:01:09.054 --> 10:01:13.180
Unfortunately, I made a mistake in this process. Can you spot it?

7636
10:01:13.180 --> 10:01:18.494
The way the organization column is spelled is not consistent with the American style

7637
10:01:18.494 --> 10:01:23.681
spelling of this table, using an S instead of a Z. In the first exercise after the

7638
10:01:23.681 --> 10:01:28.868
video, you'll correct this with the known alter table syntax. You do this with the

7639
10:01:28.868 --> 10:01:33.802
rename column command by specifying the old column name first and then the new

7640
10:01:33.802 --> 10:01:39.053
column name. That is rename column old name to new name. Also, the university short

7641
10:01:39.053 --> 10:01:41.330
name column is not even needed here.

7642
10:01:41.330 --> 10:01:46.243
So I want you to delete it. The syntax for this is again very simple. You use a drop

7643
10:01:46.243 --> 10:01:50.337
column command followed by the name of the column. Dropping columns is

7644
10:01:50.337 --> 10:01:55.074
straightforward when the tables are still empty. So it's not too late to fix this

7645
10:01:55.074 --> 10:01:57.940
error. But why is it an error in the first place?

7646
10:01:57.940 --> 10:02:03.826
Well, I queried the university professors table and saw that there are 551 unique

7647
10:02:03.826 --> 10:02:09.858
combinations of first names, last names and associated universities. I then queried

7648
10:02:09.858 --> 10:02:15.672
the table again and only looked for unique combinations of first and last names.

7649
10:02:15.672 --> 10:02:21.776
Turns out, this is also 551 records. This means that the columns first name and last

7650
10:02:21.776 --> 10:02:24.320
name uniquely identify a professor.

7651
10:02:24.320 --> 10:02:29.469
So the university short name column is not needed in order to reference a professor

7652
10:02:29.469 --> 10:02:34.680
in the affiliations table. You can remove it, and this will reduce the redundancy in

7653
10:02:34.680 --> 10:02:39.706
your database again. In other words, the columns first name, last name, function,

7654
10:02:39.706 --> 10:02:44.855
and organization are enough to store the affiliation a professor has with a certain

7655
10:02:44.855 --> 10:02:49.880
organization. Time to prepare the database for data migration. After this, you'll

7656
10:02:49.890 --> 10:02:54.960
So far you've learned how to set up a simple database that consists of multiple

7657
10:02:54.960 --> 10:03:00.286
tables. Apart from storing different entity types, such as professors, in different

7658
10:03:00.286 --> 10:03:05.484
tables, you haven't made much use of database features. In the end, the idea of a

7659
10:03:05.484 --> 10:03:10.617
database is to push data into a certain structure, a predefined model, where you

7660
10:03:10.617 --> 10:03:13.890
enforce data types, relationships, and other rules.

7661
10:03:13.890 --> 10:03:19.155
Generally, these rules are called integrity constraints, although different names

7662
10:03:19.155 --> 10:03:24.225
exist. Integrity constraints can roughly be divided into three types. The most

7663
10:03:24.225 --> 10:03:29.685
simple ones are probably the so-called attribute constraints. For example, a certain

7664
10:03:29.685 --> 10:03:34.950
attribute represented through a database column could have the integer data type,

7665
10:03:34.950 --> 10:03:40.215
allowing only for integers to be stored in this column. They'll be the subject of

7666
10:03:40.215 --> 10:03:41.710
this chapter. Secondly,

7667
10:03:41.710 --> 10:03:46.816
There are so-called key constraints. Primary keys, for example, uniquely identify

7668
10:03:46.816 --> 10:03:51.923
each record or row of a database table. They'll be discussed in the next chapter.

7669
10:03:51.923 --> 10:03:57.092
Lastly, there are referential integrity constraints. In short, they glue different

7670
10:03:57.092 --> 10:04:01.947
database tables together. You'll learn about them in the last chapter of this

7671
10:04:01.947 --> 10:04:05.540
course. So why should you know about constraints? Well...

7672
10:04:05.540 --> 10:04:10.939
They press the data into a certain form. With good constraints in place, people who

7673
10:04:10.939 --> 10:04:16.208
type in birthdates, for example, have to enter them in always the same form. Data

7674
10:04:16.208 --> 10:04:21.282
entered by humans is often very tedious to preprocess, so constraints give you

7675
10:04:21.282 --> 10:04:26.551
consistency, meaning that the row in a certain table has exactly the same form as

7676
10:04:26.551 --> 10:04:31.755
the next row, and so forth. All in all, they help to solve a lot of data quality

7677
10:04:31.755 --> 10:04:32.210
issues.

7678
10:04:32.210 --> 10:04:37.371
While enforcing constraints on human entered data is difficult and tedious, database

7679
10:04:37.371 --> 10:04:42.408
management systems can be a great help. In the next chapters and exercises, you'll

7680
10:04:42.408 --> 10:04:47.077
explore how. You'll start with attribute constraints in this chapter. In its

7681
10:04:47.077 --> 10:04:52.115
simplest form, attribute constraints are data types that can be specified for each

7682
10:04:52.115 --> 10:04:56.784
column of a table. Here you see the beginning of a list of all data types in

7683
10:04:56.784 --> 10:04:57.460
PostgreSQL.

7684
10:04:57.460 --> 10:05:02.511
There are basic data types for numbers, such as BigInt, or strings of characters,

7685
10:05:02.511 --> 10:05:07.438
such as character varying. There are also more high-level data types like CIDR,

7686
10:05:07.438 --> 10:05:12.302
which can be used for IP addresses. Implementing such a type on a column would

7687
10:05:12.302 --> 10:05:17.478
disallow anything that doesn't fit the structure of an IP. Data types also restrict

7688
10:05:17.478 --> 10:05:20.160
possible SQL operations on the stored data.

7689
10:05:20.160 --> 10:05:25.644
For example, it is impossible to calculate the product from an integer and the text

7690
10:05:25.644 --> 10:05:30.996
column, as shown here in the example. The text column WinSpeed may store numbers,

7691
10:05:30.996 --> 10:05:36.282
but Postgres SQL doesn't know how to use text in a calculation. The solution for

7692
10:05:36.282 --> 10:05:38.000
this is typecasts, that is

7693
10:05:38.000 --> 10:05:43.186
on-the-fly type conversion. In this case, you can use the cast function, followed by

7694
10:05:43.186 --> 10:05:48.125
the column name, the ask keyword, and the desired data type. And PostgreSQL will

7695
10:05:48.125 --> 10:05:53.126
turn WinSpeed into an integer right before the calculation. Okay, let's look into

7696
10:05:53.126 --> 10:05:58.003
this first. Working with data types is straightforward in a database management

7697
10:05:58.003 --> 10:06:03.190
system like PostgreSQL. As said before, data types are attribute constraints and are

7698
10:06:03.190 --> 10:06:06.400
therefore implemented for single columns of a table.

7699
10:06:06.400 --> 10:06:11.486
They define the so-called domain of values in a column. That means what form these

7700
10:06:11.486 --> 10:06:16.261
values can take and what not. Therefore, they also define what operations are

7701
10:06:16.261 --> 10:06:21.223
possible with the values in the column, as you saw in the previous exercises. Of

7702
10:06:21.223 --> 10:06:26.433
course, through this, consistent storage is enforced, so a street number will always

7703
10:06:26.433 --> 10:06:31.333
be an actual number and a postal code will always have no more than six digits,

7704
10:06:31.333 --> 10:06:35.550
according to your conventions. This greatly helps with data quality.

7705
10:06:35.550 --> 10:06:40.862
Here are the most common types in PostgreSQL. Note that these types are specific to

7706
10:06:40.862 --> 10:06:46.111
PostgreSQL, but appear in many other database management systems as well, and they

7707
10:06:46.111 --> 10:06:51.295
mostly conform to the SQL standard. The text type allows character strings of any

7708
10:06:51.295 --> 10:06:56.608
length, while the warchar and char types specify a maximum number of characters, or

7709
10:06:56.608 --> 10:07:01.664
a character string of fixed length, respectively. You'll use these two for your

7710
10:07:01.664 --> 10:07:02.240
database.

7711
10:07:02.240 --> 10:07:07.329
The boolean type allows for two boolean values, for example true and false, or one

7712
10:07:07.329 --> 10:07:12.171
or zero, and for a third unknown value, expressed through null. Then there are

7713
10:07:12.171 --> 10:07:17.384
various formats for date and time calculations, also with time zone support. Numeric

7714
10:07:17.384 --> 10:07:22.411
is a general type for any sort of numbers with arbitrary precision, while integer

7715
10:07:22.411 --> 10:07:27.501
allows only whole numbers in a certain range. If that range is not enough for your

7716
10:07:27.501 --> 10:07:30.480
numbers, there's also bigint for larger numbers.

7717
10:07:30.480 --> 10:07:35.604
Here's an example of how types are specified upon table creation. Let's say the

7718
10:07:35.604 --> 10:07:40.793
social security number, SSN, should be stored as an integer, as it only contains

7719
10:07:40.793 --> 10:07:46.242
whole numbers. The name may be a string with a maximum of 64 characters, which might

7720
10:07:46.242 --> 10:07:51.690
or might not be enough. The date of birth, DOB, is naturally stored as a date, while

7721
10:07:51.690 --> 10:07:56.620
the average grade is a numeric value with a precision of 3 and a scale of 2.

7722
10:07:56.620 --> 10:08:01.386
Meaning that numbers with a total of three digits and two digits after the

7723
10:08:01.386 --> 10:08:06.538
fractional point are allowed. Lastly, the information whether the tuition of the

7724
10:08:06.538 --> 10:08:11.754
student was paid is, of course, a boolean one, as it can be either true or false.

7725
10:08:11.754 --> 10:08:16.842
Altering types after table creation is also straightforward. Just use the shown

7726
10:08:16.842 --> 10:08:21.801
alter table, alter column statement. In this case, the maximum name length is

7727
10:08:21.801 --> 10:08:23.540
extended to 128 characters.

7728
10:08:23.540 --> 10:08:28.320
Sometimes it may be necessary to truncate column values or transform them in any

7729
10:08:28.320 --> 10:08:33.160
other way, so they fit with the new data type. Then you can use the using keyword

7730
10:08:33.160 --> 10:08:38.000
and specify a transformation that should happen before the type is altered. Let's

7731
10:08:38.000 --> 10:08:42.660
say you'd want to turn the average grade column into an integer type. Normally

7732
10:08:42.660 --> 10:08:47.620
PostgreSQL would just keep the part of the number before the fractional point. With

7733
10:08:47.620 --> 10:08:52.340
using, you can tell it to round the number to the nearest integer, for example.

7734
10:08:52.340 --> 10:08:57.245
Let's apply this to your database and add the proper types to your database. In the

7735
10:08:57.245 --> 10:09:02.209
last part of this chapter, you'll get to know two special attribute constraints, the

7736
10:09:02.209 --> 10:09:07.055
not null and unique constraints. As the name already says, the not null constraint

7737
10:09:07.055 --> 10:09:11.842
disallows any null values on a given column. This must hold true for the existing

7738
10:09:11.842 --> 10:09:16.392
state of the database, but also for any future state. Therefore, you can only

7739
10:09:16.392 --> 10:09:21.120
specify a not null constraint on a column that doesn't hold any null values yet.

7740
10:09:21.120 --> 10:09:25.851
And it won't be possible to insert null values in the future. Before I go on

7741
10:09:25.851 --> 10:09:30.706
explaining how to specify not null constraints, I want you to think about null

7742
10:09:30.706 --> 10:09:35.686
values. What do they actually mean to you? There's no clear definition. Null can

7743
10:09:35.686 --> 10:09:40.915
mean a couple of things. For example, that the value is unknown or does not exist at

7744
10:09:40.915 --> 10:09:46.144
all. It can also be possible that the value does not apply to the column. Let's look

7745
10:09:46.144 --> 10:09:47.140
into an example.

7746
10:09:47.140 --> 10:09:52.295
Let's say we define a table student. The first two columns for the social security

7747
10:09:52.295 --> 10:09:57.575
number and the last name cannot be null, which makes sense. This should be known and

7748
10:09:57.575 --> 10:10:02.666
applied to every student. The home phone and office phone columns, though, should

7749
10:10:02.666 --> 10:10:07.758
allow for null values, which is the default, by the way. Why? First of all, these

7750
10:10:07.758 --> 10:10:13.038
numbers can be unknown, for any reason, or simply not exist, because a student might

7751
10:10:13.038 --> 10:10:16.370
not have a phone. Also, some values just don't apply.

7752
10:10:16.370 --> 10:10:21.707
Some students might not have an office, so they don't have an office phone, and so

7753
10:10:21.707 --> 10:10:26.913
forth. So, one important takeaway is that two null values must not have the same

7754
10:10:26.913 --> 10:10:32.119
meaning. This also means that comparing null with null always results in a false

7755
10:10:32.119 --> 10:10:37.326
value. You've just seen how to add a not null constraint to certain columns when

7756
10:10:37.326 --> 10:10:42.662
creating a table. Just add not null after the respective columns. But you can also

7757
10:10:42.662 --> 10:10:44.940
add and remove not null constraints

7758
10:10:44.940 --> 10:10:51.019
to and from existing tables. To add a not null constraint to an existing table, you

7759
10:10:51.019 --> 10:10:57.025
can use the alter column set not null syntax as shown here. Similarly, to remove a

7760
10:10:57.025 --> 10:11:01.420
not null constraint, you can use alter column drop not null.

7761
10:11:01.420 --> 10:11:07.007
The unique constraint on a column makes sure there are no duplicates in a column, so

7762
10:11:07.007 --> 10:11:12.528
any given value in a column can only exist once. This, for example, makes sense for

7763
10:11:12.528 --> 10:11:18.049
university short names, as storing universities more than once leads to unnecessary

7764
10:11:18.049 --> 10:11:22.839
redundancy. However, it doesn't make sense for university cities, as two

7765
10:11:22.839 --> 10:11:28.160
universities can coexist in the same city. Just as with the not null constraint,

7766
10:11:28.160 --> 10:11:33.013
You can only add a unique constraint if the column doesn't hold any duplicates

7767
10:11:33.013 --> 10:11:38.178
before you apply it. Here's how to create columns with unique constraints. Just add

7768
10:11:38.178 --> 10:11:43.093
the unique keyword after the respective table column. You can also add a unique

7769
10:11:43.093 --> 10:11:47.884
constraint to an existing table. For that, you have to use the add constraint

7770
10:11:47.884 --> 10:11:48.320
syntax.

7771
10:11:48.320 --> 10:11:53.313
This is different from adding a not null constraint. However, it's a pattern that

7772
10:11:53.313 --> 10:11:58.182
frequently occurs. You'll see plenty of other examples of add constraint in the

7773
10:11:58.182 --> 10:12:02.681
remainder of this course. Okay, time now to apply the not null and unique

7774
10:12:02.681 --> 10:12:07.797
constraints to some of the columns of your current university affiliations. Welcome

7775
10:12:07.797 --> 10:12:12.851
back. Let's discuss key constraints. They are a very important concept in database

7776
10:12:12.851 --> 10:12:15.810
systems, so we'll spend a whole chapter on them.

7777
10:12:15.810 --> 10:12:20.533
Let's have a look at your current database model first. In the last chapter, you

7778
10:12:20.533 --> 10:12:25.256
specified attribute constraints, first and foremost data types. You also set not

7779
10:12:25.256 --> 10:12:30.098
null and unique constraints on certain attributes. This didn't actually change the

7780
10:12:30.098 --> 10:12:34.998
structure of the model, so it still looks the same. By the end of this chapter, the

7781
10:12:34.998 --> 10:12:39.662
database will look slightly different. You'll add so-called primary keys to the

7782
10:12:39.662 --> 10:12:42.260
three different tables. You'll name them ID.

7783
10:12:42.260 --> 10:12:47.348
In the entity relationship diagram, keys are denoted by underlined attribute names.

7784
10:12:47.348 --> 10:12:52.252
Notice that you'll add a whole new attribute to the professors table, and you'll

7785
10:12:52.252 --> 10:12:57.278
modify existing columns of the organizations and universities tables. Before we go

7786
10:12:57.278 --> 10:13:02.059
into the nitty gritty of what a primary key actually is, let's look at keys in

7787
10:13:02.059 --> 10:13:07.025
general. Typically a database table has an attribute or a combination of multiple

7788
10:13:07.025 --> 10:13:10.580
attributes whose values are unique across the whole table.

7789
10:13:10.580 --> 10:13:15.960
Such attributes identify a record uniquely. Normally, a table as a whole only

7790
10:13:15.960 --> 10:13:21.760
contains unique records, meaning that the combination of all attributes is a key in

7791
10:13:21.760 --> 10:13:27.350
itself. However, it's not called a key, but a super key, if attributes from that

7792
10:13:27.350 --> 10:13:32.800
combination can be removed and the attributes still uniquely identify records.

7793
10:13:32.800 --> 10:13:38.009
If all possible attributes have been removed, but the records are still uniquely

7794
10:13:38.009 --> 10:13:43.283
identifiable by the remaining attributes, we speak of a minimal superkey. This is

7795
10:13:43.283 --> 10:13:48.427
the actual key. So a key is always minimal. Let's look at an example. Here's an

7796
10:13:48.427 --> 10:13:53.701
example that I found in a textbook on database system. Obviously, the table shows

7797
10:13:53.701 --> 10:13:59.171
six different cars, so the combination of all attributes is a superkey. If we remove

7798
10:13:59.171 --> 10:14:01.580
the year attribute from the superkey,

7799
10:14:01.580 --> 10:14:06.934
The six records are still unique, so it's still a superkey. Actually, there are a

7800
10:14:06.934 --> 10:14:12.156
lot of possible superkeys in this example. However, there are only four minimal

7801
10:14:12.156 --> 10:14:17.510
superkeys, and these are license number, serial number, and model, as well as the

7802
10:14:17.510 --> 10:14:22.931
combination of make and year. Remember that superkeys are minimal if no attributes

7803
10:14:22.931 --> 10:14:26.500
can be removed without losing the uniqueness property.

7804
10:14:26.500 --> 10:14:32.343
This is trivial for K1 to K3, as they only consist of a single attribute. Also, if

7805
10:14:32.343 --> 10:14:37.972
we remove year from K4, make would contain duplicates and would therefore be no

7806
10:14:37.972 --> 10:14:43.815
longer suited as key. These four minimal superkeys are also called candidate keys.

7807
10:14:43.815 --> 10:14:49.658
Why candidate keys? In the end, there can only be one key for the table, which has

7808
10:14:49.658 --> 10:14:50.000
to be chosen from the candidates. More on that in the next video.

7809
10:14:50.000 --> 10:14:56.994
Okay, now it's time to look at an actual use case for superkeys, keys and candidate

7810
10:14:56.994 --> 10:15:04.073
keys. Primary keys are one of the most important concepts in database design. Almost

7811
10:15:04.073 --> 10:15:10.562
every database table should have a primary key, chosen by you from the set of

7812
10:15:10.562 --> 10:15:17.219
candidate keys. The main purpose, as already explained, is uniquely identifying

7813
10:14:54.290 --> 10:14:59.263
First, I want you to have a look at some tables in your database and OK, now it's

7814
10:14:59.263 --> 10:15:00.000
time to look

7815
10:15:18.820 --> 10:15:23.656
This makes it easier to reference these records from other tables, for instance. A

7816
10:15:23.656 --> 10:15:28.375
concept you will go through in the next and last chapter. You might have already

7817
10:15:28.375 --> 10:15:32.858
guessed it, but primary keys need to be defined on columns that don't accept

7818
10:15:32.858 --> 10:15:37.399
duplicate or null values. Lastly, primary key constraints are time-invariant,

7819
10:15:37.399 --> 10:15:42.059
meaning that they must hold for the current data in the table, but also for any

7820
10:15:42.059 --> 10:15:44.300
future data that the table might hold.

7821
10:15:44.300 --> 10:15:49.888
It is therefore wise to choose columns where values will always be unique and not

7822
10:15:49.888 --> 10:15:55.544
null. So these two tables accept exactly the same data. However, the latter has an

7823
10:15:55.544 --> 10:16:01.201
explicit primary key specified. As you can see, specifying primary keys upon table

7824
10:16:01.201 --> 10:16:06.926
creation is very easy. Primary keys can also be specified like so. This notation is

7825
10:16:06.926 --> 10:16:12.100
necessary if you want to designate more than one column as the primary key.

7826
10:16:12.100 --> 10:16:17.057
That's still only one primary key. It is just formed by the combination of two

7827
10:16:17.057 --> 10:16:22.332
columns. Ideally though, primary keys consist of as few columns as possible. Adding

7828
10:16:22.332 --> 10:16:27.480
primary key constraints to existing tables is the same procedure as adding unique

7829
10:16:27.480 --> 10:16:32.247
constraints, which you might remember from the last chapter. As with unique

7830
10:16:32.247 --> 10:16:36.060
constraints, you have to give the constraint a certain name.

7831
10:16:36.060 --> 10:16:41.175
In the exercises that follow, you will add primary keys to the table's universities

7832
10:16:41.175 --> 10:16:46.166
and organization. You'll add a special type of primary key, a so-called surrogate

7833
10:16:46.166 --> 10:16:50.911
key, to the table professors in the last part of this chapter. Let's go at...

7834
10:16:50.911 --> 10:16:55.964
Surrogate keys are sort of an artificial primary key. In other words, they are not

7835
10:16:55.964 --> 10:17:01.140
based on a native column in your data, but on a column that just exists for the sake

7836
10:17:01.140 --> 10:17:04.160
of having a primary key. Why would you need that?

7837
10:17:04.160 --> 10:17:09.295
There are several reasons for creating an artificial surrogate key. As mentioned

7838
10:17:09.295 --> 10:17:14.238
before, a primary key is ideally constructed from as few columns as possible.

7839
10:17:14.238 --> 10:17:19.502
Secondly, the primary key of a record should never change over time. If you define

7840
10:17:19.502 --> 10:17:24.830
an artificial primary key, ideally consisting of a unique number or string, you can

7841
10:17:24.830 --> 10:17:29.901
be sure that this number stays the same for each record. Other attributes might

7842
10:17:29.901 --> 10:17:30.350
change.

7843
10:17:30.350 --> 10:17:35.562
But the primary key always has the same value for a given record. Let's look back at

7844
10:17:35.562 --> 10:17:40.712
the example in the first video of this chapter. I altered it slightly and added the

7845
10:17:40.712 --> 10:17:45.490
color column. In this table, the license number column would be suited as the

7846
10:17:45.490 --> 10:17:50.640
primary key. The license number is unlikely to change over time, not like the color

7847
10:17:50.640 --> 10:17:55.790
column, for example, which might change if the car is repainted. So there's no need

7848
10:17:55.790 --> 10:17:57.900
for a surrogate key here. However,

7849
10:17:57.900 --> 10:18:02.994
Let's say there were only these three attributes in the table. The only sensible

7850
10:18:02.994 --> 10:18:08.216
primary key would be the combination of make and model, but that's two columns for

7851
10:18:08.216 --> 10:18:13.501
the primary key. You could add a new surrogate key column, called ID, to solve this

7852
10:18:13.501 --> 10:18:18.341
problem. Actually, there's a special data type in PostgreSQL that allows the

7853
10:18:18.341 --> 10:18:23.563
addition of auto-incrementing numbers to an existing table, the serial type. It is

7854
10:18:23.563 --> 10:18:26.110
specified just like any other data type.

7855
10:18:26.110 --> 10:18:31.376
Once you add a column with the serial type, all the records in your table will be

7856
10:18:31.376 --> 10:18:36.642
numbered. Whenever you add a new record to the table, it will automatically get a

7857
10:18:36.642 --> 10:18:41.713
number that does not exist yet. There are similar data types in other database

7858
10:18:41.713 --> 10:18:46.719
management systems like MySQL. Also, if you try to specify an ID that already

7859
10:18:46.719 --> 10:18:51.010
exists, the primary key constraint will prevent you from doing so.

7860
10:18:51.010 --> 10:18:56.382
So after all, the ID column uniquely identifies each record in this table, which is

7861
10:18:56.382 --> 10:19:01.431
very useful, for example, when you want to refer to these records from another

7862
10:19:01.431 --> 10:19:06.415
table. But this will be the subject of the next chapter. Another strategy for

7863
10:19:06.415 --> 10:19:11.787
creating a surrogate key is to combine two existing columns into a new one. In this

7864
10:19:11.787 --> 10:19:17.095
example, we first add a new column with the varchar data type. We then update that

7865
10:19:17.095 --> 10:19:20.590
column with the concatenation of two existing columns.

7866
10:19:20.590 --> 10:19:25.660
The concat function glues together the values of two or more existing columns.

7867
10:19:25.660 --> 10:19:30.795
Lastly, we turn that new column into a surrogate primary key. In the exercises,

7868
10:19:30.795 --> 10:19:36.190
you'll add a surrogate key to the professors table, because the existing attributes

7869
10:19:36.190 --> 10:19:41.455
are not really suited as primary key. Theoretically, there could be more than one

7870
10:19:41.455 --> 10:19:46.720
professor with the same name working for one university, resulting in duplicates.

7871
10:19:46.720 --> 10:19:51.782
With an auto-incrementing ID column as the primary key, you make sure that each

7872
10:19:51.782 --> 10:19:57.101
professor can be uniquely referred to. This was not necessary for organizations and

7873
10:19:57.101 --> 10:20:02.227
universities, as their names can be assumed to be unique across these tables. In

7874
10:20:02.227 --> 10:20:07.546
other words, it is unlikely that two organizations with the same name exist, solely

7875
10:20:07.546 --> 10:20:12.928
for trademark reasons. The same goes for universities. Let's try this out before you

7876
10:20:12.928 --> 10:20:14.210
move on to the last.

7877
10:20:14.210 --> 10:20:18.918
Welcome to the last chapter of this course. Now it's time to make use of key

7878
10:20:18.918 --> 10:20:24.060
constraints. Here's your current database model. The three entity types professors,

7879
10:20:24.060 --> 10:20:29.201
organizations, and universities all have primary keys, but affiliations doesn't for

7880
10:20:29.201 --> 10:20:34.157
a specific reason that will be revealed in this chapter. Next up, you'll model a

7881
10:20:34.157 --> 10:20:39.113
so-called relationship type between professors and universities. As you know, in

7882
10:20:39.113 --> 10:20:39.980
your database,

7883
10:20:39.980 --> 10:20:45.298
Each professor works for a university. In the ER diagram, this is drawn with a

7884
10:20:45.298 --> 10:20:50.957
rhombus. The small numbers specify the cardinality of the relationship. A professor

7885
10:20:50.957 --> 10:20:56.071
works for at most one university, while a university can have any number of

7886
10:20:56.071 --> 10:21:01.321
professors working for it, even zero. Such relationships are implemented with

7887
10:21:01.321 --> 10:21:06.775
foreign keys. Foreign keys are designated columns that point to a primary key of

7888
10:21:06.775 --> 10:21:07.730
another table.

7889
10:21:07.730 --> 10:21:12.690
There are some restrictions for foreign keys. First, the domain and the data type

7890
10:21:12.690 --> 10:21:17.651
must be the same as the one of the primary key. Secondly, only foreign key values

7891
10:21:17.651 --> 10:21:22.734
are allowed that exist as values in the primary key of the reference table. This is

7892
10:21:22.734 --> 10:21:27.633
the actual foreign key constraint, also called referential integrity. You'll dig

7893
10:21:27.633 --> 10:21:30.940
into referential integrity at the end of this chapter.

7894
10:21:30.940 --> 10:21:36.531
Lastly, a foreign key is not necessarily an actual key because duplicates and null

7895
10:21:36.531 --> 10:21:42.122
values are allowed. Let's have a look at your database. As you can see, the column

7896
10:21:42.122 --> 10:21:47.508
University short name of professors has the same domain as the ID column of the

7897
10:21:47.508 --> 10:21:53.235
universities table. If you go through each record of professors, you can always find

7898
10:21:53.235 --> 10:21:58.826
the respective ID in the universities table. So both criteria for a foreign key in

7899
10:21:58.826 --> 10:22:00.190
the table professors

7900
10:22:00.190 --> 10:22:06.202
Referencing universities are fulfilled. Also, you see that university short name is

7901
10:22:06.202 --> 10:22:11.852
not really a key because there are duplicates. For example, the ID EPF and UBE

7902
10:22:11.852 --> 10:22:17.719
occurred three times each. When you create a new table, you can specify a foreign

7903
10:22:17.719 --> 10:22:23.803
key similarly to a primary key. Let's look at two example tables. First, we create a

7904
10:22:23.803 --> 10:22:27.570
manufacturer's table with a primary key called name.

7905
10:22:27.570 --> 10:22:33.282
Then we create a table cars that also has a primary key called model. As each car is

7906
10:22:33.282 --> 10:22:38.994
produced by a certain manufacturer, it makes sense to also add a foreign key to this

7907
10:22:38.994 --> 10:22:44.638
table. We do that by writing the references keyword followed by the reference table

7908
10:22:44.638 --> 10:22:50.010
and its primary key in brackets. From now on, only cars with valid and existing

7909
10:22:50.010 --> 10:22:53.070
manufacturers may be entered into that table.

7910
10:22:53.070 --> 10:22:57.611
Trying to enter models with manufacturers that are not yet stored in the

7911
10:22:57.611 --> 10:23:02.910
manufacturers tables won't be possible, thanks to the foreign key constraint. Again,

7912
10:23:02.910 --> 10:23:07.956
the syntax for adding foreign keys to existing tables is the same as the one for

7913
10:23:07.956 --> 10:23:13.254
adding primary keys and unique constraints. Okay, let's have a look at your database

7914
10:23:13.254 --> 10:23:16.660
and implement a simple relationship between professors

7915
10:23:16.660 --> 10:23:21.559
In the last few exercises, you made your first steps in modeling and implementing

7916
10:23:21.559 --> 10:23:26.216
one-to-end relationships. Now it's time to look at more complex relationships

7917
10:23:26.216 --> 10:23:31.054
between tables. So you've added a one-to-end relationship between professors and

7918
10:23:31.054 --> 10:23:31.840
universities.

