10:42:11.146 --> 10:42:15.637
foreign keys to purple dimension tables. These dimension tables expand on the

8170
10:42:15.637 --> 10:42:20.420
attributes of a fact table, such as the album it's in, and the artist who made it.

8171
10:42:20.420 --> 10:42:25.018
The record and fact tables often change as new songs get inserted. Albums, labels,

8172
10:42:25.018 --> 10:42:29.224
artists, and genres will be shared by more than one song, hence records and

8173
10:42:29.224 --> 10:42:33.879
dimension tables won't change as much. Summing it up, to decide the fact table in a

8174
10:42:33.879 --> 10:42:38.421
dimensional model, consider what is being analyzed and how often entities change.

8175
10:42:38.421 --> 10:42:42.907
Let's do some extra. Congrats on finishing the first chapter. We're now going to

8176
10:42:42.907 --> 10:42:47.618
jump in where we left off with the star schema. The star schema is the simplest form

8177
10:42:47.618 --> 10:42:49.020
of the dimensional model.

8178
10:42:49.020 --> 10:42:53.457
Some use the term star schema and dimensional model interchangeably. Remember that

8179
10:42:53.457 --> 10:42:57.785
the star schema is made up of two tables, fact and dimension tables. Fact tables

8180
10:42:57.785 --> 10:43:02.222
hold records of metrics that are described further by dimension tables. Throughout

8181
10:43:02.222 --> 10:43:06.766
this chapter, we are going to use another bookstore example. However, this time, you

8182
10:43:06.766 --> 10:43:11.257
work for a company that sells books in bulk to bookstores across the US and Canada.

8183
10:43:11.257 --> 10:43:15.477
You have a database to keep track of book sales. Let's take a look at the star

8184
10:43:15.477 --> 10:43:16.830
schema for this database.

8185
10:43:16.830 --> 10:43:21.032
Excluding primary and foreign keys, the fact table holds the sales amount and

8186
10:43:21.032 --> 10:43:25.342
quantity of books. It's connected to dimension tables with details on the books

8187
10:43:25.342 --> 10:43:29.817
sold, the time the sale took place, and the store buying the books. You may notice

8188
10:43:29.817 --> 10:43:34.236
the lines connecting these tables have a special pattern. These lines represent a

8189
10:43:34.236 --> 10:43:38.711
one-to-many relationship. For example, a store can be part of many book sales, but

8190
10:43:38.711 --> 10:43:43.294
one sale can only belong to one store. The star schema got its name because it tends

8191
10:43:43.294 --> 10:43:46.350
to look like a star with its different extension points.

8192
10:43:46.350 --> 10:43:50.776
of the star schema, let's look at the snowflake schema. The snowflake schema is an

8193
10:43:50.776 --> 10:43:55.202
extension of the star schema. Off the bat, we see that it has more tables. You may

8194
10:43:55.202 --> 10:43:59.736
not be able to see all the details in this slide, but don't worry, it will be broken

8195
10:43:59.736 --> 10:44:04.054
down in later slides. The information contained in the schema is the same as the

8196
10:44:04.054 --> 10:44:08.480
star schema. In fact, the fact table is the same, but the way the dimension tables

8197
10:44:08.480 --> 10:44:12.690
are structured is different. We see that they extend more, hence its namesake.

8198
10:44:12.690 --> 10:44:17.091
The star schema extends one dimension, while the snowflake schema extends over more

8199
10:44:17.091 --> 10:44:21.492
than one dimension. This is because the dimension tables are normalized. So what is

8200
10:44:21.492 --> 10:44:25.894
normalization? Normalization is a technique that divides tables into smaller tables

8201
10:44:25.894 --> 10:44:30.242
and connects them via relationships. The goal is to reduce redundancy and increase

8202
10:44:30.242 --> 10:44:34.590
data integrity. So how does this happen? There are several forms of normalization,

8203
10:44:34.590 --> 10:44:38.991
which we'll delve into later. But the basic idea is to identify repeating groups of

8204
10:44:38.991 --> 10:44:40.900
data and create new tables for them.

8205
10:44:40.900 --> 10:44:45.232
Let's go back to our example to see how these tables were normalized. Here's the

8206
10:44:45.232 --> 10:44:49.617
book dimension in the star schema. What could be repeating here? Primary keys are

8207
10:44:49.617 --> 10:44:54.003
inherently unique. For book titles, although there is possible repeat here, it is

8208
10:44:54.003 --> 10:44:58.064
not as common. On the other hand, authors often publish more than one book,

8209
10:44:58.064 --> 10:45:02.450
publishers definitely publish many books, and a lot of books share genres. We can

8210
10:45:02.450 --> 10:45:06.998
create new tables for them, and it results in the following snowflake schema. Do you

8211
10:45:06.998 --> 10:45:10.030
see how these repeating groups now have their own table?

8212
10:45:10.030 --> 10:45:14.311
Onto the store dimension. City, states, and countries can definitely have more than

8213
10:45:14.311 --> 10:45:18.644
one bookstore within them. Here are the normalized dimension tables representing the

8214
10:45:18.644 --> 10:45:22.924
bookstores. Do you notice that the way we structure these repeating groups is a bit

8215
10:45:22.924 --> 10:45:27.205
different from the book dimension? An author can have published in different genres

8216
10:45:27.205 --> 10:45:31.332
and with various publishers, hence why they are different dimensions. However, a

8217
10:45:31.332 --> 10:45:35.510
city stays in the same state and country. Thus, they extend each other over three

8218
10:45:35.510 --> 10:45:38.140
dimensions. The same is done on the time dimension.

8219
10:45:38.140 --> 10:45:42.323
A day is part of a month, that is part of a quarter, and so on. And here we put all

8220
10:45:42.323 --> 10:45:46.506
the normalized dimensions together to get the snowflake schema. Getting the hang of

8221
10:45:46.506 --> 10:45:50.135
this? Let's work through this. Welcome back. Now that we have a grasp on

8222
10:45:50.135 --> 10:45:54.167
normalization, let's talk about why we would want to normalize the database. You

8223
10:45:54.167 --> 10:45:58.199
should be familiar with these two schemas by now. They're both storing fictional

8224
10:45:58.199 --> 10:46:02.130
company data on the sales of books in bulk to stores across the US and Canada.

8225
10:46:02.130 --> 10:46:06.477
On the left you have the star schema with denormalized dimension tables, and on the

8226
10:46:06.477 --> 10:46:10.877
right you have the snowflake schema with normalized dimension tables. The normalized

8227
10:46:10.877 --> 10:46:15.225
database looks way more complicated, and it is in some ways. For example, let's say

8228
10:46:15.225 --> 10:46:19.520
you wanted to get the quantity of all books by Octavia E. Butler sold in Vancouver

8229
10:46:19.520 --> 10:46:23.867
in Q4 of 2018. Based on the denormalized schema, you can run the following query to

8230
10:46:23.867 --> 10:46:28.214
accomplish this. It's composed of three joins, which makes sense based on the three

8231
10:46:28.214 --> 10:46:30.100
dimension tables in the star schema.

8232
10:46:30.100 --> 10:46:34.495
What would the query look like on the normalized schema? A lot longer. It doesn't

8233
10:46:34.495 --> 10:46:38.999
even fit on one slide. There's a total of eight inner joins. This makes sense based

8234
10:46:38.999 --> 10:46:43.395
on the snowflake schema diagram. The normalized snowflake schema has considerably

8235
10:46:43.395 --> 10:46:47.953
more tables. This means more joins, which means slower queries. So why would we want

8236
10:46:47.953 --> 10:46:52.457
to normalize a database? Normalization saves space. This is an intuitive seeing how

8237
10:46:52.457 --> 10:46:56.906
normalized databases have more tables. Let's take a look at the store table in our

8238
10:46:56.906 --> 10:46:58.100
denormalized database.

8239
10:46:58.100 --> 10:47:02.298
Here we see a lot of repeated information in bold, such as USA, California, New

8240
10:47:02.298 --> 10:47:06.389
York, and Brooklyn. This type of denormalized structure enables a lot of data

8241
10:47:06.389 --> 10:47:10.799
redundance. If we normalize that previous schema, we get this. We see that although

8242
10:47:10.799 --> 10:47:15.103
we are using more tables, there's no data redundancy. The string Brooklyn is only

8243
10:47:15.103 --> 10:47:19.460
stored once, and the state records are stored separately because many cities share

8244
10:47:19.460 --> 10:47:23.763
the same state and country. We don't need to repeat that information. Instead, we

8245
10:47:23.763 --> 10:47:26.420
can have one record holding the string California.

8246
10:47:26.420 --> 10:47:30.683
Here we see how normalization eliminates data redundancy. Normalization ensures

8247
10:47:30.683 --> 10:47:35.161
better data integrity through its design. First, it enforces data consistency. Data

8248
10:47:35.161 --> 10:47:39.370
entry can get messy, and at times people will fill out fields differently. For

8249
10:47:39.370 --> 10:47:43.902
example, when referring to California, someone might enter the initial CA. Since the

8250
10:47:43.902 --> 10:47:48.165
states are already entered in a table, we can ensure naming conventions through

8251
10:47:48.165 --> 10:47:52.697
referential integrity. Secondly, because duplicates are reduced, modification of any

8252
10:47:52.697 --> 10:47:54.370
data becomes safer and simpler.

8253
10:47:54.370 --> 10:47:58.273
Say in the previous example you wanted to update the spelling of a state, you

8254
10:47:58.273 --> 10:48:02.530
wouldn't have to find each record referring to the state. Instead, you can make that

8255
10:48:02.530 --> 10:48:06.737
change in the states table by altering one record. From there, you can be confident

8256
10:48:06.737 --> 10:48:10.843
that the new spelling will be enacted for all stores in that state. Lastly, since

8257
10:48:10.843 --> 10:48:14.999
tables are smaller and organized more by object, it's easier to alter the database

8258
10:48:14.999 --> 10:48:18.902
schema. You can extend a smaller table without having to alter a larger table

8259
10:48:18.902 --> 10:48:20.270
holding all the vital data.

8260
10:48:20.270 --> 10:48:24.610
To recap, here are the pros and cons of normalization. Now, normalization seems

8261
10:48:24.610 --> 10:48:29.060
appealing, especially for database maintenance. However, normalization requires a

8262
10:48:29.060 --> 10:48:33.675
lot more joins, making queries more complicated, which can make indexing and reading

8263
10:48:33.675 --> 10:48:38.290
of data slower. Deciding between normalization and denormalization comes down to how

8264
10:48:38.290 --> 10:48:42.740
read or write intensive your database is going to be. Remember OLTP and OLAP? Can

8265
10:48:42.740 --> 10:48:47.080
you guess which prefers normalization? Pause and think about it. Did you get it

8266
10:48:47.080 --> 10:48:47.410
right?

8267
10:48:47.410 --> 10:48:51.834
OLTP is write-intensive, meaning we're updating and writing often. Normalization

8268
10:48:51.834 --> 10:48:55.870
makes sense because we want to add data quickly and consistently. OLAP is

8269
10:48:55.870 --> 10:49:00.349
read-intensive because we're running analytics on the data. This means we want to

8270
10:49:00.349 --> 10:49:04.883
prioritize quicker read queries. Remember how much more joins the normalized query

8271
10:49:04.883 --> 10:49:09.196
had over the denormalized query? OLAP should try to avoid that. Let's see how-

8272
10:49:09.196 --> 10:49:13.454
Welcome back! Earlier, I introduced the concept of normalization. I gave this

8273
10:49:13.454 --> 10:49:14.670
simplified definition.

8274
10:49:14.670 --> 10:49:19.491
Here's a more formal definition provided by Adrian Watt. Pause to read it. So what

8275
10:49:19.491 --> 10:49:24.371
are these levels? These are different extents to which you can normalize. These are

8276
10:49:24.371 --> 10:49:29.310
called normal forms. Below is a list of them from least to most normalized. Each has

8277
10:49:29.310 --> 10:49:34.249
its own set of rules and some build on top of each other. We'll only cover the first

8278
10:49:34.249 --> 10:49:39.187
three normal forms. Let's begin with the first normal form. To comply with 1NF, each

8279
10:49:39.187 --> 10:49:42.480
record must be unique and each cell must hold one value.

8280
10:49:42.480 --> 10:49:47.717
Here's a simple table with student IDs and emails, along with courses completed. All

8281
10:49:47.717 --> 10:49:52.953
these rows are unique, but the courses completed column has more than one course and

8282
10:49:52.953 --> 10:49:58.065
two records. To rectify this, we can split the original table as such. Now all the

8283
10:49:58.065 --> 10:50:03.177
records are unique, and each column has one value. Next is 2NF, which must satisfy

8284
10:50:03.177 --> 10:50:07.790
1NF. Beyond that, if the primary key is one column, then the table is 2NF.

8285
10:50:07.790 --> 10:50:12.766
A composite primary key is when a primary key is made up of two or more columns. If

8286
10:50:12.766 --> 10:50:17.802
the table has a composite primary key, then each non-key column must be dependent on

8287
10:50:17.802 --> 10:50:22.719
all keys. Let's look at a concrete example. In this table, we have the student and

8288
10:50:22.719 --> 10:50:27.515
course ID as a composite primary key. We then review the other columns and their

8289
10:50:27.515 --> 10:50:32.491
dependence on these two keys. First is the instructor, which isn't dependent on the

8290
10:50:32.491 --> 10:50:34.350
student ID, only the course ID.

8291
10:50:34.350 --> 10:50:38.825
Meaning, an instructor solely depends on the course, not the students who take the

8292
10:50:38.825 --> 10:50:43.299
course. The same goes for the instructor ID column. However, the percent completed

8293
10:50:43.299 --> 10:50:47.883
is dependent on both the student and the course ID. To convert it, we can create two

8294
10:50:47.883 --> 10:50:52.084
new tables that satisfy the conditions of 2NF. Onto 3NF, where you might have

8295
10:50:52.084 --> 10:50:56.286
correctly guessed, requires 2NF to be satisfied. 3NF doesn't allow transitive

8296
10:50:56.286 --> 10:51:00.270
dependency. This means that non-primary key columns can't depend on other

8297
10:51:00.270 --> 10:51:03.380
non-primary key columns. Let's take a look at an example.

8298
10:51:03.380 --> 10:51:07.487
Course ID is the primary key, so we can ignore this column. Instructor ID and

8299
10:51:07.487 --> 10:51:11.914
instructor definitely depend on each other. Tech does not depend on the instructor,

8300
10:51:11.914 --> 10:51:16.182
as an instructor can teach different technologies. We can replace the table from

8301
10:51:16.182 --> 10:51:20.556
before into these two tables to meet 3NF criteria. These tables have no transitive

8302
10:51:20.556 --> 10:51:24.876
dependencies, and they also meet 2NF as there are no composite primary key. We've

8303
10:51:24.876 --> 10:51:28.610
covered these first three normal forms that increase in normalization.

8304
10:51:28.610 --> 10:51:33.036
It's time to consider why we'd want to put so much effort into normalizing a

8305
10:51:33.036 --> 10:51:37.927
database even more. Why isn't 1NF enough? A database that isn't normalized enough is

8306
10:51:37.927 --> 10:51:42.702
prone to three types of anomaly errors. Update, insertion, and deletion. An update

8307
10:51:42.702 --> 10:51:47.127
anomaly is a data inconsistency that can arise when updating a database with

8308
10:51:47.127 --> 10:51:51.728
redundancy. For example, take this simple table. It holds the IDs and emails of

8309
10:51:51.728 --> 10:51:56.561
students and their enrolled courses. If we want to update the email of student 520,

8310
10:51:56.561 --> 10:51:57.900
we would have to update

8311
10:51:57.900 --> 10:52:02.139
multiple records. It may sound easy to update multiple records, but it's risky

8312
10:52:02.139 --> 10:52:06.596
because it depends on the user updating if they remember this redundancy. And this

8313
10:52:06.596 --> 10:52:11.052
is a simple example. As we scale, it's harder to keep track of these redundancies.

8314
10:52:11.052 --> 10:52:15.291
An insertion anomaly is when you are unable to add a new record due to missing

8315
10:52:15.291 --> 10:52:19.693
attributes. For example, if a student signs up for DataCamp but doesn't start any

8316
10:52:19.693 --> 10:52:23.824
courses, they cannot be put into this database. The only exception is if the

8317
10:52:23.824 --> 10:52:25.780
enrolled in column can accept nulls.

8318
10:52:25.780 --> 10:52:29.979
The dependency between columns in the same table unintentionally restricts what can

8319
10:52:29.979 --> 10:52:34.178
be inserted into the table. A deletion anomaly happens when you delete a record and

8320
10:52:34.178 --> 10:52:38.326
unintentionally delete other data. For example, if you were to delete any of these

8321
10:52:38.326 --> 10:52:42.525
students, you would lose the course information provided in the columns enrolled in

8322
10:52:42.525 --> 10:52:46.673
and taught by. This could be resolved if we put that information in another table.

8323
10:52:46.673 --> 10:52:50.670
The more normalized the database, the less prone it will be to these anomalies.

8324
10:52:50.670 --> 10:52:55.292
For example, most 3NF tables can have an update, insertion, or deletion anomaly.

8325
10:52:55.292 --> 10:52:59.337
This makes normalization sound great, but don't forget the downside of

8326
10:52:59.337 --> 10:53:03.728
normalizations from the last video. Remember those long queries? Okay, let's

8327
10:53:03.728 --> 10:53:08.350
practice. In this chapter, we'll learn about database views. So, what are views?

8328
10:53:08.350 --> 10:53:13.030
Wikipedia provides the following definition. Pause to read it. Essentially, views

8329
10:53:13.030 --> 10:53:17.826
are virtual tables that are not part of the physical schema. A view isn't stored in

8330
10:53:17.826 --> 10:53:18.750
physical memory.

8331
10:53:18.750 --> 10:53:23.254
Instead, the query to create the view is. The data in a view comes from data and

8332
10:53:23.254 --> 10:53:27.983
tables of the same database. Once a view is created, you can query it like a regular

8333
10:53:27.983 --> 10:53:32.487
table. The benefit of a view is that you don't need to retype common queries. It

8334
10:53:32.487 --> 10:53:37.104
allows you to add virtual tables without altering the database's schema. Views are

8335
10:53:37.104 --> 10:53:41.664
simple to create. You take the query of interest and add a line before it to name

8336
10:53:41.664 --> 10:53:46.056
the view, as such. Here's an example. This is part of last chapter's Snowflake

8337
10:53:46.056 --> 10:53:46.450
schema.

8338
10:53:46.450 --> 10:53:50.351
Let's say analysts at your company are often running analytics on the science

8339
10:53:50.351 --> 10:53:54.252
fiction genre. To help their workflow, you want to create a view specifically

8340
10:53:54.252 --> 10:53:58.406
dedicated to the science fiction genre and its associated book titles and authors.

8341
