8797
11:28:30.130 --> 11:28:35.806
At first think, it seems like having a variable that you can't change is a terrible

8798
11:28:35.806 --> 11:28:41.141
idea, and that we should always use vars over vals. But, in Scala, we actually

8799
11:28:41.141 --> 11:28:46.886
prefer immutable variables, vals, that is, where possible. Here's a few reasons why.

8800
11:28:46.886 --> 11:28:52.631
First, your data won't be changed accidentally, by an error in your program's logic,

8801
11:28:52.631 --> 11:28:57.760
for example. Preferring immutability is a form of defensive coding. Second,

8802
11:28:57.760 --> 11:29:03.150
Your code is easier to reason about since you don't have to mentally juggle all of

8803
11:29:03.150 --> 11:29:08.342
the places where your data can change. Third, immutability means you'll have to

8804
11:29:08.342 --> 11:29:13.337
write fewer unit tests to make sure your program works as expected. The main

8805
11:29:13.337 --> 11:29:18.596
drawback of immutability is the extra memory generated by copying objects. Since

8806
11:29:18.596 --> 11:29:24.051
vowels can't be reassigned, to change them you'll need to create a new object. Your

8807
11:29:24.051 --> 11:29:26.220
programs will be a little larger.

8808
11:29:26.220 --> 11:29:31.533
Fortunately, unless you are working with massive data structures and copying them

8809
11:29:31.533 --> 11:29:36.977
thousands of times a second, the pros often outweigh the cons. As you'll experience

8810
11:29:36.977 --> 11:29:42.224
throughout your Scala journey on DataCamp, Scala nudges us towards immutability.

8811
11:29:42.224 --> 11:29:47.472
Because we are forced to copy our objects each time we want to make a change, we

8812
11:29:47.472 --> 11:29:52.260
become much more conscious of how and when we change our program's state.

8813
11:29:52.260 --> 11:29:57.834
With this philosophy, we receive fewer possible states within our programs, fewer

8814
11:29:57.834 --> 11:30:03.477
defects, and code bases that are easier to maintain. We've defined vals, like four

