Using Spark transformations, we can create a data frame with these properties and

10037
13:08:46.690 --> 13:08:51.409
continue processing afterwards. A primary function of data cleaning is to verify all

10038
13:08:51.409 --> 13:08:55.734
data is in the expected format. Spark provides a built-in ability to validate

10039
13:08:55.734 --> 13:09:00.453
datasets with schemas. You may have used schemas before with databases or XML. Spark

10040
13:09:00.453 --> 13:09:04.946
is similar. A schema defines and validates the number and types of columns for a

10041
13:09:04.946 --> 13:09:09.328
given data frame. A schema can contain many different types of data, integers,

10042
13:09:09.328 --> 13:09:10.620
floats, dates, strings.

10043
13:09:10.620 --> 13:09:15.190
And even arrays or mapping structures. A defined schema allows Spark to filter out

10044
13:09:15.190 --> 13:09:19.760
data that doesn't conform during read, ensuring expected correctness. In addition,

10045
13:09:19.760 --> 13:09:24.386
schemas also have performance benefits. Normally, a data import will try to infer a

10046
13:09:24.386 --> 13:09:29.012
schema on read. This requires reading the data twice. Defining a schema limits this

10047
13:09:29.012 --> 13:09:33.470
to a single read operation. Here's an example schema to import the data from our

10048
13:09:33.470 --> 13:09:37.260
previous example. First, we'll import the PySpark.SQL.Types library.

10049
13:09:37.260 --> 13:09:41.747
Next, we'll define the actual struct type list of struct fields, containing an entry

10050
13:09:41.747 --> 13:09:46.128
for each field in the data. Each struct field consists of a field name, data type,

10051
13:09:46.128 --> 13:09:50.615
and whether the data can be null. Once our schema is defined, we can add it into our

10052
13:09:50.615 --> 13:09:54.996
spark.read.format.load call and process it against our data. The load method takes

10053
13:09:54.996 --> 13:09:59.430
two arguments, the file name and a schema. This is where we apply our schema to the

10054
13:09:59.430 --> 13:10:03.863
data being loaded. We've gone over a lot of information regarding data cleaning and

10055
13:10:03.863 --> 13:10:05.840
the importance of data frame schemas.

10056
13:10:05.840 --> 13:10:10.159
Let's put that information. Welcome back. We've had a quick discussion about data

10057
13:10:10.159 --> 13:10:14.425
cleaning, data types, and schemas. Let's move on to some further sport concepts,

10058
13:10:14.425 --> 13:10:18.584
immutability and lazy processing. Normally in Python and most other languages,

10059
13:10:18.584 --> 13:10:22.956
variables are fully mutable. The values can be changed at any given time, assuming

10060
13:10:22.956 --> 13:10:27.382
the scope of the variable is valid. While very flexible, this does present problems

10061
13:10:27.382 --> 13:10:31.647
anytime there are multiple concurrent components trying to modify the same data.

10062
13:10:31.647 --> 13:10:33.780
Most languages work around these issues.

10063
13:10:33.780 --> 13:10:38.492
using constructs like mutexes, semaphores, etc. This can add complexity, especially

10064
13:10:38.492 --> 13:10:43.034
with non-trivial programs. Unlike typical Python variables, Spark dataframes are

10065
13:10:43.034 --> 13:10:47.349
immutable. While not strictly required, immutability is often a component of

10066
13:10:47.349 --> 13:10:51.550
functional programming. We won't go into everything that implies here, but

10067
13:10:51.550 --> 13:10:56.262
understand that Spark is designed to use immutable objects. Practically, this means

10068
13:10:56.262 --> 13:11:00.860
Spark dataframes are defined once and are not modifiable after initialization. If

10069
13:11:00.860 --> 13:11:02.450
the variable name is reused,

10070
13:11:02.450 --> 13:11:06.807
The original data is removed, assuming it's not in use elsewhere, and the variable

10071
13:11:06.807 --> 13:11:11.269
name is reassigned to the new data. While this seems inefficient, it actually allows

10072
13:11:11.269 --> 13:11:15.732
Spark to share data between all cluster components. It can do so without worry about

10073
13:11:15.732 --> 13:11:20.142
concurrent data objects. This is a quick example of the immutability of data frames

10074
13:11:20.142 --> 13:11:20.620
in Spark.

10075
13:11:20.660 --> 13:11:25.281
It's okay if you don't understand the actual code. This example is more about the

10076
13:11:25.281 --> 13:11:29.673
concepts of what happens. First, we create a dataframe from a CSV file called

10077
13:11:29.673 --> 13:11:34.009
VoterData.CSV. This creates a new dataframe definition and assigns it to the

10078
13:11:34.009 --> 13:11:38.230
variable name Voter underscore DF. Once created, we want to do two further

10079
13:11:38.230 --> 13:11:42.737
operations. The first is to create a full year column by using a two-digit year

10080
13:11:42.737 --> 13:11:47.472
present in the dataset and adding 2000 to each entry. This does not actually change

10081
13:11:47.472 --> 13:11:48.670
the dataframe at all.

10082
13:11:48.670 --> 13:11:52.923
It copies the original definition, adds the transformation, and assigns it to the

10083
13:11:52.923 --> 13:11:57.228
voter underscore df variable name. Our second operation is similar. Now we want to

10084
13:11:57.228 --> 13:12:01.060
drop the original year column from the data frame. Again, this copies the

10085
13:12:01.060 --> 13:12:05.155
definition, adds a transformation, and reassigns the variable name to this new

10086
13:12:05.155 --> 13:12:09.250
object. The original objects are destroyed. Please note that the original year

10087
13:12:09.250 --> 13:12:13.503
column is now permanently gone from this instance, though not from the underlying

10088
13:12:13.503 --> 13:12:17.650
data. For example, you could simply reload it into a new data frame if desired.

10089
13:12:17.650 --> 13:12:22.221
You may be wondering how Spark does this so quickly, especially on large datasets.

10090
13:12:22.221 --> 13:12:26.736
Spark can do this because of something called lazy processing. Lazy processing in

10091
13:12:26.736 --> 13:12:31.418
Spark is the idea that very little actually happens until an action is performed. In

10092
13:12:31.418 --> 13:12:35.989
our previous example, we read a CSV file, added a new column, and deleted another.

10093
13:12:35.989 --> 13:12:40.671
The trick is that no data was actually read, added, or modified. We only updated the

10094
13:12:40.671 --> 13:12:44.350
instructions, aka transformations, for what we wanted Spark to do.

10095
13:12:44.350 --> 13:12:48.584
This functionality allows Spark to perform the most efficient set of operations to

10096
13:12:48.584 --> 13:12:52.870
get the desired result. The code example is the same as the previous slide but with

10097
13:12:52.870 --> 13:12:57.156
the added count method call. This classifies as an action in Spark and will process

10098
13:12:57.156 --> 13:13:01.338
all the transformation operations. These concepts can be a little tricky to grasp

10099
13:13:01.338 --> 13:13:05.418
without some examples. Let's practice these ideas. Welcome back! As we've seen,

10100
13:13:05.418 --> 13:13:09.394
Spark can read in text and CSV files. While this gives us access to many data

10101
13:13:09.394 --> 13:13:12.750
sources, it's not always the most convenient format to work with.

10102
13:13:12.750 --> 13:13:17.293
Let's take a look at a few problems with CSV files. Some common issues with CSV

10103
13:13:17.293 --> 13:13:21.778
files include the schema is not defined. There are no data types included, nor

10104
13:13:21.778 --> 13:13:26.205
column names beyond a header row. Using content containing a comma or another

10105
13:13:26.205 --> 13:13:30.000
delimiter requires escaping. Using the escape character within the

10106
13:13:30.537 --> 13:13:35.389
content requires even further escaping. The available encoding formats are limited

10107
13:13:35.389 --> 13:13:40.360
depending on the language used. In addition to the issues with CSV files in general,

10108
13:13:40.360 --> 13:13:45.154
Spark has some specific problems processing CSV data. CSV files are quite slow to

10109
13:13:45.154 --> 13:13:46.160
import and parse.

10110
13:13:46.160 --> 13:13:50.616
The files cannot be shared between workers during the import process. If no schema

10111
13:13:50.616 --> 13:13:54.855
is defined, all data must be read before a schema can be inferred. Spark has a

10112
13:13:54.855 --> 13:13:59.312
feature known as predicate pushdown. Basically, this is the idea of ordering tasks

10113
13:13:59.312 --> 13:14:03.659
to do the least amount of work. Filtering data prior to processing is one of the

10114
13:14:03.659 --> 13:14:08.170
primary optimizations of predicate pushdown. This drastically reduces the amount of

10115
13:14:08.170 --> 13:14:12.463
information that must be processed in large datasets. Unfortunately, you cannot

10116
13:14:12.463 --> 13:14:14.800
filter the CSV data via predicate pushdown.

10117
13:14:14.800 --> 13:14:19.617
Finally, Spark processes are often multi-step and may utilize an intermediate file

10118
13:14:19.617 --> 13:14:23.905
representation. These representations allow data to be used later without

10119
13:14:23.905 --> 13:14:28.605
regenerating the data from source. Using CSV would instead require a significant

10120
13:14:28.605 --> 13:14:32.952
amount of extra work defining schemas, encoding formats, etc. Parquet is a

10121
13:14:32.952 --> 13:14:37.769
compressed columnar data format developed for use in any Hadoop-based system. This

10122
13:14:37.769 --> 13:14:39.120
includes Spark, Hadoop,

10123
13:14:39.120 --> 13:14:43.408
Apache, and so forth. The Parquet format is structured with data accessible in

10124
13:14:43.408 --> 13:14:48.025
chunks, allowing efficient read-write operations without processing the entire file.

10125
13:14:48.025 --> 13:14:52.588
This structured format supports Spark's predicate pushdown functionality, providing

10126
13:14:52.588 --> 13:14:57.041
significant performance improvement. Finally, Parquet files automatically include

10127
13:14:57.041 --> 13:15:01.439
schema information and handle data encoding. This is perfect for intermediary or

10128
13:15:01.439 --> 13:15:06.001
on-disk representation of processed data. Note that Parquet files are a binary file

10129
13:15:06.001 --> 13:15:08.750
format and can only be used with the proper tools.

10130
13:15:08.750 --> 13:15:13.475
This is in contrast to CSV files, which can be edited with any text editor.

10131
13:15:13.475 --> 13:15:18.704
Interacting with Parquet files is very straightforward. To read a Parquet file into

10132
13:15:18.704 --> 13:15:23.869
a dataframe, you have two options. The first is using the spark.read.format method

10133
13:15:23.869 --> 13:15:28.846
we've seen previously. The dataframe, df equals spark.read.format, parquet, dot

10134
13:15:28.846 --> 13:15:33.571
load, filename, dot parquet. The second option is the shortcut version. The

10135
13:15:33.571 --> 13:15:37.540
dataframe, df equals spark.read.parquet, filename, dot parquet.

10136
13:15:37.540 --> 13:15:42.832
Typically, the shortcut version is the easiest to use, but you can use them

10137
13:15:42.832 --> 13:15:47.277
interchangeably. Writing Parquet files is similar, using either

10138
13:15:47.277 --> 13:15:53.204
df.write.format.parquet.save filename.parquet, or df.write.parquet filename.parquet.

10139
13:15:53.204 --> 13:15:58.990
The long-form versions of each permit extra option flags, such as when overwriting

10140
13:15:58.990 --> 13:16:04.000
an existing Parquet file. Parquet files have various uses within Spark.

10141
13:16:04.000 --> 13:16:08.759
We've discussed using them as an intermediate data format, but they are also perfect

10142
13:16:08.759 --> 13:16:13.291
for performing SQL operations. To perform a SQL query against a Parquet file, we

10143
13:16:13.291 --> 13:16:17.880
first need to create a data frame via the spark.read.parquet method. Once we have

10144
13:16:17.880 --> 13:16:22.639
the data frame, we can use the create or replace temp view method to add an alias of

10145
13:16:22.639 --> 13:16:27.285
the Parquet data as a SQL table. Finally, we run our query using normal SQL syntax

10146
13:16:27.285 --> 13:16:31.590
and the spark.sql method. In this case, we're looking for all flights with a

10147
13:16:31.590 --> 13:16:33.120
duration under 100 minutes.

10148
13:16:33.120 --> 13:16:36.889
Because we're using Parquet as the backing store, we get all the performance

10149
13:16:36.889 --> 13:16:41.055
benefits we've discussed previously, primarily defined schemas and the available use

10150
13:16:41.055 --> 13:16:45.122
of predicate pushdown. You've seen a bit about what a Parquet file is and why we'd

10151
13:16:45.122 --> 13:16:48.941
want to use them. Now let's practice this working. Welcome back. In the first

10152
13:16:48.941 --> 13:16:52.661
chapter, we've spent some time discussing the basics of Spark data and file

10153
13:16:52.661 --> 13:16:56.827
handling. Let's now take a look at how to use Spark column operations to clean data.

10154
13:16:56.827 --> 13:17:00.944
Before we discuss manipulating data frames in depth, let's talk about some of their

10155
13:17:00.944 --> 13:17:01.390
features.

10156
13:17:01.390 --> 13:17:05.886
Data frames are made up of rows and columns and are generally analogous to database

10157
13:17:05.886 --> 13:17:10.436
table. Data frames are immutable. Any change to the structure or content of the data

10158
13:17:10.436 --> 13:17:14.174
creates a new data frame. Data frames are modified through the use of

10159
13:17:14.174 --> 13:17:18.724
transformations. An example is the filter command to only return rows where the name

10160
13:17:18.724 --> 13:17:23.166
starts with the letter M. Another operation is select, in this case returning only

10161
13:17:23.166 --> 13:17:27.662
the name and position fields. There are many different transformations for use on a

10162
13:17:27.662 --> 13:17:30.750
data frame. They vary depending on what you'd like to do.

10163
13:17:30.750 --> 13:17:35.773
Some common transformations include the filter clause, which includes only rows that

10164
13:17:35.773 --> 13:17:40.496
satisfy the requirement defined in the argument. This is analogous to the where

10165
13:17:40.496 --> 13:17:45.101
clause in SQL. Spark includes a where alias you can use in place of filter if

10166
13:17:45.101 --> 13:17:49.824
desired. This call returns only rows where the vote occurred after January 1st,

10167
13:17:49.824 --> 13:17:54.309
2019. Another common option is the select method, which returns the columns

10168
13:17:54.309 --> 13:17:59.152
requested from the data frame. The with column method creates a new column in the

10169
13:17:59.152 --> 13:17:59.810
data frame.

10170
13:17:59.810 --> 13:18:04.128
The first argument is the name of the column, and the second is the command to

10171
13:18:04.128 --> 13:18:08.224
create it. In this case, we create a column called year with just the year

10172
13:18:08.224 --> 13:18:12.763
information. We can also use the drop method to remove a column from a data frame.

10173
13:18:12.763 --> 13:18:17.358
Among the most common operations used when cleaning a data frame, filtering lets us

10174
13:18:17.358 --> 13:18:21.786
use only the data matching our desired result. We can use filter for many tasks,

10175
13:18:21.786 --> 13:18:26.270
such as removing null values, removing odd entries, anything that doesn't fit our

10176
13:18:26.270 --> 13:18:27.100
desired format,

10177
13:18:27.100 --> 13:18:31.632
We can also split a data frame containing combined data, such as a syslog file. As

10178
13:18:31.632 --> 13:18:35.888
mentioned previously, use the filter method to return only rows that meet the

10179
13:18:35.888 --> 13:18:40.365
specified criteria. The contains function takes a string argument that the column

10180
13:18:40.365 --> 13:18:44.842
must have to return true. You can negate these results using the tilde character.

10181
13:18:44.842 --> 13:18:48.932
Some of the most common operations used in data cleaning are modifying and

10182
13:18:48.932 --> 13:18:52.801
converting strings. You will typically apply these to each column as a

10183
13:18:52.801 --> 13:18:53.630
transformation.

10184
13:18:53.630 --> 13:18:58.649
Many of these functions are in the spark.sql.functions library. For brevity, we'll

10185
13:18:58.649 --> 13:19:03.485
import it as the alias f. We use the withColumn function to create a new column

10186
13:19:03.485 --> 13:19:08.627
called upper, using pySpark.sql.functions.upper on the name column. The upper column

10187
13:19:08.627 --> 13:19:13.524
will contain uppercase versions of all names. We can create intermediary columns

10188
13:19:13.524 --> 13:19:18.360
that are only for processing. This is useful to clarify complex transformations

10189
13:19:18.360 --> 13:19:19.890
requiring multiple steps.

10190
13:19:19.890 --> 13:19:24.075
In this instance, we call the split function with the name of the column and the

10191
13:19:24.075 --> 13:19:28.470
space character to split on. This returns a list of words in a column called splits.

10192
13:19:28.550 --> 13:19:32.809
A very common operation is converting string data to a different type, such as

10193
13:19:32.809 --> 13:19:37.231
converting a string column to an integer. We use the cast function to perform the

10194
13:19:37.231 --> 13:19:41.654
conversion to an integer type. While performing data cleaning with Spark, you may

10195
13:19:41.654 --> 13:19:46.022
need to interact with array type columns. These are analogous to lists in normal

10196
13:19:46.022 --> 13:19:50.499
Python environments. One function we will use is size, which returns the number of

10197
13:19:50.499 --> 13:19:54.976
items present in the specified array type argument. Another commonly used function

10198
13:19:54.976 --> 13:19:56.450
for array types is getItem.

10199
13:19:56.450 --> 13:20:00.733
It takes an index argument and returns the item present at that index in the list

10200
13:20:00.733 --> 13:20:05.016
column. Spark has many more transformations and utility functions available. When

10201
13:20:05.016 --> 13:20:09.298
using Spark in production, make sure to reference the documentation for available

10202
13:20:09.298 --> 13:20:13.423
options. We've discussed some of the common operations used on Spark dataframe

10203
13:20:13.423 --> 13:20:17.600
columns. Let's practice. We've looked at some of the power available when using

10204
13:20:17.600 --> 13:20:21.882
Spark's functions to filter and modify our dataframes. Let's spend some time with

10205
13:20:21.882 --> 13:20:23.310
some more advanced options.

10206
13:20:23.310 --> 13:20:27.546
The dataframe transformations we've covered thus far are blanket transformations,

10207
13:20:27.546 --> 13:20:31.887
meaning they're applied regardless of the data. Often, you'll want to conditionally

10208
13:20:31.887 --> 13:20:36.280
change some aspect of the contents. Spark provides some built-in conditional clauses

10209
13:20:36.280 --> 13:20:40.203
which act similar to an if-then-else statement in a traditional programming

10210
13:20:40.203 --> 13:20:44.230
environment. While it is possible to perform a traditional if-then-else style

10211
13:20:44.230 --> 13:20:48.571
statement in Spark, it can lead to serious performance degradation as each row of a

10212
13:20:48.571 --> 13:20:50.820
dataframe would be evaluated independently.

10213
13:20:50.820 --> 13:20:55.548
Using the optimized built-in conditionals alleviates this. There are two components

10214
13:20:55.548 --> 13:21:00.219
to the conditional clauses, when and the optional otherwise. Let's look at them in

10215
13:21:00.219 --> 13:21:04.776
more depth. The when clause is a method available from the pyspark.sql.functions

10216
13:21:04.776 --> 13:21:09.390
library that is looking for two components, the if condition and what to do if it

10217
13:21:09.390 --> 13:21:14.175
evaluates to true. This is best seen from an example. Consider a data frame with the

10218
13:21:14.175 --> 13:21:18.732
name and age columns. We can actually add an extra argument to our select method

10219
13:21:18.732 --> 13:21:20.270
when using the when clause.

10220
13:21:20.270 --> 13:21:25.034
We select the df.name and df.age as usual. For the third argument, we'll define a

10221
13:21:25.034 --> 13:21:29.916
when conditional. If the age column is 18 or up, we'll add the string adult. If the

10222
13:21:29.916 --> 13:21:34.739
clause doesn't match, nothing is returned. Note that our return dataframe contains

10223
13:21:34.739 --> 13:21:39.680
an unnamed column we didn't define using with column. The select function can create

10224
13:21:39.680 --> 13:21:44.150
columns dynamically based on the arguments provided. Let's look at some more

10225
13:21:44.150 --> 13:21:49.032
examples. You can chain multiple when statements together, similar to an if-else-if

10226
13:21:49.032 --> 13:21:49.620
structure.

10227
13:21:49.620 --> 13:21:54.682
In this case, we define two when clauses and return adult or minor based on the age

10228
13:21:54.682 --> 13:21:59.805
column. You can chain as many when clauses together as required. In addition to when

10229
13:21:59.805 --> 13:22:04.746
is the otherwise clause. Otherwise is analogous to the else statement. It takes a

10230
13:22:04.746 --> 13:22:09.747
single argument, which is what to return in case the when clause or clauses do not

10231
13:22:09.747 --> 13:22:14.565
evaluate as true. In this example, we return adult when the age column is 18 or

10232
13:22:14.565 --> 13:22:16.700
higher. Otherwise, we return minor.

10233
13:22:16.700 --> 13:22:20.913
The resulting data frame is the same, but the method is different. While you can

10234
13:22:20.913 --> 13:22:25.336
have multiple when statements chained together, you can only have a single otherwise

10235
13:22:25.336 --> 13:22:29.759
per when chain. Let's try a couple of examples of using when and otherwise to model.

10236
13:22:29.759 --> 13:22:34.025
We've looked at the built-in functions in Spark, and I've had great results using

10237
13:22:34.025 --> 13:22:38.448
these. But let's consider what you would do if you needed to apply some custom logic

10238
13:22:38.448 --> 13:22:42.871
to your data cleaning processes. A user-defined function, or UDF, is a Python method

10239
13:22:42.871 --> 13:22:45.820
that the user writes to perform a specific bit of logic.

10240
13:22:45.820 --> 13:22:50.215
Once written, the method is called via the PySpark.sql.functions.udf method. The

10241
13:22:50.215 --> 13:22:54.720
result is stored as a variable and can be called as a normal Spark function. Let's

10242
13:22:54.720 --> 13:22:59.280
look at a couple examples. Here is a fairly trivial example to illustrate how a UDF

10243
13:22:59.280 --> 13:23:03.181
is defined. First, we define a Python function. We'll call our function

10244
13:23:03.181 --> 13:23:07.576
reverseString with an argument called myStir. We'll use some Python shorthand to

10245
13:23:07.576 --> 13:23:11.971
reverse the string and return it. Don't worry about understanding how the return

10246
13:23:11.971 --> 13:23:12.850
statement works.

10247
13:23:12.850 --> 13:23:17.814
only that it will reverse the lettering of whatever is fed into it. For example,

10248
13:23:17.814 --> 13:23:23.026
help becomes P-L-E-H. The next step is to wrap the function and store it on variable

10249
13:23:23.026 --> 13:23:27.679
for later use. We'll use the pyspark.sql.functions.udf method. It takes two

10250
13:23:27.679 --> 13:23:32.829
arguments, the name of the method you just defined, and the Spark data type it will

10251
13:23:32.829 --> 13:23:37.979
return. This can be any of the options in pyspark.sql.types, and can even be a more

10252
13:23:37.979 --> 13:23:41.330
complex type, including a fully defined schema object.

10253
13:23:41.330 --> 13:23:45.949
Most often, you'll return either a simple object type, or perhaps an array type.

10254
13:23:45.949 --> 13:23:50.799
We'll call UDF with our new method name, and use the string type, then store this as

10255
13:23:50.799 --> 13:23:55.533
UDF reverse string. Finally, we use our new UDF to add a column to the userDF data

10256
13:23:55.533 --> 13:24:00.267
frame within the with column method. Note that we pass the column we're interested

10257
13:24:00.267 --> 13:24:05.117
in as the argument to UDF reverse string. The UDF function is called for each row of

10258
13:24:05.117 --> 13:24:09.678
the data frame. Under the hood, the UDF function takes the value stored for the

10259
13:24:09.678 --> 13:24:10.660
specified column.

10260
13:24:10.660 --> 13:24:15.363
per row, and passes it to the Python method. The result is fed back to the resulting

10261
13:24:15.363 --> 13:24:19.730
data frame. Another quick example is using a function that does not require an

10262
13:24:19.730 --> 13:24:24.376
argument. We're defining our sortingcap function to return one of the letters G, H,

10263
13:24:24.376 --> 13:24:28.967
R, or S at random. We still create our UDFwrap function and define the return type

10264
13:24:28.967 --> 13:24:33.502
as string type. The primary difference is calling the function, this time without

10265
13:24:33.502 --> 13:24:38.037
passing in an argument as it is not required. As always, the best way to learn is

10266
13:24:38.037 --> 13:24:39.940
practice. Let's create some useful

10267
13:24:39.940 --> 13:24:44.069
Welcome back to our discussion about modifying and cleaning data frames. We've

10268
13:24:44.069 --> 13:24:48.303
discussed various transformations and methods to modify our data, but we haven't

10269
13:24:48.303 --> 13:24:52.591
covered much about how Spark actually processes the data. Let's look at that now.

10270
13:24:52.591 --> 13:24:57.037
Spark breaks data frames into partitions, or chunks of data. These partitions can be

10271
13:24:57.037 --> 13:25:01.483
automatically defined, enlarged, shrunk, and can differ greatly based on the type of

10272
13:25:01.483 --> 13:25:05.876
sport cluster being used. The size of the partition does vary, but generally try to

10273
13:25:05.876 --> 13:25:07.570
keep your partition sizes equal.

10274
13:25:07.570 --> 13:25:12.011
We'll discuss more about optimizing partitioning and cluster details later on. For

10275
13:25:12.011 --> 13:25:16.560
now, let's assume that each partition is handled independently. This is part of what

10276
13:25:16.560 --> 13:25:21.055
provides the performance levels and horizontal scaling ability in Spark. If a Spark

10277
13:25:21.055 --> 13:25:25.441
node doesn't need to compete for resources nor consult with other Spark nodes for

10278
13:25:25.441 --> 13:25:29.990
answers, it can reliably schedule the processing for the best performance. In Spark,

10279
13:25:29.990 --> 13:25:34.160
any transformation operation is lazy. It's more like a recipe than a command.

10280
13:25:34.160 --> 13:25:38.851
It defines what should be done with a data frame rather than actually doing it. Most

10281
13:25:38.851 --> 13:25:43.319
operations at Spark are actually transformations, including with column, select,

10282
13:25:43.319 --> 13:25:47.898
filter, and so forth. The set of transformations you define are only executed when

10283
13:25:47.898 --> 13:25:52.533
you run a Spark action. This includes count, write, etc. Anything that requires the

10284
13:25:52.533 --> 13:25:56.610
transformations to be run to properly obtain an answer. Spark can reorder

10285
13:25:56.610 --> 13:25:58.900
transformations for the best performance.

10286
13:25:58.900 --> 13:26:03.483
Usually, this isn't noticeable, but can occasionally cause unexpected behavior, such

10287
13:26:03.483 --> 13:26:07.684
as IDs not being added until after other transformations have completed. This

10288
13:26:07.684 --> 13:26:12.103
doesn't actually cause a problem, but the data can look unusual if you don't know

10289
13:26:12.103 --> 13:26:16.632
what to expect. Relational databases tend to have a field used to identify the row,

10290
13:26:16.632 --> 13:26:21.160
whether it is for an actual relationship reference or just for data identification.

10291
13:26:21.160 --> 13:26:25.689
These IDs are typically an integer that increases in value, is sequential, and most

10292
13:26:25.689 --> 13:26:26.780
importantly, unique.

10293
13:26:26.780 --> 13:26:31.058
The problem with these IDs is they're not very parallel in nature. Given that the

10294
13:26:31.058 --> 13:26:35.230
values are given out sequentially, if there are multiple workers, they must all

10295
13:26:35.230 --> 13:26:39.244
refer to a common source for the next entry. This is okay in a single-server

10296
13:26:39.244 --> 13:26:43.417
environment, but in a distributed platform such as Spark, it creates some undue

10297
13:26:43.417 --> 13:26:47.853
bottlenecks. Let's take a look at how to generate IDs in Spark. Spark has a built-in

10298
13:26:47.853 --> 13:26:52.237
function called monotonically increasing ID, designed to provide an integer ID that

10299
13:26:52.237 --> 13:26:53.980
increases in value and is unique.

10300
13:26:53.980 --> 13:26:58.794
These IDs are not necessarily sequential. There can be gaps, often quite large,

10301
13:26:58.794 --> 13:27:03.852
between values. Unlike a normal relational ID, Spark's is completely parallel. Each

10302
13:27:03.852 --> 13:27:08.910
partition is allocated up to 8 billion IDs that can be assigned. Notice that the ID

10303
13:27:08.910 --> 13:27:13.358
fields in the sample table are integers, increasing in value, but are not

10304
13:27:13.358 --> 13:27:18.416
sequential. It's a little out of scope, but the IDs are a 64-bit number effectively

10305
13:27:18.416 --> 13:27:21.280
split into groups based on the Spark partition.

10306
13:27:21.280 --> 13:27:26.452
Each group contains 8.4 billion IDs, and there are 2.1 billion possible groups, none

10307
13:27:26.452 --> 13:27:31.439
of which overlap. There's a lot of nuance to how partitions and the monotonically

10308
13:27:31.439 --> 13:27:32.670
increasing IDs work.

10309
13:27:32.710 --> 13:27:37.027
Remembering that Spark is lazy, it often helps in troubleshooting what can happen.

10310
13:27:37.027 --> 13:27:41.291
Operations are often out of order, especially if joins are involved. It's best to

10311
13:27:41.291 --> 13:27:45.713
test your transformations. We've discussed a lot of detail in this lesson. Let's now

10312
13:27:45.713 --> 13:27:49.925
take a look at a few exercises that will help solidify. Now that we've discussed

10313
13:27:49.925 --> 13:27:54.242
some data cleaning tasks using Spark, let's look at how to improve the performance

10314
13:27:54.242 --> 13:27:56.190
of running those tasks using caching.

10315
13:27:56.190 --> 13:28:00.495
Caching in Spark refers to storing the results of a data frame in memory or on disk

10316
13:28:00.495 --> 13:28:04.593
of the processing nodes in a cluster. Caching improves the speed for subsequent

10317
13:28:04.593 --> 13:28:08.846
transformations or actions as the data likely no longer needs to be retrieved from

10318
13:28:08.846 --> 13:28:12.943
the original data source. Using caching reduces the resource utilization of the

10319
13:28:12.943 --> 13:28:17.248
cluster. There is less need to access the storage, networking, and CPU of the Spark

10320
13:28:17.248 --> 13:28:21.242
nodes as the data is likely already present. There are a few disadvantages of

10321
13:28:21.242 --> 13:28:22.850
caching you should be aware of.

10322
13:28:22.850 --> 13:28:27.068
Very large datasets may not fit in the memory reserved for cached dataframes.

10323
13:28:27.068 --> 13:28:31.560
Depending on the later transformations requested, the cache may not do anything to

10324
13:28:31.560 --> 13:28:36.051
help performance. If a dataset does not stay cached in memory, it may be persisted

10325
13:28:36.051 --> 13:28:40.653
to disk. Depending on the disk configuration of a support cluster, this may not be a

10326
13:28:40.653 --> 13:28:45.144
large performance improvement. If you're reading from a local network resource and

10327
13:28:45.144 --> 13:28:49.581
have slow local disk I.O, it may be better to avoid caching the objects. Finally,

10328
13:28:49.581 --> 13:28:52.320
the lifetime of a cached object is not guaranteed.

10329
13:28:52.320 --> 13:28:56.766
Spark handles regenerating data frames for you automatically, but this can cause

10330
13:28:56.766 --> 13:29:01.378
delays in processing. Caching is incredibly useful, but only if you plan to use the

10331
13:29:01.378 --> 13:29:06.046
data frame again. If you only need it for a single task, it's not worth caching. The

10332
13:29:06.046 --> 13:29:10.547
best way to gauge performance with caching is to test various configurations. Try

10333
13:29:10.547 --> 13:29:15.103
caching your data frames at various points in the processing cycle and check if it

10334
13:29:15.103 --> 13:29:19.660
improves your processing time. Try to cache in memory or fast NVMe or SSD storage.

10335
13:29:19.660 --> 13:29:24.002
While still slower than main memory, modern SSD-based storage is drastically faster

10336
13:29:24.002 --> 13:29:28.029
than spinning disk. Local spinning hard drives can still be useful if you are

10337
13:29:28.029 --> 13:29:32.266
processing large data frames that require a lot of steps to degenerate or must be

10338
13:29:32.266 --> 13:29:36.608
accessed over the internet. Testing this is crucial. If normal caching doesn't seem

10339
13:29:36.608 --> 13:29:41.002
to work, try creating intermediate parquet representations like we did in Chapter 1.

10340
13:29:41.002 --> 13:29:45.238
These can provide a checkpoint in case a job fails mid-task and can still be used

10341
13:29:45.238 --> 13:29:47.540
with caching to further improve performance.

10342
13:29:47.540 --> 13:29:52.017
Finally, you can manually stop caching a dataframe when you're finished with it.

10343
13:29:52.017 --> 13:29:56.718
This frees up cache resources for other dataframes. Implementing caching in Spark is

10344
13:29:56.718 --> 13:30:01.420
simple. The primary way is to call the function cache on a dataframe object prior to

10345
13:30:01.420 --> 13:30:06.009
a given action. It requires no arguments. One example is creating a dataframe from

10346
13:30:06.009 --> 13:30:10.654
some original CSV data. Prior to running a count on the data, we call cache to tell

10347
13:30:10.654 --> 13:30:15.131
Spark to store it in cache. Another option is to call cache separately. Here, we

10348
13:30:15.131 --> 13:30:17.090
create an ID in one transformation.

10349
13:30:17.090 --> 13:30:21.578
Then we call cache on the data frame. When we call the show action, the voter df

10350
13:30:21.578 --> 13:30:26.178
data frame will be cached. If you're following closely, this means that cache is a

10351
13:30:26.178 --> 13:30:30.891
Spark transformation. Nothing is actually cached until an action is called. A couple

10352
13:30:30.891 --> 13:30:35.267
other options are available with caching on Spark. To check if a data frame is

10353
13:30:35.267 --> 13:30:39.867
cached, use the isCached boolean property, which returns true, as in this case, or

10354
13:30:39.867 --> 13:30:44.411
false. To uncache a data frame, we call unpersist with no arguments. This removes

10355
13:30:44.411 --> 13:30:45.870
the object from the cache.

10356
13:30:45.870 --> 13:30:50.282
We've discussed caching in depth. We've just finished working with improving import

10357
13:30:50.282 --> 13:30:54.589
performance in Spark. Let's take a look at cluster configurations. Spark has many

10358
13:30:54.589 --> 13:30:59.001
available configuration settings controlling all aspects of the installation. These

10359
13:30:59.001 --> 13:31:03.467
configurations can be modified to best match the specific needs for the cluster. The

10360
13:31:03.467 --> 13:31:07.401
configurations are available in the configuration files, via the Spark web

10361
13:31:07.401 --> 13:31:11.866
interface, and via the runtime code. Our test cluster is only accessible via command

10362
13:31:11.866 --> 13:31:13.780
shell, so we'll use the last option.

10363
13:31:13.780 --> 13:31:18.756
To read a configuration setting, call spark.conf.get with the name of the setting as

10364
13:31:18.756 --> 13:31:23.732
the argument. To write a configuration setting, call spark.conf.set with the name of

10365
13:31:23.732 --> 13:31:28.530
the setting and the actual value as the function arguments. Spark deployments can

10366
13:31:28.530 --> 13:31:33.506
vary depending on the exact needs of the users. One component of a deployment is the

10367
13:31:33.506 --> 13:31:38.363
cluster management mechanism. Spark clusters can be single node clusters deploying

10368
13:31:38.363 --> 13:31:43.280
all components on a single system, physical, VM, or container, standalone clusters,

10369
13:31:43.280 --> 13:31:47.664
With dedicated machines as the driver and workers. Or managed clusters, meaning that

10370
13:31:47.664 --> 13:31:51.892
the cluster components are handled by a third-party cluster manager such as Yarn,

10371
13:31:51.892 --> 13:31:55.911
Mesos, or Kubernetes. In this course, we're using a single node cluster. Your

10372
13:31:55.911 --> 13:32:00.295
production environment can vary wildly, but we'll discuss standalone clusters as the

10373
13:32:00.295 --> 13:32:04.523
concepts flow across all management types. If you recall, there is one driver per

10374
13:32:04.523 --> 13:32:08.386
sport cluster. The driver is responsible for several things, including the

10375
13:32:08.386 --> 13:32:12.352
following. Handling task assignment to the various nodes or processes in the

10376
13:32:12.352 --> 13:32:12.770
cluster.

10377
13:32:12.770 --> 13:32:16.960
The driver monitors the state of all processes and tasks and handles any task

10378
13:32:16.960 --> 13:32:21.314
retries. The driver is also responsible for consolidating results from the other

10379
13:32:21.314 --> 13:32:25.831
processes in the cluster. The driver handles any access to shared data and verifies

10380
13:32:25.831 --> 13:32:29.913
each worker process has the necessary resources, code, data, etc. Given the

10381
13:32:29.913 --> 13:32:34.267
importance of the driver, it is often worth increasing the specifications of the

10382
13:32:34.267 --> 13:32:38.784
node compared to the other systems. Doubling the memory compared to the other nodes

10383
13:32:38.784 --> 13:32:39.600
is recommended.

10384
13:32:39.600 --> 13:32:44.045
This is useful for task monitoring and data consolidation tasks. As with all Spark

10385
13:32:44.045 --> 13:32:48.598
systems, fast local storage is useful for running Spark in an ideal setup. The Spark

10386
13:32:48.598 --> 13:32:53.043
worker handles running tasks assigned by the driver and communicates those results

10387
13:32:53.043 --> 13:32:57.542
back to the driver. Ideally, the worker has a copy of all code, data, and access to

10388
13:32:57.542 --> 13:33:01.770
the necessary resources required to complete a given task. If any of these are

10389
13:33:01.770 --> 13:33:06.215
unavailable, the worker must pause to obtain the resources. When sizing a cluster,

10390
13:33:06.215 --> 13:33:07.950
there are a few recommendations.

10391
13:33:07.950 --> 13:33:12.206
Depending on the type of task, more worker nodes is often better than larger nodes.

10392
13:33:12.206 --> 13:33:16.513
This can be especially obvious during import and export operations as there are more

10393
13:33:16.513 --> 13:33:20.000
machines available to do the work. As with everything in Spark, test

10394
13:33:20.449 --> 13:33:24.725
various configurations to find the correct balance for your workload. Assuming a

10395
13:33:24.725 --> 13:33:29.001
cloud environment, 16 worker nodes may complete a job in an hour and cost $50 in

10396
13:33:29.001 --> 13:33:33.277
resources. An eight worker configuration might take one and a quarter hours, but

10397
13:33:33.277 --> 13:33:37.660
don't cost only half as much. Finally, workers can make use of fast local storage.

10398
13:33:37.660 --> 13:33:41.967
SSD or NVMe, forecaching, intermediate files, etc. Now that we've discussed cluster

10399
13:33:41.967 --> 13:33:46.171
sizing and configuration, let's practice working with them. We've discussed Spark

10400
13:33:46.171 --> 13:33:50.063
clusters and improving import performance. Let's look at how to improve the

10401
13:33:50.063 --> 13:33:54.214
performance of Spark tasks in general. To understand performance applications of

10402
13:33:54.214 --> 13:33:58.573
Spark, you must be able to see what it's doing under the hood. The easiest way to do

10403
13:33:58.573 --> 13:34:01.220
this is to use the explain function on a dataframe.

10404
13:34:01.220 --> 13:34:05.369
This example is taken from an earlier exercise, simply requesting a single column

10405
13:34:05.369 --> 13:34:09.569
and running distinct against it. The result is the estimated plan that will be run

10406
13:34:09.569 --> 13:34:13.820
to generate the results from the data frame. Don't worry about the specifics of the

10407
13:34:13.820 --> 13:34:16.330
plan yet, just remember how to view it if needed.

10408
13:34:16.500 --> 13:34:20.607
Spark distributes data amongst the various nodes in the cluster. A side effect of

10409
13:34:20.607 --> 13:34:24.867
this is what is known as shuffling. Shuffling is moving of data fragments to various

10410
13:34:24.867 --> 13:34:29.126
workers as required to complete certain tasks. Shuffling is useful and hides overall

10411
13:34:29.126 --> 13:34:33.335
complexity from the user. The user doesn't have to know which nodes have what data.

10412
13:34:33.335 --> 13:34:37.594
That being said, it can be slow to complete the necessary transfers, especially if a

10413
13:34:37.594 --> 13:34:41.549
few nodes require all the data. Shuffling lowers the overall throughput of the

10414
13:34:41.549 --> 13:34:45.200
cluster as the workers must spend time waiting for the data to transfer.

10415
13:34:45.200 --> 13:34:49.235
This limits the amount of available workers for the remaining tasks in the system.

10416
13:34:49.235 --> 13:34:53.319
Shuffling is often a necessary component, but it's helpful to try to minimize it as

10417
13:34:53.319 --> 13:34:57.206
much as possible. It can be tricky to remove shuffling operations entirely, but

10418
13:34:57.206 --> 13:35:01.339
there are a few things that can limit it. The dataframe repartition function takes a

10419
13:35:01.339 --> 13:35:05.472
single argument of the number of partitions requested. We've used this in an earlier

10420
13:35:05.472 --> 13:35:09.457
chapter to illustrate the effect of partitions with a monotonically increasing ID

10421
13:35:09.457 --> 13:35:09.900
function.

10422
13:35:09.900 --> 13:35:14.281
Repartitioning requires a full shuffle of data between nodes and processes and it's

10423
13:35:14.281 --> 13:35:18.662
quite costly. If you need to reduce the number of partitions, use the COLS function

10424
13:35:18.662 --> 13:35:22.515
instead. It takes a number of partitions smaller than the current one and

10425
13:35:22.515 --> 13:35:26.948
consolidates the data without requiring a full data shuffle. Note, calling COLS with

10426
13:35:26.948 --> 13:35:31.329
a larger number of partitions does not actually do anything. The JOIN function is a

10427
13:35:31.329 --> 13:35:33.810
great use of Spark and provides a lot of power.

10428
13:35:33.810 --> 13:35:37.588
Calling Join indiscriminately can often cause shuffle operations, leading to

10429
13:35:37.588 --> 13:35:41.565
increased cluster load and slower processing times. To avoid some of the shuffle

10430
13:35:41.565 --> 13:35:45.641
operations when joining Spark dataframes, you can use the Broadcast function. I'll

10431
13:35:45.641 --> 13:35:49.618
talk about this more in a moment. Finally, an important note about data cleaning

10432
13:35:49.618 --> 13:35:53.644
operations is remembering to optimize for what matters. The speed of your initial

10433
13:35:53.644 --> 13:35:57.273
code may be perfectly acceptable, and time may be better spent elsewhere.

10434
13:35:57.273 --> 13:36:01.250
Broadcasting in Spark is a method to provide a copy of an object to each worker.

10435
13:36:01.250 --> 13:36:05.487
When each worker has its own copy of the data, there is less need for communication

10436
13:36:05.487 --> 13:36:09.674
between nodes. This limits data shuffles, and it's more likely a node will fulfill

10437
13:36:09.674 --> 13:36:13.809
tasks independently. Using broadcasting can drastically speed up join operations,

10438
13:36:13.809 --> 13:36:18.097
especially if one of the data frames being joined is much smaller than the other. To

10439
13:36:18.097 --> 13:36:21.517
implement broadcasting, you must import the broadcast function from

10440
13:36:21.517 --> 13:36:25.653
PySpark.SQL.functions. Once imported, simply call the broadcast function with the

10441
13:36:25.653 --> 13:36:27.950
name of the data frame you wish to broadcast.

10442
13:36:27.950 --> 13:36:32.202
Note, broadcasting can slow operations when using very small dataframes, or if you

10443
13:36:32.202 --> 13:36:36.403
broadcast the larger dataframe in a join. Spark will often optimize this for you,

10444
13:36:36.403 --> 13:36:40.655
but as usual, run tests in your environment for best performance. We've looked how

10445
13:36:40.655 --> 13:36:44.596
to limit shuffling and implement broadcasting for dataframes. Let's practice

10446
13:36:44.596 --> 13:36:48.174
utilizing it. We've spent most of this course working with individual

10447
13:36:48.174 --> 13:36:52.323
transformations and actions to clean data in Spark, but data is rarely so simple

10448
13:36:52.323 --> 13:36:56.160
that a couple transformations or actions can prepare it for real analysis.

10449
13:36:56.160 --> 13:37:00.650
Let's look now at data pipelines. Data pipelines are simply the set of steps needed

10450
13:37:00.650 --> 13:37:05.194
to move from an input data source or sources and convert it to the desired output. A

10451
13:37:05.194 --> 13:37:09.521
data pipeline can consist of any number of steps or components and can span many

10452
13:37:09.521 --> 13:37:13.849
systems. For our purposes, we'll be setting up a data pipeline within Spark, but

10453
13:37:13.849 --> 13:37:18.068
realize that a full production data pipeline will likely communicate with many

10454
13:37:18.068 --> 13:37:22.504
systems. Much like Spark in general, a data pipeline typically consists of inputs,

10455
13:37:22.504 --> 13:37:25.100
transformations, and the outputs of those steps.

10456
13:37:25.100 --> 13:37:29.459
In addition, there is often validation and analysis steps before delivery of the

10457
13:37:29.459 --> 13:37:34.036
data to the next user. An input can be any of the data types we've looked at so far,

10458
13:37:34.036 --> 13:37:38.449
including CSV, JSON, text, etc. It can be from the local file system, or from web

10459
13:37:38.449 --> 13:37:43.026
services, APIs, databases, and so on. The basic idea is to read the data into a data

10460
13:37:43.026 --> 13:37:47.494
frame, as we've done previously. Once we have the data in a data frame, we need to

10461
13:37:47.494 --> 13:37:51.635
transform it in some fashion. You've done the individual steps several times

10462
13:37:51.635 --> 13:37:53.760
throughout this course, adding columns,

10463
13:37:53.760 --> 13:37:58.262
filtering rows, performing calculations as needed. In our previous examples, we've

10464
13:37:58.262 --> 13:38:02.874
only done one or two of these steps at a time, but a pipeline can consist of as many

10465
13:38:02.874 --> 13:38:07.321
of these steps as needed so we can format the data into our desired output. After

10466
13:38:07.321 --> 13:38:11.768
we've defined our transformations, we need to output the data into a usable form.

10467
13:38:11.768 --> 13:38:16.105
You've already written files out to CSV or Parquet format, but it could include

10468
13:38:16.105 --> 13:38:20.607
multiple copies with various formats, or instead write the output to a database, a

10469
13:38:20.607 --> 13:38:21.540
web service, etc.

10470
13:38:21.540 --> 13:38:26.146
The last two steps vary greatly depending on your needs. We'll discuss validation in

10471
13:38:26.146 --> 13:38:30.751
a later lesson, but the idea is to run some form of testing on the data to verify it

10472
13:38:30.751 --> 13:38:35.302
is as expected. Analysis is often the final step before handing the data off to the

10473
13:38:35.302 --> 13:38:35.850
next user.

10474
13:38:36.050 --> 13:38:40.315
This can include things such as row counts, specific calculations, or pretty much

10475
13:38:40.315 --> 13:38:44.737
anything that makes it easier for the user to consume the dataset. It's important to

10476
13:38:44.737 --> 13:38:49.055
note that in Spark, a data pipeline is not a formally defined object, but rather a

10477
13:38:49.055 --> 13:38:53.372
concept. This is different than if you've used the pipeline object in spark.ml. If

10478
13:38:53.372 --> 13:38:57.742
you haven't, don't worry, it's not needed for the scores. For our purposes, a Spark

10479
13:38:57.742 --> 13:39:02.112
pipeline is all the normal code required to complete a task. In this example, we're

10480
13:39:02.112 --> 13:39:04.850
doing the various tasks required to define a schema,

10481
13:39:04.850 --> 13:39:09.163
Read a data file, add an ID, then write out two separate data types. The task could

10482
13:39:09.163 --> 13:39:13.269
be much more complex, but the concept is usually the same. When you look at the

10483
13:39:13.269 --> 13:39:17.427
components, a data pipeline in Spark is fairly simple, but can be very powerful.

10484
13:39:17.427 --> 13:39:21.584
Let's start working on a more elaborate. We've worked with many aspects of Spark

10485
13:39:21.584 --> 13:39:25.690
when it comes to data cleaning operations. Let's look at how to use some of the

10486
13:39:25.690 --> 13:39:29.899
methods we've learned to parse unconventional data. When reading data into Spark,

10487
13:39:29.899 --> 13:39:32.030
you're rarely given a fully uniform file.

10488
13:39:32.030 --> 13:39:36.588
Often there is content that needs to be removed or reformatted. Some common issues

10489
13:39:36.588 --> 13:39:41.146
include incorrect data consisting of empty rows, commented lines, headers, or even

10490
13:39:41.146 --> 13:39:45.592
rows that don't match the intended schema. Real-world data often includes nested

10491
13:39:45.592 --> 13:39:50.206
structures, including columns that use different delimiters. This could include the

10492
13:39:50.206 --> 13:39:54.874
primary columns separated via a comma, but including some components separated via a

10493
13:39:54.874 --> 13:39:59.488
semicolon. Real data often won't fit into a tabular format, sometimes consisting of

10494
13:39:59.488 --> 13:40:01.600
a differing number of columns per row.

10495
13:40:01.600 --> 13:40:06.007
There are various ways to parse data in all of these situations. The way you choose

10496
13:40:06.007 --> 13:40:10.468
will depend on your specific needs. We are focusing on CSV data for this course, but

10497
13:40:10.468 --> 13:40:14.769
the general scenarios described apply to other formats as well. For this chapter,

10498
13:40:14.769 --> 13:40:19.017
we're going to use the Stanford ImageNet annotations, which focus on finding and

10499
13:40:19.017 --> 13:40:23.372
identifying dogs in various ImageNet images. The annotations provide a list of all

10500
13:40:23.372 --> 13:40:27.620
identified dogs in an image, including when multiple dogs are in the same image.

10501
13:40:27.620 --> 13:40:32.188
Other metadata is included, including the folder within the ImageNet dataset, the

10502
13:40:32.188 --> 13:40:36.701
image dimensions, and the bounding box or boxes of the dogs in the image. In the

10503
13:40:36.701 --> 13:40:41.213
example rows, we have the folder names, the ImageNet image reference, width, and

10504
13:40:41.213 --> 13:40:45.950
height. Then there is the image data for the type of dog or dogs in each image. Each

10505
13:40:45.950 --> 13:40:50.688
breed column consists of the breed name and the bounding box in the image. The first

10506
13:40:50.688 --> 13:40:52.380
row contains one Newfoundland.

10507
13:40:52.380 --> 13:40:56.658
But notice that the second row actually has two bull mastiffs identified and an

10508
13:40:56.658 --> 13:41:01.207
additional column defined. Spark's CSV parser can handle many common data issues via

10509
13:41:01.207 --> 13:41:05.430
optional parameters. Blake lines are automatically removed unless specifically

10510
13:41:05.430 --> 13:41:09.763
instructed otherwise when using the CSV parsing. Comments can be removed with an

10511
13:41:09.763 --> 13:41:14.311
optional named argument, comment, and specifying the character that any comment line

10512
13:41:14.311 --> 13:41:18.427
would be defined by. Note that this handles lines that begin with a specific

10513
13:41:18.427 --> 13:41:18.860
comment.

10514
13:41:18.860 --> 13:41:23.218
parsing more complex comment usage requires more involved procedures. Header rows

10515
13:41:23.218 --> 13:41:27.738
can be parsed via an optional parameter named header and set to true or false. If no

10516
13:41:27.738 --> 13:41:32.258
schema is defined, column names will be initially set as defined by the header. If a

10517
13:41:32.258 --> 13:41:36.670
schema is defined, the row is not used as data, but the header names are otherwise

10518
13:41:36.670 --> 13:41:37.100
ignored.

10519
13:41:37.170 --> 13:41:41.626
When importing CSV data into Spark, it will automatically create data frame columns

10520
13:41:41.626 --> 13:41:46.081
if it can. It will split a row of text from the CSV on a defined separator argument

10521
13:41:46.081 --> 13:41:50.483
named sep. If sep is not defined, it will default to using a comma. The CSV parser

10522
13:41:50.483 --> 13:41:54.724
will still succeed in parsing data if the separator character is not within the

10523
13:41:54.724 --> 13:41:59.019
string. It will store the entire row in a column named underscore C0 by default.

10524
13:41:59.019 --> 13:42:03.421
Using this trick allows parsing of nested or complex data. We'll look at this more

10525
13:42:03.421 --> 13:42:06.910
later on. Let's practice working with this data and extending it.

10526
13:42:06.910 --> 13:42:11.298
Welcome back! Validation is one step of a data pipeline we haven't covered yet, but

10527
13:42:11.298 --> 13:42:15.579
it is very important in verifying the quality of the data we're delivering. Let's

10528
13:42:15.579 --> 13:42:19.703
look at how to implement validation steps in a data cleaning pipeline. In this

10529
13:42:19.703 --> 13:42:23.985
context, validation is verifying that a dataset complies with an expected format.

10530
13:42:23.985 --> 13:42:28.372
This can include verifying the number of rows and columns as expected. For example,

10531
13:42:28.372 --> 13:42:32.813
is the row count within 2% of the previous month's row count? Another common test is

10532
13:42:32.813 --> 13:42:36.460
do the data types match? If not specifically validated with a schema,

10533
13:42:36.460 --> 13:42:41.133
Does the content meet the requirements? Only nine characters or less, etc. Finally,

10534
13:42:41.133 --> 13:42:45.861
you can validate against more complex rules. This includes verifying that the values

10535
13:42:45.861 --> 13:42:50.590
of a set of sensor readings are within physically possible quantities. One technique

10536
13:42:50.590 --> 13:42:55.263
used to validate data in Spark is using joins to verify the content of a data frame

10537
13:42:55.263 --> 13:42:59.936
matches a known set. Validating via a join will compare data against a set of known

10538
13:42:59.936 --> 13:43:03.820
values. This could be a list of known IDs, companies, addresses, etc.

10539
13:43:03.820 --> 13:43:08.336
Joins make it easy to determine if data is present in a set. This can be only rows

10540
13:43:08.336 --> 13:43:12.796
that are in one dataframe, present in both, or present in neither. Joins are also

10541
13:43:12.796 --> 13:43:17.422
comparatively fast, especially versus validating individual rows against a long list

10542
13:43:17.422 --> 13:43:22.048
of entries. The simplest example of this is using an inner join of two dataframes to

10543
13:43:22.048 --> 13:43:26.564
validate the data. A new dataframe, parsedDF, is loaded from a given parquet file.

10544
13:43:26.564 --> 13:43:30.859
The second dataframe is loaded containing a list of known company names. A new

10545
13:43:30.859 --> 13:43:31.410
dataframe,

10546
13:43:31.410 --> 13:43:36.046
is created by joining parseDF and companyDF on the company name. As this is an inner

10547
13:43:36.046 --> 13:43:40.628
join, only rows from parseDF with company names that are present in companyDF would

10548
13:43:40.628 --> 13:43:45.209
be included in the new data frame, verifiedDF. This has the effect of automatically

10549
13:43:45.209 --> 13:43:49.514
filtering out rows that don't meet any of the specified criteria. This is done

10550
13:43:49.514 --> 13:43:54.095
without any kind of Spark filter or comparison code. Complex rule validation is the

10551
13:43:54.095 --> 13:43:56.800
idea of using Spark components to validate logic.

10552
13:43:56.800 --> 13:44:01.071
This may be as simple as using the various Spark calculations to verify the number

10553
13:44:01.071 --> 13:44:05.341
of columns in an irregular dataset. You've done something like this already in the

10554
13:44:05.341 --> 13:44:09.716
previous lessons. The validation can also be applied against an external source, web

10555
13:44:09.716 --> 13:44:13.779
service, local files, API calls. These rules are often implemented as a UDF to

10556
13:44:13.779 --> 13:44:17.633
encapsulate the logic to one place and easily run against the content of a

10557
13:44:17.633 --> 13:44:21.956
dataframe. Let's try validating our data against our specific requirements for this

10558
13:44:21.956 --> 13:44:24.820
dataset. Enjoy the exercises and we'll get to the last.

10559
13:44:24.820 --> 13:44:29.084
We've worked with a lot of Spark components while exploring data cleaning. Let's

10560
13:44:29.084 --> 13:44:33.135
