from a given list of sales. A Python function takes a sales list argument. For every

10565
13:44:50.352 --> 13:44:53.390
sale in the sales list, the function adds the sale entry,

10566
13:44:53.390 --> 13:44:57.603
from value 2 and 3 in the sale double. Once complete, it calculates the actual

10567
13:44:57.603 --> 13:45:01.923
average per row and returns it. The remaining code is what we've done previously

10568
13:45:01.923 --> 13:45:06.352
when defining a UDF and using it within a data frame. Spark UDFs are very powerful

10569
13:45:06.352 --> 13:45:10.457
and flexible and are sometimes the only way to handle certain types of data.

10570
13:45:10.457 --> 13:45:14.939
Unfortunately, UDFs do come at a performance penalty compared to the built-in Spark

10571
13:45:14.939 --> 13:45:18.828
functions, especially for certain operations. The solution is to perform

10572
13:45:18.828 --> 13:45:20.610
calculations in line if possible.

10573
13:45:20.610 --> 13:45:24.682
Spark columns can be defined using inline math operations, which can then be

10574
13:45:24.682 --> 13:45:29.075
optimized for the best performance. In this case, we read in a data file, and then

10575
13:45:29.075 --> 13:45:33.200
have two examples of adding calculated columns. The first is a simple average

10576
13:45:33.200 --> 13:45:37.700
computed by using two columns in the data frame. The second option computes a square

10577
13:45:37.700 --> 13:45:42.146
footage by multiplying the value in the two columns together to create a third. The

10578
13:45:42.146 --> 13:45:45.950
final line shows the option of mixing a UDF with an inline calculation.

10579
13:45:45.950 --> 13:45:50.124
There's often a better way to do this, but it does illustrate Spark does not care

10580
13:45:50.124 --> 13:45:54.349
about the source of the info, as long as it conforms to the expected input format.

10581
13:45:54.349 --> 13:45:58.522
Let's finish up this course by performing some analysis on our data frame and add

10582
13:45:58.522 --> 13:46:02.747
meaningful information. Congratulations! You've successfully completed this course

10583
13:46:02.747 --> 13:46:06.972
by performing data cleaning operations on several datasets using Python and Apache

10584
13:46:06.972 --> 13:46:11.043
Spark. While we've touched on many topics, there is a great deal to learn about

