Creating training sets for predictive models. A special case of this is when you are

10769
14:00:35.956 --> 14:00:40.623
trying to predict a word from previous words in a sequence. Suppose you have a

10770
14:00:40.623 --> 14:00:45.589
sequence of things and you want to predict the last items in this sequence, such as

10771
14:00:45.589 --> 14:00:50.495
the end word of a sequence of words. You can use previous words to predict the end

10772
14:00:50.495 --> 14:00:54.983
word. In general, we represent things using symbolic tokens. These could be

10773
14:00:54.983 --> 14:00:59.470
something else, such as song IDs in a person's listening history or videos.

10774
14:00:59.470 --> 14:01:05.267
Categorical data can take one of a limited number of possible values. This is

10775
14:01:05.267 --> 14:01:11.516
sometimes also referred to as nominal data and also as qualitative data. Categories

10776
14:01:11.516 --> 14:01:17.841
that are near each other in lexical order are not necessarily qualitatively similar.

10777
14:01:17.841 --> 14:01:24.165
For example, he and she are both gender pronouns but are far away alphabetically. He

10778
14:01:24.165 --> 14:01:28.080
and hi are close together alphabetically but are not

10779
14:01:28.080 --> 14:01:33.511
very similar qualitatively. Categorical data generally have no logical order. When

10780
14:01:33.511 --> 14:01:38.611
they do, they are called ordinal data. Another powerful class of applications

10781
14:01:38.611 --> 14:01:44.043
involve sequence analysis. Suppose you want to determine what words tend to appear

10782
14:01:44.043 --> 14:01:49.540
together. You will now learn how to do just that. We will use the same dataset used

10783
14:01:49.540 --> 14:01:54.640
in the previous video lesson. Here is a moving window query that we used in a

10784
14:01:54.640 --> 14:01:55.700
previous lesson.

10785
14:01:55.700 --> 14:02:01.549
This query gives all sequences of length 3 in the text document. In general, these

10786
14:02:01.549 --> 14:02:07.113
tokens could be things other than words, such as song IDs or video IDs. A more

10787
14:02:07.113 --> 14:02:13.033
general way to refer to sequences of length 3 is 3-tuples. In each row, the columns

10788
14:02:13.033 --> 14:02:18.811
1, w2, and w3 identify a 3-tuple. Let's use this query as a subquery to tally the

10789
14:02:18.811 --> 14:02:24.660
most common 3-tuples. Here, we use the previous moving window query as a subquery.

10790
14:02:24.660 --> 14:02:29.468
This groups on W1, W2, and W3, counting the number of occurrences of each

10791
14:02:29.468 --> 14:02:34.934
three-tuple. OrderByCountDescending gives the most common three-tuples. We are thus

10792
14:02:34.934 --> 14:02:40.334
able to obtain the most common three-tuples in this dataset in two statements, the

10793
14:02:40.334 --> 14:02:45.669
first one defining a query, the second one running and displaying it. Here is the

10794
14:02:45.669 --> 14:02:51.069
same result displayed for the previous query, expanded so that you may see more of

10795
14:02:51.069 --> 14:02:53.440
its rows. The three-tuple one of the

