14:03:12.100 --> 14:03:17.406
Note that Spark is able to run this query across multiple workers in parallel

10800
14:03:17.406 --> 14:03:23.126
without us having to tell it exactly how to do so. Because the data is partitioned,

10801
14:03:23.126 --> 14:03:28.777
Spark is able to automatically parallelize the window function SQL. Now, it's your

10802
14:03:28.777 --> 14:03:34.428
turn. We've learned how to perform complex operations using simple syntax. In this

10803
14:03:34.428 --> 14:03:38.770
lesson, you will now learn how to cache data frames and tables.

10804
14:03:38.770 --> 14:03:43.780
Caching is keeping data in memory so that it does not have to be refetched or

10805
14:03:43.780 --> 14:03:48.855
recalculated each time it is used. Using caching properly is an important best

10806
14:03:48.855 --> 14:03:54.256
practice to know when working with Spark. Spark is aggressive about freeing up data

10807
14:03:54.256 --> 14:03:59.526
from memory. It will error on the side of unloading data from memory if it is not

10808
14:03:59.526 --> 14:04:04.797
used, even if it is going to be needed later. Eviction policy determines when and

10809
14:04:04.797 --> 14:04:08.180
which data is removed from cache. The policy is LRU.

10810
14:04:08.180 --> 14:04:15.399
each worker manages its own cache, and eviction depends on the memory available to

10811
14:04:15.399 --> 14:04:22.793
each worker. To cache a dataframe, use df.cache. To uncache it, use df.onpersist. To

10812
14:04:22.793 --> 14:04:29.924
determine whether a dataframe is cached, use the dataframe property is underscore

10813
14:04:29.924 --> 14:04:36.350
cached. df.is cached confirmed that it was not cached. Then we cached it.

10814
14:04:36.350 --> 14:04:43.493
using df.is cached again confirms that it was cached. You uncache a data frame using

10815
14:04:43.493 --> 14:04:50.635
unpersist. A data frame storage level specifies five details about how it is cached.

10816
14:04:50.635 --> 14:04:57.778
Use disk, use memory, use off heap, deserialized, and replication. We can set any or

10817
14:04:57.778 --> 14:05:03.220
all of these. However, the cache operation sets them to default.

10818
14:05:03.220 --> 14:05:09.402
UseDisk specifies whether to spill the data frame to disk if it falls out of memory.

10819
14:05:09.402 --> 14:05:15.363
UseMemory specifies whether to keep the data in memory. UseOffHeap tells Spark to

10820
14:05:15.363 --> 14:05:21.471
use off-heap storage instead of on-heap memory. Even though the best performance is

10821
14:05:21.471 --> 14:05:27.432
obtained when operating solely in on-heap memory, Spark also makes it possible to

10822
14:05:27.432 --> 14:05:30.670
use off-heap storage for certain operations.

10823
14:05:30.670 --> 14:05:36.509
Off-heap storage is slightly slower than on-heap but still faster than disk. The

10824
14:05:36.509 --> 14:05:42.493
downside is that the user has to manually deal with managing the allocated memory.

10825
14:05:42.493 --> 14:05:48.551
Deserialized true is faster but uses more memory. Deserialized or rather serialized

10826
14:05:48.551 --> 14:05:54.609
data is more space efficient but slower to read than deserialized data. This option

10827
14:05:54.609 --> 14:05:57.090
only applies to in-memory storage.

10828
14:05:57.090 --> 14:06:04.504
disk cache is always serialized. Replication is used to tell Spark to replicate data

10829
14:06:04.504 --> 14:06:11.478
on multiple nodes. This allows faster fault recovery when a node fails. You may

10830
14:06:11.478 --> 14:06:18.627
wonder why you use df.cache to cache, but df.unpersist to uncache. That's because

10831
14:06:18.627 --> 14:06:25.600
df.cache is shorthand for df.persist with the first argument set to its default

10832
14:06:25.600 --> 14:06:26.130
value.

10833
14:06:26.130 --> 14:06:31.616
The persist command allows you to specify the desired storage level using the first

10834
14:06:31.616 --> 14:06:37.037
argument. If that argument is not provided, it uses a default setting. When memory

10835
14:06:37.037 --> 14:06:42.259
is scarce, it is recommended to use memory and disk caching strategy. This will

10836
14:06:42.259 --> 14:06:47.613
spill the data frame to disk if memory runs low. Reading the data frame from disk

10837
14:06:47.613 --> 14:06:53.166
cache is slower than reading it from memory, but can still be faster than recreating

10838
14:06:53.166 --> 14:06:54.620
it from scratch. Cache

10839
14:06:54.620 --> 14:07:00.655
is equivalent to using persist with the default storage level. spark.catalog.is

10840
14:07:00.655 --> 14:07:06.538
cached tells you whether a table has been cached. You cache a table using the

10841
14:07:06.538 --> 14:07:12.879
operation spark.catalog.cachedtable giving the table name as the first argument. To

10842
14:07:12.879 --> 14:07:18.914
uncache the table, use spark.catalog.uncachedtable giving the table name as the

10843
14:07:18.914 --> 14:07:21.970
first argument. spark.catalog.clearcache

10844
14:07:21.970 --> 14:07:27.845
removes all cache tables. Caching is a lazy operation. A data frame won't appear in

10845
14:07:27.845 --> 14:07:33.436
the cache until an action is performed on the data frame. Don't overdo it. Only

10846
14:07:33.436 --> 14:07:39.382
cache if more than one operation is to be performed and it takes substantial time to

10847
14:07:39.382 --> 14:07:45.044
create the data frame. Unpersist unneeded object. Caching incurs a cost. Caching

10848
14:07:45.044 --> 14:07:50.990
everything generally slows things down. Now, it's your turn to practice caching with

10849
