we can take is replacing the string Florida with the string FL.

11249
14:42:38.660 --> 14:42:44.245
This won't be enough because we still have the string fl in a small letter. To solve

11250
14:42:44.245 --> 14:42:49.630
it, we can use the upper function. Upper converts a given string to uppercase. In

11251
14:42:49.630 --> 14:42:54.816
the following code, we can see that the upper function wraps the result of the

11252
14:42:54.816 --> 14:43:00.068
replace function. This results in the following output. Let's have fun cleaning

11253
14:43:00.068 --> 14:43:05.387
messy strings. In this lesson, we will learn how to check the similarity between

11254
14:43:05.387 --> 14:43:07.780
strings using soundex and different.

11255
14:43:07.780 --> 14:43:13.828
As we said in previous lessons, it is common to find messy strings. Sometimes, these

11256
14:43:13.828 --> 14:43:19.588
strings are written differently, but their sounds are similar or almost similar.

11257
14:43:19.588 --> 14:43:25.492
With the use of the functions, Soundex and Difference, SQL Server provides help to

11258
14:43:25.492 --> 14:43:31.540
detect those strings. Let's start with Soundex. Soundex is a phonetic algorithm that

11259
14:43:31.540 --> 14:43:36.940
returns a 4-character code used to evaluate the similarity between strings.

11260
14:43:36.940 --> 14:43:42.673
based on their pronunciation. Although the algorithm is based on the English

11261
14:43:42.673 --> 14:43:49.010
language, it also works with many words in other languages. For example, if we apply

11262
14:43:49.010 --> 14:43:54.593
Soundex to the string Illinois, to this other string Illinois, and also to

11263
14:43:54.593 --> 14:44:00.703
California, we will get the following output. The first two strings have the same

11264
14:44:00.703 --> 14:44:06.210
code, which means they are similar to the Soundex algorithm. By contrast,

11265
14:44:06.210 --> 14:44:12.398
California has another code. This string is different from the others. Let's see how

11266
14:44:12.398 --> 14:44:18.587
it works with the word Illinois. First of all, SQL Server writes the first letter of

11267
14:44:18.587 --> 14:44:24.775
the word I in this example. Then, it replaces to zero all the vowels and the letters

11268
14:44:24.775 --> 14:44:30.964
H, W, and W after the first letter. After that, the rest of the consonants after the

11269
14:44:30.964 --> 14:44:35.900
first letter are replaced following this table. In this example, L,

11270
14:44:35.900 --> 14:44:42.647
will be replaced by 4, n by 5, and s by 2. After that, SQL Server replaces the same

11271
14:44:42.647 --> 14:44:49.475
adjacent digits with just 1. Then, it removes all the zeros. Finally, if the letters

11272
14:44:49.475 --> 14:44:56.141
digit is the same as the first digit, it removes the first digit. It appends zeros

11273
14:44:56.141 --> 14:45:02.726
if the code contains less than 3 digits, and removes final digits if the code has

11274
14:45:02.726 --> 14:45:04.270
more than 3 digits.

11275
14:45:04.270 --> 14:45:09.947
All these final steps don't apply to our sample. Soundex is not 100% perfect.

11276
14:45:09.947 --> 14:45:16.139
Although it is very useful we need to check the results, because some exceptions may

11277
14:45:16.139 --> 14:45:22.184
occur. Here is an example of two words, Arizona and Arkansas, that sound different

11278
14:45:22.184 --> 14:45:26.460
but return the same code. Let's put Soundex into practice.

11279
14:45:26.460 --> 14:45:31.981
In the following code, we are getting all the values from the airport state column

11280
14:45:31.981 --> 14:45:37.367
that have the same Soundex code between them, but are written differently. To do

11281
14:45:37.367 --> 14:45:42.484
this, we are joining the airport table with itself. The output we get is the

11282
14:45:42.484 --> 14:45:47.668
following. We can see that Soundex detected different strings for California,

11283
14:45:47.668 --> 14:45:53.122
Illinois and Texas. Between the results, we have New Jersey and New York, because

11284
14:45:53.122 --> 14:45:55.680
Soundex only applies to the first war.

11285
14:45:55.680 --> 14:46:01.104
new in this example. Let's see how to avoid these results and check the strings

11286
14:46:01.104 --> 14:46:06.528
correctly. We included in the previous code the replace function to omit spaces

11287
14:46:06.528 --> 14:46:12.089
between words, like New York. Soundex didn't find any matches between states with

11288
14:46:12.089 --> 14:46:17.513
more than one word, like New York or New Jersey. Let's study now the difference

11289
14:46:17.513 --> 14:46:23.280
function. Difference compares two Soundex values and returns an integer from 0 to 4.

11290
14:46:23.280 --> 14:46:29.242
0 indicates little or no similarity, while 4 represents very similar or identically

11291
14:46:29.242 --> 14:46:34.988
matching. If we apply difference to these strings, the result we get is 4, which

11292
14:46:34.988 --> 14:46:40.663
means they are very similar or identical. However, applying difference to these

11293
14:46:40.663 --> 14:46:46.337
other strings will output 1, which means low similarity. Similar to the Soundex

11294
14:46:46.337 --> 14:46:51.940
example, we can check similarities between states that have a difference of 4,

