14:47:02.608 --> 14:47:08.043
value of 4. So, as in the Soundex function, we always need to review the results.

11298
14:47:08.043 --> 14:47:13.277
Let's put Soundex and difference in the results. Congratulations on completing

11299
14:47:13.277 --> 14:47:18.510
chapter 1. In this chapter, we will learn how to deal with missing data, avoid

11300
14:47:18.510 --> 14:47:21.060
duplicate data, and clean messy dates.

11301
14:47:21.060 --> 14:47:26.577
On some occasions, we can find that our data has missing values. In SQL Server,

11302
14:47:26.577 --> 14:47:32.304
missing values are represented by null. However, in other cases, it is also common

11303
14:47:32.304 --> 14:47:37.891
to find blank values. Missing data can happen for some reason. Sometimes, it can

11304
14:47:37.891 --> 14:47:43.618
happen intentionally. For example, a person who doesn't want to reveal the H, or a

11305
14:47:43.618 --> 14:47:47.040
person who doesn't know the answer to a question.

11306
14:47:47.040 --> 14:47:52.742
In other cases, an error could have occurred when inserting the data. And finally,

11307
14:47:52.742 --> 14:47:58.374
sometimes the data simply doesn't exist. For example, the number of children of a

11308
14:47:58.374 --> 14:48:04.076
person that doesn't have any children. We have different ways to deal with missing

11309
14:48:04.076 --> 14:48:09.847
values. First of all, it is recommended to investigate if we can find those missing

11310
14:48:09.847 --> 14:48:13.950
values. Sometimes we can find them in the original dataset.

11311
14:48:13.950 --> 14:48:19.726
If we don't find the missing values, we will have the following options depending on

11312
14:48:19.726 --> 14:48:25.296
the business assumptions behind our data. We can leave the data as it is, knowing

11313
14:48:25.296 --> 14:48:31.072
that we have missing values in our dataset. Another option is to avoid the rows with

11314
14:48:31.072 --> 14:48:36.642
missing data by removing them from our analysis. And finally, we can fill missing

11315
14:48:36.642 --> 14:48:41.730
values with another value, such as a text, the average of the values, etc.

11316
14:48:41.730 --> 14:48:47.217
In the next slides, we will learn how to remove the rows with missing values and

11317
14:48:47.217 --> 14:48:52.499
fill null values with other values. Let's see how to remove the rows from the

11318
14:48:52.499 --> 14:48:58.260
airports table that have null values for the airport state column. In SQL Server, we

11319
14:48:58.260 --> 14:49:04.022
can use isNotNull to select the values that aren't null. Note that we didn't get any

11320
14:49:04.022 --> 14:49:07.520
row with a null value for the airport state column.

11321
14:49:07.520 --> 14:49:12.909
If we want to check which are the rows that have null values for the airport state

11322
14:49:12.909 --> 14:49:18.036
column, we can do it by using isNull. As we said previously, we can find blank

11323
14:49:18.036 --> 14:49:23.491
spaces instead of null values. If we want to remove the rows that have blank values

11324
14:49:23.491 --> 14:49:29.012
for the airport state column, we can use the comparison operator to exclude them. We

11325
14:49:29.012 --> 14:49:34.138
can substitute null values using the isNull function. isNull replaces the null

11326
14:49:34.138 --> 14:49:36.110
values with a specified value.

11327
14:49:36.110 --> 14:49:41.263
Note that it is written together unlike the isNull predicate that we already

11328
14:49:41.263 --> 14:49:46.822
studied. In this example, we replaced the null values for the airport state column

11329
14:49:46.822 --> 14:49:52.110
with the unknown string. On some occasions, null values can be replaced with a

11330
14:49:52.110 --> 14:49:57.669
number, for example, the average of the values for that column as long as it makes

11331
14:49:57.669 --> 14:50:02.890
sense. To get the average of the cancelled column, we need to use the average

11332
14:50:02.890 --> 14:50:03.500
function.

11333
14:50:03.500 --> 14:50:09.498
If we use average in combination with the isNull function, we will get the average

11334
14:50:09.498 --> 14:50:15.495
value in case there is a null value. Finally, another way of replacing null values

11335
14:50:15.495 --> 14:50:20.835
can be performed using coalesce. Coalesce evaluates the arguments between

11336
14:50:20.835 --> 14:50:26.686
parentheses and returns the first argument that is not null. In this example, we

11337
14:50:26.686 --> 14:50:31.660
used coalesce to evaluate the values of airport state, airport city,

11338
14:50:31.660 --> 14:50:37.865
For the first row, we can see that as the airport state is not null, we get the

11339
14:50:37.865 --> 14:50:43.912
value Utah. In the second row, as the airport state is null, Coales evaluates

11340
14:50:43.912 --> 14:50:50.509
airport city. Airport city is not null, so it returns Philadelphia. In the last row,

11341
14:50:50.509 --> 14:50:56.871
as both airport state and airport city are null, we get the string UNKNOWN. Let's

11342
14:50:56.871 --> 14:50:59.620
practice with this. In this lesson,

11343
14:50:59.620 --> 14:51:04.990
We will learn how to avoid duplicate data in our results. Let's first explain what

11344
14:51:04.990 --> 14:51:10.492
duplicate data is. In our data, we can find rows with identical information. In this

11345
14:51:10.492 --> 14:51:15.993
example, we have two rows that have the same values in each column. We can also find

11346
14:51:15.993 --> 14:51:21.232
rows with some common information. For instance, two or more rows with identical

11347
14:51:21.232 --> 14:51:26.275
information, but with a different registration code. This example is the most

11348
14:51:26.275 --> 14:51:28.240
typical case of duplicate data

11349
14:51:28.240 --> 14:51:33.769
where all the important information is duplicated. But we can also have two or more

11350
14:51:33.769 --> 14:51:39.032
rows with only some common information and different values for the rest of the

11351
14:51:39.032 --> 14:51:44.095
columns. Duplicate data can interfere in our analysis, so it is essential to

11352
14:51:44.095 --> 14:51:49.691
identify it. To detect duplicate data, first of all, we need to define which columns

11353
14:51:49.691 --> 14:51:55.287
form a repeating group. A repeating group will depend on our dataset. For the flight

11354
14:51:55.287 --> 14:51:56.420
statistics table,

11355
14:51:56.420 --> 14:52:02.209
We can consider that the columns that form a repeating group are the airport code,

11356
14:52:02.209 --> 14:52:07.928
carrier code, and registration date. The reason is that only once every month the

11357
14:52:07.928 --> 14:52:13.364
statistics of the flights for a particular airport and a specific carrier are

11358
14:52:13.364 --> 14:52:19.294
registered. Considering this repeating group, we can see that we won't have problems

11359
14:52:19.294 --> 14:52:21.200
to exclude from our results

11360
14:52:21.200 --> 14:52:26.874
Those rows that have in the same airport code, carrier code, and registration date,

11361
14:52:26.874 --> 14:52:32.617
have a different registration code, or even different statistician name and surname.

11362
14:52:32.617 --> 14:52:38.291
The critical information is still the same. Obviously, we won't have any problem to

11363
14:52:38.291 --> 14:52:39.986
exclude from our results.

11364
14:52:39.986 --> 14:52:45.679
duplicate rows that have the same values for every column. But, what happens if

11365
14:52:45.679 --> 14:52:51.589
having the same airport code, carrier code, and registration date, the data of the

11366
14:52:51.589 --> 14:52:57.571
columns cancelled, on time, delayed, or diverted are different? Which one should we

11367
14:52:57.571 --> 14:52:58.220
maintain?

11368
14:52:58.220 --> 14:53:02.965
We have a problem here. If we find this kind of duplicates, we will need to

11369
14:53:02.965 --> 14:53:08.026
investigate which one is correct. Once we have found the repeating group, we are

11370
14:53:08.026 --> 14:53:12.834
going to find the duplicate rows for that group. To do that, we will use the

11371
14:53:12.834 --> 14:53:17.895
rowNumber function. For the partitionBy argument, we will specify the columns we

11372
14:53:17.895 --> 14:53:23.019
considered as a repeating group. So, for each row, with common information in the

11373
14:53:23.019 --> 14:53:25.550
columns considered as a repeating group,

11374
14:53:25.550 --> 14:53:30.840
RowNumber will give a number which begins at 1 for the first row and provides a

11375
14:53:30.840 --> 14:53:36.197
sequential number for each row within the same partition or repeating group. The

11376
14:53:36.197 --> 14:53:41.822
ORDER BY clause is required. Let's demonstrate how it works. In this example, we can

11377
14:53:41.822 --> 14:53:47.313
see that the columns that form the repeating group, airport code, carrier code and

11378
14:53:47.313 --> 14:53:52.938
registration date will be the partitions to which the RowNumber function is going to

11379
14:53:52.938 --> 14:53:53.340
apply.

11380
14:53:53.340 --> 14:53:58.502
This code will return the following output. As we can see here, row number gave the

11381
14:53:58.502 --> 14:54:03.602
number 1 for this row, and number 2 for this other row. Because both rows have the

11382
14:54:03.602 --> 14:54:08.640
same information in the columns that form their partition. The first row receives

11383
14:54:08.640 --> 14:54:13.740
the number 1, and the second row within the same partition receives the sequential

11384
14:54:13.740 --> 14:54:14.300
number 2.

11385
14:54:14.300 --> 14:54:19.758
If we want to get only the duplicate rows in our results, we will need to select

11386
14:54:19.758 --> 14:54:25.490
those rows that have rowNum greater than 1. However, if we want to exclude duplicate

11387
14:54:25.490 --> 14:54:31.085
rows, we will filter those rows with rowNum equals to 1. To get just the duplicate

11388
14:54:31.085 --> 14:54:36.679
rows, we can include the previous query in a with clause, and then select only the

11389
14:54:36.679 --> 14:54:42.274
rows with a rowNum greater than 1. These are all the duplicate rows that have been

11390
14:54:42.274 --> 14:54:43.980
detected. As you can see,

11391
14:54:43.980 --> 14:54:49.184
All of them have a row num greater than 1. To exclude the duplicate rows, we can

11392
14:54:49.184 --> 14:54:54.453
also include the same query in a with clause, and then select the rows with a row

11393
14:54:54.453 --> 14:54:59.916
num equals to 1. As you can see, all the rows have a row num equals to 1. Now, it is

11394
14:54:59.916 --> 14:55:05.380
time to... In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with dates when they don't have

11395
14:55:05.380 --> 14:55:10.844
the format that we want. Dates can have different formats depending on the language.

11396
14:55:10.844 --> 14:55:11.820
For instance...

11397
14:55:11.820 --> 14:55:17.026
In American English, it is common to write dates like this. First, we write the

11398
14:55:17.026 --> 14:55:22.363
month, then the day, and finally the year. However, in other languages, dates can

11399
14:55:22.363 --> 14:55:27.832
have different formats, like in Spanish, where the day comes first, then the month,

11400
14:55:27.832 --> 14:55:33.301
and finally the year. In the Italian language, it is the year the first part of the

11401
14:55:33.301 --> 14:55:36.530
date, followed by the month, and finally the day.

11402
14:55:36.530 --> 14:55:41.602
We can find more variations in other languages. Let's analyze the format of the

11403
14:55:41.602 --> 14:55:46.994
entry date column for the pilots table. The format starts with the year, followed by

11404
14:55:46.994 --> 14:55:52.258
the month, and ends with the day. But, what should we do if this format is not the

11405
14:55:52.258 --> 14:55:57.394
one that we need to get in our reports? If we want to modify the entry date with

11406
14:55:57.394 --> 14:56:02.466
another format, we will need to use the convert or the format function. Convert

11407
14:56:02.466 --> 14:56:05.740
converts an expression of one data type to another.

11408
14:56:05.740 --> 14:56:11.386
Determining optionally how the function will translate the expression. Applied to

11409
14:56:11.386 --> 14:56:16.823
dates, we can use convert as follows. As we can see, we used the function cast

11410
14:56:16.823 --> 14:56:22.609
because the values of the EntryDate column are restored as varter, and we need them

11411
14:56:22.609 --> 14:56:27.907
to be as dates. If they were dates, the cast wouldn't be necessary. The last

11412
14:56:27.907 --> 14:56:33.762
parameter of the convert function, 0, 1, 101, and so on, is used to define the style

11413
14:56:33.762 --> 14:56:34.320
we want.

11414
14:56:34.320 --> 14:56:39.662
Here, we can see some of the possible formats we can use. Note that we can get the

11415
14:56:39.662 --> 14:56:44.938
century of the year by adding 10 at the beginning of the number. For the complete

11416
14:56:44.938 --> 14:56:50.410
catalog, please visit the Microsoft documentation. The format function can also help

11417
14:56:50.410 --> 14:56:55.752
us to convert dates into the format that we want, optionally specifying a culture.

11418
14:56:55.752 --> 14:57:01.159
It is more flexible than convert, but it also performs worse, resulting in a slower

11419
14:57:01.159 --> 14:57:01.680
process.

11420
14:57:01.680 --> 14:57:07.203
It is not recommended for high data volumes. Let's see how to use it. As a first

11421
14:57:07.203 --> 14:57:12.932
parameter, we need to cast the values for the EntryDate column as a date. Again, if

11422
14:57:12.932 --> 14:57:18.731
they were dates, we wouldn't need to do it. The second parameter receives the format

11423
14:57:18.731 --> 14:57:24.391
we want. The letter D in lowercase is used to get short dates, and the letter D in

11424
14:57:24.391 --> 14:57:29.845
capital letters is used to get long dates. In the last format function, we just

11425
14:57:29.845 --> 14:57:30.190
added

11426
14:57:30.190 --> 14:57:35.793
dd slash mm slash yyyyy to get the desired format. Finally, the third parameter

11427
14:57:35.793 --> 14:57:41.608
optionally receives the locale of the language. Let's practice with different date

11428
14:57:41.608 --> 14:57:47.140
formats. Welcome back! In this lesson, we will learn how to select and exclude

11429
14:57:47.140 --> 14:57:53.097
out-of-range values and inaccurate data. Throughout this chapter, we will be working

11430
14:57:53.097 --> 14:57:57.920
on a new dataset. It contains information about series and episodes.

11431
14:57:57.920 --> 14:58:03.576
Let's start defining out-of-range values. Out-of-range values are values that are

11432
14:58:03.576 --> 14:58:09.233
outside the expected range of valid data. For example, a person who is 400 inches

11433
14:58:09.233 --> 14:58:14.889
tall. If we don't detect this kind of dirty data before analyzing, it can disrupt

11434
14:58:14.889 --> 14:58:20.545
our results. If we have the chance, we can investigate why these data are outside

11435
14:58:20.545 --> 14:58:26.271
the expected values and correct it. We will illustrate out-of-range values with an

11436
14:58:26.271 --> 14:58:26.830
example.

11437
14:58:26.830 --> 14:58:32.694
In our series table, there is a column named RATING. It stores the ratings given by

11438
14:58:32.694 --> 14:58:38.276
the audience of the series on a scale of 0 to 10. Unfortunately, in this table,

11439
14:58:38.276 --> 14:58:43.998
there aren't any constraints that checks this range. Because of that, some values

11440
14:58:43.998 --> 14:58:49.933
are out of this range due to input errors. If we want to detect which values are out

11441
14:58:49.933 --> 14:58:55.515
of this range, we can use the between logical operator in its negation. In this

11442
14:58:55.515 --> 14:58:56.080
example,

11443
14:58:56.080 --> 14:59:02.143
We are selecting those rows which ratings are not between the expected range. As we

11444
14:59:02.143 --> 14:59:08.206
can see in the output, the first series has a rating of 11,4 and the second one has

11445
14:59:08.206 --> 14:59:14.124
a rating of 11. Similarly, we can select these rows by applying the less than and

11446
14:59:14.124 --> 14:59:20.114
greater than operators. It is those rows which ratings are strictly less than 0 or

11447
14:59:20.114 --> 14:59:21.940
strictly greater than 10.

11448
14:59:21.940 --> 14:59:27.746
If we choose to exclude from our results the rows that have out of range of values,

11449
14:59:27.746 --> 14:59:33.272
we can do it by using the between logical operator in its positive way. In this

11450
14:59:33.272 --> 14:59:39.008
example, we are selecting those rows which ratings are between the expected range.

11451
14:59:39.008 --> 14:59:44.534
We can also select these rows by applying the greater than equals and less than

11452
14:59:44.534 --> 14:59:49.920
operators. It is, those rows whose ratings are greater than or equal to zero.

11453
14:59:49.920 --> 14:59:55.885
Another kind of messy data is inaccurate data. Inaccurate data happens when two or

11454
14:59:55.885 --> 15:00:01.268
more values are contradictory. For example, a man who is pregnant. As with

11455
15:00:01.268 --> 15:00:07.161
out-of-range values, inaccurate data can lead us to wrong results. If we have the

11456
15:00:07.161 --> 15:00:12.908
chance, we can also investigate why these data are inaccurate and correct them.

11457
15:00:12.908 --> 15:00:18.800
Let's illustrate inaccurate data with an example. If we look at the series table,

11458
15:00:18.800 --> 15:00:24.490
We can see that there is a column named Premiered that stores the date on which each

11459
15:00:24.490 --> 15:00:29.978
series was premiered. On the other hand, in the episodes table, there is a column

11460
15:00:29.978 --> 15:00:35.194
named Air Date that stores the date when each episode was aired. It should be

11461
15:00:35.194 --> 15:00:40.545
logical that the air dates of the episodes have the same date or later than the

11462
15:00:40.545 --> 15:00:42.510
premiered date of the series.

11463
15:00:42.510 --> 15:00:47.683
However, as we can see, there are some episodes with error dates before the

11464
15:00:47.683 --> 15:00:53.064
premiered date of the series. These dates are not accurate. Let's learn how to

11465
15:00:53.064 --> 15:00:58.789
detect them. To detect these inaccurate values, we can join both tables, series and

11466
15:00:58.789 --> 15:01:04.376
episodes, by the series identifier, applying the condition that the error date of

11467
15:01:04.376 --> 15:01:08.860
the episodes is strictly before the premiered date of the series.

11468
15:01:08.860 --> 15:01:13.974
This comparison can be done with the logical operator less than. If we choose to

11469
15:01:13.974 --> 15:01:19.216
exclude from our results the rows that have inaccurate data, we can do it applying

11470
15:01:19.216 --> 15:01:24.585
the condition that the error date of the episodes is later or equals to the premiere

11471
15:01:24.585 --> 15:01:29.763
date of the series. Let's practice what we have. In this lesson, we will focus on

11472
15:01:29.763 --> 15:01:34.110
converting data from one type into another type. In the last lesson,

11473
15:01:34.110 --> 15:01:39.505
We learned how to select and exclude out-of-range values and inaccurate data by

11474
15:01:39.505 --> 15:01:44.901
using between and comparison operators. We worked with the rating column of the

11475
15:01:44.901 --> 15:01:50.638
series table, whose type was a float number. Now, suppose that the rating column was

11476
15:01:50.638 --> 15:01:56.170
designed as vartar instead of float. Vartar is a variable size string data, so we

11477
15:01:56.170 --> 15:02:01.907
may not perceive the differences between these two types unless we explore the table

11478
15:02:01.907 --> 15:02:02.590
definition

11479
15:02:02.590 --> 15:02:08.510
or perform forbidden operations to any of these types. So, what would happen if we

11480
15:02:08.510 --> 15:02:14.357
try to execute the same queries we performed in the last lesson using BETWEEN and

