11712
15:21:08.374 --> 15:21:13.863
be analyzed in the next slides. The rest of the lines are the message text of the

11713
15:21:13.863 --> 15:21:14.270
error.

11714
15:21:14.270 --> 15:21:20.621
In the first line of the error, the first value is the error number, 2627 in this

11715
15:21:20.621 --> 15:21:27.128
example. When SQL Server raises an error, this number can be in the range from 1 to

11716
15:21:27.128 --> 15:21:33.714
49999. You can also create your own errors starting from 500001. If you want to know

11717
15:21:33.714 --> 15:21:39.908
the complete catalogue of error numbers, you can check them in the sys.messages

11718
15:21:39.908 --> 15:21:40.300
view.

11719
15:21:40.300 --> 15:21:45.930
The second value is the severity level, 14 in this example. Severity levels from 0

11720
15:21:45.930 --> 15:21:51.697
to 10 are informational messages. The errors with this level of severity are treated

11721
15:21:51.697 --> 15:21:57.396
as warnings. Levels from 11 to 16 are errors that can be corrected by the user. For

11722
15:21:57.396 --> 15:22:03.163
example, constraint violations, as we got in our error message. Levels from 17 to 24

11723
15:22:03.163 --> 15:22:07.900
are other kinds of errors, such as software problems or fatal errors.

11724
15:22:07.900 --> 15:22:13.115
A complete list of severity levels is available through Microsoft. The third value

11725
15:22:13.115 --> 15:22:18.266
is the state. It can give you more information about the error. It will be one if

11726
15:22:18.266 --> 15:22:23.291
SQL Server displays the error to the user. If you erase your own error, you can

11727
15:22:23.291 --> 15:22:27.170
choose a value between 0 and 225. The last value is the line.

11728
15:22:27.170 --> 15:22:32.373
It indicates on which line the error occurred. Finally, if the error happened within

11729
15:22:32.373 --> 15:22:37.453
a stroke procedure or a trigger, you would receive this extra data, giving you the

11730
15:22:37.453 --> 15:22:42.470
name of the stroke procedure or the name of the trigger. The Tri-Cut construct we

11731
15:22:42.470 --> 15:22:47.550
learned can't catch every kind of error, for example, errors with a severity level

11732
15:22:47.550 --> 15:22:52.195
lower than 11. As you learned in the error anatomy slides, these errors are

11733
15:22:52.195 --> 15:22:53.620
considered as warnings.

11734
15:22:53.620 --> 15:22:58.964
Errors with severity from 11 to 19 are cutable. Errors with a severity of 20 or

11735
15:22:58.964 --> 15:23:04.579
higher aren't cut either if they stop the connection. However, if the connection is

11736
15:23:04.579 --> 15:23:10.059
not stopped, the try-cut will cut the error. And finally, compilation errors. For

11737
15:23:10.059 --> 15:23:15.606
example, if we write the name of a table that doesn't exist. Let's see one example

11738
15:23:15.606 --> 15:23:17.500
where an error can't be cut.

11739
15:23:17.500 --> 15:23:22.470
In this code, we are trying to select from the product table a column named

11740
15:23:22.470 --> 15:23:27.903
non-existent column. If we execute this block of code, we get an error because the

11741
15:23:27.903 --> 15:23:33.402
product table doesn't have this column. Notice the output doesn't show the text, we

11742
15:23:33.402 --> 15:23:38.968
are in the catch block. As this is a compilation error, the catch block can't handle

11743
15:23:38.968 --> 15:23:41.420
the error. Let's have fun with error!

11744
15:23:41.420 --> 15:23:46.447
In this lesson, you will learn how to use some functions that can give you

11745
15:23:46.447 --> 15:23:52.018
information about errors. Before learning these functions, let's recall first what

11746
15:23:52.018 --> 15:23:57.724
happens if we don't surround our code by a try-cut construct and we get an error. In

11747
15:23:57.724 --> 15:24:03.295
the output, we get the complete error with the error number, severity, state, line

11748
15:24:03.295 --> 15:24:04.110
and message.

11749
15:24:04.110 --> 15:24:09.061
However, if we surround the same code by a try-catch construct, it seems that we

11750
15:24:09.061 --> 15:24:14.198
lost the original error information getting just what we caught in the catch block.

11751
15:24:14.198 --> 15:24:19.273
The information we lost can be useful sometimes. Luckily, we can still retrieve it

11752
15:24:19.273 --> 15:24:24.472
using some functions in our catch blocks. Let's see these functions. The ErrorNumber

11753
15:24:24.472 --> 15:24:29.609
function returns the number of the error. ErrorSeverity returns the severity of the

11754
15:24:29.609 --> 15:24:29.980
error.

11755
15:24:29.980 --> 15:24:35.390
Remember that you can only cut those errors with a severity between 11 and 19. The

11756
15:24:35.390 --> 15:24:40.866
ErrorState function returns the state of the error. ErrorLine returns the number of

11757
15:24:40.866 --> 15:24:46.209
the line where the error occurred. ErrorProcedure provides the name of the stored

11758
15:24:46.209 --> 15:24:51.619
procedure or trigger where the error happened. It returns null if the error didn't

11759
15:24:51.619 --> 15:24:56.963
happen within a stored procedure or a trigger. Finally, the ErrorMessage function

11760
15:24:56.963 --> 15:24:59.470
returns the text of the error message.

11761
15:24:59.470 --> 15:25:05.098
Let's see some examples. In this example, we added to the catch block the functions

11762
15:25:05.098 --> 15:25:10.454
we just explained. We added them using the select statement. If we execute this

11763
15:25:10.454 --> 15:25:15.811
code, we get the following output. We can see the error number, severity level,

11764
15:25:15.811 --> 15:25:21.303
error state, error line, and message. Notice that error procedure is null because

11765
15:25:21.303 --> 15:25:25.710
this block of code is not within any stored procedure or trigger.

11766
15:25:25.710 --> 15:25:30.510
If we compare this last output with the original error, we can see that the

11767
15:25:30.510 --> 15:25:35.693
information is the same. The only difference is the error line. That's because in

11768
15:25:35.693 --> 15:25:40.621
the example we just did, we added try syntax to the first line, so the insert

11769
15:25:40.621 --> 15:25:45.996
statement is now on the second line. Let's see what happens when we try to use these

11770
15:25:45.996 --> 15:25:51.244
functions outside a catch block. If we execute this code, all these functions will

11771
15:25:51.244 --> 15:25:52.460
return null values.

11772
15:25:52.460 --> 15:25:57.718
You can also use these functions in nested try-catch constructs. In this example,

11773
15:25:57.718 --> 15:26:03.105
the catch block has another try-catch construct to handle errors encountered by the

11774
15:26:03.105 --> 15:26:08.493
catch code. Let's imagine that there is an error when trying to insert the product.

11775
15:26:08.493 --> 15:26:13.750
The control passes to the catch block and the insert error statement is executed.

11776
15:26:13.750 --> 15:26:18.943
Then, another error occurs. Let's suppose that it happens because the text error

11777
15:26:18.943 --> 15:26:21.020
exceeds the limit of characters.

11778
15:26:21.020 --> 15:26:26.638
Consequently, the second catch block will be executed, showing the following output.

11779
15:26:26.638 --> 15:26:32.055
This error information is about the last error that occurred, not about the first

11780
15:26:32.055 --> 15:26:37.673
one. Congratulations on completing chapter 1 on error handling. In this chapter, you

11781
15:26:37.673 --> 15:26:43.224
will learn how to raise errors, retrow original errors, and create your own defined

11782
15:26:43.224 --> 15:26:47.170
errors. SQL Server provides two statements to raise errors.

11783
15:26:47.170 --> 15:26:52.530
The first one is the raise error statement, which will be studied in this lesson.

11784
15:26:52.530 --> 15:26:57.956
The second one is the throw statement, which will be explained in the next lesson.

11785
15:26:57.956 --> 15:27:03.184
Microsoft suggests using the throw statement in the new applications, but it is

11786
15:27:03.184 --> 15:27:08.610
still common to see the raise error statement in our current applications. This is

11787
15:27:08.610 --> 15:27:14.102
the raise error syntax. It starts with the word raise error. Notice it only has one

11788
15:27:14.102 --> 15:27:14.830
e, not two.

11789
15:27:14.830 --> 15:27:20.463
The first parameter can be a message string, a message ID, or a variable that

11790
15:27:20.463 --> 15:27:26.316
contains the message string. The second parameter is the severity, and the third

11791
15:27:26.316 --> 15:27:32.169
parameter is the state. After these parameters, you can optionally add arguments

11792
15:27:32.169 --> 15:27:38.241
like strings or numbers. If the message string has some parameter placeholders such

11793
15:27:38.241 --> 15:27:41.680
as %s or %d, these arguments will replace them.

11794
15:27:41.680 --> 15:27:47.556
We will explain it in detail in the next slides. Finally, you can specify additional

11795
15:27:47.556 --> 15:27:53.432
options. These options won't be covered in this course, so, for further information,

11796
15:27:53.432 --> 15:27:58.678
you can have a look at Microsoft documentation. Let's see an example of the

11797
15:27:58.678 --> 15:28:04.484
RAISEERROR statement with message string. In the first line, we check if the recent

11798
15:28:04.484 --> 15:28:09.731
staff member whose ID is 15. In the recent, we invoke RAISEERROR with three

11799
15:28:09.731 --> 15:28:10.500
parameters.

11800
15:28:10.500 --> 15:28:16.545
The first parameter is the message text, no staff member with such ID. The second

11801
15:28:16.545 --> 15:28:22.591
one, 16, is the severity level. Finally, the last one is the state. If we execute

11802
15:28:22.591 --> 15:28:28.711
this example as there is no staff member whose ID is 15, we will get the following

11803
15:28:28.711 --> 15:28:34.980
output. If we don't specify an error number, the error number will always be 50,000.

11804
15:28:34.980 --> 15:28:40.628
Let's change the message text included on the previous raceError statement and add

11805
15:28:40.628 --> 15:28:46.276
some parameter placeholders. Instead of no staff member with such ID, let's change

11806
15:28:46.276 --> 15:28:51.855
the words staff member by the percent symbol followed by the letter S. Let's also

11807
15:28:51.855 --> 15:28:57.434
include percent D. Percent S is used for stream values and it will be replaced by

11808
15:28:57.434 --> 15:29:01.360
the penultimate parameter of this function, staff member.

11809
15:29:01.360 --> 15:29:07.097
%d is used for integer values, and it will be replaced by the last parameter of this

11810
15:29:07.097 --> 15:29:12.629
function, 15. If we execute this example, we will get this output. If you want to

11811
15:29:12.629 --> 15:29:18.297
include the %character in your message, you will have to write it twice. Apart from

11812
15:29:18.297 --> 15:29:23.693
the letters s and d, the % symbol can be followed by other characters for other

11813
15:29:23.693 --> 15:29:29.020
purposes. You can deepen your knowledge by looking at Microsoft documentation.

11814
15:29:29.020 --> 15:29:34.059
In the RAISEERROR statement, we can also specify an error number instead of a

11815
15:29:34.059 --> 15:29:39.491
message string. This error number comes from sys.messages. We will learn more about

11816
15:29:39.491 --> 15:29:44.661
the use of sys.messages in the lesson dedicated to the format message function.

11817
15:29:44.661 --> 15:29:49.635
Let's include the RAISEERROR statement inside a TRICAT construct to see what

11818
15:29:49.635 --> 15:29:54.740
happens. In this example, the severity level of the RAISEERROR statement is 9.

11819
15:29:54.740 --> 15:29:59.628
If you remember from the previous chapter, severity levels lower than 11 are not

11820
15:29:59.628 --> 15:30:04.394
catchable by the catch block. So here, this error won't be caught by the catch

11821
15:30:04.394 --> 15:30:09.466
block. This will be the output. However, if we set up the severity level between 11

11822
15:30:09.466 --> 15:30:14.476
and 19, the error will be caught by the catch block. In this example, the severity

11823
15:30:14.476 --> 15:30:19.120
level is 16, so the output will be you are in the catch block. Now, you will

11824
15:30:19.120 --> 15:30:21.870
practice applying the raise errors technique.

11825
15:30:21.870 --> 15:30:26.533
Now that you have learned the raise error statement, let's discover the throw

11826
15:30:26.533 --> 15:30:31.499
statement. As we said in the previous lesson, Microsoft recommends using the throw

11827
15:30:31.499 --> 15:30:36.587
statement over the raise error statement. Let's see the throw syntax. It starts with

11828
15:30:36.587 --> 15:30:41.432
the word throw and can optionally be followed by a list of parameters. The first

11829
15:30:41.432 --> 15:30:46.337
parameter is the error number, the second parameter is the message, and the third

11830
15:30:46.337 --> 15:30:47.730
parameter is the state.

11831
15:30:47.730 --> 15:30:52.911
Unlike the eraseError statement, the throw statement allows re-throwing an original

11832
15:30:52.911 --> 15:30:58.030
error caught by a catch block. Let's see some examples. The throw statement can be

11833
15:30:58.030 --> 15:31:03.149
invoked without parameters within a catch block. In this example, we intentionally

11834
15:31:03.149 --> 15:31:08.143
created an error, dividing the price of the orders by zero. This error makes the

11835
15:31:08.143 --> 15:31:13.200
control pass to the catch block, invoking the throw statement without parameters.

11836
15:31:13.200 --> 15:31:18.428
The throw statement rethrows the original error caused in the try block and stops

11837
15:31:18.428 --> 15:31:23.656
the execution, so this selected statement, this line is executed, isn't executed.

11838
15:31:23.656 --> 15:31:28.819
The rethrown error has the same error number, severity and state as the original

11839
15:31:28.819 --> 15:31:34.047
one. You have to be careful when using a statement before the throw statement. In

11840
15:31:34.047 --> 15:31:39.404
such a case, this statement must always end with a semicolon. Otherwise, SQL Server

11841
15:31:39.404 --> 15:31:41.340
will have unexpected behavior.

11842
15:31:41.340 --> 15:31:46.602
In this example, the SELECT statement that goes before the THROW statement doesn't

11843
15:31:46.602 --> 15:31:51.736
end with a semicolon. SQL Server thinks that the word THROW is an alias for this

11844
15:31:51.736 --> 15:31:57.063
SELECT statement. So, if we execute this example, we will get the following output.

11845
15:31:57.063 --> 15:32:00.400
We can see that the THROW statement wasn't executed.

11846
15:32:00.400 --> 15:32:05.896
Let's correct this script and add a semicolon after the SELECT statement before the

11847
15:32:05.896 --> 15:32:10.928
THROW statement. If we execute this code, we will see the text, this line is

11848
15:32:10.928 --> 15:32:16.358
executed with no column name, as it doesn't have an alias. Now the THROW statement

11849
15:32:16.358 --> 15:32:19.855
is executed, so we get the information about the error.

11850
15:32:19.855 --> 15:32:25.207
The throw statement can also be used with parameters for the error number, message,

11851
15:32:25.207 --> 15:32:30.495
and state. This syntax can be included within a catch block or outside of it. Here

11852
15:32:30.495 --> 15:32:35.912
is an example of the use of the throw statement. The first parameter, 52,000, is the

11853
15:32:35.912 --> 15:32:36.750
error number.

11854
15:32:36.750 --> 15:32:42.364
This number can be greater than or equal to 50,000. The second parameter, this is an

11855
15:32:42.364 --> 15:32:47.845
example, is the message. Unlike the eraseError statement, the throw statement does

11856
15:32:47.845 --> 15:32:53.460
not allow the inclusion of parameter placeholders such as %d or %s. However, we will

11857
15:32:53.460 --> 15:32:58.940
learn how to do something similar in the next lesson. Finally, the third parameter

11858
15:32:58.940 --> 15:33:04.020
is the state, one in this example. This code generates the following output.

11859
15:33:04.020 --> 15:33:09.089
The throw statement doesn't allow the specification of the severity. SQL Server

11860
15:33:09.089 --> 15:33:14.350
always sets it to 16. You can include the throw statement with parameters within a

11861
15:33:14.350 --> 15:33:19.290
try block. In this example, as the recent any staff whose ID is 15, the throw

11862
15:33:19.290 --> 15:33:24.616
statement with parameters is invoked. As SQL Server always sets the severity of the

11863
15:33:24.616 --> 15:33:29.556
throw statement to 16, the control passes to the cat block, showing the error

11864
15:33:29.556 --> 15:33:33.470
message. Now, you will practice applying the throw statement.

11865
15:33:33.470 --> 15:33:38.439
In this lesson, we will learn how to customize error messages in the throw

11866
15:33:38.439 --> 15:33:43.878
statement. As we learned in a previous lesson, the throw statement does not allow

11867
15:33:43.878 --> 15:33:49.452
parameter placeholders such as %s or %d as the erase error statement does. However,

11868
15:33:49.452 --> 15:33:55.092
there are two ways to customize our errors for the throw statement. The first one is

11869
15:33:55.092 --> 15:34:00.598
by using a variable and concatenate strings. The second one is by using the format

11870
15:34:00.598 --> 15:34:01.740
message function.

11871
15:34:01.740 --> 15:34:06.725
Let's see how to use a variable to do it. In this example, we declared two

11872
15:34:06.725 --> 15:34:12.316
variables. Staff ID, with a value of 500, and myMessage. With the use of the concat

11873
15:34:12.316 --> 15:34:17.773
function, we concatenate in the second variable, myMessage, the text, there is no

11874
15:34:17.773 --> 15:34:23.431
staff member for ID. With the value of the variable, staff ID, and finally, with the

11875
15:34:23.431 --> 15:34:25.250
text, try with another one.

11876
15:34:25.250 --> 15:34:31.175
After that, the code takes, if it exists, any staff member whose ID has the value of

11877
15:34:31.175 --> 15:34:36.960
the variable staff ID. In the recent any, the throw statement is executed, passing

11878
15:34:36.960 --> 15:34:42.885
the variable myMessage as a message parameter. If we execute this code as the recent

11879
15:34:42.885 --> 15:34:48.810
any staff member with that staff ID, we have the following output. There is no staff

11880
15:34:48.810 --> 15:34:50.080
member for ID 500.

11881
15:34:50.080 --> 15:34:55.285
Try with another one. The second way of customizing an error message is by using the

11882
15:34:55.285 --> 15:35:00.428
format message function. This is the syntax. It starts with the word format message

11883
15:35:00.428 --> 15:35:05.633
followed by a message string or a message number. After that, you can optionally add

11884
15:35:05.633 --> 15:35:10.652
arguments to replace the parameter placeholders of the first parameter. Let's see

11885
15:35:10.652 --> 15:35:14.990
how to use the format message function with the message string option.

11886
15:35:14.990 --> 15:35:20.371
In this example, we assigned the result of the format message function to the

11887
15:35:20.371 --> 15:35:26.241
variable myMessage. Note that in the first parameter of the format message function,

11888
15:35:26.241 --> 15:35:32.111
we included the parameter placeholders %d and %s. The value of the variable staff ID

11889
15:35:32.111 --> 15:35:37.981
will replace %d and the text tried with another one will replace %s. After that, the

11890
15:35:37.981 --> 15:35:43.851
code also checks if there is any staff member whose ID has the value of the variable

11891
15:35:43.851 --> 15:35:44.480
staff ID.

11892
15:35:44.480 --> 15:35:49.876
If the recent any, the throw statement is executed, passing the variable myMessage

11893
15:35:49.876 --> 15:35:55.141
as the message parameter. If we execute this code as the recent any staff member

11894
15:35:55.141 --> 15:36:00.340
with that staff ID, we have the following output. Now, let's see how to use the

11895
15:36:00.340 --> 15:36:05.539
format message function with a message number. The possible message numbers are

11896
15:36:05.539 --> 15:36:10.541
cataloged in the sys.messages view. It contains a row for each message ID or

11897
15:36:10.541 --> 15:36:13.700
language ID of the error messages in the system.

11898
15:36:13.700 --> 15:36:19.443
We can choose any message ID or add a new message to this view to customize our

11899
15:36:19.443 --> 15:36:25.186
errors. To add a new message to sys.messages, we need to do it by executing the

11900
15:36:25.186 --> 15:36:31.148
spAddMessageStored procedure. The first three parameters of this stored procedure,

11901
15:36:31.148 --> 15:36:37.036
message ID, severity and message text are mandatory. The number of the message ID

11902
15:36:37.036 --> 15:36:43.070
must be greater than 50,000. You can optionally specify the language. If you don't,

11903
15:36:43.070 --> 15:36:49.137
It will be the default language of the session. In this course, we are not covering

11904
15:36:49.137 --> 15:36:55.276
the rest of the parameters. Let's add a new message to sys.messages by executing the

11905
15:36:55.276 --> 15:37:00.904
stored procedure spAddMessage. In this example, the message ID is 55,000, the

11906
15:37:00.904 --> 15:37:06.898
severity 16, the message text, there is no staff member for ID %d %s, and finally,

11907
15:37:06.898 --> 15:37:09.310
the language is American English.

11908
15:37:09.310 --> 15:37:15.147
Now that we have the message, we can use it in the format message function, passing

11909
15:37:15.147 --> 15:37:20.632
the number 55000 as the first parameter. In this lesson, you will learn what a

11910
15:37:20.632 --> 15:37:26.539
transaction is and how to combine transactions with error handling. We will practice

11911
15:37:26.539 --> 15:37:32.375
concepts related to transactions with a dataset based on bank account transactions.

11912
15:37:32.375 --> 15:37:37.790
The dataset contains information about customers, accounts, and transactions.

11913
15:37:37.790 --> 15:37:43.967
What is a transaction? A transaction is the execution of one or more statements such

11914
15:37:43.967 --> 15:37:49.777
that either all or none of these statements are executed. Let's see an example.

11915
15:37:49.777 --> 15:37:55.587
Imagine we want to transfer $100 from account A to account B. First, we have to

11916
15:37:55.587 --> 15:38:01.470
subtract $100 from account A and after that, add those $100 to account B. If the

11917
15:38:01.470 --> 15:38:06.030
second operation fails, we can't subtract $100 from account A.

11918
15:38:06.030 --> 15:38:11.573
These two operations must behave as an atomic operation. We execute both of them or

11919
15:38:11.573 --> 15:38:16.782
none of them. They need to be in the same transaction. SQL Server defines some

11920
15:38:16.782 --> 15:38:22.258
transaction statements. BeginTran or transaction is used to set the starting point

11921
15:38:22.258 --> 15:38:27.600
of a transaction. You can optionally add a name for the transaction and the with

11922
15:38:27.600 --> 15:38:31.540
mark option. These options won't be covered in this course.

11923
15:38:31.540 --> 15:38:36.225
Optionally followed by tran or transaction marks the end of a successful

11924
15:38:36.225 --> 15:38:41.496
transaction. When executed, the effect of the transaction cannot be reversed. You

11925
15:38:41.496 --> 15:38:46.962
can optionally add the name of the transaction and set to on or off the with delayed

11926
15:38:46.962 --> 15:38:52.298
durability option. These options won't be covered in this course. Rollback tran or

11927
15:38:52.298 --> 15:38:57.699
transaction reverts a transaction to the beginning of it or a save point inside the

11928
15:38:57.699 --> 15:38:58.480
transaction.

11929
15:38:58.480 --> 15:39:04.677
After rollback tran or transaction, you can optionally add the transaction or the

11930
15:39:04.677 --> 15:39:11.027
save point name. We will define save points in the next lesson. Let's see all these

11931
15:39:11.027 --> 15:39:17.300
statements. In this example, we tried to transfer $100 from account 1 with $24,400

11932
15:39:17.300 --> 15:39:23.420
to account 5 with $35,300. We mark the start of the transaction with begin tran.

11933
15:39:23.420 --> 15:39:29.352
The first operation updates account 1, subtracting $100. Then, we register that

11934
15:39:29.352 --> 15:39:35.584
movement into the transactions table. After that, we add $100 to account 5 and then

11935
15:39:35.584 --> 15:39:41.891
register the movement into the transactions table. Finally, commit tran confirms the

11936
15:39:41.891 --> 15:39:47.898
changes. By executing this code, we can see that account 1 decreases by $100 and

11937
15:39:47.898 --> 15:39:50.000
account 2 increases by $100.

11938
15:39:50.000 --> 15:39:54.992
Besides, two rows are inserted into the transactions table. Let's change the

11939
15:39:54.992 --> 15:40:00.050
previous example using rollback tran instead of commit tran to revert all the

11940
15:40:00.050 --> 15:40:05.437
changes as if nothing had happened. Accounts 1 and 5 have the same balance as they

11941
15:40:05.437 --> 15:40:10.560
had before the execution of the script. Besides, no rows are inserted into the

11942
15:40:10.560 --> 15:40:16.012
transactions table. Let's surround the previous code with a try catch. We start the

11943
15:40:16.012 --> 15:40:18.180
transaction within the try block.

11944
15:40:18.180 --> 15:40:23.434
If everything between begin tran and commit tran executed without errors, the

11945
15:40:23.434 --> 15:40:29.098
changes will be confirmed. However, if there is an error, the catch block will take

11946
15:40:29.098 --> 15:40:34.762
the control and revert the changes with the rollback tran statement. Executing this

11947
15:40:34.762 --> 15:40:40.494
code, as there are no errors, account 1 decreases by $100 and account 2 increases by

11948
15:40:40.494 --> 15:40:44.930
$100. Besides, two rows are inserted into the transactions table.

11949
15:40:44.930 --> 15:40:50.244
Let's introduce an error in the previous script inserting $100 into the transactions

11950
15:40:50.244 --> 15:40:55.557
table for account 500, which doesn't exist. If we execute this code, as there is one

11951
15:40:55.557 --> 15:41:00.302
error, the catch block will take the control, executing the rollback trance

11952
15:41:00.302 --> 15:41:05.615
statement. All the changes are reverted as if nothing had happened. Accounts 1 and 5

11953
15:41:05.615 --> 15:41:09.980
have the same balance as they had before the execution of the script.

11954
15:41:09.980 --> 15:41:15.711
Finally, let's see what happens if we don't explicitly specify a transaction. SQL

11955
15:41:15.711 --> 15:41:21.159
Server considers each statement as an individual transaction. The three first

11956
15:41:21.159 --> 15:41:27.103
statements are executed correctly, but the last statement fails, and this row is not

11957
15:41:27.103 --> 15:41:33.046
inserted. Account 1 decreases by $100 and Account 2 increases by $100. However, only

11958
15:41:33.046 --> 15:41:38.990
one row is inserted into the transactions table, resulting in an inconsistent state.

11959
15:41:38.990 --> 15:41:44.750
In this lesson, you will understand TRANCOUNT and SAFEPOINT. TRANCOUNT returns the

11960
15:41:44.750 --> 15:41:50.230
number of BEGIN TRAN statements that are active in your current connection. It

11961
15:41:50.230 --> 15:41:56.060
returns a value greater than zero if you are in an open transaction and zero if you

11962
15:41:56.060 --> 15:42:01.399
are not. It can be modified by the BEGIN TRAN statement, which increases the

11963
15:42:01.399 --> 15:42:07.159
TRANCOUNT value by one. The COMMIT TRAN statement decreases the TRANCOUNT value by

11964
15:42:07.159 --> 15:42:07.440
one.

11965
15:42:07.440 --> 15:42:13.028
The rollback tran statement decrements the tran count to zero except if there is a

11966
15:42:13.028 --> 15:42:18.412
save point. Let's see an example of tran count in a nested transaction. In this

11967
15:42:18.412 --> 15:42:24.068
example, we can see a nested transaction. There is a begin tran after another begin

11968
15:42:24.068 --> 15:42:29.656
tran before commit tran and rollback tran. Initially, the tran count value is zero

11969
15:42:29.656 --> 15:42:35.380
as there is no open transaction yet. After that, a begin tran statement is executed.

11970
15:42:35.380 --> 15:42:41.463
incrementing the trancount value by one. Then, a delete statement deletes every row

11971
15:42:41.463 --> 15:42:47.472
from the transactions table. After that, another transaction is open, incrementing

11972
15:42:47.472 --> 15:42:53.628
the value of trancount by one. The next statement deletes every row from the account

11973
15:42:53.628 --> 15:42:59.124
table. Then, a commit tran statement is executed, decrementing the value of

11974
15:42:59.124 --> 15:43:00.370
trancount by one.

11975
15:43:00.370 --> 15:43:05.846
If the value of tranCount is 1 before executing the commit tran statement, the

11976
15:43:05.846 --> 15:43:11.603
current transaction is closed and the changes are confirmed. However, if its value

11977
15:43:11.603 --> 15:43:17.149
is richer than 1, like in this example where it is 2, the commit tran statement

11978
15:43:17.149 --> 15:43:22.414
decrements tranCount by 1 but doesn't commit the deletion of the table. The

11979
15:43:22.414 --> 15:43:24.380
transaction is still active.

11980
15:43:24.380 --> 15:43:30.024
Finally, rollback tran decreases the value of trancount to zero and reverts all the

11981
15:43:30.024 --> 15:43:35.736
uncommitted statements, including the deletion of the account table. Let's check the

11982
15:43:35.736 --> 15:43:41.108
content of transactions and accounts. We see that rows of these tables were not

11983
15:43:41.108 --> 15:43:46.548
deleted. Let's change the previous example using commit tran instead of rollback

11984
15:43:46.548 --> 15:43:48.860
tran in the penultimate statement.

11985
15:43:48.860 --> 15:43:54.576
The last commit tran statement will be executed when the tranCount value is 1, so it

11986
15:43:54.576 --> 15:44:00.225
will commit the deletion of the tables. If we check the content of transactions and

11987
15:44:00.225 --> 15:44:05.329
accounts, we can see that the rows of these tables were deleted. We can use

11988
15:44:05.329 --> 15:44:10.705
tranCount in a tricat construct to determine whether any transactions are still

11989
15:44:10.705 --> 15:44:14.720
open, by checking if the tranCount value is greater than 0.

11990
15:44:14.720 --> 15:44:20.215
If there are any open transactions, we can commit or roll back the changes. Let's

11991
15:44:20.215 --> 15:44:25.643
study save points. Save points are markers we can set within a transaction. They

11992
15:44:25.643 --> 15:44:31.138
allow us to roll back to the different save points we set. Save points start with

11993
15:44:31.138 --> 15:44:36.566
the word Savetran or transaction followed by the name. Let's see an example that

11994
15:44:36.566 --> 15:44:42.062
uses save points. This code opens a new transaction and after that marks the save

11995
15:44:42.062 --> 15:44:42.740
point one.

11996
15:44:42.740 --> 15:44:48.625
Then, the data of a new customer is inserted. Next, another save point, save point 2

11997
15:44:48.625 --> 15:44:54.299
is marked and the data of another customer is inserted. After that, rollback tran

11998
15:44:54.299 --> 15:45:00.044
save point 2 rollbacks the changes between the declaration of save point 2 and the

11999
15:45:00.044 --> 15:45:05.788
rollback of save point 2. In other words, rollback tran rollbacks the insertion of

12000
15:45:05.788 --> 15:45:07.680
the customer, Zack Roberts.

12001
15:45:07.680 --> 15:45:13.933
RollbackTran savepoint1 rollbacks the changes between savepoint1 and savepoint2. It

12002
15:45:13.933 --> 15:45:20.110
is the data of Mark Davis. Then, a new savepoint, savepoint3, is marked. Then, the

12003
15:45:20.110 --> 15:45:26.062
code inserts the data of another customer. Finally, the commitTran statement is

12004
15:45:26.062 --> 15:45:32.164
executed. If we execute this code, we can see that only the last insert statement

12005
15:45:32.164 --> 15:45:36.910
took place, because savepoint1 and savepoint2 were rolled back.

12006
15:45:36.910 --> 15:45:42.830
If we select the value of TRANCOUNT after every save point rollback, we can see that

12007
15:45:42.830 --> 15:45:48.751
these statements don't decrease the value of TRANCOUNT. In this example, we see that

12008
15:45:48.751 --> 15:45:54.530
the outputs of both are 1. In this lesson, you will learn how to control errors in

12009
15:45:54.530 --> 15:46:00.099
transactions with the use of EXACT abort and EXACT state. EXACT abort specifies

12010
15:46:00.099 --> 15:46:05.667
whether the current transaction will be automatically rolled back when an error

12011
15:46:05.667 --> 15:46:06.160
occurs.

12012
15:46:06.160 --> 15:46:12.135
Exact abort can be set to on or off. Off is the default setting. If an error occurs

12013
15:46:12.135 --> 15:46:17.606
under this setting, the transaction can automatically be rolled back or not,

12014
15:46:17.606 --> 15:46:23.222
depending on the error. If the transaction isn't rolled back, it remains open.

12015
15:46:23.222 --> 15:46:28.909
Setting exact abort to on ensures that if there is an error, the transaction is

12016
15:46:28.909 --> 15:46:33.660
rolled back and the execution is aborted. Let's see some examples.

12017
15:46:33.660 --> 15:46:39.165
In this example, we set exact award to off. We insert the data of two customers

12018
15:46:39.165 --> 15:46:44.878
within a transaction. Finally, we commit the transaction. If we execute this code,

12019
15:46:44.878 --> 15:46:50.592
we get an error when inserting the data of the second customer. That is because in

12020
15:46:50.592 --> 15:46:56.097
the customers table, there is a constraint that checks that every email must be

12021
15:46:56.097 --> 15:47:01.532
unique, and Dylan's email is already registered. But if we check the customers

12022
15:47:01.532 --> 15:47:01.950
table,

12023
15:47:01.950 --> 15:47:07.551
We see that the first insert statement was committed. That's because we didn't roll

12024
15:47:07.551 --> 15:47:13.085
back anything in case of an error. Let's change the previous example setting exact

12025
15:47:13.085 --> 15:47:18.146
abort to own. Now, the second insert statement also generates an error. The

12026
15:47:18.146 --> 15:47:23.747
difference is that, with this setting, the transaction is rolled back, so the first

12027
15:47:23.747 --> 15:47:29.010
insert statement doesn't take place. Let's study the exact abort behavior with

12028
15:47:29.010 --> 15:47:29.820
phraseError.

12029
15:47:29.820 --> 15:47:35.413
In this example, we set Xactable to own. After that, we open a transaction and

12030
15:47:35.413 --> 15:47:41.437
insert the data of two customers with new emails. Between these insertions, we raise

12031
15:47:41.437 --> 15:47:47.389
an error using RaiseError. Finally, we commit the transaction. If we run this code,

12032
15:47:47.389 --> 15:47:53.270
we can see that RaiseError produces an error. However, the execution continues and

12033
15:47:53.270 --> 15:47:59.150
the transaction remains open, so the data of the second customer is also inserted.

12034
15:47:59.150 --> 15:48:04.494
Checking the content of the customer's table, we see that both insert statements

12035
15:48:04.494 --> 15:48:09.905
took place. Let's modify the previous example using throw instead of raise error.

12036
15:48:09.905 --> 15:48:15.316
This time, we also get an error. However, none of the customer's data is inserted

12037
15:48:15.316 --> 15:48:20.527
because the transaction is automatically rolled back. If you remember from the

12038
15:48:20.527 --> 15:48:25.604
previous chapter, Microsoft suggests throw over raise error, and this is one

12039
15:48:25.604 --> 15:48:27.140
important reason. Next,

12040
15:48:27.140 --> 15:48:32.540
Let's study the exactState function. ExactState doesn't take any parameters and

12041
15:48:32.540 --> 15:48:37.939
returns 0 when there is no open transaction. 1 when the transaction is open and

12042
15:48:37.939 --> 15:48:43.544
committable, and minus 1 when the transaction is open but uncomittable, also known

12043
15:48:43.544 --> 15:48:48.875
as doomed transaction. When a transaction is open but uncomittable, you cannot

12044
15:48:48.875 --> 15:48:54.480
commit the transaction or rollback to a save point. The only option you have is to

12045
15:48:54.480 --> 15:48:56.530
rollback the full transaction.

12046
15:48:56.530 --> 15:49:02.398
You cannot make any changes, but you can read data. Let's see some examples. This

12047
15:49:02.398 --> 15:49:08.337
code sets exacta board to off. Then, a transaction starts within the try block and

12048
15:49:08.337 --> 15:49:13.988
inserts the data of two customers. If everything goes fine, the transaction is

12049
15:49:13.988 --> 15:49:20.000
committed. Otherwise, the catch block takes control and rollbacks if exact state is

12050
15:49:20.000 --> 15:49:25.940
minus one, or commits if exact state is one. Finally, we select the error message.

12051
15:49:25.940 --> 15:49:31.459
Resetting of exact award makes that when the catch block takes the control, the

12052
15:49:31.459 --> 15:49:37.257
transaction state remains open and committable. Therefore, exact state is 1, so the

12053
15:49:37.257 --> 15:49:43.055
transaction is committed. As the only statement to be committed is the insertion of

12054
15:49:43.055 --> 15:49:48.923
the first customer's data, it is inserted. Let's change the previous example setting

12055
15:49:48.923 --> 15:49:50.180
exact award to on.

12056
15:49:50.180 --> 15:49:56.141
This will cause that when the catch block takes the control, the transaction remains

12057
15:49:56.141 --> 15:50:01.818
open but uncommittable, so exact state is minus 1. The only option we have is to

12058
15:50:01.818 --> 15:50:07.637
rollback. If we execute this code, we can see that the first customer's data isn't

12059
15:50:07.637 --> 15:50:13.385
inserted because the transaction was rolled back. Let's play with exact abort. In

12060
15:50:13.385 --> 15:50:18.707
this final chapter, we will study what concurrency is and how it can affect

12061
15:50:18.707 --> 15:50:19.630
transactions.

12062
15:50:19.630 --> 15:50:25.257
Concurrency happens when two or more transactions that read or change shared data

12063
15:50:25.257 --> 15:50:31.023
are executed at the same time. Depending on the purpose of the transactions, we may

12064
15:50:31.023 --> 15:50:36.650
want to isolate our transaction from other transactions. We can achieve different

12065
15:50:36.650 --> 15:50:42.486
levels of isolation using the transaction isolation levels that SQL Server provides.

12066
15:50:42.486 --> 15:50:48.183
These are read committed, which is the default level, read uncommitted, repeatable

12067
15:50:48.183 --> 15:50:48.530
read,

12068
15:50:48.530 --> 15:50:54.546
and snapshot. This is the syntax for setting an isolation level. It starts with

12069
15:50:54.546 --> 15:51:00.944
setTransactionIsolationLevel followed by the specific isolation level. If we want to

12070
15:51:00.944 --> 15:51:06.884
know the current transaction isolation level of our session, we can select the

12071
15:51:06.884 --> 15:51:12.824
TransactionIsolationLevel column from sys.dmxx sessions. This column returns a

12072
15:51:12.824 --> 15:51:17.470
different value depending on the transaction isolation level.

12073
15:51:17.470 --> 15:51:22.800
In this example, the current isolation level is ReadCommitted. In this lesson, we

12074
15:51:22.800 --> 15:51:28.327
will explain ReadUncommitted, and the rest of the isolation levels will be explained

12075
15:51:28.327 --> 15:51:33.657
in the next lessons. ReadUncommitted is the least restrictive isolation level. It

12076
15:51:33.657 --> 15:51:39.118
allows you to read rows modified by another transaction which hasn't been committed

12077
15:51:39.118 --> 15:51:44.184
or rolled back yet. Under ReadUncommitted, it is possible to encounter dirty,

12078
15:51:44.184 --> 15:51:46.290
non-repeatable or phantom reads.

12079
15:51:46.290 --> 15:51:51.218
These are called concurrency phenomena because they occur when two or more

12080
15:51:51.218 --> 15:51:56.680
transactions are running concurrently. So data that might have been chained by one

12081
15:51:56.680 --> 15:52:02.074
transaction is read by another. To understand what dirty reads are, let's suppose

12082
15:52:02.074 --> 15:52:07.336
that account 5 has $35,000. One person starts a transaction, transaction 1, and

12083
15:52:07.336 --> 15:52:09.912
updates the current balance of accounts.

12084
15:52:09.912 --> 15:52:16.120
5 to $30,000. But transaction 1 doesn't commit or roll back yet. After this update,

12085
15:52:16.120 --> 15:52:22.328
another transaction, transaction 2, selects the current balance of account 5, under

12086
15:52:22.328 --> 15:52:23.600
return committed.

12087
15:52:23.600 --> 15:52:29.570
As this isolation level allows to read uncommitted changes, transaction 2 sees that

12088
15:52:29.570 --> 15:52:35.467
the current balance of account 5 is $30,000, that is, a dirty read. After that, if

12089
15:52:35.467 --> 15:52:41.365
transaction 1 rollbacks, the current balance of account 5 doesn't change. However,

12090
15:52:41.365 --> 15:52:47.118
transaction 2 still thinks that the current balance is $30,000. Read uncommitted

12091
15:52:47.118 --> 15:52:49.420
also allows non-reputable reads.

12092
15:52:49.420 --> 15:52:55.375
Let's suppose that Transaction 1 selects the current balance of Account 5, which is

12093
15:52:55.375 --> 15:53:00.971
$35,000. After that, Transaction 2 updates the current balance of Account 5 to

12094
15:53:00.971 --> 15:53:06.997
$30,000 and commits. If Transaction 1 wants to select the current balance of Account

12095
15:53:06.997 --> 15:53:12.952
5 again, it will output $30,000, resulting in a different value from the first time

12096
15:53:12.952 --> 15:53:16.180
it selected the current balance of Account 5.

12097
15:53:16.180 --> 15:53:22.237
This difference can cause problems if we make a business decision based on the first

12098
15:53:22.237 --> 15:53:27.788
result. Finally, let's explain phantom reads. Let's imagine one person starts

12099
15:53:27.788 --> 15:53:33.629
transaction 1 under written committed and selects accounts with a balance between

12100
15:53:33.629 --> 15:53:39.469
$45,000 and $50,000. There is only one account that matches that criterion. After

12101
15:53:39.469 --> 15:53:42.930
some seconds, another person opens transaction 2

12102
15:53:42.930 --> 15:53:48.758
and inserts one account which fulfills the selection criteria of Transaction 1,

12103
15:53:48.758 --> 15:53:54.954
adding one account with a balance of $45,000 and commits. After that, if Transaction

12104
15:53:54.954 --> 15:54:01.151
1 wants to select accounts with a balance between $45,000 and $50,000 again, it will

12105
15:54:01.151 --> 15:54:07.052
get one additional account, the Fountain Read. This result is different from the

12106
15:54:07.052 --> 15:54:07.790
first one.

12107
15:54:07.790 --> 15:54:13.392
This difference can also cause problems if we make a business decision based on the

12108
15:54:13.392 --> 15:54:18.859
first result. Let's finish with the pros, cons, and when to use read uncommitted.

12109
15:54:18.859 --> 15:54:24.529
Read uncommitted can return the results of a query faster than using other isolation

12110
15:54:24.529 --> 15:54:30.131
levels. As we will see, other isolation levels can block other transactions, so the

12111
15:54:30.131 --> 15:54:34.855
information may not be returned immediately. However, it allows dirty,

12112
15:54:34.855 --> 15:54:37.150
non-repeatable, and phantom reads.

12113
15:54:37.150 --> 15:54:42.862
You can use ReadUncommitted when you don't want to be blocked by other transactions

12114
15:54:42.862 --> 15:54:48.506
but don't mind concurrency phenomena. You can also use it when you explicitly want

12115
15:54:48.506 --> 15:54:54.287
to watch uncommitted data. Let's practice! In this lesson, you will be introduced to

12116
15:54:54.287 --> 15:54:59.725
the ReadCommitted and the RepeatableRead isolation levels. ReadCommitted is the

12117
15:54:59.725 --> 15:55:02.340
default isolation level in SQL Server.

12118
15:55:02.340 --> 15:55:07.791
Under this isolation level, you can't read data modified by other transactions that

12119
15:55:07.791 --> 15:55:12.782
hasn't committed or rolled back yet. This is the syntax for setting the read

12120
15:55:12.782 --> 15:55:18.298
committed isolation level. If you remember from the previous lesson, we learned that

12121
15:55:18.298 --> 15:55:22.895
under the read uncommitted isolation level, we could find dirty reads,

12122
15:55:22.895 --> 15:55:27.820
non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads. The read committed isolation level

12123
15:55:27.820 --> 15:55:33.542
is more restrictive than read uncommitted in the sense that read uncommitted

12124
15:55:33.542 --> 15:55:39.791
prevents dirty reads. Let's demonstrate this behavior. Let's suppose that account 5

12125
15:55:39.791 --> 15:55:45.739
has $35,000. One person starts transaction 1 and updates the current balance of

12126
15:55:45.739 --> 15:55:51.913
account 5 to $30,000. But transaction 1 doesn't commit or rollback yet. After this

12127
15:55:51.913 --> 15:55:55.150
update, another transaction, transaction 2,

12128
15:55:55.150 --> 15:56:00.548
selects the current balance of account 5 under the read committed isolation level.

12129
15:56:00.548 --> 15:56:06.011
As this isolation level doesn't allow us to read uncommitted changes, transaction 2

12130
15:56:06.011 --> 15:56:11.474
is blocked and has to wait until transaction 1 commits or rollbacks, preventing the

12131
15:56:11.474 --> 15:56:16.609
dirty read. After that, when transaction 1 commits or rollbacks, transaction 2

12132
15:56:16.609 --> 15:56:19.900
immediately gets the output of the selected query.

12133
15:56:19.900 --> 15:56:25.340
