the instead of trigger prevents the initial statement from executing. This

12348
16:14:25.867 --> 16:14:31.349
difference will influence the use cases for each type of trigger. One example of

12349
16:14:31.349 --> 16:14:36.968
using an after trigger is for a large insert of data into a sales table. Once that

12350
16:14:36.968 --> 16:14:38.270
data gets inserted,

12351
16:14:38.270 --> 16:14:43.404
A cleansing step should run to remove or repair any unwanted information. The

12352
16:14:43.404 --> 16:14:48.871
cleansing step will be started by the trigger. When the cleansing step finishes, a

12353
16:14:48.871 --> 16:14:54.072
report with the results will be generated. This report should be analyzed by a

12354
16:14:54.072 --> 16:14:59.472
database administrator, so the trigger will then send an email to the responsible

12355
16:14:59.472 --> 16:15:04.873
people. This is all part of the trigger definition. Now, let's look at an example

12356
16:15:04.873 --> 16:15:07.140
where using an instead of trigger,

12357
16:15:07.140 --> 16:15:12.828
Imagine you have a table containing lightbulb information and stock details for a

12358
16:15:12.828 --> 16:15:18.587
sales platform. The power column values have changed for some models and an update

12359
16:15:18.587 --> 16:15:24.064
is initiated to change the information in the table. There are still some bulb

12360
16:15:24.064 --> 16:15:29.542
models in stock that have the old power values, however, and they shouldn't be

12361
16:15:29.542 --> 16:15:35.300
updated. An instead of trigger can help you deal with this more complex situation.

12362
16:15:35.300 --> 16:15:41.054
The correct approach is to update the characteristics only for the models that don't

12363
16:15:41.054 --> 16:15:46.602
have the old version in stock. The new models need to be in the table too, but as

12364
16:15:46.602 --> 16:15:52.356
new rows instead of updated ones. The only limit on the use cases of DML triggers is

12365
16:15:52.356 --> 16:15:58.110
your imagination. This doesn't mean triggers are the answer to all of your problems,

12366
16:15:58.110 --> 16:16:03.453
but that's a topic we'll discuss in the next lesson. For now, let's recap what

12367
16:16:03.453 --> 16:16:04.480
you've learned.

12368
16:16:04.480 --> 16:16:10.223
So far, we've talked about the types of triggers and the scenarios that best fit

12369
16:16:10.223 --> 16:16:16.254
their use. But as with any feature, triggers are not always the best solution in SQL

12370
16:16:16.254 --> 16:16:22.212
Server. In this lesson, we will compare triggers with other objects and take a look

12371
16:16:22.212 --> 16:16:28.027
at the appropriate situations for using them. The first comparison of triggers is

12372
16:16:28.027 --> 16:16:33.340
with regular stored procedures. The trigger is a stored procedure as well.

12373
16:16:33.340 --> 16:16:39.816
What makes it so special? A trigger is fired when a defined database or server event

12374
16:16:39.816 --> 16:16:46.060
occurs. Regular stored procedures run when called explicitly. A trigger runs only

12375
16:16:46.060 --> 16:16:52.382
when the event that fires it is starting. Triggers don't allow input parameters or

12376
16:16:52.382 --> 16:16:58.626
transaction statements, like begin transaction or commit. They also cannot return

12377
16:16:58.626 --> 16:17:01.170
output values. On the other hand,

12378
16:17:01.170 --> 16:17:07.307
Regular stored procedures allow input parameters, transaction statements, and the

12379
16:17:07.307 --> 16:17:13.445
possibility to return output. These considerations impact the real-life use cases

12380
16:17:13.445 --> 16:17:19.809
for triggers and regular stored procedures. We use triggers for audit and to enforce

12381
16:17:19.809 --> 16:17:25.719
integrity in most cases, and we use regular stored procedures for more general

12382
16:17:25.719 --> 16:17:30.720
tasks. The second comparison of triggers is with computed columns.

12383
16:17:30.720 --> 16:17:36.354
Computed columns are a good way to automate calculation of the values contained by

12384
16:17:36.354 --> 16:17:41.713
some columns. Computed column values are determined based on values from other

12385
16:17:41.713 --> 16:17:47.072
columns but only from the same table. This limitation can be overcome by using

12386
16:17:47.072 --> 16:17:52.705
triggers. A trigger can use columns from other tables as well to calculate values.

12387
16:17:52.705 --> 16:17:58.270
While this calculation will be done with insert or update statements when using a

12388
16:17:58.270 --> 16:17:58.820
trigger,

12389
16:17:58.820 --> 16:18:04.482
For a calculated column, it will be part of the table definition. In this example,

12390
16:18:04.482 --> 16:18:09.316
the column total amount is a computed column. Its value comes from the

12391
16:18:09.316 --> 16:18:14.979
multiplication of the quantity and price columns from the same table. But if those

12392
16:18:14.979 --> 16:18:20.089
two columns were not part of the same table, we wouldn't be able to do the

12393
16:18:20.089 --> 16:18:25.613
calculation using a computed column. In that case, we could replace the computed

12394
16:18:25.613 --> 16:18:28.030
column with its trigger definition.

12395
16:18:28.030 --> 16:18:33.792
Here, the price column is not part of the sales without price table. The trigger

12396
16:18:33.792 --> 16:18:39.698
determines the total amount by using the price column from another table, products

12397
16:18:39.698 --> 16:18:45.748
in this example. Now that you've seen how triggers can substitute for other database

12398
16:18:45.748 --> 16:18:51.726
objects, let's see how to use them in practice. After triggers can be used for both

12399
16:18:51.726 --> 16:18:56.480
DML statements and DDL statements. However, this lesson will focus

12400
16:18:56.480 --> 16:19:02.635
DML after triggers. An after trigger is used for DML statements to perform an

12401
16:19:02.635 --> 16:19:09.190
additional set of actions, one or more. This set of actions is performed after the

12402
16:19:09.190 --> 16:19:15.905
DML event that fired the trigger is finished. The DML events that can make use of an

12403
16:19:15.905 --> 16:19:22.140
after trigger are insert, update, and delete statements, run against tables or

12404
16:19:22.140 --> 16:19:22.620
views.

12405
16:19:22.620 --> 16:19:27.854
The set of actions is comprised of T-SQL code and is defined when the trigger is

12406
16:19:27.854 --> 16:19:33.023
created. Let's break down the list of database objects and events involved when

12407
16:19:33.023 --> 16:19:38.258
working with triggers. To make use of an insert, update, or delete statement, we

12408
16:19:38.258 --> 16:19:43.754
need to have a table or view to work with. Remember the fact that a trigger needs to

12409
16:19:43.754 --> 16:19:48.858
be attached to a database object? This means a table is needed for the trigger

12410
16:19:48.858 --> 16:19:49.970
creation process.

12411
16:19:49.970 --> 16:19:55.268
We will use the products table for this example. Here is a preview of its columns

12412
16:19:55.268 --> 16:20:00.761
and contents. Once you have a table defined for the scope of the trigger, you should

12413
16:20:00.761 --> 16:20:05.994
create a description of what you are trying to achieve with the trigger. In this

12414
16:20:05.994 --> 16:20:11.357
example, we want to keep some details of products that are not sold anymore. These

12415
16:20:11.357 --> 16:20:16.785
products will be removed from the products table, but their details will be kept in

12416
16:20:16.785 --> 16:20:18.420
a retired products table,

12417
16:20:18.420 --> 16:20:23.902
for financial accounting reasons. You can see that we already have the answer to the

12418
16:20:23.902 --> 16:20:29.189
question what kinds of DML statements should fire the trigger. Since rows will be

12419
16:20:29.189 --> 16:20:34.672
removed from the products table, the answer is delete statements. The description of

12420
16:20:34.672 --> 16:20:39.828
the trigger will also help us in deciding what actions will be performed by the

12421
16:20:39.828 --> 16:20:40.350
trigger.

12422
16:20:40.350 --> 16:20:45.638
In this case, the trigger will save information about the deleted rows from the

12423
16:20:45.638 --> 16:20:50.992
products table to the retired products table. The trigger should have a uniquely

12424
16:20:50.992 --> 16:20:56.615
identifying name. For this example, it will be track retired products. To recap, our

12425
16:20:56.615 --> 16:21:02.237
new trigger will fire whenever rows are removed from the products table. The deleted

12426
16:21:02.237 --> 16:21:06.320
rows information will be saved to the retired products table.

12427
16:21:06.320 --> 16:21:11.541
With all this information in hand, we can now create the after trigger. To create

12428
16:21:11.541 --> 16:21:16.826
the trigger, we use the create trigger statement, followed by the trigger name. We

12429
16:21:16.826 --> 16:21:21.854
attach the trigger to the products table. We choose the trigger type, an after

12430
16:21:21.854 --> 16:21:26.946
trigger in this case, and specify the DML statement that will fire the trigger.

12431
16:21:26.946 --> 16:21:32.167
Delete. And then we start the section that defines the actions to be performed by

12432
16:21:32.167 --> 16:21:32.940
the trigger.

12433
16:21:32.940 --> 16:21:38.570
Notice that we are not getting the information from the products table, but from a

12434
16:21:38.570 --> 16:21:44.062
table called deleted. DML triggers use two special tables, deleted and inserted.

12435
16:21:44.062 --> 16:21:49.829
These tables are automatically created by SQL Server and you can make use of them in

12436
16:21:49.829 --> 16:21:55.528
your trigger actions. Depending on the operation you are performing, they will hold

12437
16:21:55.528 --> 16:22:01.020
different information. The inserted table will store the values of the new rows,

12438
16:22:01.020 --> 16:22:06.202
for insert and update statement. For delete statements, this table is empty. The

12439
16:22:06.202 --> 16:22:11.450
deleted table will store the values of the modified rows for update statements or

12440
16:22:11.450 --> 16:22:16.891
the values of the removed rows for delete statements. The deleted table is empty for

12441
16:22:16.891 --> 16:22:22.268
insert statement. The deleted table holds the information we need for the rows that

12442
16:22:22.268 --> 16:22:27.515
will be removed from products. We get the relevant information directly from this

12443
16:22:27.515 --> 16:22:30.430
table. Now, let's create the example trigger.

12444
16:22:30.430 --> 16:22:35.583
and see how it performs. In contrast with after triggers, instead of triggers can

12445
16:22:35.583 --> 16:22:40.927
only be used for DML statements, not DDL. This is because they were designed to work

12446
16:22:40.927 --> 16:22:46.144
with DML statements, insert, update and delete. An instead of trigger will perform

12447
16:22:46.144 --> 16:22:50.979
an additional set of actions when fired in place of the event that fired the

12448
16:22:50.979 --> 16:22:56.132
trigger. That event is not run when using an instead of trigger. This is the main

12449
16:22:56.132 --> 16:22:58.740
difference between the two trigger types.

12450
16:22:58.740 --> 16:23:04.438
In terms of the DML statements that are able to fire an instead of trigger, we have

12451
16:23:04.438 --> 16:23:09.862
the same list, insert, update and delete. As before, let's explore the steps we

12452
16:23:09.862 --> 16:23:15.355
would perform to create an instead of trigger. For this example, we will use the

12453
16:23:15.355 --> 16:23:20.847
orders table that holds details of the orders placed by the fresh fruit delivery

12454
16:23:20.847 --> 16:23:22.220
company's customers.

12455
16:23:22.220 --> 16:23:27.432
Following the same steps for our analysis, we know that our target table is the

12456
16:23:27.432 --> 16:23:32.907
orders table. The trigger we will create should prevent updates to existing entries

12457
16:23:32.907 --> 16:23:38.185
in this table. This will ensure that placed orders cannot be modified. This is a

12458
16:23:38.185 --> 16:23:43.726
rule enforced by the company. This means that the trigger will fire as a response to

12459
16:23:43.726 --> 16:23:48.740
update statements. The chosen name for the trigger is prevent orders update.

12460
16:23:48.740 --> 16:23:54.153
Having an informative name is important when creating triggers. It's good if the

12461
16:23:54.153 --> 16:23:59.634
name can set some expectations about what the trigger is intended to achieve. Now

12462
16:23:59.634 --> 16:24:05.318
that we have gathered all the needed details, we can start creating the trigger. The

12463
16:24:05.318 --> 16:24:10.934
trigger definition is quite straightforward. Do not allow row updates on the orders

12464
16:24:10.934 --> 16:24:11.340
table.

12465
16:24:11.340 --> 16:24:16.495
You should already be familiar with the create trigger syntax from past examples.

12466
16:24:16.495 --> 16:24:21.269
Here we use prevent orders update for the trigger name. As we know from the

12467
16:24:21.269 --> 16:24:26.552
preceding analysis, the trigger will be attached to the orders table and it will be

12468
16:24:26.552 --> 16:24:31.771
executed when update statements are run. We also want to inform the end user about

12469
16:24:31.771 --> 16:24:37.053
the rule we set in place through the trigger. So we're going to use the raise error

12470
16:24:37.053 --> 16:24:39.790
syntax to throw an error message as output.

12471
16:24:39.790 --> 16:24:45.510
The numbers after the error message represent the severity and the state of the

12472
16:24:45.510 --> 16:24:51.230
thrown error. The severity of the error in this example is 16. This is the most

12473
16:24:51.230 --> 16:24:57.239
common value. It means we are throwing a medium level error. The state parameter is

12474
16:24:57.239 --> 16:25:03.177
used to identify the error statement in the SQL code if it is used multiple times.

12475
16:25:03.177 --> 16:25:08.100
We will be using the value 1 because the error is used only one time

12476
16:25:08.100 --> 16:25:13.250
and can be easily identified in the SQL code. A further explanation of the

12477
16:25:13.250 --> 16:25:19.096
raiseError function is outside of the scope of this course. Don't you think it would

12478
16:25:19.096 --> 16:25:24.734
be a good idea to actually create a trigger and see it in practice? So far, we've

12479
16:25:24.734 --> 16:25:30.580
only talked about DML triggers, but as mentioned earlier, triggers also come in data

12480
16:25:30.580 --> 16:25:34.060
definition language, DDL, flavors. In this lesson,

12481
16:25:34.060 --> 16:25:39.582
You are going to learn how to create DDL triggers and how they are different from

12482
16:25:39.582 --> 16:25:45.308
DML triggers. The simplest way to illustrate the specific properties of DDL triggers

12483
16:25:45.308 --> 16:25:50.762
is to compare them with DML triggers. The biggest difference is indicated by the

12484
16:25:50.762 --> 16:25:56.079
names of the trigger types. DML triggers respond to events associated with DML

12485
16:25:56.079 --> 16:25:58.670
statements, insert, update and delete.

12486
16:25:58.670 --> 16:26:04.879
On the other hand, DDL triggers respond to events associated with DDL statements

12487
16:26:04.879 --> 16:26:11.399
like create, alter, and drop. While DML triggers can be used with both the after and

12488
16:26:11.399 --> 16:26:17.686
instead of keywords, the DDL triggers accept only the after keyword. DML triggers

12489
16:26:17.686 --> 16:26:24.051
are attached to tables or views, while DDL triggers are created at the database or

12490
16:26:24.051 --> 16:26:25.060
server level.

12491
16:26:25.060 --> 16:26:30.180
We can make use of the inserted and deleted special tables when working with DML

12492
16:26:30.180 --> 16:26:35.492
triggers, but there are no special tables for use with DDL triggers. That said, you

12493
16:26:35.492 --> 16:26:40.676
can use your own defined tables to keep track of changes when using DDL triggers.

12494
16:26:40.676 --> 16:26:45.988
When dealing with DDL triggers, you'll often see syntax like this. You might expect

12495
16:26:45.988 --> 16:26:51.300
the after keyword to come before create table, but the for keyword is used instead.

12496
16:26:51.300 --> 16:26:56.603
That's because for SQL Server triggers, the for and after keywords have the same

12497
16:26:56.603 --> 16:27:02.039
results. To minimize the potential for confusion, people often use the for keyword

12498
16:27:02.039 --> 16:27:07.078
for DDL triggers and the after keyword for DML triggers. We'll stick to that

12499
16:27:07.078 --> 16:27:12.580
convention here, but be aware that both versions are correct and will behave in the

12500
16:27:12.580 --> 16:27:16.690
same way. Let's do a quick analysis of an example DDL trigger.

12501
16:27:16.690 --> 16:27:22.412
We'll create a trigger that will log information about table changes in a database.

12502
16:27:22.412 --> 16:27:28.134
The trigger will be created at the database level. The changes will be written to a

12503
16:27:28.134 --> 16:27:33.649
dedicated log table and will include details about the creation, alteration, and

12504
16:27:33.649 --> 16:27:39.164
deletion of tables. The trigger name will be track table changes. Creating a DDL

12505
16:27:39.164 --> 16:27:44.886
trigger is not much different from creating a DML trigger. We start with the create

12506
16:27:44.886 --> 16:27:45.920
trigger syntax,

12507
16:27:45.920 --> 16:27:51.430
Only this time we attach the trigger at the database level. As mentioned, we use the

12508
16:27:51.430 --> 16:27:56.875
for keyword for DDL triggers. After the keyword, we include the DDL statements that

12509
16:27:56.875 --> 16:28:02.385
will fire the trigger. Note that although the DDL statements included in the trigger

12510
16:28:02.385 --> 16:28:07.764
definition resemble the actual DDL syntax, an underscore is used between the words

12511
16:28:07.764 --> 16:28:13.012
instead of a space. The statement that is going to perform the data insert comes

12512
16:28:13.012 --> 16:28:13.340
next.

12513
16:28:13.340 --> 16:28:18.544
This statement will log the information from the eventData function as well as

12514
16:28:18.544 --> 16:28:24.082
details about the user performing the change. The eventData function actually holds

12515
16:28:24.082 --> 16:28:29.687
information about the event that runs and fires the trigger. Pretty cool, right? The

12516
16:28:29.687 --> 16:28:35.225
instead of statement cannot be used to prevent the triggering action from happening

12517
16:28:35.225 --> 16:28:40.830
for DDL triggers. So are DDL triggers used only as after triggers? The answer is no.

12518
16:28:40.830 --> 16:28:46.380
You can define a trigger to roll back the statements that fired it. In this example,

12519
16:28:46.380 --> 16:28:51.863
we don't want the users to remove tables from the database, so we set drop table as

12520
16:28:51.863 --> 16:28:57.215
the event firing the trigger. When the trigger fires, we throw an error, but also

12521
16:28:57.215 --> 16:29:02.632
roll back the initial operation, so the table deletion does not take place. We now

12522
16:29:02.632 --> 16:29:07.917
have a database trigger that prevents the firing event from happening. It's that

12523
16:29:07.917 --> 16:29:10.560
simple. How about we put those examples?

12524
16:29:10.560 --> 16:29:16.041
In this lesson, you will learn about a special type of trigger called the logon

12525
16:29:16.041 --> 16:29:21.662
trigger. Like other triggers, logon triggers perform a set of actions when fired.

12526
16:29:21.662 --> 16:29:27.421
Their defining characteristic is that they are fired by logon events. A logon event

12527
16:29:27.421 --> 16:29:32.000
occurs when a user logs on and creates a connection to SQL Server.

12528
16:29:32.000 --> 16:29:37.418
The trigger is fired after the authentication phase, meaning after the username and

12529
16:29:37.418 --> 16:29:42.248
password are checked, but before the user session is established, when the

12530
16:29:42.248 --> 16:29:47.731
information from SQL Server becomes available for queries. This is important to know

12531
16:29:47.731 --> 16:29:53.149
when using these triggers to audit and control server sessions. A logon trigger can

12532
16:29:53.149 --> 16:29:58.110
only be attached at the server level and the firing event can only be logon.

12533
16:29:58.110 --> 16:30:03.608
When you create a logon trigger, you define the set of actions to be performed by

12534
16:30:03.608 --> 16:30:09.241
the trigger when it's fired. We will just audit the logon sessions in this example,

12535
16:30:09.241 --> 16:30:14.535
and we'll use logon audit as the trigger name. Now let's see how to create the

12536
16:30:14.535 --> 16:30:20.101
trigger. We start with the same syntax we used for the other triggers, and pass in

12537
16:30:20.101 --> 16:30:25.598
the name we chose. Logon triggers are attached at the server level, so we use the

12538
16:30:25.598 --> 16:30:26.820
all server syntax.

12539
16:30:26.820 --> 16:30:32.435
When the event that fires the trigger starts, the trigger will be executed under the

12540
16:30:32.435 --> 16:30:37.850
same credentials, username and password, as the firing event. Regular users don't

12541
16:30:37.850 --> 16:30:43.399
usually have access to sensitive information like logon details, so we instruct the

12542
16:30:43.399 --> 16:30:48.880
trigger to run under the SE account. This is a built-in administrator account that

12543
16:30:48.880 --> 16:30:51.220
has full permissions on the server.

12544
16:30:51.220 --> 16:30:56.738
Running it under this account ensures that the trigger will be able to extract

12545
16:30:56.738 --> 16:31:02.680
information about the logon details. This information will be stored into the server

12546
16:31:02.680 --> 16:31:08.480
logon log table. To summarize, we are creating a trigger called logon audit at the

12547
16:31:08.480 --> 16:31:14.139
server level. To avoid permission issues, the trigger will be executed as the SA

12548
16:31:14.139 --> 16:31:19.940
account with administrator privileges. The trigger will be fired for logon events,

12549
16:31:19.940 --> 16:31:25.585
And it will store details about the user connecting to SQL Server into the server

12550
16:31:25.585 --> 16:31:31.020
logon log table. You'll find dealing with logon triggers much easier with some

12551
16:31:31.020 --> 16:31:36.526
hands-on experience. Nothing is perfect in real life, including triggers. Let's

12552
16:31:36.526 --> 16:31:41.962
recap the advantages of triggers and learn about their most common limitation.

12553
16:31:41.962 --> 16:31:44.540
Triggers provide numerous advantages.

12554
16:31:44.540 --> 16:31:47.418
As we've seen, they can be used successfully for database integrity purposes.

12555
16:31:47.418 --> 16:31:53.445
Business rules can be enforced and stored directly in the database when using

12556
16:31:53.445 --> 16:31:59.942
triggers. This makes it simpler to change or update the applications that are using

12557
16:31:59.942 --> 16:32:05.500
the database because the business logic is kept in the database itself.

12558
16:32:05.500 --> 16:32:10.991
Triggers give you control over which statements are allowed in a database, a good

12559
16:32:10.991 --> 16:32:16.415
feature when permission schemes don't offer you enough flexibility. Triggers can

12560
16:32:16.415 --> 16:32:22.109
help you implement complex business logic fired by a single event, and they are also

12561
16:32:22.109 --> 16:32:27.532
used to audit the database for changes or user activity. Triggers also have some

12562
16:32:27.532 --> 16:32:31.600
disadvantages. First, they are difficult to view and detect.

12563
16:32:31.600 --> 16:32:36.751
It can be hard for an administrator to have a clear overview of the existing

12564
16:32:36.751 --> 16:32:42.038
triggers in a database and their behavior. This means triggers are not easy to

12565
16:32:42.038 --> 16:32:47.664
manage in a centralized manner. Triggers are also invisible to client applications.

12566
16:32:47.664 --> 16:32:53.086
When debugging code, triggers are difficult to trace in most situations. And for

12567
16:32:53.086 --> 16:32:58.306
some triggers, their complex code can make it hard to follow their logic when

12568
16:32:58.306 --> 16:32:59.390
troubleshooting.

12569
16:32:59.390 --> 16:33:04.551
Triggers can also affect server performance when they are overused or poorly

12570
16:33:04.551 --> 16:33:10.119
designed. Before learning some best practices to overcome their limitations, let's

12571
16:33:10.119 --> 16:33:15.619
see how to get a good overview of all your triggers. Triggers can be difficult to

12572
16:33:15.619 --> 16:33:21.323
manage when they are undocumented, when they have complex logic in their design, and

12573
16:33:21.323 --> 16:33:25.940
because they can be created on many levels, server, database, table.

12574
16:33:25.940 --> 16:33:31.673
Luckily, SQL Server offers system views that gather all the information about

12575
16:33:31.673 --> 16:33:37.927
triggers in one place. For example, this is the statement you need to run to get all

12576
16:33:37.927 --> 16:33:44.107
the information about server level triggers. A similar view will get you details on

12577
16:33:44.107 --> 16:33:50.137
both database level triggers and table triggers. The type of trigger, database or

12578
16:33:50.137 --> 16:33:54.530
table, can be determined from the parent class desk column.

12579
16:33:54.530 --> 16:33:59.790
If you only want to look at a couple of triggers, using the graphical interface of

12580
16:33:59.790 --> 16:34:04.922
SQL Server Management Studio is a good solution. Just right-click on the trigger

12581
16:34:04.922 --> 16:34:10.246
name and script the trigger definition. A smarter approach is needed, however, when

12582
16:34:10.246 --> 16:34:15.506
you want to view the definitions for lots of triggers in the database. If you turn

12583
16:34:15.506 --> 16:34:20.573
your attention to SQL System Views again, you'll find there are several ways to

12584
16:34:20.573 --> 16:34:23.460
extract trigger definitions. SQL System Views

12585
16:34:23.460 --> 16:34:28.770
are like virtual tables in the database, helping you to reach information that

12586
16:34:28.770 --> 16:34:34.421
cannot be reached otherwise. This example is based on the SQL module's system view.

12587
16:34:34.421 --> 16:34:40.140
It extracts the definition of a trigger based on its ID, but you don't actually need

12588
16:34:40.140 --> 16:34:45.791
to know the ID. You can use the function objectID to get the trigger's ID using its

12589
16:34:45.791 --> 16:34:50.080
name. This example returns the definition for only one trigger.

12590
16:34:50.080 --> 16:34:55.695
But the code can be adjusted to output more than one result. You can also get the

12591
16:34:55.695 --> 16:35:01.310
definition of a trigger using the object definition function. You need to pass in

12592
16:35:01.310 --> 16:35:06.925
the ID of the trigger as the function parameter. To get the ID, use the object ID

12593
16:35:06.925 --> 16:35:12.610
function first, passing it the trigger name. The last option you can use is the sp

12594
16:35:12.610 --> 16:35:16.700
help text procedure, which uses a parameter called obgname.

12595
16:35:16.700 --> 16:35:22.271
You can pass the name of the trigger directly to this parameter to get the trigger

12596
16:35:22.271 --> 16:35:27.773
definition. In practice, the use of the sphelp text procedure is not that common,

12597
16:35:27.773 --> 16:35:33.412
mostly because procedures cannot be used inside of select syntax. Use of the object

12598
16:35:33.412 --> 16:35:38.847
definition function or the sys.sqlmodules view is more widespread. Which one you

12599
16:35:38.847 --> 16:35:41.700
choose is a matter of personal preference.

12600
16:35:41.700 --> 16:35:47.221
We've seen a couple of helpful hints on how to discover and analyze existing

12601
16:35:47.221 --> 16:35:52.959
triggers in a database. But the following tips should also always be taken into

12602
16:35:52.959 --> 16:35:58.843
account. Make sure your database design is well documented so that it's clear for

12603
16:35:58.843 --> 16:36:04.872
anybody working with it. Keep your trigger design simple. Avoid complex logic where

12604
16:36:04.872 --> 16:36:09.230
possible. Avoid overusing triggers when they are not needed.

12605
16:36:09.230 --> 16:36:15.313
In this lesson, we are going to see the most popular use cases for after triggers in

12606
16:36:15.313 --> 16:36:21.396
real-world scenarios. A common use for after triggers is to store historical data in

12607
16:36:21.396 --> 16:36:27.407
other tables. In practice, this usually means having a history of changes performed

12608
16:36:27.407 --> 16:36:33.201
on a table. For example, here we have the customers table containing information

12609
16:36:33.201 --> 16:36:38.270
about existing customers. The customers' details may change over time.

12610
16:36:38.270 --> 16:36:43.877
And the information in the table will need to be updated. It is considered a good

12611
16:36:43.877 --> 16:36:49.484
practice to keep an overview of the changes for the most important tables in your

12612
16:36:49.484 --> 16:36:55.299
database. To start with, the customer's history table holds exactly the same details

12613
16:36:55.299 --> 16:37:00.975
as customer, but it keeps a record of any changes that are made. Suppose the phone

12614
16:37:00.975 --> 16:37:04.990
number for the customer eFruits changes. After the change,

12615
16:37:04.990 --> 16:37:10.459
The tables will hold the following details for this customer. The customer's table

12616
16:37:10.459 --> 16:37:15.795
always shows the current information. The customer's history table shows all the

12617
16:37:15.795 --> 16:37:21.264
changes that have occurred for the customer along with the change date. The result

12618
16:37:21.264 --> 16:37:26.734
you saw previously is obtained using a trigger. The trigger will be fired for both

12619
16:37:26.734 --> 16:37:32.203
insert and update statements. You can include both statements, comma separated, as

12620
16:37:32.203 --> 16:37:33.470
the firing actions.

12621
16:37:33.470 --> 16:37:38.789
This will ensure that when new rows are added to the customers table, they are

12622
16:37:38.789 --> 16:37:44.313
copied to the customers history table. And any changes to the details of existing

12623
16:37:44.313 --> 16:37:50.042
customers will also end up in the history table. The insert statements will be taken

12624
16:37:50.042 --> 16:37:55.702
care of using the inserted special table. Another major use of after triggers is to

12625
16:37:55.702 --> 16:37:58.430
audit changes occurring in the database.

12626
16:37:58.430 --> 16:38:03.889
Auditing means tracking any changes that occur within the defined scope. In this

12627
16:38:03.889 --> 16:38:09.211
example, the scope of the audit is comprised of very important tables from the

12628
16:38:09.211 --> 16:38:14.738
database. A trigger will be created on the orders table. It will fire for any DML

12629
16:38:14.738 --> 16:38:20.470
statement. Inside the trigger, we will declare two boolean variables that will check

12630
16:38:20.470 --> 16:38:25.929
the special tables inserted and deleted. When one of the special tables contains

12631
16:38:25.929 --> 16:38:26.270
data,

12632
16:38:26.270 --> 16:38:32.001
The associated variable will be set to true. The combination of values will tell us

12633
16:38:32.001 --> 16:38:37.662
if the operation is an insert, update, or delete. A table called tables audit will

12634
16:38:37.662 --> 16:38:43.255
be used to track the changes. The trigger will insert into that table information

12635
16:38:43.255 --> 16:38:48.986
about the rows being modified, the user making the change, and the date and time of

12636
16:38:48.986 --> 16:38:54.233
the change. A simple and effective use case of triggers is to have them send

12637
16:38:54.233 --> 16:38:55.200
notifications.

12638
16:38:55.200 --> 16:39:01.018
Most of the notifications will be about events happening in the database and will be

12639
16:39:01.018 --> 16:39:06.628
sent to interested users. For example, the sales department must be notified when

12640
16:39:06.628 --> 16:39:12.030
new orders are placed. A trigger attached to the orders table will execute the

12641
16:39:12.030 --> 16:39:17.779
procedure that sends an email when insert statements are executed. Now it's time to

12642
16:39:17.779 --> 16:39:22.350
practice. If after triggers are used mostly for auditing and logs,

12643
16:39:22.350 --> 16:39:27.629
The story is different with instead of triggers. We are going to learn the details

12644
16:39:27.629 --> 16:39:32.587
in this lesson. The use cases for these triggers are suggested by their name,

12645
16:39:32.587 --> 16:39:37.673
instead of. These kinds of triggers focus on preventing certain operations from

12646
16:39:37.673 --> 16:39:43.017
happening in your database. If you can control the permitted statements inside your

12647
16:39:43.017 --> 16:39:48.104
database and set some rules, you can enforce data integrity through triggers as

12648
16:39:48.104 --> 16:39:49.520
well. In this example,

12649
16:39:49.520 --> 16:39:54.876
updates to the products table are not permitted for regular database users. When a

12650
16:39:54.876 --> 16:40:00.167
non-administrator runs an update statement, the trigger will raise an error using

12651
16:40:00.167 --> 16:40:05.523
the raiseError function. An error message will be included to inform the user that

12652
16:40:05.523 --> 16:40:10.814
they aren't allowed to make any changes to this table. The restriction is applied

12653
16:40:10.814 --> 16:40:16.104
because the table contains information about stock. An incorrect update statement

12654
16:40:16.104 --> 16:40:17.280
could wreak havoc,

12655
16:40:17.280 --> 16:40:22.573
by considering the real stock numbers. In the second example, the trigger doesn't

12656
16:40:22.573 --> 16:40:28.062
just raise an error message to prevent the deletion of a customer. An alert destined

12657
16:40:28.062 --> 16:40:33.486
for the database administrator is also sent. When a user tries to remove a customer

12658
16:40:33.486 --> 16:40:38.845
from the customers table, an email will be sent to the database administrator. The

12659
16:40:38.845 --> 16:40:42.700
removal action is, of course, denied with an error message.

12660
16:40:42.700 --> 16:40:48.288
The body of the message is stored in the email body text variable and will include

12661
16:40:48.288 --> 16:40:53.944
the name of the user who attempted to make the change. After raising the error, the

12662
16:40:53.944 --> 16:40:59.328
trigger executes the send notification procedure to send the email alert to the

12663
16:40:59.328 --> 16:41:04.643
database administrator. Instead of triggers should not always be considered as

12664
16:41:04.643 --> 16:41:07.710
objects that deny operations on the database.

12665
16:41:07.710 --> 16:41:13.076
They can be used to decide whether or not some operations should succeed. In this

12666
16:41:13.076 --> 16:41:18.640
example, we create a new trigger on the orders table. It makes no sense for an order

12667
16:41:18.640 --> 16:41:24.005
to be placed if there is insufficient stock of the product. An instead of trigger

12668
16:41:24.005 --> 16:41:29.371
can check whether there is sufficient stock for an order through an if statement.

12669
16:41:29.371 --> 16:41:34.670
The process used to decide what the trigger will do is called conditional logic.

12670
16:41:34.670 --> 16:41:39.987
It gets this name because operations will be performed or not based on logical

12671
16:41:39.987 --> 16:41:45.578
conditions like the IF statement. In this example, the IF statement checks for the

12672
16:41:45.578 --> 16:41:51.168
condition where the order quantity is higher than the existing stock level. If the

12673
16:41:51.168 --> 16:41:56.553
condition is true, an error will be raised and the order will be denied. If the

12674
16:41:56.553 --> 16:42:00.780
condition is false, indicating that there is sufficient stock,

12675
16:42:00.780 --> 16:42:06.298
The initial insert operation can only be executed if stated explicitly in the

12676
16:42:06.298 --> 16:42:11.959
trigger code. Here's what the conditional logic used in the trigger looks like.

12677
16:42:11.959 --> 16:42:17.691
First, the insert operation fires the trigger. The trigger then verifies whether

12678
16:42:17.691 --> 16:42:23.496
there is sufficient stock of the product included in the insert operation. If the

12679
16:42:23.496 --> 16:42:29.372
condition is true, the order is placed by adding a new row in the orders table. If

12680
16:42:29.372 --> 16:42:30.160
it's false,

12681
16:42:30.160 --> 16:42:35.513
The trigger throws an error and the insert operation is dropped. Ready to practice

12682
16:42:35.513 --> 16:42:40.867
with some triggers? Now it's time to look at some use cases for DDL triggers. This

12683
16:42:40.867 --> 16:42:46.090
lesson will only give you an overview of DDL triggers at the database and server

12684
16:42:46.090 --> 16:42:51.182
level. The topic is more complex than what you will be learning here, but this

12685
16:42:51.182 --> 16:42:56.536
lesson will provide you with the fundamentals to get you started. DDL triggers can

12686
16:42:56.536 --> 16:42:58.690
be created at the database level,

12687
16:42:58.690 --> 16:43:04.823
Where the trigger is created will influence the types of statements able to fire it.

12688
16:43:04.823 --> 16:43:10.517
For example, a trigger created at the database level can respond to statements

12689
16:43:10.517 --> 16:43:16.577
related to tables, view interactions and index management, as well as more specific

12690
16:43:16.577 --> 16:43:22.637
statements to do with permissions management or statistics. At the server level, we

12691
16:43:22.637 --> 16:43:25.630
can use triggers for database management,

12692
16:43:25.630 --> 16:43:30.991
and to control server permissions and the use of credentials. Don't worry about

12693
16:43:30.991 --> 16:43:36.352
remembering exactly which DDL statements can be used to fire database or server

12694
16:43:36.352 --> 16:43:41.917
level triggers. You can always check the online documentation from Microsoft for a

12695
16:43:41.917 --> 16:43:47.346
full list of DDL events. So what exactly can we do with database level triggers?

12696
16:43:47.346 --> 16:43:52.911
Well, we can keep a trace of any activity happening at the database level. This is

12697
16:43:52.911 --> 16:43:54.540
called a database audit.

12698
16:43:54.540 --> 16:43:59.663
We are going to use a group event to fire our database audit trigger,

12699
16:43:59.663 --> 16:44:05.528
DDLTableViewEvents. This DML event includes any operations dealing with tables,

12700
16:44:05.528 --> 16:44:11.690
views, indexes, or statistics. The advantage of using a group event is that you can

12701
16:44:11.690 --> 16:44:17.852
specify a single event to cover all the cases that should fire the trigger. In this

12702
16:44:17.852 --> 16:44:20.450
case, more than a dozen statements.

12703
16:44:20.450 --> 16:44:26.398
We are going to insert details about the operations performed in the database into

12704
16:44:26.398 --> 16:44:32.418
the database audit table. The details of the operations will be extracted using the

12705
16:44:32.418 --> 16:44:38.148
eventData function. This function returns information about an operation in XML

12706
16:44:38.148 --> 16:44:44.023
format. In order to extract specific information in clear text, we call the value

12707
16:44:44.023 --> 16:44:49.390
function. For example, the first call of the eventData and value functions

12708
16:44:49.390 --> 16:44:54.940
We'll get the event type from the XML and convert it to the SQL and varchart data

12709
16:44:54.940 --> 16:45:00.354
type. The same logic is applied for the other columns. Here's what the database

12710
16:45:00.354 --> 16:45:06.110
audit table looks like after using the trigger. All the user actions are kept in the

12711
16:45:06.110 --> 16:45:11.455
table. Any breaking changes can then be traced back to the responsible person.

12712
16:45:11.455 --> 16:45:15.840
Remember, it is not possible to use instead of for DDL triggers.

12713
16:45:15.840 --> 16:45:21.682
But that doesn't mean you cannot prevent operations from happening when using DDL

12714
16:45:21.682 --> 16:45:27.380
triggers. DDL triggers are used in the real world to prevent database or server

12715
16:45:27.380 --> 16:45:33.367
changes that could lead to data loss. The trigger in this example prevents database

12716
16:45:33.367 --> 16:45:39.354
deletion. As you can see, it uses the rollback statement to prevent drop operations

12717
16:45:39.354 --> 16:45:42.960
on databases. The statement that fired the trigger

12718
16:45:42.960 --> 16:45:48.248
was prevented without the use of instead of. The best way to understand use cases

12719
16:45:48.248 --> 16:45:53.732
for DDL triggers is by practicing. You've learned a lot about creating various types

12720
16:45:53.732 --> 16:45:59.021
of triggers and had some practice. But what happens when you don't want a trigger

12721
16:45:59.021 --> 16:46:04.244
anymore? Or when you decide to change the trigger actions? You'll learn how it's

12722
16:46:04.244 --> 16:46:09.401
done in this lesson. It's easy to delete a trigger in SQL Server. The syntax is

12723
16:46:09.401 --> 16:46:11.360
straightforward. Drop trigger.

12724
16:46:11.360 --> 16:46:16.503
followed by the trigger name. This syntax is applicable when you are removing

12725
16:46:16.503 --> 16:46:21.713
triggers attached to tables or views. In this example, the trigger prevent new

12726
16:46:21.713 --> 16:46:27.257
discounts will be deleted. If you want to remove a database level trigger, you have

12727
16:46:27.257 --> 16:46:32.868
to make that explicit in the syntax. You will use drop trigger with the trigger name

12728
16:46:32.868 --> 16:46:38.212
as you did before, but this time the syntax will be expanded with the onDatabase

12729
16:46:38.212 --> 16:46:38.880
statement.

12730
16:46:38.880 --> 16:46:44.275
This will inform SQL server to look at the database level for the trigger to be

12731
16:46:44.275 --> 16:46:49.669
deleted. In the second example, the trigger prevent views modifications will be

12732
16:46:49.669 --> 16:46:55.132
deleted at the database level. A slightly different syntax should be used if you

12733
16:46:55.132 --> 16:47:00.799
want to delete triggers at the server level. As before, you'll use drop trigger and

12734
16:47:00.799 --> 16:47:06.399
the trigger name, but then you'll instruct SQL server to search for the trigger on

12735
16:47:06.399 --> 16:47:07.150
all server.

12736
16:47:07.150 --> 16:47:13.142
In this case, the trigger disallow linked servers will be deleted from the server. A

12737
16:47:13.142 --> 16:47:18.921
deleted trigger can never be used again unless you recreate the trigger. Deleting

12738
16:47:18.921 --> 16:47:24.699
triggers is okay when you no longer need them, but what if you just want to avoid

12739
16:47:24.699 --> 16:47:30.263
them for a brief period? Luckily, SQL Server offers the possibility to disable

12740
16:47:30.263 --> 16:47:34.900
triggers. A disabled trigger still exists as a SQL Server object,

12741
16:47:34.900 --> 16:47:40.586
But as long as it's disabled, it will not be fired. The syntax to disable a trigger

12742
16:47:40.586 --> 16:47:45.930
is also straightforward. But note that when disabling, you need to specify the

12743
16:47:45.930 --> 16:47:51.342
object the trigger is attached to, even if it is a table. To switch off a table

12744
16:47:51.342 --> 16:47:57.028
level trigger, use disable trigger and the trigger name and specify the name of the

12745
16:47:57.028 --> 16:48:02.646
table. For example, own discounts. Replace that with the ownDatabase statement for

12746
16:48:02.646 --> 16:48:04.290
database level triggers.

12747
16:48:04.290 --> 16:48:10.145
or the onAllServer statement for triggers created at the server level. When you want

12748
16:48:10.145 --> 16:48:15.372
a disabled trigger to start working again, you simply re-enable it. This is

12749
16:48:15.372 --> 16:48:21.087
accomplished by using the enabled trigger syntax, followed by the trigger name and

12750
16:48:21.087 --> 16:48:26.524
the scope of the trigger. Specify the name of the table or view the trigger is

12751
16:48:26.524 --> 16:48:29.730
attached to, or use onDatabase or onAllServer.

12752
16:48:29.730 --> 16:48:34.946
Now all the triggers we disabled previously will be working again. There will be

12753
16:48:34.946 --> 16:48:40.162
situations where you need to modify a trigger. It might be malfunctioning or you

12754
16:48:40.162 --> 16:48:45.248
might want to add new functionality. Whatever the reason, at some point you're

12755
16:48:45.248 --> 16:48:50.594
likely to end up having to change triggers, either during development or later. In

12756
16:48:50.594 --> 16:48:55.745
this example, a simple trigger is created on the discounts table to prevent any

12757
16:48:55.745 --> 16:48:59.070
deletions. You run the code and create the trigger.

12758
16:48:59.070 --> 16:49:04.319
But you notice you forgot to include the word remove between to and data in the

12759
16:49:04.319 --> 16:49:09.768
print statement. To fix the error, simply drop the existing trigger, then fix your

12760
16:49:09.768 --> 16:49:15.150
code and run it again. This can become a hassle in the development phase when you

12761
16:49:15.150 --> 16:49:20.732
want to test the trigger and make changes immediately. To avoid this create and drop

12762
16:49:20.732 --> 16:49:25.848
flow, you can use the ALTER statement to modify the definition of an existing

12763
16:49:25.848 --> 16:49:26.380
trigger.

12764
16:49:26.380 --> 16:49:31.683
Simply replace the create keyword with alter while keeping everything else the same,

12765
16:49:31.683 --> 16:49:36.859
except for the changes you wish to make, of course. This example shows how you can

12766
16:49:36.859 --> 16:49:42.098
add the word remove to the message the trigger prints using alter. Now it's time to

12767
16:49:42.098 --> 16:49:47.275
practice deleting and alter. As you saw in previous lessons, you can end up having

12768
16:49:47.275 --> 16:49:52.262
lots of triggers in your database. One of the main challenges is the ability to

12769
16:49:52.262 --> 16:49:53.840
manage all these triggers

12770
16:49:53.840 --> 16:49:59.159
and have a good overview. You will learn how it's done in this lesson. All the

12771
16:49:59.159 --> 16:50:04.477
necessary information about existing triggers can be extracted from SQL Server

12772
16:50:04.477 --> 16:50:10.205
system tables or views. These objects are used to store information about everything

12773
16:50:10.205 --> 16:50:15.114
that is happening in SQL Server. We will first turn our attention to the

12774
16:50:15.114 --> 16:50:20.364
sys.triggers view and explore the information it can provide us. sys.triggers

12775
16:50:20.364 --> 16:50:21.660
contains 13 columns

12776
16:50:21.660 --> 16:50:26.891
But we will only look at the most important. The name column contains the trigger

12777
16:50:26.891 --> 16:50:31.992
name, given when the trigger is created. The object ID column contains a unique

12778
16:50:31.992 --> 16:50:37.288
identifier for the trigger object. There are a few double columns where one column

12779
16:50:37.288 --> 16:50:42.260
contains an integer code and the other column holds the text explanation. For

12780
16:50:42.260 --> 16:50:47.620
example, the parent class column contains an integer representing the trigger type.

12781
16:50:47.620 --> 16:50:53.163
If it's a table trigger, the value will be 1, and for a database trigger, the value

12782
16:50:53.163 --> 16:50:58.640
will be 0. The column parent class desk gives a textual description of the trigger

12783
16:50:58.640 --> 16:51:03.716
type. The parent ID column will tell you the ID of the object the trigger is

12784
16:51:03.716 --> 16:51:08.992
attached to. The create date column stores the trigger creation date, while the

12785
16:51:08.992 --> 16:51:14.469
modify date column tells you when the trigger was last modified. If the trigger is

12786
16:51:14.469 --> 16:51:15.070
disabled,

12787
16:51:15.070 --> 16:51:21.210
The isDisabled column will have the value 1, otherwise it will be 0. Similarly, the

12788
16:51:21.210 --> 16:51:27.054
value in the isInsteadOfTrigger column will be 1 for instead of and 0 for after

12789
16:51:27.054 --> 16:51:32.602
triggers. The sys.server triggers view holds information about server level

12790
16:51:32.602 --> 16:51:38.520
triggers. It has exactly the same structure as sys.triggers and the columns hold

12791
16:51:38.520 --> 16:51:38.765
similar information.

12792
16:51:38.765 --> 16:51:44.224
What about identifying the events that will fire a trigger? This information is

12793
16:51:44.224 --> 16:51:49.889
stored in sys.trigger events. Among the most useful columns in this view is object

12794
16:51:49.889 --> 16:51:55.624
ID which identifies the trigger. The columns type and type desk will tell you which

12795
16:51:55.624 --> 16:51:57.490
events will fire a trigger.

12796
16:51:57.490 --> 16:52:03.157
The columns EventGroupType and EventGroupTypeDesk will point you to any group events

12797
16:52:03.157 --> 16:52:08.825
that will fire the trigger. Trigger group events are special events that are used to

12798
16:52:08.825 --> 16:52:14.425
fire a trigger. They can contain multiple regular events. The advantage is that you

12799
16:52:14.425 --> 16:52:19.148
do not need to specify the events individually. For example, the group

12800
16:52:19.148 --> 16:52:24.410
TDLTableViewEvents contains more than a dozen events related to table and view

12801
16:52:24.410 --> 16:52:25.220
interaction.

12802
16:52:25.220 --> 16:52:31.528
A similar view can be used to extract information about the server level triggers.

12803
16:52:31.528 --> 16:52:37.913
The view is called sys.ServerTriggerEvents. As mentioned before, you do not need to

12804
16:52:37.913 --> 16:52:44.375
memorize all the existing events that can be used to fire triggers. The full list is

12805
16:52:44.375 --> 16:52:50.837
accessible in the sys.TriggerEventTypes view, which shows the event types as numbers

12806
16:52:50.837 --> 16:52:51.760
and as text.

12807
16:52:51.760 --> 16:52:58.175
It will also show the parent type when the event is part of a larger group. In this

12808
16:52:58.175 --> 16:53:04.666
example, create table, alter table and drop table have the value 10018 in the parent

12809
16:53:04.666 --> 16:53:11.081
type column. In the last row shown here, you can see that this type is actually the

12810
16:53:11.081 --> 16:53:17.573
DDL table events group. In practice, all of this information has to be packed into a

12811
16:53:17.573 --> 16:53:18.500
useful form.

12812
16:53:18.500 --> 16:53:23.687
For example, if you want to see a list of triggers along with their firing events

12813
16:53:23.687 --> 16:53:28.937
and the objects they're attached to, you need to combine information from multiple

12814
16:53:28.937 --> 16:53:33.996
views. You can get the trigger name and type from sys.triggers. If you join the

12815
16:53:33.996 --> 16:53:39.310
output with sys.trigger events based on the object ID, you can determine the firing

12816
16:53:39.310 --> 16:53:44.561
events for each trigger. If you further join the parent ID of the trigger with the

12817
16:53:44.561 --> 16:53:46.290
object ID from sys.objects,

12818
16:53:46.290 --> 16:53:51.428
You can extract the name and type of the object the trigger is attached to. The

12819
16:53:51.428 --> 16:53:56.500
second join is chosen to be a left join because database level triggers do not

12820
16:53:56.500 --> 16:54:01.898
appear as attached to an object. A left join will still keep the results in case of

12821
16:54:01.898 --> 16:54:07.101
non-matching join parameters. If we'd used an inner join, the results would have

12822
16:54:07.101 --> 16:54:12.173
been filtered only for matching rows and the database triggers would have been

12823
16:54:12.173 --> 16:54:15.620
filtered out from the list. This is the query result.

12824
16:54:15.620 --> 16:54:20.742
There are no values for attached to and object type for database level triggers,

12825
16:54:20.742 --> 16:54:25.672
because they are not attached to any kind of object. They simply exist on the

12826
16:54:25.672 --> 16:54:30.474
database. This script is just one of many you can use in the real world. An

12827
16:54:30.474 --> 16:54:35.404
essential thing to remember is that the views seen in this lesson are usually

12828
16:54:35.404 --> 16:54:40.269
combined to get more detailed results, not used in isolation. Let's practice

12829
16:54:40.269 --> 16:54:41.870
tricking. In this lesson,

12830
16:54:41.870 --> 16:54:47.168
You will learn how to troubleshoot and optimize triggers. You will learn how to keep

12831
16:54:47.168 --> 16:54:51.961
a history of trigger runs and how to search for triggers causing issues. One

12832
16:54:51.961 --> 16:54:56.754
important factor when troubleshooting triggers is to have a history of their

12833
16:54:56.754 --> 16:55:01.674
execution. This allows you to associate the timing of trigger runs with issues

12834
16:55:01.674 --> 16:55:06.719
caused by triggers. SQL Server provides information on the execution of triggers

12835
16:55:06.719 --> 16:55:08.990
that are currently stored in memory.

12836
16:55:08.990 --> 16:55:14.232
The information can be seen in sys.dmxec trigger stats. Unfortunately, when a

12837
16:55:14.232 --> 16:55:19.745
trigger is removed from the memory, the information about that trigger is removed

12838
16:55:19.745 --> 16:55:25.055
from the view as well. This leaves you with a major problem regarding tracking

12839
16:55:25.055 --> 16:55:30.569
trigger execution history. There is no other option to see the history of trigger

12840
16:55:30.569 --> 16:55:33.360
runs unless you create a custom solution.

12841
16:55:33.360 --> 16:55:38.687
For example, suppose we want to create a permanent record of executions of the

12842
16:55:38.687 --> 16:55:44.288
trigger prevent orders update, designed to prevent any updates to the order table.

12843
16:55:44.288 --> 16:55:49.751
We can use the trigger audit table to store information on trigger execution. To

12844
16:55:49.751 --> 16:55:54.874
enable this, we will update the trigger definition to specify that, when it

12845
16:55:54.874 --> 16:56:00.474
executes, the trigger name and the current date and time will be inserted into the

12846
16:56:00.474 --> 16:56:01.840
trigger audit table.

12847
16:56:01.840 --> 16:56:07.588
will enhance the trigger definition with the new code using the ALTER statement as

12848
16:56:07.588 --> 16:56:13.195
shown here. An attempt to update the quantity for the order with number 784 will

12849
16:56:13.195 --> 16:56:18.943
result in the PREVENT ORDERS UPDATE trigger being fired. The trigger will throw an

12850
16:56:18.943 --> 16:56:24.620
error message saying that updates are not permitted on the orders table. However,

12851
16:56:24.620 --> 16:56:29.874
With our change to the trigger definition in place, it will also insert information

12852
16:56:29.874 --> 16:56:35.065
about the trigger execution into the trigger audit table. If we check the contents

12853
16:56:35.065 --> 16:56:40.382
of that table, we will see the information about this trigger execution. We now have

12854
16:56:40.382 --> 16:56:44.940
a permanent record that we can use to track the history of trigger runs.

12855
16:56:44.940 --> 16:56:50.055
A solution like this will allow you to associate the timings of trigger executions

12856
16:56:50.055 --> 16:56:55.232
with issues potentially caused by the trigger in future investigations. If the date

12857
16:56:55.232 --> 16:56:59.910
and time of the issue are similar to the date and time when the trigger was

12858
16:56:59.910 --> 16:57:04.962
executed, you can assume the trigger might be causing the problem and investigate

12859
16:57:04.962 --> 16:57:10.077
further. Sometimes you may have issues with certain tables and suspect triggers to

12860
16:57:10.077 --> 16:57:11.200
be the root cause.

12861
16:57:11.200 --> 16:57:16.508
But how can you investigate them if no documentation is available? In this example,

12862
16:57:16.508 --> 16:57:21.817
we want to find out what triggers are attached to the products table. Starting from

12863
16:57:21.817 --> 16:57:27.125
the table name, we can query the sys.objects view to find the table ID of products.

12864
16:57:27.125 --> 16:57:32.497
We can then use this ID to determine what triggers are attached to the table and get

12865
16:57:32.497 --> 16:57:37.549
some information about them. We enhance the script further by joining the first

12866
16:57:37.549 --> 16:57:39.660
query with the sys.triggers view.

12867
16:57:39.660 --> 16:57:45.011
This will help us to find the triggers attached to the products table. The join is

12868
16:57:45.011 --> 16:57:50.296
made by matching the parent ID column of the trigger with the object ID column of

12869
16:57:50.296 --> 16:57:55.777
the products table. We add the following information to our script. The trigger name

12870
16:57:55.777 --> 16:58:01.258
from the name column, whether it's disabled, the isDisabled column, and whether it's

12871
16:58:01.258 --> 16:58:06.674
an instead of trigger, the isInsteadOfTrigger column. Columns with the O prefix are

12872
16:58:06.674 --> 16:58:08.240
coming from sys.objects,

12873
16:58:08.240 --> 16:58:13.157
And columns with the t prefix are coming from sys.triggers. The end result is

12874
16:58:13.157 --> 16:58:18.138
starting to look better, as we now have some insights on the existing triggers

12875
16:58:18.138 --> 16:58:23.055
attached to the products table. Now, we'll add one more important column with

12876
16:58:23.055 --> 16:58:28.036
another inner join on sys.trigger events. The addition of the type this column

12877
16:58:28.036 --> 16:58:33.273
brings us details on the events capable of firing the triggers. The last important

12878
16:58:33.273 --> 16:58:36.210
information to add is the trigger definitions.

12879
16:58:36.210 --> 16:58:41.487
To troubleshoot a trigger's results or behavior, you need to know what it's intended

12880
16:58:41.487 --> 16:58:46.135
to do. To view the trigger definition code, we will make use of the object

12881
16:58:46.135 --> 16:58:51.035
definition function. This function will return the definition for an object ID

12882
16:58:51.035 --> 16:58:56.312
passed as an argument. In this particular example, it will be the ID of the trigger.

12883
16:58:56.312 --> 16:59:01.589
The trigger definitions can now be inspected and modified if needed. There was a lot

12884
16:59:01.589 --> 16:59:04.290
of interesting stuff in this lesson, right?

12885
16:59:04.290 --> 16:59:10.020
This is the end of our datacam course on SQL Server triggers. It has been a

12886
16:59:10.020 --> 16:59:16.132
fascinating journey and I'm confident you learned a lot of things. We started by

12887
16:59:16.132 --> 16:59:22.015
learning what a trigger is, then we learned how DML triggers are used and the

12888
16:59:22.015 --> 16:59:28.279
trigger alternatives that exist in the real world. We then made a deep analysis of

12889
16:59:28.279 --> 16:59:33.780
after triggers instead of triggers and we saw the difference between DML

12890
16:59:33.780 --> 16:59:39.346
DDL and logon triggers. Next, we learn that triggers also have their limitations.

12891
16:59:39.346 --> 16:59:45.049
These limitations will not occur when triggers are used correctly. And we learn how

12892
16:59:45.049 --> 16:59:50.683
to use them correctly. Finally, we analyze the need of managing triggers. Managing

12893
16:59:50.683 --> 16:59:55.768
implies removing unused triggers and optimize existing ones after a proper

12894
16:59:55.768 --> 17:00:01.265
investigation. I hope this course was useful to you. And I hope all the examples

12895
17:00:01.265 --> 17:00:03.120
provided within this course

12896
17:00:03.120 --> 17:00:07.671
will help you achieve your goals in real life. To master trigger creation and usage

12897
17:00:07.671 --> 17:00:12.276
takes time, but you should practice working with triggers as often as you can. Thank

12898
17:00:12.276 --> 17:00:16.881
you for joining us. Hi, welcome. My name's Dean Smith, and I'm the founder of Atamai

12899
17:00:16.881 --> 17:00:21.377
Analytics, and I'll be your instructor for this course. When writing an SQL query,

12900
17:00:21.377 --> 17:00:25.983
there are often many ways to achieve the same results. In this course, we'll look at

12901
17:00:25.983 --> 17:00:30.588
good coding practices and different ways we can improve query performance to achieve

12902
17:00:30.588 --> 17:00:31.520
the same outcome.

12903
17:00:31.520 --> 17:00:36.571
We'll be using three simple databases. The first is the Earthquakes database which

12904
17:00:36.571 --> 17:00:41.561
consists of three tables. Earthquakes lists all recorded earthquakes between 1900

12905
17:00:41.561 --> 17:00:46.120
and 2018 with a magnitude of seven or more. Cities lists world cities with

12906
17:00:46.120 --> 17:00:51.110
associated data and statistics and nations which contains all the world's nations

12907
17:00:51.110 --> 17:00:55.853
and related data and statistics. Earthquakes table is related to cities via a

12908
17:00:55.853 --> 17:01:00.720
country code and city name and cities is related to nations via a country code.

12909
17:01:00.720 --> 17:01:06.541
The second is the NBA season 2017-2018 database, which consists of three related

12910
17:01:06.541 --> 17:01:11.999
tables, teams, players, and player stats. Each table contains data from the

12911
17:01:11.999 --> 17:01:18.111
2017-2018 season of the US National Basketball Association League, or NBA for short.

12912
17:01:18.111 --> 17:01:24.223
And finally the customers orders database, which consists of two related tables. The

12913
17:01:24.223 --> 17:01:28.880
customers table contains sales customer IDs and associated data,

12914
17:01:28.880 --> 17:01:33.696
And the orders table contains sales orders and associated data for each customer.

12915
17:01:33.696 --> 17:01:38.453
This database is a subset of the freely available Northwind sample database from

12916
17:01:38.453 --> 17:01:43.329
Microsoft. In this first lesson, we'll apply formatting and commenting to make the

12917
17:01:43.329 --> 17:01:48.085
code easier to read and understand. Unlike many other programming languages, SQL

12918
17:01:48.085 --> 17:01:53.020
syntax is case-insensitive, which means it doesn't matter if the case of the syntax

12919
17:01:53.020 --> 17:01:56.350
is upper, lower or a mixture, it'll still work the same.

12920
17:01:56.350 --> 17:02:01.199
Check this query out. Is it easy to read? When writing queries, it's good practice

12921
17:02:01.199 --> 17:02:06.167
to stick to a consistent format. Consistent formatting makes queries easier to read,

12922
17:02:06.167 --> 17:02:11.016
easier to understand, and easier to debug. The main thing is to be consistent with

12923
17:02:11.016 --> 17:02:15.925
our formatting across queries. Here are some suggestions, all of which we'll use in

12924
17:02:15.925 --> 17:02:20.597
this course. Use uppercase for all SQL syntax. Create a new line for each major

12925
17:02:20.597 --> 17:02:23.790
processing syntax, for example, select from and where.

12926
17:02:23.790 --> 17:02:28.841
Apply an indent to subqueries, on statements, and or conditions, and to avoid long

12927
17:02:28.841 --> 17:02:33.645
single lines of code, for example many column names. Complete the query with a

12928
17:02:33.645 --> 17:02:38.758
semicolon, and alias where required using as. Here's the same query from two slides

12929
17:02:38.758 --> 17:02:43.809
back. This query looks much better. It's easier to read and understand. So what is

12930
17:02:43.809 --> 17:02:48.798
the purpose of this query? Adding comments is an excellent way to convey what the

12931
17:02:48.798 --> 17:02:52.740
query is about, or information about certain parts of the query.

12932
17:02:52.740 --> 17:02:57.970
Code or text that is within a comment or commented out will not be executed when the

12933
17:02:57.970 --> 17:03:02.951
query is run. To comment a block or multiple lines enclose the code or text in a

12934
17:03:02.951 --> 17:03:08.119
forward slash plus star and star plus forward slash. Here a block comment above the

12935
17:03:08.119 --> 17:03:13.349
query is used to describe what this query should return. To comment on a single line

12936
17:03:13.349 --> 17:03:18.330
instead of an entire block place two dashes in front of the code or text. Common

12937
17:03:18.330 --> 17:03:20.820
uses for commenting single lines include

12938
17:03:20.820 --> 17:03:25.704
Conveying information about a particular line of code. Marking a section break or

12939
17:03:25.704 --> 17:03:30.648
comment in a query. In commenting out a line of code, that will not be executed at

12940
17:03:30.648 --> 17:03:35.411
runtime, but may be reused later on, for example, when debugging a query. Let's

12941
17:03:35.411 --> 17:03:40.415
practice some good tips. In this lesson, we'll discuss how to use aliasing to avoid

12942
17:03:40.415 --> 17:03:44.997
some kinds of errors and how it can make code easier to read and understand.

12943
17:03:44.997 --> 17:03:49.700
Aliasing refers to providing a short name, code, or simple identity to tables,

12944
17:03:49.700 --> 17:03:54.747
Columns and subqueries in a query. Aliases are temporary and only applied during the

12945
17:03:54.747 --> 17:03:59.674
running of the query. They do not alter the table or column names in the database.

12946
17:03:59.674 --> 17:04:04.541
They can be handy for making code in a query easier to read and in many cases are

12947
17:04:04.541 --> 17:04:09.528
required, for example, a new column or subquery that will be reused in another part

12948
17:04:09.528 --> 17:04:14.515
of the query. Let's take a look at some reasons why we need to use aliasing. We use

12949
17:04:14.515 --> 17:04:18.300
aliasing to avoid repetitive use of long table or column names.

12950
17:04:18.300 --> 17:04:23.544
Easily identify joined tables and associated columns, identify new columns, identify

12951
17:04:23.544 --> 17:04:28.787
subqueries, avoid ambiguity when columns from joined tables share the same name, and

12952
17:04:28.787 --> 17:04:33.968
rename columns. In this example, we want to know the 2017 population of each nation

12953
17:04:33.968 --> 17:04:38.588
capital. We can get this by joining the nations and cities tables from the

12954
17:04:38.588 --> 17:04:43.457
earthquakes database. When run, it returns an error because both tables have a

12955
17:04:43.457 --> 17:04:44.830
column named POP-2017.

12956
17:04:44.830 --> 17:04:49.338
These refer to the 2017 census populations. In the nations table, it's the

12957
17:04:49.338 --> 17:04:54.151
population of each nation, and in the cities table, it's the population of each

12958
17:04:54.151 --> 17:04:58.842
city. This query needs to know which is which. In this case, we want the city

12959
17:04:58.842 --> 17:05:03.838
population. Aliasing tables can correct this. Nations is N, cities is C. Now if we

12960
17:05:03.838 --> 17:05:08.773
look at the select statement, we see aliases used to indicate the source table of

12961
17:05:08.773 --> 17:05:13.890
each selected column with the column name prefixed with the alias of the table name.

12962
17:05:13.890 --> 17:05:19.430
The cities table has a C alias, and therefore, 2017 population column is identified

12963
17:05:19.430 --> 17:05:24.704
as C.pop2017. Note there are no rules to what aliases should be, just make sure

12964
17:05:24.704 --> 17:05:30.311
they're intuitive and consistent. To make the output a little easier to read, we can

12965
17:05:30.311 --> 17:05:35.852
choose to rename columns by aliasing the source columns in the select statement. In

12966
17:05:35.852 --> 17:05:40.991
this example, we renamed country name to country, code 2 to country code, and

12967
17:05:40.991 --> 17:05:42.460
pop2017 to population.

12968
17:05:42.460 --> 17:05:46.923
Any time a new column is created it should be given an alias. By doing this

12969
17:05:46.923 --> 17:05:51.743
indicates what information is in the column and is also required if the column is

12970
17:05:51.743 --> 17:05:56.445
referenced or used elsewhere in the query. In this example we're creating a new

12971
17:05:56.445 --> 17:06:01.384
column which is the maximum magnitude earthquake by country and nearest population.

12972
17:06:01.384 --> 17:06:06.204
We alias the new column as max magnitude. When using subqueries we need to use an

12973
17:06:06.204 --> 17:06:09.120
alias to refer to it in other parts of the query.

12974
17:06:09.120 --> 17:06:13.453
In this example, we want a query that returns the biggest earthquake magnitude,

12975
17:06:13.453 --> 17:06:17.677
where country capital is the nearest population to the epicenter. We join the

12976
17:06:17.677 --> 17:06:22.285
nations table, aliased as N, to a subquery, aliased as E. Let's now practice. If SQL

12977
17:06:22.285 --> 17:06:26.618
syntax is not in a specific order in the query, running the query will raise an

12978
17:06:26.618 --> 17:06:30.787
error. The order of the syntax as written in the query is different from the

12979
17:06:30.787 --> 17:06:35.395
processing order in the database. In this lesson, we'll examine the effects of query

12980
17:06:35.395 --> 17:06:37.150
processing order in the database

12981
17:06:37.150 --> 17:06:41.900
versus the order of SQL syntax in a query. Here's a simple query that returns all

12982
17:06:41.900 --> 17:06:46.767
earthquakes with a magnitude of 9 or more. Syntax in this query, from start to end,

12983
17:06:46.767 --> 17:06:51.341
begins with select, identifying the columns to be returned, country, place and

12984
17:06:51.341 --> 17:06:55.974
magnitude. We identify the name of the data source next with from, which is the

12985
17:06:55.974 --> 17:07:00.607
earthquakes table. We apply a filtering condition on the earthquakes table with

12986
17:07:00.607 --> 17:07:05.474
where, in this case magnitude greater than or equal to 9. The query ends with order

12987
17:07:05.474 --> 17:07:05.650
by,

12988
17:07:05.650 --> 17:07:10.034
which orders the final results. In the example, this is the largest magnitude

12989
17:07:10.034 --> 17:07:14.419
descending. In contrast, the processing order of the query in the database is

12990
17:07:14.419 --> 17:07:19.145
different. The data source is processed first with FROM. The filtering condition is

12991
17:07:19.145 --> 17:07:23.928
applied next by processing WHERE. Only when the data has been extracted and filtered

12992
17:07:23.928 --> 17:07:28.711
with FROM and WHERE is SELECT processed, telling the database what columns to return

12993
17:07:28.711 --> 17:07:31.330
from the data that was extracted and filtered.

12994
17:07:31.330 --> 17:07:35.714
Once we have our required rows and columns, order by can be processed to sort the

12995
17:07:35.714 --> 17:07:40.261
final output. This query returns several errors and will use it to demonstrate how a

12996
17:07:40.261 --> 17:07:44.807
query is processed. When a query is run, it returns a single error message only. The

12997
17:07:44.807 --> 17:07:49.246
error messages reflect the processing order. Only after correcting an error in the

12998
17:07:49.246 --> 17:07:53.522
preceding syntax can the following syntax be processed. Take note of each error

12999
17:07:53.522 --> 17:07:57.798
message. From is processed first, and because there is no table in the database

13000
17:07:57.798 --> 17:08:00.450
called large earthquakes, it cannot be processed.

13001
17:08:00.450 --> 17:08:05.012
It should be earthquakes. Where is processed next? There is no column in the

13002
17:08:05.012 --> 17:08:09.933
earthquakes table called strength for where to apply a filter condition. It should

13003
17:08:09.933 --> 17:08:14.855
be magnitude. Select is only processed after from and where. The column place name

13004
17:08:14.855 --> 17:08:19.597
cannot be selected for the output because it does not exist. It's a mistake. It

13005
17:08:19.597 --> 17:08:24.579
should be place, not place name. Order by is processed last. Magnitude is misspelt.

13006
17:08:24.579 --> 17:08:28.180
Finally, once all the errors are fixed, it runs as expected.

13007
17:08:28.180 --> 17:08:32.702
Here's the logical processing order for the most commonly used SQL syntax in a

13008
17:08:32.702 --> 17:08:37.513
query. This is the order the database processes the syntax in a query. Note how far

13009
17:08:37.513 --> 17:08:42.209
down the order select occurs. We can imagine that all processing before select is

13010
17:08:42.209 --> 17:08:46.846
concerned with finding, merging, aggregating, and filtering the data. Processing

13011
17:08:46.846 --> 17:08:51.020
after select is more concerned with actions on the final data extracted.

13012
17:08:51.020 --> 17:08:55.407
The syntax after select tends to be quite expensive on the database processing

13013
17:08:55.407 --> 17:09:00.131
resources. We should only use when necessary. We'll look at this in a later chapter.

13014
17:09:00.131 --> 17:09:04.856
As data scientists working on real world large data sets, having an understanding of

13015
17:09:04.856 --> 17:09:09.468
the processing order can help determine why a query will not execute and also help

13016
17:09:09.468 --> 17:09:14.023
to look at ways to optimize the query for performance. Let's practice. Welcome to

13017
17:09:14.023 --> 17:09:18.523
chapter two. In this first lesson, we'll discuss how to use where correctly in a

13018
17:09:18.523 --> 17:09:18.860
query.

13019
17:09:18.860 --> 17:09:24.091
WHERE is used in a query to filter out individual rows from a data source specified

13020
17:09:24.091 --> 17:09:29.069
in the FROM statement. In this example, we're using a WHERE filter condition to

13021
17:09:29.069 --> 17:09:34.237
restrict the player stats table to only rows WHERE position equals SG for shooting

13022
17:09:34.237 --> 17:09:39.153
guard. The player stats table is scanned and only the rows that meet the WHERE

13023
17:09:39.153 --> 17:09:43.879
filter condition are extracted. Remember from the previous chapter WHERE is

13024
17:09:43.879 --> 17:09:48.480
processed after FROM but before SELECT. Let's look at an example of this.

13025
17:09:48.480 --> 17:09:53.038
Here we apply a filtering condition to a new calculated column, total rebounds, and

13026
17:09:53.038 --> 17:09:57.541
where is used to filter the results, where total rebounds is greater than or equal

13027
17:09:57.541 --> 17:09:58.310
to a thousand.

13028
17:09:58.390 --> 17:10:02.997
The query looks right, but when run, returns an error. The total rebounds column is

13029
17:10:02.997 --> 17:10:07.548
created in the select statement, but remember where is processed before select, so

13030
17:10:07.548 --> 17:10:11.877
where does not know about the new column yet. We can still achieve the desired

13031
17:10:11.877 --> 17:10:16.484
results by creating the new calculated column in a subquery first. Here we have the

13032
17:10:16.484 --> 17:10:20.979
subquery after from that creates a new column, total rebounds, which then becomes

13033
17:10:20.979 --> 17:10:25.641
available to be used in the where filter condition in the outer query. Now the query

13034
17:10:25.641 --> 17:10:27.140
runs with no error message.

13035
17:10:27.140 --> 17:10:31.719
Using subqueries will be discussed in more detail later in this course. But why use

13036
17:10:31.719 --> 17:10:36.243
a subquerie to get the total rebounds column in the previous example when we could

13037
17:10:36.243 --> 17:10:40.823
have just used the same calculation in the WHERE filtering condition? This is still

13038
17:10:40.823 --> 17:10:45.402
valid and will return the same results. However, WHERE will run that calculation on

13039
17:10:45.402 --> 17:10:49.926
every row to check if the row meets the condition or not. For a simple calculation

13040
17:10:49.926 --> 17:10:52.850
like the one in the example, this is not a big issue.

13041
17:10:52.850 --> 17:10:57.095
But if the calculation is complex, it could increase the time it takes the query to

13042
17:10:57.095 --> 17:11:01.341
run. Note also the unnecessary repeating of the calculation in the SELECT statement

13043
17:11:01.341 --> 17:11:03.080
and the WHERE filtering condition.

13044
17:11:03.130 --> 17:11:07.738
Similarly, functions applied to a column in the where filtering condition will also

13045
17:11:07.738 --> 17:11:12.290
run on every row. Simple functions on small data sets will not show any noticeable

13046
17:11:12.290 --> 17:11:16.843
effect on performance. However, applying complex or multiple functions to a column

13047
17:11:16.843 --> 17:11:21.339
or columns in the where filtering condition could noticeably increase the time it

13048
17:11:21.339 --> 17:11:26.003
takes for the query to run, especially on large data sets. You should always try and

13049
17:11:26.003 --> 17:11:30.000
make your query as simple as possible while still getting what you want.

13050
17:11:31.909 --> 17:11:36.174
One way to do this is by avoiding using calculations and functions in the WHERE

13051
17:11:36.174 --> 17:11:40.277
filtering condition. Here, simply applying a wildcard filter avoided using a

13052
17:11:40.277 --> 17:11:44.758
function while still returning the same result. Let's summarize what we've learned.

13053
17:11:44.758 --> 17:11:46.540
WHERE is processed before select.

13054
17:11:46.540 --> 17:11:50.671
and will return an error if the syntax order is not correct. Calculations or

13055
17:11:50.671 --> 17:11:55.019
functions on columns in the WHERE filtering condition could increase the time it

13056
17:11:55.019 --> 17:11:59.367
takes the query to run. And remember there may be a simpler way to apply a WHERE

13057
17:11:59.367 --> 17:12:03.606
filter condition. Let's practice. In this lesson we'll examine the differences

13058
17:12:03.606 --> 17:12:08.009
between WHERE and HAVING and some best practices for how and how not to use them.

13059
17:12:08.009 --> 17:12:12.465
HAVING, like WHERE, is also used to filter a query. Although they both occur after

13060
17:12:12.465 --> 17:12:15.020
FROM and BEFORE SELECT in the processing order,

13061
17:12:15.020 --> 17:12:19.682
That's where the similarity stops. Remember from a previous lesson, where is used to

13062
17:12:19.682 --> 17:12:24.067
filter individual rows. Having, on the other hand, is used to filter grouped or

13063
17:12:24.067 --> 17:12:28.674
aggregated rows. Here is an example from the player stats table. I want to know the

13064
17:12:28.674 --> 17:12:33.058
sum of total points by team, but restricted to shooting guards. We use where to

13065
17:12:33.058 --> 17:12:37.332
filter just for rows, where position equals SG. This works because the filter

13066
17:12:37.332 --> 17:12:41.994
condition is on individual rows. But we're grouping by team. I could also use having

13067
17:12:41.994 --> 17:12:44.270
to restrict rows to only shooting guards.

13068
17:12:44.270 --> 17:12:49.021
This query produces the same results, but it's not the correct way to use having. In

13069
17:12:49.021 --> 17:12:53.659
this case, where is more efficient because rows are filtered first before grouping

13070
17:12:53.659 --> 17:12:57.958
and summing. Using having in this way would filter the individual rows after

13071
17:12:57.958 --> 17:13:02.653
grouping, therefore unnecessarily tying up resources and potentially increasing the

13072
17:13:02.653 --> 17:13:07.404
time it takes for a query to run. Don't use having to filter individual or ungrouped

13073
17:13:07.404 --> 17:13:09.440
rows. So what's the point of having?

13074
17:13:09.440 --> 17:13:14.316
HAVing is generally used to apply a numeric aggregate filter on grouped rows. Let's

13075
17:13:14.316 --> 17:13:18.722
say we want a query that returns all rebound statistics by team, where team

13076
17:13:18.722 --> 17:13:23.245
offensive rebounds are 1000 or more. First, we create a query that calculates

13077
17:13:23.245 --> 17:13:27.827
rebound statistics grouped by team. We have the column O rebound for offensive

13078
17:13:27.827 --> 17:13:32.762
rebounds already, therefore we can apply a WHERE filter so that O rebound is greater

13079
17:13:32.762 --> 17:13:37.579
than or equal to 1000. The query looks right and runs without error. No teams meet

13080
17:13:37.579 --> 17:13:38.460
that condition.

13081
17:13:38.460 --> 17:13:43.120
But take a closer look at the query. This filter is not the one we want. We want a

13082
17:13:43.120 --> 17:13:47.780
filter for TEAM offensive rebounds, not individual players, which is what this one

13083
17:13:47.780 --> 17:13:52.099
does. I'm pretty confident no single player had a thousand or more offensive

13084
17:13:52.099 --> 17:13:56.532
rebounds in a season. We need to use HAVING because the filter is on a grouped

13085
17:13:56.532 --> 17:14:00.908
numeric aggregate. Can we substitute WHERE for HAVING and use the same filter

13086
17:14:00.908 --> 17:14:05.170
condition? No, merely doing this will raise an error when we run the query.

13087
17:14:05.170 --> 17:14:09.788
A numeric column that is using a having filter condition must be enclosed in an

13088
17:14:09.788 --> 17:14:14.463
aggregate function. Also, note the position of having in this query. It's always

13089
17:14:14.463 --> 17:14:19.373
after group by. When applied correctly, as in this example, we enclose the filtering

13090
17:14:19.373 --> 17:14:24.166
column in the sum aggregate function. One team meets that condition. Incidentally,

13091
17:14:24.166 --> 17:14:29.076
the team I support, go OKC. Let's summarize what we've learned. Do not use having to

13092
17:14:29.076 --> 17:14:31.180
filter individual or ungrouped rows.

13093
17:14:31.180 --> 17:14:36.224
Use WHERE to filter individual rows and HAVING for a numeric filter on grouped rows.

13094
17:14:36.224 --> 17:14:41.027
We can only apply HAVING to a numeric column in an aggregate function filter. If

13095
17:14:41.027 --> 17:14:46.071
we're creating a query, we may want to interrogate the data first. What does it look

13096
17:14:46.071 --> 17:14:51.114
like? How many columns are there? What columns are required? And what columns can we

13097
17:14:51.114 --> 17:14:55.678
join on? Previously we said that any syntax processed after SELECT is mainly

13098
17:14:55.678 --> 17:14:58.740
concerned with actions on the final data extracted.

13099
17:14:58.740 --> 17:15:03.346
Most of these actions are commonly used to interrogate the data before working on a

13100
17:15:03.346 --> 17:15:07.729
query. We also said that these processes tend to be quite expensive as they can

13101
17:15:07.729 --> 17:15:12.224
potentially tie up database processing resources and therefore likely to increase

13102
17:15:12.224 --> 17:15:16.386
the time it takes a query to run. In this lesson we'll look at some ways to

13103
17:15:16.386 --> 17:15:20.825
interrogate data and the effects these may have on performance. One of the first

13104
17:15:20.825 --> 17:15:24.820
things we learn when taking a course in SQL is select star from a table.

13105
17:15:24.820 --> 17:15:29.887
SELECT star returns all rows and all columns from the table. This is great for data

13106
17:15:29.887 --> 17:15:34.831
interrogation and getting to know your data, but potentially bad for performance,

13107
17:15:34.831 --> 17:15:39.836
especially if the table has millions of rows and many columns. We could be waiting

13108
17:15:39.836 --> 17:15:44.903
for a while. Our query should only select the columns we need. Another problem with

13109
17:15:44.903 --> 17:15:49.420
SELECT star is that in JOINS it returns duplicates of the joining columns.

13110
17:15:49.420 --> 17:15:53.960
In this example, the player name column is in both the players and player stats

13111
17:15:53.960 --> 17:15:58.788
tables. We need to explicitly state the columns to be returned and from what tables.

13112
17:15:58.788 --> 17:16:03.329
This is where aliasing is useful. Is there a way to restrict the number of rows

13113
17:16:03.329 --> 17:16:08.156
returned but still provide us enough to interrogate the data adequately? Yes, we can

13114
17:16:08.156 --> 17:16:12.927
use the top function. Although top is one of the last syntaxes to be processed in a

13115
17:16:12.927 --> 17:16:16.720
query, we use it at the beginning of the query, just after select.

13116
17:16:16.720 --> 17:16:21.245
Top can be useful to limit the exact number of rows returned, or, used in

13117
17:16:21.245 --> 17:16:26.389
conjunction with percent, a proportion of the rows to be returned. Because top only

13118
17:16:26.389 --> 17:16:31.472
processes rows, we still need to specify the columns we require, either using star

13119
17:16:31.472 --> 17:16:36.617
for all columns, or explicitly identifying them after top. Select top 5 will return

13120
17:16:36.617 --> 17:16:41.699
the first 5 rows, and select top 1% will return the first 10,000 rows in a million

13121
17:16:41.699 --> 17:16:46.100
row table. What if we want to check the last rows of the table instead?

13122
17:16:46.100 --> 17:16:51.767
Top is only for the top of the dataset, right? Technically, yes, but there is no top

13123
17:16:51.767 --> 17:16:57.029
or bottom. By using Autobuy and experimenting with ASC and DESC, we can view a

13124
17:16:57.029 --> 17:17:02.359
subset of the dataset sorted by any column in the SELECT statement ascending or

13125
17:17:02.359 --> 17:17:07.621
descending. Autobuy is supported by most major database vendors. However, when

13126
17:17:07.621 --> 17:17:12.883
interrogating data by limiting rows returned, be aware that top is specific to

13127
17:17:12.883 --> 17:17:14.300
Microsoft SQL Server.

13128
17:17:14.300 --> 17:17:19.379
In other courses, we may have been introduced to Limit or RowNum. These perform the

13129
17:17:19.379 --> 17:17:24.335
same function but are specific to PostgreSQL and Oracle respectively. Also, their

13130
17:17:24.335 --> 17:17:29.474
position in the query is quite different. Use Autobi with caution. It works well for

13131
17:17:29.474 --> 17:17:34.614
interrogating data but slows performance in a query. Often data scientists will only

13132
17:17:34.614 --> 17:17:39.693
use SQL to extract data from a database and use some other program like R or Python

13133
17:17:39.693 --> 17:17:42.140
to perform analytics on the data output.

13134
17:17:42.140 --> 17:17:46.353
If a dataset requires sorting before analysis, these programs are a lot more

13135
17:17:46.353 --> 17:17:50.954
efficient at this. Unless there's a good reason to sort the data in a query, or you

13136
17:17:50.954 --> 17:17:55.112
want to interrogate the data, leave order buyout. A common problem that SQL

13137
17:17:55.112 --> 17:17:59.547
practitioners come across is duplicate rows in the results of a query. Duplicate

13138
17:17:59.547 --> 17:18:04.148
rows can be the result of a poor database design, a poorly designed query, or both.

13139
17:18:04.148 --> 17:18:08.583
In any case, we may need a mechanism to remove duplicates. In this lesson, we'll

13140
17:18:08.583 --> 17:18:10.800
look at the roles of distinct and union,

13141
17:18:10.800 --> 17:18:15.757
And removing duplicates and their potential effects on performance. If we want a

13142
17:18:15.757 --> 17:18:20.715
simple query on the player stats table that returns the name of each player that

13143
17:18:20.715 --> 17:18:25.920
participated in the 2017-2018 NBA season, there are 605 rows returned. But there's a

13144
17:18:25.920 --> 17:18:30.692
problem. Team is also a column in the table and players could have played for

13145
17:18:30.692 --> 17:18:35.773
multiple teams that season. So a simple query produces duplicate player name rows.

13146
17:18:35.773 --> 17:18:37.570
We can remove duplicate rows.

13147
17:18:37.570 --> 17:18:42.130
by enclosing the player name column in the distinct clause after select. But could

13148
17:18:42.130 --> 17:18:46.689
have we just used group by instead? Yes, and there'd be not much difference in the

13149
17:18:46.689 --> 17:18:51.193
time it takes for either query to run. If we want to remove duplicates and return

13150
17:18:51.193 --> 17:18:55.864
unique rows, then use distinct. However, if we're going to apply aggregate function,

13151
17:18:55.864 --> 17:19:00.368
for example, we want the count of teams each player has played for, we'll need to

13152
17:19:00.368 --> 17:19:04.260
use group by. Often there is an easier way to get the results we want.

13153
17:19:04.260 --> 17:19:09.155
In this case, we wanted to know the players that participated in the 2017-2018 NBA

13154
17:19:09.155 --> 17:19:13.930
season. By looking at the database, we see that we could have simply queried the

13155
17:19:13.930 --> 17:19:18.944
players table, where player name is a unique key, so there are no duplicate rows. We

13156
17:19:18.944 --> 17:19:23.779
can also produce duplicates when we append queries together to form one data set.

13157
17:19:23.779 --> 17:19:28.554
Appending is different from a join, because when we append, we are combining the

13158
17:19:28.554 --> 17:19:31.300
query rows rather than joining on key columns.

13159
17:19:31.300 --> 17:19:35.778
Appending rows together may create the issue of duplicates, as we can see in this

13160
17:19:35.778 --> 17:19:36.220
example.

13161
17:19:36.260 --> 17:19:41.136
The rows for orange and strawberry duplicate when we append. In SQL, we'll use the

13162
17:19:41.136 --> 17:19:45.714
union operator for appending. Union will do the work of checking and removing

13163
17:19:45.714 --> 17:19:50.590
duplicate rows for us. In some cases, we may require the return results to contain

13164
17:19:50.590 --> 17:19:55.466
duplicate rows, or we may know that there will be no duplicate rows when we append

13165
17:19:55.466 --> 17:20:00.283
queries. In this case, we can use union all, which returns all rows regardless if

13166
17:20:00.283 --> 17:20:04.980
there are duplicates or not. Interrogating our data before creating your query,

13167
17:20:04.980 --> 17:20:09.635
We'll give you an idea whether union or union all is the appropriate operator to use

13168
17:20:09.635 --> 17:20:14.234
for appending queries. Using distinct and union in queries to remove duplicate rows

13169
17:20:14.234 --> 17:20:18.778
should be used with caution. Depending on the design of the database, both may use

13170
17:20:18.778 --> 17:20:23.322
an internal sort mechanism to order and check for duplicates. This sorting has the

13171
17:20:23.322 --> 17:20:27.977
potential to increase the time it takes for a query to run significantly. When using

13172
17:20:27.977 --> 17:20:32.521
distinct, check if there is an alternative way to achieve the results we want. For

13173
17:20:32.521 --> 17:20:34.460
example, there may be another table

13174
17:20:34.460 --> 17:20:39.215
with the columnary require as a unique key, or use group by if the query is using an

13175
17:20:39.215 --> 17:20:43.913
aggregate function. When appending data, we need to ask ourselves if duplicates are

13176
17:20:43.913 --> 17:20:48.612
okay, or whether the appending queries will not produce duplicates. In these cases,

13177
17:20:48.612 --> 17:20:53.253
consider using union all, which does not make use of the internal sort. We'll look

13178
17:20:53.253 --> 17:20:57.895
at this internal sorting and how it affects performance later in this course. This

13179
17:20:57.895 --> 17:21:02.480
practice managing. Welcome to chapter three. A subquery is another query within a

13180
17:21:02.480 --> 17:21:02.820
query.

13181
17:21:02.820 --> 17:21:07.095
Subqueries are used to retrieve information from another table or query that is

13182
17:21:07.095 --> 17:21:11.369
separate to the main query. In this lesson we'll examine the different types of

13183
17:21:11.369 --> 17:21:15.752
subqueries and their potential impacts on query performance. So how do subqueries

13184
17:21:15.752 --> 17:21:20.081
look? A subquery generally has its own SELECT statement and returns results just

13185
17:21:20.081 --> 17:21:24.626
like a regular query. However, results from a subquery are used by an outer query. A

13186
17:21:24.626 --> 17:21:28.901
subquerie is processed first before returning the results for processing by the

13187
17:21:28.901 --> 17:21:29.550
outer query.

13188
17:21:29.550 --> 17:21:34.111
Therefore, a query that contains a subquery runs from the inside out. SQL

13189
17:21:34.111 --> 17:21:39.047
practitioners will often indent subqueries to identify them from outer or other

13190
17:21:39.047 --> 17:21:43.984
queries. In the following three examples, the subquery is in red text and outer

13191
17:21:43.984 --> 17:21:47.670
query in blue. The most common places to use a subquery are

13192
17:21:47.720 --> 17:21:52.417
First, with from, the subquery acts as a virtual table or data source. In this

13193
17:21:52.417 --> 17:21:57.414
example, the subquery calculates a new column from the orders table, numdays, which

13194
17:21:57.414 --> 17:22:02.292
is a difference in days from ordering to shipping. The new column is then used by

13195
17:22:02.292 --> 17:22:07.290
the outer query to filter for numdays of 35 or more. The results of this query show

13196
17:22:07.290 --> 17:22:12.227
seven customers had to wait 35 days or more for their order to be shipped. Second,

13197
17:22:12.227 --> 17:22:15.840
with where, to return a filter condition to the outer query.

13198
17:22:15.840 --> 17:22:20.573
In this example, the subquery returns customer IDs from the orders table, where a

13199
17:22:20.573 --> 17:22:25.188
freight weight is greater than 800 kilograms. The customer IDs are then used to

13200
17:22:25.188 --> 17:22:29.862
filter on the customers table in the outer query. The results of this query show

13201
17:22:29.862 --> 17:22:34.770
three customers having made orders with a freight weight of more than 800 kilograms.

13202
17:22:34.770 --> 17:22:39.444
And third, with select, to derive a new column. In this example, the subquery is

13203
17:22:39.444 --> 17:22:44.060
calculating the average freight weight for each customer from the orders table.

13204
17:22:44.060 --> 17:22:49.505
The new column, avgfreight, is added to the SELECT statement of the outer query. The

13205
17:22:49.505 --> 17:22:54.886
results return the avgfreight column with the customer ID and company name from the

13206
17:22:54.886 --> 17:23:00.072
customers table. There are two types of subqueries, uncorrelated and correlated.

13207
17:23:00.072 --> 17:23:05.452
Uncorrelated subqueries do not contain a reference to the outer query and therefore

13208
17:23:05.452 --> 17:23:08.110
can run independently of the outer query.

13209
17:23:08.110 --> 17:23:12.732
On the other hand, correlated subqueries include a reference to the outer query and

13210
17:23:12.732 --> 17:23:17.299
therefore cannot run independently of the outer query. Uncorrelated subqueries are

13211
17:23:17.299 --> 17:23:21.921
commonly found with WHERE and FROM and correlated subqueries with WHERE and SELECT.

13212
17:23:21.921 --> 17:23:26.488
Correlated subqueries can be quite inefficient because the subqueries executes for

13213
17:23:26.488 --> 17:23:31.055
each row in the outer query. Compare this to the uncorrelated subqueries where the

13214
17:23:31.055 --> 17:23:35.287
subqueries executes only once and returns the results to the outer query for

13215
17:23:35.287 --> 17:23:35.900
processing.

13216
17:23:35.900 --> 17:23:40.581
Often the results from a correlated subquery can be replicated using an inner join.

13217
17:23:40.581 --> 17:23:45.150
Because the correlated subquery must execute for each row in the outer query, the

13218
17:23:45.150 --> 17:23:49.719
query may take some time to complete, especially if the outer query contains many

13219
17:23:49.719 --> 17:23:54.400
rows. In this case, consider using an inner join, which only makes one pass through

13220
17:23:54.400 --> 17:23:58.912
the data. In this example, we replace the correlated subquery with an inner join

13221
17:23:58.912 --> 17:24:03.142
between the customers and orders table. Let's practice our understanding of

13222
17:24:03.142 --> 17:24:03.650
subquery.

13223
17:24:03.650 --> 17:24:08.543
A common question that data scientists ask when analyzing SQL databases is whether

13224
17:24:08.543 --> 17:24:13.496
the data in one table is present or absent in a related table. Fortunately, this is

13225
17:24:13.496 --> 17:24:18.449
an easy question to answer using SQL. However, depending on the requirements, there

13226
17:24:18.449 --> 17:24:23.402
may be more than one method, and some methods may be more efficient than others. In

13227
17:24:23.402 --> 17:24:28.116
this lesson, we'll discuss what we mean by is data present or absent in related

13228
17:24:28.116 --> 17:24:32.830
tables, and we'll look at using intersect and accept to help us determine this.

13229
17:24:32.830 --> 17:24:37.621
First, what do we mean by data is present or absent in related tables? We'll use

13230
17:24:37.621 --> 17:24:42.293
Venn diagrams to demonstrate this. Here, the overlapping circles represent the

13231
17:24:42.293 --> 17:24:47.144
outputs of queries on two tables. Where the outputs intersect is where we'll find

13232
17:24:47.144 --> 17:24:52.056
the same data present in both tables. If we take a portion in the left output that

13233
17:24:52.056 --> 17:24:57.027
does not intersect the right output, then we say that the data in the table that we

13234
17:24:57.027 --> 17:25:00.800
query on the left is absent in the table we query on the right.

13235
17:25:00.800 --> 17:25:05.211
Let's apply this concept to our customers orders database. Any customers placing

13236
17:25:05.211 --> 17:25:09.732
orders will have their customer information in the customers table and their order

13237
17:25:09.732 --> 17:25:14.253
information in the orders table. We say these tables are related because they both

13238
17:25:14.253 --> 17:25:18.554
contain customer ID. Customers that have not placed any orders will have their

13239
17:25:18.554 --> 17:25:23.130
customer information in the customers table, but not in the orders table. If we are

13240
17:25:23.130 --> 17:25:26.990
working on a sales project, we may ask who is ordering and who is not?

13241
17:25:26.990 --> 17:25:32.319
We can find out which customers have placed orders by using the intersect operator.

13242
17:25:32.319 --> 17:25:37.584
We select customer ID from the customers table, the left query, and intersect, and

13243
17:25:37.584 --> 17:25:42.721
then select customer ID from the orders table, the right query. To check for the

13244
17:25:42.721 --> 17:25:48.114
absence of customers in the orders table that are present in the customers table, we

13245
17:25:48.114 --> 17:25:52.801
substitute intersect with accept. Intersect and accept are great for data

13246
17:25:52.801 --> 17:25:53.700
interrogation.

13247
17:25:53.700 --> 17:25:58.575
One feature they provide is removing duplicates, meaning that the results will only

13248
17:25:58.575 --> 17:26:03.273
contain unique values of the data that is present or absent. One disadvantage of

13249
17:26:03.273 --> 17:26:07.972
intersect and accept is the number and order of columns in the select statements

13250
17:26:07.972 --> 17:26:12.494
must be the same between queries. We'll discuss this disadvantage in upcoming

13251
17:26:12.494 --> 17:26:17.311
lessons. Let's practice intersect. In this chapter, we've already looked at how we

13252
17:26:17.311 --> 17:26:22.244
can use intersect and accept to determine if data in one table was present or absent

13253
17:26:22.244 --> 17:26:23.360
in a related table.

13254
17:26:23.360 --> 17:26:28.125
In this lesson, we'll examine alternative methods, exists, not exists, in, and not

13255
17:26:28.125 --> 17:26:33.007
in. Let's start with exists. Exists filters the outer query when there is a match of

13256
17:26:33.007 --> 17:26:37.656
data between the outer query and a subquery. Exists evaluates for either true or

13257
17:26:37.656 --> 17:26:42.364
false on a match. Therefore, we can specify anything in the select statement of a

13258
17:26:42.364 --> 17:26:47.187
subquery. In this example, if customer ID from the customers table matches customer

13259
17:26:47.187 --> 17:26:48.640
ID from the orders table,

13260
17:26:48.640 --> 17:26:53.182
A one is returned indicating it is true and the customers table is filtered on the

13261
17:26:53.182 --> 17:26:57.558
match. Next, in. In works in a similar way with subqueries. However, we need to

13262
17:26:57.558 --> 17:27:02.211
specify the columns to match on in the where filter condition of the outer query and

13263
17:27:02.211 --> 17:27:06.809
the select statement of the subquery. Here we're looking for a match of customer ID

13264
17:27:06.809 --> 17:27:11.351
from the customers table with customer ID from the subquery, which in this case is

13265
17:27:11.351 --> 17:27:15.617
just the orders table. In here two examples of exists and in, the results are

13266
17:27:15.617 --> 17:27:18.220
identical. Is one method better than the other?

13267
17:27:18.220 --> 17:27:22.989
Well, it depends. The subquery used with exists looks like a correlated subquery,

13268
17:27:22.989 --> 17:27:27.758
which we said previously can be inefficient. Technically, yes, it is a correlated

13269
17:27:27.758 --> 17:27:32.468
subquery. However, exists will stop searching the subquery when the condition is

13270
17:27:32.468 --> 17:27:37.119
true, whereas in with a subquery will collect all the results from the subquery

13271
17:27:37.119 --> 17:27:41.888
before further processing, which is potentially slower. For this reason, consider

13272
17:27:41.888 --> 17:27:44.420
using exists instead of in with a subquery.

13273
17:27:44.420 --> 17:27:49.089
Not exists and not in are used to do the opposite of exists and in. In a query,

13274
17:27:49.089 --> 17:27:53.876
substitute exists and in with not exists and not in respectively to check for the

13275
17:27:53.876 --> 17:27:58.485
presence of data on one table that is absent in another. This example uses not

13276
17:27:58.485 --> 17:28:03.213
exists to return customer details from the customers table where the customer is

13277
17:28:03.213 --> 17:28:07.823
absent from the orders table. And this example uses not in to perform the same

13278
17:28:07.823 --> 17:28:12.669
check. Here we need to specify the column we want to filter on in the where filter

13279
17:28:12.669 --> 17:28:13.260
condition.

13280
17:28:13.260 --> 17:28:18.471
One major issue with using NOTIN is the way it handles null values. If the columns

13281
17:28:18.471 --> 17:28:23.745
in the subquery being evaluated for a non-match contain null values, no results are

13282
17:28:23.745 --> 17:28:28.829
returned. In this example we want to know which nation capitals from the nations

13283
17:28:28.829 --> 17:28:34.040
table are absent from the nearest pop column in the earthquakes table. Using NOTIN

13284
17:28:34.040 --> 17:28:39.187
returns no results. Does this mean every capital city has been near to a recorded

13285
17:28:39.187 --> 17:28:40.140
earthquake? No.

13286
17:28:40.140 --> 17:28:45.347
It's because the nearest pop column contains null values. To handle null values, the

13287
17:28:45.347 --> 17:28:50.244
subquery needs an isNotNull filter. Here, we filter the subquery to remove null

13288
17:28:50.244 --> 17:28:55.451
values. Because of this issue, we should always consider using not exists instead of

13289
17:28:55.451 --> 17:29:00.410
not in with the subquery. An advantage of exists, not exists in, and not in over

13290
17:29:00.410 --> 17:29:05.555
other methods is the results can contain any column from the outer query and in any

13291
17:29:05.555 --> 17:29:08.220
order. Compare this to intersect and accept

13292
17:29:08.220 --> 17:29:12.443
where the number and order of columns in the SELECT statement must be the same

13293
17:29:12.443 --> 17:29:16.829
between queries. As previously discussed, one disadvantage of NOTIN is the way it

13294
17:29:16.829 --> 17:29:21.323
handles null values in the subquery. Let's practice these. Another way to determine

13295
17:29:21.323 --> 17:29:25.817
if data in one table is present or absent in a related table is with joins. In this

13296
17:29:25.817 --> 17:29:30.365
lesson, we'll discuss the inner join and the exclusive left outer join. We'll finish

13297
17:29:30.365 --> 17:29:34.480
off with a review of the different methods presented in this chapter and the

13298
17:29:34.480 --> 17:29:36.700
advantages and disadvantages of each one.

13299
17:29:36.700 --> 17:29:41.158
Joining queries with an inner join, the left query joins the right query through

13300
17:29:41.158 --> 17:29:45.728
related columns. An inner join will match values in the related columns and return

13301
17:29:45.728 --> 17:29:50.187
the results of the match. Therefore, this is a check for the presence of data in

13302
17:29:50.187 --> 17:29:54.701
related tables. In this example, the customers and orders tables both contain the

13303
17:29:54.701 --> 17:29:59.327
column customer ID. This is the column which relates these tables. To check for the

13304
17:29:59.327 --> 17:30:03.841
presence of data in one table that is absent in another table with a join, we can

13305
17:30:03.841 --> 17:30:05.680
use an exclusive left outer join.

13306
17:30:05.680 --> 17:30:10.548
An inclusive left outer join returns all the rows in the left query, whereas an

13307
17:30:10.548 --> 17:30:15.602
exclusive left outer join returns only rows in the left query that are not present

13308
17:30:15.602 --> 17:30:20.224
in the right query. We can demonstrate this concept with Venn diagrams. The

13309
17:30:20.224 --> 17:30:25.092
highlighted areas represent the results of the join between queries. To make an

13310
17:30:25.092 --> 17:30:30.084
exclusive left outer join, we add ISNULL to a WHERE filter condition of the right

13311
17:30:30.084 --> 17:30:32.980
query to restrict it to rows that do not match.

13312
17:30:32.980 --> 17:30:37.658
This example returns customer data from the customers table that is absent from the

13313
17:30:37.658 --> 17:30:42.281
orders table. To end this chapter, let's review the different methods to determine

13314
17:30:42.281 --> 17:30:46.959
if data in one table is present or absent in a related table and the advantages and

13315
17:30:46.959 --> 17:30:51.469
disadvantages of each one. First, intersect and accept. Intersect checks for the

13316
17:30:51.469 --> 17:30:56.203
presence and accept for the absence. The advantages are that they are great for data

13317
17:30:56.203 --> 17:30:59.980
interrogation and they remove duplicates from the returned results.

13318
17:30:59.980 --> 17:31:04.714
A disadvantage is that the number and order of columns in the select statement must

13319
17:31:04.714 --> 17:31:09.334
be the same between queries. Next, exists and not exists. Both of these operators

13320
17:31:09.334 --> 17:31:14.068
use a subquery in the where filter condition. Exist checks for the presence and not

13321
17:31:14.068 --> 17:31:18.688
exists for the absence. One advantage is that the subquery will stop searching as

13322
17:31:18.688 --> 17:31:20.000
soon as it evaluates to

13323
17:31:20.638 --> 17:31:25.759
TRUE, which is a match for EXISTS and non-match for NOT EXISTS. Another advantage is

13324
17:31:25.759 --> 17:31:30.758
the results can contain any column from the outer query and in any order, which is

13325
17:31:30.758 --> 17:31:35.573
an advantage over INTERSECT and EXCEPT. Restricting results to columns from the

13326
17:31:35.573 --> 17:31:39.170
outer query only is also a disadvantage compared to a JOIN.

13327
17:31:39.170 --> 17:31:43.921
Next in and not in. In checks for the presence and not in for the absence. An

13328
17:31:43.921 --> 17:31:48.918
advantage is the results can contain any column from the outer query. Like exists

13329
17:31:48.918 --> 17:31:54.040
and not exists, this is an advantage over intersect and accept. Like exists and not

13330
17:31:54.040 --> 17:31:59.099
exists, restricting results to columns from the outer query only is a disadvantage

13331
17:31:59.099 --> 17:32:04.158
compared to a join. Another disadvantage is no results returned with not in if the

13332
17:32:04.158 --> 17:32:07.490
column being evaluated in the subquery contains nulls.

13333
17:32:07.490 --> 17:32:12.410
And finally, the inner join and exclusive left outer join. Inner join checks for the

13334
17:32:12.410 --> 17:32:17.154
presence and exclusive left outer join for the absence. One advantage of joins is

13335
17:32:17.154 --> 17:32:21.664
that the results can contain any column from all joined queries in any order.

13336
17:32:21.664 --> 17:32:26.525
Compare this to exists, not exists, in and not in, where the results are restricted

13337
17:32:26.525 --> 17:32:31.386
to columns from the outer query only. A possible disadvantage of the exclusive left

13338
17:32:31.386 --> 17:32:35.310
outer join is the requirement to add isNull where filter condition.

13339
17:32:35.310 --> 17:32:40.842
In a large complex query, this could be easily missed. Let's practice. In this final

13340
17:32:40.842 --> 17:32:46.111
chapter, we'll introduce some tools and commands available in SQL Server to help

13341
17:32:46.111 --> 17:32:51.380
analyze and tune query performance. Most SQL Server practitioners use SQL Server

13342
17:32:51.380 --> 17:32:56.715
Management Studio, or SSMS for short, for creating and editing queries. The tools

13343
17:32:56.715 --> 17:33:00.930
and commands discussed in this chapter are accessed within SSMS,

13344
17:33:00.930 --> 17:33:05.890
The SSMS graphics are from 2017 version and may vary slightly from other releases.

13345
17:33:05.890 --> 17:33:10.971
Throughout this course we've heard terms like increase the time it takes for a query

13346
17:33:10.971 --> 17:33:15.870
to run. In SQL Server there are many tools and commands available to quantify and

13347
17:33:15.870 --> 17:33:20.770
measure performance. One way to measure query time is with the command statistics

13348
17:33:20.770 --> 17:33:25.790
time, which reports the milliseconds required to pass, compile and execute a query.

13349
17:33:25.790 --> 17:33:31.010
In SSMS, if settings are set to results to grid, the statistics are displayed in the

13350
17:33:31.010 --> 17:33:35.858
messages tab to the right of the results tab. The output contains several time

13351
17:33:35.858 --> 17:33:41.016
statistics, however, we'll only discuss CPU time and elapsed time under the heading

13352
17:33:41.016 --> 17:33:45.988
SQL server execution times. CPU time is the actual time taken up by the database

13353
17:33:45.988 --> 17:33:51.208
server processes to process the query, and elapsed time is the total duration of the

13354
17:33:51.208 --> 17:33:55.310
query from execution to returning the complete results back to us.

13355
17:33:55.310 --> 17:34:00.313
Let's look at statistics time and action. In this example, our query returns region,

13356
17:34:00.313 --> 17:34:05.018
country and capital, where the 2017 population of a capital city is more than 1

13357
17:34:05.018 --> 17:34:09.961
million. This query uses two subqueries in a where filter condition, first to check

13358
17:34:09.961 --> 17:34:14.606
for the presence of the capital city in the cities table, and second to return

13359
17:34:14.606 --> 17:34:19.609
cities with populations of more than 1 million. To get the time statistics, we first

13360
17:34:19.609 --> 17:34:24.195
need to run set statistics time on before executing our query. Looking at the

13361
17:34:24.195 --> 17:34:24.850
statistics,

13362
17:34:24.850 --> 17:34:29.769
output, both CPU and elapsed time are close to 400 milliseconds. We can rewrite the

13363
17:34:29.769 --> 17:34:34.569
query and replace the two subqueries in the where filter condition with exists in

13364
17:34:34.569 --> 17:34:39.547
one subquery. Because statistics time is already on, we do not need to rerun it. The

13365
17:34:39.547 --> 17:34:44.347
output shows a significant improvement in time. The database processes spent less

13366
17:34:44.347 --> 17:34:48.614
than one millisecond on the query, and total execution time was only two

13367
17:34:48.614 --> 17:34:52.940
milliseconds. Once we've completed our assessment of the time statistics,

13368
17:34:52.940 --> 17:34:58.504
When we compare our two examples, the first query was slower than the second because

13369
17:34:58.504 --> 17:35:03.605
of the two subqueries in the where filter condition. In the first query, each

13370
17:35:03.605 --> 17:35:09.037
subquery must collect all the results first before further processing, whereas the

13371
17:35:09.037 --> 17:35:14.536
second query uses exists, which stops searching the subquery when there is a match,

13372
17:35:14.536 --> 17:35:19.769
much more efficient. When analyzing query time statistics, the elapsed time may

13373
17:35:19.769 --> 17:35:20.100
vary,

13374
17:35:20.100 --> 17:35:25.118
Because it is sensitive to several factors, including load on the server and network

13375
17:35:25.118 --> 17:35:30.136
bandwidth between the database server and a query editing application like SSMS, the

13376
17:35:30.136 --> 17:35:35.095
CPU time is less sensitive and will generally show little variation. One issue with

13377
17:35:35.095 --> 17:35:39.994
using CPU time is when the server processes are running in parallel. In this case,

13378
17:35:39.994 --> 17:35:44.773
it is possible that CPU time is multiplied by the number of processes, making it

13379
17:35:44.773 --> 17:35:47.760
appear significantly higher than the elapsed time,

13380
17:35:47.760 --> 17:35:52.494
and therefore making it less useful for query performance tuning. In most cases, the

13381
17:35:52.494 --> 17:35:57.171
desired outcome is to tune a query to return the results to us as fast as possible.

13382
17:35:57.171 --> 17:36:01.623
Therefore, the elapsed time is the best measure to use. Because of variability,

13383
17:36:01.623 --> 17:36:06.131
particularly in the elapsed time, it's good practice to execute a query multiple

13384
17:36:06.131 --> 17:36:10.865
times and take an average of time measurements. We saw that with statistics time, we

13385
17:36:10.865 --> 17:36:14.190
could get a measure of the total time a query takes to run.

13386
17:36:14.190 --> 17:36:19.052
Another way we can review query performance is by examining the amount of disk or

13387
17:36:19.052 --> 17:36:23.914
memory activity generated by the query. We can do this by using the statistics IO

13388
17:36:23.914 --> 17:36:28.955
command. Before we start, we need a brief understanding of how data is stored in the

13389
17:36:28.955 --> 17:36:33.877
database table. All data, either in memory or on disk, is stored in eight kilobyte

13390
17:36:33.877 --> 17:36:38.679
size pages. Depending on the size of each row of data, a single page could store

13391
17:36:38.679 --> 17:36:41.920
many rows or just one value could span multiple pages.

13392
17:36:41.920 --> 17:36:46.941
A page can only belong to one table. If tables are small, each table would require

13393
17:36:46.941 --> 17:36:52.023
its own page. SQL Server will do most of its work from pages cached in memory. If a

13394
17:36:52.023 --> 17:36:56.798
query requests a page that is not in memory, it is read from the disk into the

13395
17:36:56.798 --> 17:37:01.819
memory cache. This is an example of how we might imagine the first four data pages

13396
17:37:01.819 --> 17:37:06.840
of our customer table is stored. Like statistics times, statistics.io is initiated

13397
17:37:06.840 --> 17:37:09.840
before running a query with set statistics.io on.

13398
17:37:09.840 --> 17:37:14.775
The statistics are displayed in SSMS in the messages tab to the right of the results

13399
17:37:14.775 --> 17:37:19.592
tab. All the statistics reported by Statistics.io are useful for query performance

13400
17:37:19.592 --> 17:37:24.469
tuning. However, as an introduction to Statistics.io in this lesson, we'll focus on

13401
17:37:24.469 --> 17:37:29.404
the logical reads measure and how it will be used to tune query performance. Logical

13402
17:37:29.404 --> 17:37:33.869
reads are reported for each table. They are a measure of the number of eight

13403
17:37:33.869 --> 17:37:38.510
kilobyte pages read from memory to process and return the results of our query.

13404
17:37:38.510 --> 17:37:43.351
In general, the more pages that need to be read, the slower our query will run.

13405
17:37:43.351 --> 17:37:48.498
Let's look at an example of the logical reads measure using Statistics.io. Our query

13406
17:37:48.498 --> 17:37:53.400
returns the maximum earthquake magnitude where a country capital was the closest

13407
17:37:53.400 --> 17:37:58.364
city to an earthquake of magnitude 7.5 or more. This query uses three subqueries.

13408
17:37:58.364 --> 17:38:03.143
The first is a correlated subquerie in the SELECT statement to get the maximum

13409
17:38:03.143 --> 17:38:04.430
magnitude earthquake.

13410
17:38:04.430 --> 17:38:09.094
The second is another correlated subquery to check for the presence of country

13411
17:38:09.094 --> 17:38:13.879
capitals in the Earthquakes table, and the third subquery applies a filter to be

13412
17:38:13.879 --> 17:38:18.304
passed back to the Nations table. To get the statistics to display, we run

13413
17:38:18.304 --> 17:38:23.088
setStatistics.io on before executing our query. The results are reported against

13414
17:38:23.088 --> 17:38:27.992
each table that participates in the query. The output shows 54 pages read from the

13415
17:38:27.992 --> 17:38:31.460
Earthquakes table and 3 pages read from the Nations table.

13416
17:38:31.460 --> 17:38:36.015
We can rewrite the query this time using an inner join between the Nations and

13417
17:38:36.015 --> 17:38:40.921
Earthquakes tables. Because Statistics.io is already on, we do not need to rerun it.

13418
17:38:40.921 --> 17:38:45.534
The output shows the same amount of page reads from the Nations table, 3, but 3

13419
17:38:45.534 --> 17:38:50.323
times fewer page reads from the Earthquakes table, only 18. Once we have completed

13420
17:38:50.323 --> 17:38:55.054
our assessment of the statistics, we turn it off by running setStatistics.io off.

13421
17:38:55.054 --> 17:38:59.842
Using an inner join on the Earthquakes table in the second query, the entire table

13422
17:38:59.842 --> 17:39:00.660
was read once.

13423
17:39:00.660 --> 17:39:05.578
when the query was processed, 18 pages. Multiply that by the number of subqueries

13424
17:39:05.578 --> 17:39:10.193
accessing the earthquakes table in the first query and this increases to 54.

13425
17:39:10.193 --> 17:39:15.172
Therefore we may expect the first query to run slower than the second because more

13426
17:39:15.172 --> 17:39:20.091
pages are accessed. We can confirm this by running the statistics time command on

13427
17:39:20.091 --> 17:39:25.070
each query. 29 milliseconds for the first query and 3 milliseconds for the second.

13428
17:39:25.430 --> 17:39:30.071
When it comes to query performance tuning, adding an index to a table can be a game

13429
17:39:30.071 --> 17:39:34.713
changer. In this lesson, we'll examine a brief overview of indexes and how they can

13430
17:39:34.713 --> 17:39:39.243
be used to improve query performance. An index is a structure added to a table to

13431
17:39:39.243 --> 17:39:43.940
improve the speed of accessing data. Indexes are used to locate data quickly without

13432
17:39:43.940 --> 17:39:48.526
having to scan or search every row in a table. This makes them particularly useful

13433
17:39:48.526 --> 17:39:53.167
for improving performance of queries with filter conditions. Indexes are applied to

13434
17:39:53.167 --> 17:39:53.950
table columns.

13435
17:39:53.950 --> 17:39:58.866
And they can be added at any time. Adding an index to a table is typically done by

13436
17:39:58.866 --> 17:40:03.783
the database administrator. SQL servers support several different index types. The

13437
17:40:03.783 --> 17:40:07.500
two most common table indexes are clustered and non-clustered.

13438
17:40:07.570 --> 17:40:11.872
A good analogy for a clustered index is a dictionary where words are stored

13439
17:40:11.872 --> 17:40:16.634
alphabetically. With the clustered index, the data in the data pages are stored and

13440
17:40:16.634 --> 17:40:21.395
ordered by the columns with the index. Because the data is ordered one way, a table

13441
17:40:21.395 --> 17:40:26.156
can only have one clustered index. Clustered indexes reduce the number of data page

13442
17:40:26.156 --> 17:40:30.975
reads by a query, which helps speed up search operations. For a non-clustered index,

13443
17:40:30.975 --> 17:40:34.130
a good analogy is a textbook with an index at the back.

13444
17:40:34.130 --> 17:40:38.900
Data in the book is unordered, and the index at the back indicates the page numbers

13445
17:40:38.900 --> 17:40:43.613
containing a search condition. Like a textbook, a table with a non-clustered index

13446
17:40:43.613 --> 17:40:48.153
allows data to be unordered in the table data pages. Another layer in the index

13447
17:40:48.153 --> 17:40:52.980
structure contains ordered pointers to the data pages. A table can contain more than

13448
17:40:52.980 --> 17:40:57.635
one non-clustered index. Non-clustered indexes are commonly used to improve table

13449
17:40:57.635 --> 17:41:02.060
insert and update operations. Let's look at how a clustered index can improve

13450
17:41:02.060 --> 17:41:02.750
performance.

13451
17:41:02.750 --> 17:41:07.596
A clustered index creates what is called a B-tree structure on a table. We can think

13452
17:41:07.596 --> 17:41:11.980
of a B-tree as an upside-down tree, where the trunk is called the root node,

13453
17:41:11.980 --> 17:41:16.825
branches the branch nodes, and leaves the page nodes. The root node contains ordered

13454
17:41:16.825 --> 17:41:21.440
pointers to the branch nodes, which in turn contain ordered pointers to the page

13455
17:41:21.440 --> 17:41:26.228
nodes. The page node level contains all the 8KB data pages from the table, with the

13456
17:41:26.228 --> 17:41:29.920
data physically ordered by the columns with the clustered index.

13457
17:41:29.920 --> 17:41:35.354
Without a clustered index, there is no guarantee the data is ordered in the table

13458
17:41:35.354 --> 17:41:40.654
data pages. Using our customers table as an example, if we queried for all rows

13459
17:41:40.654 --> 17:41:46.223
where customer ID is equal to PARIS, without an index, all pages in the table would

13460
17:41:46.223 --> 17:41:51.858
be scanned for a match. If our customers table has a clustered index on customer ID,

13461
17:41:51.858 --> 17:41:57.225
then the search would enter the B tree at pointers nearest to our match. In this

13462
17:41:57.225 --> 17:41:58.500
case, between OLDWO

13463
17:41:58.500 --> 17:42:03.752
and W-O-L-Z-A. The ordered pointers at each branch node would in turn direct the

13464
17:42:03.752 --> 17:42:09.070
search to the page or pages containing the customer ID we're searching for. Let's

13465
17:42:09.070 --> 17:42:14.520
look at this in action. We'll query the player stats table for all rows with a team

13466
17:42:14.520 --> 17:42:20.035
as Oklahoma City, team code OKC. We'll turn on Statistics.io so we can determine the

13467
17:42:20.035 --> 17:42:25.484
number of page reads used by our query. Without an index the whole table is scanned

13468
17:42:25.484 --> 17:42:28.110
for rows that meet the filter condition.

13469
17:42:28.110 --> 17:42:33.008
logical reads indicates 12 pages read. When we add a clustered index to the team

13470
17:42:33.008 --> 17:42:38.028
column, the number of page reads drops to two. In general, the fewer pages read to

13471
17:42:38.028 --> 17:42:42.987
complete the query, the faster it will run. In this final lesson, we'll introduce

13472
17:42:42.987 --> 17:42:48.007
SQL execution plans and some basic examples of what they can tell us. When a query

13473
17:42:48.007 --> 17:42:52.844
is submitted to a database engine, after passing all the syntax, table and data

13474
17:42:52.844 --> 17:42:55.660
checks, it is passed to an optimization phase.

13475
17:42:55.660 --> 17:43:00.716
which evaluates several execution plans to determine which one will return results

13476
17:43:00.716 --> 17:43:05.710
at the lowest cost. Among other parameters, costs include processor usage, memory

13477
17:43:05.710 --> 17:43:10.766
usage, and data pages read. Once the best execution plan is selected, it is passed

13478
17:43:10.766 --> 17:43:15.699
to the execution engine to process the query. Reading execution plans is useful.

13479
17:43:15.699 --> 17:43:20.817
Among other important information, they can tell us if indexes were used, the types

13480
17:43:20.817 --> 17:43:21.680
of joins used,

13481
17:43:21.680 --> 17:43:26.198
if and where filter conditions, sorting and aggregations occurred, and their

13482
17:43:26.198 --> 17:43:30.656
relative costs. Any issue with a query should be immediately apparent in an

13483
17:43:30.656 --> 17:43:35.055
execution plan, which makes it an excellent tool for troubleshooting query

13484
17:43:35.055 --> 17:43:39.929
performance. This image shows how we can view an estimated execution plan in SSMS.

13485
17:43:39.929 --> 17:43:44.744
We highlight the query, and select the Display Estimated Execution Plan icon from

13486
17:43:44.744 --> 17:43:49.381
the menu toolbar. A graphical execution plan for the query is displayed in the

13487
17:43:49.381 --> 17:43:50.510
Execution Plan tab.

13488
17:43:50.510 --> 17:43:55.212
Each icon in an execution plan represents an operator that is used to perform a

13489
17:43:55.212 --> 17:44:00.153
specific task. Hovering over an operator provides a detailed statistics of the task

13490
17:44:00.153 --> 17:44:05.093
used in the query. Execution plans are read from right to left, as indicated by the

13491
17:44:05.093 --> 17:44:09.855
direction of arrows between operators. The width of each arrow reflects how much

13492
17:44:09.855 --> 17:44:14.855
data was passed from one operator to the next. Let's take a look at some information

13493
17:44:14.855 --> 17:44:16.700
execution plans can provide us.

13494
17:44:16.700 --> 17:44:21.687
An execution plan can tell us if an index is accessed while processing the query. In

13495
17:44:21.687 --> 17:44:26.615
this example, we have two versions of the customers table from the customers orders

13496
17:44:26.615 --> 17:44:31.067
database. Customers CI has a clustered index on the customer ID column, and

13497
17:44:31.067 --> 17:44:35.520
customers does not. We'll query both tables for a customer ID of PARIS. The

13498
17:44:35.520 --> 17:44:40.269
execution plan for the query on the customers table shows a table scan operator.

13499
17:44:40.269 --> 17:44:44.900
This operator indicates the entire table was scanned for the filter condition.

13500
17:44:44.900 --> 17:44:49.317
In contrast, the execution plan for the query on the customer CI table shows a

13501
17:44:49.317 --> 17:44:53.733
clustered index seek operator. This operator indicates an index was used to go

13502
17:44:53.733 --> 17:44:58.490
directly to the data pages that meet the filter condition. In an earlier chapter, we

13503
17:44:58.490 --> 17:45:03.189
said that we could append queries together using union and union all. We noted that

13504
17:45:03.189 --> 17:45:07.889
union removes duplicates and union all returns everything, including duplicates. To

13505
17:45:07.889 --> 17:45:11.230
check for and removes duplicates requires a sort operation.

13506
17:45:11.230 --> 17:45:16.104
We can see this by comparing two queries with Union and Unionall. The execution plan

13507
17:45:16.104 --> 17:45:20.572
with Union uses a sort operator internally to check and remove duplicates and

13508
17:45:20.572 --> 17:45:25.329
Unionall does not. Be aware of the sort operator in execution plans because it can

13509
17:45:25.329 --> 17:45:30.203
be costly on a query. Examining an execution plan could tell us particular syntax or

13510
17:45:30.203 --> 17:45:35.019
operators that are unnecessary. Often queries using different methods to return the

13511
17:45:35.019 --> 17:45:37.920
same results will produce the same execution plan.

13512
17:45:37.920 --> 17:45:42.514
In an earlier chapter, we compared in and exists operators. In this example, we have

13513
17:45:42.514 --> 17:45:47.053
two simple queries, one using in, the other using exists. The optimizer has decided

13514
17:45:47.053 --> 17:45:51.483
to use the same execution plan for both. This is not always the case, and it does

13515
17:45:51.483 --> 17:45:56.077
not mean we should let the optimizer do the query performance tuning for us. If time

13516
17:45:56.077 --> 17:46:00.562
is important, we should always ask ourselves if there is a better or faster way to

13517
17:46:00.562 --> 17:46:05.101
return the results we require. We'll complete this chapter with some final notes on

13518
17:46:05.101 --> 17:46:06.140
tools and commands.

13519
17:46:06.140 --> 17:46:10.623
That are available with SQL Server to help analyze and tune query performance. In

13520
17:46:10.623 --> 17:46:15.162
SQL Server, time statistics are commonly reported in milliseconds. In the examples

13521
17:46:15.162 --> 17:46:19.756
and exercises presented in this chapter, we compared queries and said that based on

13522
17:46:19.756 --> 17:46:24.239
the differences in time, one would likely run slower or faster than the other. In

13523
17:46:24.239 --> 17:46:28.500
reality, the time differences reported in the examples and exercises would be

13524
17:46:28.500 --> 17:46:29.220
unnoticeable.

13525
17:46:29.220 --> 17:46:33.899
In the real world, it would not be uncommon to work with large, complex queries that

13526
17:46:33.899 --> 17:46:38.466
run for 10 minutes, one hour, or more. This is where utilizing time statistics for

13527
17:46:38.466 --> 17:46:42.978
query tuning performance is invaluable. This chapter provided a brief overview of

13528
17:46:42.978 --> 17:46:47.434
query statistics, indexes, and execution plans. All of these are advanced topics

13529
17:46:47.434 --> 17:46:51.667
with many entire books and websites devoted to each of these and other query

13530
17:46:51.667 --> 17:46:56.067
performance tuning tools and commands. The use of SQL Server tools and commands

13531
17:46:56.067 --> 17:46:57.460
discussed in this chapter

13532
17:46:57.460 --> 17:47:02.065
may require elevated levels of database permissions. We recommend communicating with

13533
17:47:02.065 --> 17:47:06.561
your database administrator about your requirements before starting a project that

13534
17:47:06.561 --> 17:47:11.111
may require analyzing query performance. When trying to tune query performance with

13535
17:47:11.111 --> 17:47:15.661
any of the tools and commands available in SQL Server, don't just rely on one. Each

13536
17:47:15.661 --> 17:47:20.102
one can provide a different insight and often they complement one another. Thanks

13537
17:47:20.102 --> 17:47:24.543
for taking this course. We hope you enjoyed it and we look forward to seeing you.

13538
17:47:24.543 --> 17:47:26.900
Welcome to my intro to MongoDB with Python.

13539
17:47:26.900 --> 17:47:32.057
MongoDB is a tool that helps you explore data without requiring it to have a strict,

13540
17:47:32.057 --> 17:47:36.969
known structure. Because of this, you can handle diverse data together and unify

13541
17:47:36.969 --> 17:47:42.004
analytics. You can also keep improving and fix issues as your requirements evolve.

13542
17:47:42.004 --> 17:47:47.099
Most application programming interfaces, or APIs, on the web today expose a certain

13543
17:47:47.099 --> 17:47:52.134
data format. If you can express your data in this format, then you can get started

13544
17:47:52.134 --> 17:47:54.160
with MongoDB. Here's what I mean.

13545
17:47:54.160 --> 17:48:00.426
JavaScript is the language of web browsers. JavaScript Object Notation, or JSON, is

13546
17:48:00.426 --> 17:48:06.693
a common way that web services and client code pass data. JSON is also the basis of

13547
17:48:06.693 --> 17:48:13.034
MongoDB's data format. So, what is JSON? JSON has two collection structures. Objects

13548
17:48:13.034 --> 17:48:19.150
map string keys to values, and arrays order values. Values, in turn, are one of a

13549
17:48:19.150 --> 17:48:21.490
few things. Values are strings.

13550
17:48:21.490 --> 17:48:27.817
Numbers, the value true, the value false, the value null, or another object or

13551
17:48:27.817 --> 17:48:34.632
array. That's it. These JSON data types have equivalents in Python. JSON objects are

13552
17:48:34.632 --> 17:48:41.202
like Python dictionaries with string type keys. Arrays are like Python lists. And

13553
17:48:41.202 --> 17:48:48.017
the values I mentioned also map to Python. For example, null in JSON maps to none in

13554
17:48:48.017 --> 17:48:48.990
Python. Now,

13555
17:48:48.990 --> 17:48:54.462
How are these JSON Python types expressed in MongoDB? A database maps names to

13556
17:48:54.462 --> 17:49:00.355
collections. You can access collections by name the same way you would access values

13557
17:49:00.355 --> 17:49:06.247
in a Python dictionary. A collection, in turn, is like a list of dictionaries called

13558
17:49:06.247 --> 17:49:11.719
documents by MongoDB. When a dictionary is a value within a document, that's a

13559
17:49:11.719 --> 17:49:16.700
sub-document. Values in a document can be any of the types I mentioned.

13560
17:49:16.700 --> 17:49:22.278
MongoDB also supports some types native to Python but not to JSON. Two examples are

13561
17:49:22.278 --> 17:49:27.722
dates and regular expressions. Let's make concrete how JSON maps to Python and in

13562
17:49:27.722 --> 17:49:33.233
turn to MongoDB. Here is how I accessed the Nobel Prize API and collected its data

13563
17:49:33.233 --> 17:49:38.811
into a Mongo database for you. First, I import the requests library, which will get

13564
17:49:38.811 --> 17:49:43.650
the data from the API. I also import the MongoClient class from PyMongo.

13565
17:49:43.650 --> 17:49:48.912
PyMongo is the official Python driver for MongoDB. Then I connect to my local

13566
17:49:48.912 --> 17:49:54.243
database server. I say that I want a database with the name Nobel, and MongoDB

13567
17:49:54.243 --> 17:49:59.984
creates it. Finally, I gather JSON responses for the prize and laureate endpoints. I

13568
17:49:59.984 --> 17:50:05.519
insert them into the prizes and laureates collections, which MongoDB also creates

13569
17:50:05.519 --> 17:50:10.030
for me. Now, let's go over how to count documents in a collection.

13570
17:50:10.030 --> 17:50:15.319
and how to find one to inspect. First, a note on accessing databases and collections

13571
17:50:15.319 --> 17:50:20.168
from a client object. One way is square bracket notation, as if a client is a

13572
17:50:20.168 --> 17:50:25.331
dictionary of databases, with database names as keys. A database in turn is like a

13573
17:50:25.331 --> 17:50:30.305
dictionary of collections, with collection names as keys. Another way to access

13574
17:50:30.305 --> 17:50:35.406
things is dot notation. Databases are attributes of a client, and collections are

13575
17:50:35.406 --> 17:50:36.980
attributes of a database.

13576
17:50:36.980 --> 17:50:42.422
To count documents, use the countDocuments collection method. Pass a filter document

13577
17:50:42.422 --> 17:50:47.734
to filter to limit what you count. In this case, I want an unfiltered total count,

13578
17:50:47.734 --> 17:50:52.917
so I pass an empty document as the filter. Finally, you can fetch a document and

13579
17:50:52.917 --> 17:50:58.294
infer the schema of the raw JSON data given by the Nobel Prize API. Use the findOne

13580
17:50:58.294 --> 17:51:03.477
method, again with no filter, to grab a document from the collection. Now, let's

13581
17:51:03.477 --> 17:51:04.060
practice.

13582
17:51:04.060 --> 17:51:08.775
You'll access databases and collections from a connected client. You'll count

13583
17:51:06.667 --> 17:51:12.217
In the last lesson, we learned about databases, collections, and documents. We also

13584
17:51:12.217 --> 17:51:17.699
learned how data structures in MongoDB relate to those of JSON and Python. In this

13585
17:51:17.699 --> 17:51:23.249
lesson, we will learn how to query a collection to find documents of interest. Here

13586
17:51:23.249 --> 17:51:27.060
we have an example document in the Laureate's collection.

13587
17:51:27.060 --> 17:51:32.100
Finding documents in MongoDB reminds me of the work of this particular laureate. The

13588
17:51:32.100 --> 17:51:36.540
remarkable rays named after him are today called X-rays. To find documents

13589
17:51:36.540 --> 17:51:41.220
satisfying some criteria, we express those criteria as a document. This filter

13590
17:51:41.220 --> 17:51:46.080
document mirrors the structure of documents to match in the collection. Imagine a

13591
17:51:46.080 --> 17:51:51.000
filter document as an X-ray image, like the one on the right. This image is one of

13592
17:51:51.000 --> 17:51:53.220
the first X-ray images ever recorded.

13593
17:51:53.220 --> 17:51:58.584
Imagine you use it to filter by eye a collection of color photos of people's hands.

13594
17:51:58.584 --> 17:52:03.884
You might keep ones with five fingers and a ring on the finger. Here's another way

13595
17:52:03.884 --> 17:52:09.313
to think about filter documents. Hold a pair of pants up to a collection of mammals,

13596
17:52:09.313 --> 17:52:14.612
one at a time. The pants fit a part of the structure of human beings, but not that

13597
17:52:14.612 --> 17:52:19.977
of a walrus. Sorry, Wally. Filter documents can be small. For example, there are 48

13598
17:52:19.977 --> 17:52:21.140
laureate documents

13599
17:52:21.140 --> 17:52:26.450
with a value for the field gender equal to female. We can do the same for other

13600
17:52:26.450 --> 17:52:32.029
fields, like country of death and city of birth. Also, we can merge criteria into a

13601
17:52:32.029 --> 17:52:37.406
single filter document. This filter document will have the same form as matching

13602
17:52:37.406 --> 17:52:42.985
documents. In this case, the filter matches Marie Curie. She discovered the element

13603
17:52:42.985 --> 17:52:48.430
polonium, named after her native land. Poland was under Russian rule at the time.

13604
17:52:48.430 --> 17:52:50.110
She hoped that naming the

13605
17:52:50.110 --> 17:52:55.035
An element after it would publicize its lack of independence. Many immigrants won

13606
17:52:55.035 --> 17:52:59.899
Nobel prizes, but in what proportion? Later exercises in the course will explore

13607
17:52:59.899 --> 17:53:04.946
this question. We've seen filters that match exact values in a document. What about

13608
17:53:04.946 --> 17:53:09.688
satisfying other constraints? Query operators are like different ways to input

13609
17:53:09.688 --> 17:53:14.735
values on a website form. Some values you select from options, like countries. Some

13610
17:53:14.735 --> 17:53:16.620
you select with a range slider.

13611
17:53:16.620 --> 17:53:22.102
All operators on fields wrap around their corresponding values. You might operate a

13612
17:53:22.102 --> 17:53:27.451
drop-down menu to select a country and a slider to pick a value in a range. Query

13613
17:53:27.451 --> 17:53:32.867
operators in MongoDB work the same way. You place an operator in a filter document

13614
17:53:32.867 --> 17:53:38.349
to wrap around a field and its acceptable values. For example, let's find documents

13615
17:53:38.349 --> 17:53:43.699
where the field's dyed country is either France or USA. We use the in operator to

13616
17:53:43.699 --> 17:53:45.680
wrap around acceptable values.

13617
17:53:45.680 --> 17:53:51.386
Operators in MongoDB have a dollar sign prefix. Another example, to find documents

13618
17:53:51.386 --> 17:53:57.161
where diedCountry is not equal to a certain string, we can use the not equal, or NE

13619
17:53:57.161 --> 17:54:03.005
operator. We can compose query operators for a field. For example, here we query for

13620
17:54:03.005 --> 17:54:08.502
documents with diedCountry greater than, or GT, the string Belgium. At the same

13621
17:54:08.502 --> 17:54:13.860
time, we query for diedCountry less than or equal to, or LTE, the string USA.

13622
17:54:13.860 --> 17:54:18.355
How rude that MongoDB considers some countries to be greater than or less than

13623
17:54:18.355 --> 17:54:22.907
others. Actually, this highlights MongoDB's loose requirements for comparisons.

13624
17:54:22.907 --> 17:54:27.747
Comparison operators order values in lexicographic order, meaning alphabetical order

13625
17:54:27.747 --> 17:54:32.588
for non-numeric values. This behavior is something to keep in mind when working with

13626
17:54:32.588 --> 17:54:37.428
raw, unprocessed data. Let's practice constructing filter documents, including those

13627
17:54:37.428 --> 17:54:41.980
with query options. In the last lesson, we learned how to construct and compose

13628
17:54:41.980 --> 17:54:42.960
filter documents.

13629
17:54:42.960 --> 17:54:47.600
We also learned about query operators to express criteria other than simple

13630
17:54:47.600 --> 17:54:52.797
equality. In this lesson, we are going to learn how to query arrays and subdocuments

13631
17:54:52.797 --> 17:54:57.438
using dot notation. Dot notation is how MongoDB allows us to query document

13632
17:54:57.438 --> 17:55:02.635
substructure. Let's use the FindOne method to retrieve one of my favorite laureates.

13633
17:55:02.635 --> 17:55:07.770
Walter Cohn co-developed an important technique for computational chemistry. Notice

13634
17:55:07.770 --> 17:55:10.740
that the Prizes field is an array. In this case,

13635
17:55:10.740 --> 17:55:16.083
The array has one element with data on Cohn's two-way share of the 1998 prize in

13636
17:55:16.083 --> 17:55:21.426
chemistry. Note also that a laureate may have many affiliations for a prize. The

13637
17:55:21.426 --> 17:55:27.036
affiliations field of each prize subdocument is an array. To fit text on this slide,

13638
17:55:27.036 --> 17:55:32.512
I use parentheses to form multiline strings, and I show only part of the document.

13639
17:55:32.512 --> 17:55:38.122
MongoDB allows you to query document substructure using dot notation. Here's a count

13640
17:55:38.122 --> 17:55:38.990
of laureates.

13641
17:55:38.990 --> 17:55:43.810
We reach into the prizes array to query on the affiliations field across prizes.

13642
17:55:43.810 --> 17:55:48.088
From there, we reach again, this time to query on the name field across

13643
17:55:48.088 --> 17:55:52.668
affiliations. We count laureates with at least one prize affiliation name as

13644
17:55:52.668 --> 17:55:57.729
specified. The dot notation gives a full path to a field from the document root. I'm

13645
17:55:57.729 --> 17:56:02.489
curious how many laureates had an affiliation in Berkeley, California when they

13646
17:56:02.489 --> 17:56:05.140
received a prize. Here's my query. Go Bears!

13647
17:56:05.140 --> 17:56:10.187
MongoDB allows you to specify and enforce a schema for a collection, but this is not

13648
17:56:10.187 --> 17:56:14.934
required. For example, fields do not need to have the same type of value across

13649
17:56:14.934 --> 17:56:19.380
documents in a collection. In the case of this laureate, there is an empty

13650
17:56:19.380 --> 17:56:24.307
affiliation associated with his prize. Another accommodation in MongoDB is that of

13651
17:56:24.307 --> 17:56:29.355
field presence. Even root-level fields don't need to be present in all documents. In

13652
17:56:29.355 --> 17:56:33.020
this document, for example, the born-country field is absent.

13653
17:56:33.020 --> 17:56:38.066
Using the exists operator, we can query for the existence or non-existence of

13654
17:56:38.066 --> 17:56:43.506
fields. Here we see that many laureates documents do not have a born country field.

13655
17:56:43.506 --> 17:56:48.880
Do all laureates have a prizes field? With the help of the exists operator, we see

13656
17:56:48.880 --> 17:56:54.385
that, in fact, they do. But are any of those prizes fields empty arrays? I hope not.

13657
17:56:54.385 --> 17:56:59.300
We can check using dot notation to access array elements. This borrows from

13658
17:56:59.300 --> 17:57:00.480
JavaScript syntax.

13659
17:57:00.480 --> 17:57:05.106
Here we see a filter document for the criteria that a value exists for the field

13660
17:57:05.106 --> 17:57:09.790
zero within the prizes field. You can reference an array element by its numerical

13661
17:57:09.790 --> 17:57:14.185
index using dot notation. Thus, this expression counts documents that have a

13662
17:57:14.185 --> 17:57:18.927
non-empty prizes array. We see, to our relief, that all laureate documents contain

13663
17:57:18.927 --> 17:57:23.669
at least one prize. Are there laureates with more than one prize? Yes! We see that

13664
17:57:23.669 --> 17:57:28.180
Marie Curie is in this group, along with a few other people you may recognize.

13665
17:57:28.180 --> 17:57:33.105
We've learned about dot notation to query array and sub-document fields. We've also

13666
17:57:33.105 --> 17:57:37.852
learned about the exists operator to check for the presence of fields. Now let's

13667
17:57:37.852 --> 17:57:42.777
explore. In this lesson, we'll learn to use the distinct method on MongoCollection.

13668
17:57:42.777 --> 17:57:47.643
Using this method, we can collect the set of values assigned to a field across all

13669
17:57:47.643 --> 17:57:52.508
documents. We found an exceptional laureate in the last chapter. This laureate has

13670
17:57:52.508 --> 17:57:55.950
received three Nobel Prizes, more than any other laureate.

13671
17:57:55.950 --> 17:58:00.914
Here we can see that this is the International Committee of the Red Cross. You may

13672
17:58:00.914 --> 17:58:05.939
not have known that organizations can win Nobel Prizes, but they have. For example,

13673
17:58:05.939 --> 17:58:10.782
23 employees of Lawrence Berkeley Lab shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 2007. They

13674
17:58:10.782 --> 17:58:15.747
were part of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change. A future exercise will

13675
17:58:15.747 --> 17:58:20.832
be about the proportion of Nobel Prizes awarded to immigrants. Keep in mind that the

13676
17:58:20.832 --> 17:58:23.980
idea of immigration doesn't apply to some laureates.

13677
17:58:23.980 --> 17:58:28.828
In this document, I see that the gender field has a value of org, presumably short

13678
17:58:28.828 --> 17:58:33.558
for organization. What are the values that this field stores across documents in

13679
17:58:33.558 --> 17:58:38.525
this collection? MongoDB provides a built-in collection method for such aggregation.

13680
17:58:38.525 --> 17:58:43.196
Here, we call the distinct method on the laureates collection. We pass a single

13681
17:58:43.196 --> 17:58:48.103
argument, gender. MongoDB collects the distinct values that this field takes across

13682
17:58:48.103 --> 17:58:48.990
the collection.

13683
17:58:48.990 --> 17:58:54.178
We see that there are three and only three values for the gender field across the

13684
17:58:54.178 --> 17:58:59.493
collection. You may be wondering where this method comes from or how you can define

13685
17:58:59.493 --> 17:59:04.553
a similar operation yourself. The distinct method is a convenience for a common

13686
17:59:04.553 --> 17:59:09.933
aggregation. The count documents method we have been using is a similar convenience.

13687
17:59:09.933 --> 17:59:15.248
An aggregation processes data across the collection and produces a computed result.

13688
17:59:15.248 --> 17:59:17.490
In the last chapter of this course,

13689
17:59:17.490 --> 17:59:22.678
We'll learn how to create custom aggregations. You may be wondering about the

13690
17:59:22.678 --> 17:59:28.270
efficiency of aggregations in MongoDB. You can register so-called indexes on fields

13691
17:59:28.270 --> 17:59:33.053
for MongoDB to maintain. These indexes can ensure efficient queries and

13692
17:59:33.053 --> 17:59:38.645
aggregations. In some cases, a query might not even need to run on a collection. We

13693
17:59:38.645 --> 17:59:44.237
will learn how to create indexes in the next chapter. But if we're not working with

13694
17:59:44.237 --> 17:59:45.180
a lot of data,

13695
17:59:45.180 --> 17:59:50.095
Indexes are generally not needed. The laureates collection we're using in this

13696
17:59:50.095 --> 17:59:55.199
course fits in memory. It weighs in at under a megabyte and has on the order of a

13697
17:59:55.199 --> 18:00:00.051
thousand documents or fewer. It doesn't matter much if you use an inefficient

13698
18:00:00.051 --> 18:00:05.155
algorithm to sort a list of a few hundred items. Likewise, a full collection scan

13699
18:00:05.155 --> 18:00:10.259
isn't a big deal for this aggregation. You can use dot notation to specify fields

13700
18:00:10.259 --> 18:00:13.410
embedded deeper than the root level of a document.

13701
18:00:13.410 --> 18:00:18.755
This applies in query methods like find and find1, and it applies for aggregations

13702
18:00:18.755 --> 18:00:23.775
as well. I notice here that each sub-document in the prizes array field has a

13703
18:00:23.775 --> 18:00:29.120
category field. The .2 in the filter denotes index 2 of an array field. Thus, this

13704
18:00:29.120 --> 18:00:34.270
is a laureate where a third element exists in the prizes array. Let's fetch the

13705
18:00:34.270 --> 18:00:39.615
distinct values of this field. We see, as expected, that there is a value for each

13706
18:00:39.615 --> 18:00:41.180
category of Nobel Prize.

13707
18:00:41.180 --> 18:00:46.109
Let's use the distinct method to answer some questions about our node. In this

13708
18:00:46.109 --> 18:00:51.291
lesson, we're going to dip our toes into the world of aggregation pipelines. We'll

13709
18:00:51.291 --> 18:00:56.284
use the filter parameter of the distinct method to match certain documents. The

13710
18:00:56.284 --> 18:01:01.592
method will fetch field values only from these matching documents. Here I've found a

13711
18:01:01.592 --> 18:01:06.395
laureate document with a value of 4 for the share field in one of its prizes

13712
18:01:06.395 --> 18:01:07.280
sub-documents.

13713
18:01:07.280 --> 18:01:12.620
Pierre Curie shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with his wife Marie. They also

13714
18:01:12.620 --> 18:01:17.960
shared it with physicist Henri Becquerel. Marie and Pierre each received a quarter

13715
18:01:17.960 --> 18:01:23.170
share of the prize. Henri received the remaining half share. The Nobel Prize API

13716
18:01:23.170 --> 18:01:28.445
encodes prize share, in particular, the denominator of the fractional share, as a

13717
18:01:28.445 --> 18:01:33.460
string. Thus, the document records Pierre's quarter share as the string four.

13718
18:01:33.460 --> 18:01:38.903
Which Nobel prize categories other than physics have laureates with quarter shares?

13719
18:01:38.903 --> 18:01:43.952
We know how to get the distinct values of prize categories from the laureates

13720
18:01:43.952 --> 18:01:49.329
collection. We pass the dotted path prizes dot category to the distinct method. We

13721
18:01:49.329 --> 18:01:54.837
also know how to find and list all laureate documents satisfying some criteria using

13722
18:01:54.837 --> 18:02:00.214
a filter document. Can we compose these two ideas? Sure. The distinct method takes

13723
18:02:00.214 --> 18:02:02.050
an optional filter argument.

13724
18:02:02.050 --> 18:02:06.981
You can think of this as a two-stage pipeline. First, filter the collection for

13725
18:02:06.981 --> 18:02:12.100
documents that match a filter. Then, collect and return distinct values of a field

13726
18:02:12.100 --> 18:02:17.218
for these documents. In the last chapter of this course, we'll learn how to custom

13727
18:02:17.218 --> 18:02:22.274
build such pipelines in an explicit way. The Nobel Prize API serves its data in a

13728
18:02:22.274 --> 18:02:27.268
denormalized way. Thus, we can answer our question from a different perspective.

13729
18:02:27.268 --> 18:02:29.390
Here we ask the prizes collection.

13730
18:02:29.390 --> 18:02:38.296
Let's look at one more example of pre-filtering distinct values. Which prize

13731
18:02:38.296 --> 18:02:48.022
categories have laureates who have won more than one prize? We'll start by counting

13732
18:02:48.022 --> 18:02:54.350
laureates who won at least two prizes. In other words,

13733
18:02:54.350 --> 18:02:59.519
Those for which a second element exists in their document's prizes array. Next, we

13734
18:02:59.519 --> 18:03:04.562
pass this filter document as a second argument to the distinct method. Our first

13735
18:03:04.562 --> 18:03:09.542
argument is the dotted path to the prize category field. This returns all prize

13736
18:03:09.542 --> 18:03:14.395
category values with laureates who have won more than one prize. Here are the

13737
18:03:14.395 --> 18:03:19.375
corresponding prize categories for these six laureates. Notice that not all won

13738
18:03:19.375 --> 18:03:21.140
prizes in the same category.

13739
18:03:21.140 --> 18:03:26.060
Marie Curie, for instance, won prizes in both physics and chemistry. Linus Pauling

13740
18:03:26.060 --> 18:03:31.100
won prizes in both chemistry and peace. We'll learn in the next chapter how to fetch

13741
18:03:31.100 --> 18:03:35.960
only the document substructure we need. In this case, the MongoDB server returned

13742
18:03:35.960 --> 18:03:40.700
only enough to communicate prize categories. Now it's your turn. Let's practice

13743
18:03:40.700 --> 18:03:45.620
enumerating distinct field values of collections. In this lesson, we'll learn more

13744
18:03:45.620 --> 18:03:49.220
about how to query array fields and their structured values.

13745
18:03:49.220 --> 18:03:54.014
Here we see part of the laureates collection document for John Bardeen. He won two

13746
18:03:54.014 --> 18:03:58.341
prizes, both in physics. Each prizes array in a laureate document contains

13747
18:03:58.341 --> 18:04:03.018
sub-document. Each sub-document has a category field. We can use dot notation to

13748
18:04:03.018 --> 18:04:07.636
filter for and count laureates with a prize category equal to physics. Here's a

13749
18:04:07.636 --> 18:04:12.489
simpler example using a fictitious field. Let's imagine that laureate documents had

13750
18:04:12.489 --> 18:04:16.640
an extra field, nicknames. This field stores an array of string values.

13751
18:04:16.640 --> 18:04:21.670
Let's now find all laureates with a nickname of JB. We could use a filter document

13752
18:04:21.670 --> 18:04:26.517
like this. The filter matches all documents that have at least one value in the

13753
18:04:26.517 --> 18:04:31.547
nicknames array field equal to JB. This notation is familiar. If nicknames was not

13754
18:04:31.547 --> 18:04:36.394
an array, the filter would match for the field value being equal to JB. Because

13755
18:04:36.394 --> 18:04:41.485
nicknames is an array, the filter matches if any member of the array matches. Let's

13756
18:04:41.485 --> 18:04:44.430
go back to filtering on the real category field.

13757
18:04:44.430 --> 18:04:49.352
This field is within sub-documents of the top-level prizes array field. As before,

13758
18:04:49.352 --> 18:04:54.273
we can wrap filter document values with operators. For example, here we filter for

13759
18:04:54.273 --> 18:04:59.315
laureates with a prize not in physics. Note that these documents may contain a prize

13760
18:04:59.315 --> 18:05:03.877
sub-document with a category of physics. They need only also contain a prize

13761
18:05:03.877 --> 18:05:08.498
sub-document with another category value. Another example. Here we use the in

13762
18:05:08.498 --> 18:05:13.120
operator to find laureates with at least one prize in these three categories.

13763
18:05:13.120 --> 18:05:18.358
And here, we use the NOT IN operator to find laureates with at least one prize not

13764
18:05:18.358 --> 18:05:23.405
in these three categories. But what if we want to filter on more than one field

13765
18:05:23.405 --> 18:05:28.772
within a prize subdocument? Let's try something like this to count laureates who won

13766
18:05:28.772 --> 18:05:34.074
unshared prizes in physics. Hmm, that's not quite what we want. This filter matches

13767
18:05:34.074 --> 18:05:39.121
prize subdocuments that have two and only two fields. No laureates have a prize

13768
18:05:39.121 --> 18:05:41.740
subdocument that looks exactly like this.

13769
18:05:41.740 --> 18:05:46.587
All prize sub-documents also have a year field, for instance. This next filter is

13770
18:05:46.587 --> 18:05:51.313
better, but it's not quite what we want. This filter matches laureate documents

13771
18:05:51.313 --> 18:05:55.861
satisfying two conditions. The first is that a prizes field has at least one

13772
18:05:55.861 --> 18:06:00.648
sub-document with a category field equal to physics. The second is that a prizes

13773
18:06:00.648 --> 18:06:05.674
field has at least one sub-document with a share field equal to one. The prizes that

13774
18:06:05.674 --> 18:06:10.640
match for a laureate could be different prizes. This is where the element match, or

13775
18:06:10.640 --> 18:06:16.267
LM match operator comes in. Finally, we count all laureates that have at least one

13776
18:06:16.267 --> 18:06:21.962
unshared prize in physics. Within the LM match operation, as with any operation, we

13777
18:06:21.962 --> 18:06:27.658
can continue to drill down. Operations can nest to make finer grained queries. Here

13778
18:06:27.658 --> 18:06:33.079
we extend the last filter to include laureates only if they won a solo prize in

13779
18:06:33.079 --> 18:06:38.568
physics before 1945. Let's make sure you understand how to filter arrays with LM

13780
18:06:38.568 --> 18:06:38.980
match.

13781
18:06:38.980 --> 18:06:43.479
We'll do this in concert with other operators. And we'll learn some Nobel Prize

13782
18:06:43.479 --> 18:06:48.149
statistics. We've seen how to construct filters comparing a field's value exactly.

13783
18:06:48.149 --> 18:06:52.933
For string-valued fields, we may want instead to match a field's value to a pattern.

13784
18:06:52.933 --> 18:06:57.660
We may want to match a substring. We may want to constrain that substring to appear

13785
18:06:57.660 --> 18:07:02.102
at the start or end of a field's value. Or we may want something more complex.

13786
18:07:02.102 --> 18:07:06.487
Regular expressions are a powerful way to express such filters. Let's see how

13787
18:07:06.487 --> 18:07:07.740
MongoDB supports them.

13788
18:07:07.740 --> 18:07:12.459
Let's look at the laureate document for Marie Curie. Recall that she discovered a

13789
18:07:12.459 --> 18:07:17.294
new element and named it polonium. She did this to publicize her native land's lack

13790
18:07:17.294 --> 18:07:21.838
of independence. We see here that Poland is a substring of her document's born

13791
18:07:21.838 --> 18:07:26.382
country. How can we filter for values of born country that contain Poland as a

13792
18:07:26.382 --> 18:07:31.159
substring? We can use MongoDB's regular expression operator, regex. Here I use the

13793
18:07:31.159 --> 18:07:34.480
regex operator on the string Poland in a filter document.

13794
18:07:34.480 --> 18:07:39.027
This expression gets distinct values of born country that contain Poland as a

13795
18:07:39.027 --> 18:07:43.988
substring. The results show that some laureates were born in places that at the time

13796
18:07:43.988 --> 18:07:48.830
were not part of Poland but today are. Others were born in places that at the time

13797
18:07:48.830 --> 18:07:53.613
were part of Poland but today are not. And finally, some were born in places that

13798
18:07:53.613 --> 18:07:58.397
both at the time were and today are part of Poland. We can use the regex operator

13799
18:07:58.397 --> 18:08:03.180
together with the options operator. This will change the conditions for matching.

13800
18:08:03.180 --> 18:08:08.722
For example, the I option ensures case-insensitive matching. The string passed to

13801
18:08:08.722 --> 18:08:14.401
regex in the second statement is Poland, all lowercase. The assertion here is true.

13802
18:08:14.401 --> 18:08:20.080
Poland is always capitalized for this field. MongoDB also supports compiled regular

13803
18:08:20.080 --> 18:08:25.690
expression objects. The PyMongo driver includes a BSON package with a regex class,

13804
18:08:25.690 --> 18:08:28.290
which you can import and use as shown.

13805
18:08:28.290 --> 18:08:33.110
Finally, using native Python regular expression objects is possible. I do not

13806
18:08:33.110 --> 18:08:38.306
recommend this, though. Use of the bson regex class is more robust for MongoDB. The

13807
18:08:38.306 --> 18:08:43.439
syntax of regular expressions is rich. For the exercises, though, you only need to

13808
18:08:43.439 --> 18:08:48.509
know a few tricks. First, you need to know how to match the beginning or end of a

13809
18:08:48.509 --> 18:08:53.579
field's value. Second, you need to know how to escape a special character so that

13810
18:08:53.579 --> 18:08:55.520
you match the character itself.

13811
18:08:55.520 --> 18:09:01.096
To match the beginning of a field's value, use the caret character. Anchor it to the

13812
18:09:01.096 --> 18:09:06.672
beginning of the string you passed to regex. This expression returns distinct values

13813
18:09:06.672 --> 18:09:11.783
of the borncountry field that start with Poland. To escape a character, use a

13814
18:09:11.783 --> 18:09:17.160
backslash. A paren functions to capture groups in regular expressions. Because we

13815
18:09:17.160 --> 18:09:22.536
want to match a literal open paren and not use this function, we escape it with a

13816
18:09:22.536 --> 18:09:23.200
backslash.

13817
18:09:23.200 --> 18:09:28.425
This expression returns born country values for countries that used to be Poland.

13818
18:09:28.425 --> 18:09:33.778
Finally, to match the end of a field's value, use the dollar sign. Anchor it to the

13819
18:09:33.778 --> 18:09:39.067
end of what you passed to regex. This expression returns all countries that became

13820
18:09:39.067 --> 18:09:43.840
Poland after a laureate's birth. What you see here is all you need for the

13821
18:09:43.840 --> 18:09:49.258
exercises. Use a caret to match the beginning of a field, use a dollar sign to match

13822
18:09:49.258 --> 18:09:52.290
the end, and a backslash to escape parentheses.

13823
18:09:52.290 --> 18:09:56.717
We have new tools to answer questions about string-valued fields in MongoDB

13824
18:09:56.717 --> 18:10:01.616
collections. In this chapter, we're going to learn about getting only what you need

13825
18:10:01.616 --> 18:10:06.338
and fast. We'll start with what MongoDB calls projection. The term projection is

13826
18:10:06.338 --> 18:10:11.119
about reducing multidimensional data. In cartography, it's about getting what you

13827
18:10:11.119 --> 18:10:15.959
need to make a reasonable image from a 3D Earth. You can also think about asking a

13828
18:10:15.959 --> 18:10:19.500
certain part of your data to project its voice, to speak up,

13829
18:10:19.500 --> 18:10:23.936
With a table of data, it's about selecting columns. With MongoDB, it's about

13830
18:10:23.936 --> 18:10:28.840
selecting substructure. In MongoDB, we fetch projections by specifying what document

13831
18:10:28.840 --> 18:10:33.627
fields interest us. We can do this by passing a dictionary as a second argument to

13832
18:10:33.627 --> 18:10:38.180
the find method of a collection. For each field that we want to include in the

13833
18:10:38.180 --> 18:10:43.083
projection, we give a value of 1. Fields that we don't include in the dictionary are

13834
18:10:43.083 --> 18:10:47.870
not included in the projection. The exception is a document's underscore ID field.

13835
18:10:47.870 --> 18:10:52.271
The underscore ID field is always included in a projection by default. We must

13836
18:10:52.271 --> 18:10:57.010
assign it the value zero in the projection dictionary to leave it out. Here I try to

13837
18:10:57.010 --> 18:11:00.000
collect the prize affiliation data for all laureates.

13838
18:11:00.440 --> 18:11:05.233
My filter document is empty. What I get back is not the data itself, but a so-called

13839
18:11:05.233 --> 18:11:09.856
cursor, an iterable that I can fetch documents from, one at a time. In Python, we

13840
18:11:09.856 --> 18:11:14.307
can collect from an iterable into a list. We do this by passing it to the list

13841
18:11:14.307 --> 18:11:18.130
function. I don't want to print out hundreds of laureate documents.

13842
18:11:18.130 --> 18:11:22.232
Thus, I also use slicing syntax to get only the first three elements of the

13843
18:11:22.232 --> 18:11:26.608
resulting list. We can see that our projections contain only document data about

13844
18:11:26.608 --> 18:11:30.984
prize affiliations. We also retained the structure of that data. Remember how to

13845
18:11:30.984 --> 18:11:35.360
project fields this way. It's going to be very useful in the rest of the course.

13846
18:11:35.360 --> 18:11:39.517
What happens when you try to project out fields that are not present in some

13847
18:11:39.517 --> 18:11:44.002
documents? Rather than raise an error, MongoDB returns the documents without those

13848
18:11:44.002 --> 18:11:47.010
fields. This expression projects the BornCountry field.

13849
18:11:47.010 --> 18:11:51.851
This field isn't present for organization laureates, though. Only the first name and

13850
18:11:51.851 --> 18:11:56.519
ID fields get returned. Notice that I formatted a projection as a list of fields.

13851
18:11:56.519 --> 18:12:01.302
When a projection doesn't involve excluding fields, the PyMongo driver accepts this

13852
18:12:01.302 --> 18:12:05.624
format. Also, it's okay if a projected field isn't in any of a collection's

13853
18:12:05.624 --> 18:12:10.464
documents. Here, because there is no favorite ice cream flavor field, the projection

13854
18:12:10.464 --> 18:12:15.247
returns only object IDs. We're going to learn about MongoDB's aggregation framework

13855
18:12:15.247 --> 18:12:16.400
in the next chapter.

13856
18:12:16.400 --> 18:12:21.101
But already we have a new tool to fetch less data, only what we need. For example,

13857
18:12:21.101 --> 18:12:25.801
let's count the total number of prize medals awarded. That is, the total number of

13858
18:12:25.801 --> 18:12:30.560
elements in prizes arrays across all laureates. We can iterate over a cursor of all

13859
18:12:30.560 --> 18:12:35.260
laureates with only the prizes fields projected out. And this way, we avoid having

13860
18:12:35.260 --> 18:12:39.846
to download the other data in each laureate document. This can definitely affect

13861
18:12:39.846 --> 18:12:42.770
performance for very large collections. We can even

13862
18:12:42.770 --> 18:12:47.016
In this case, use a comprehension to reduce memory overhead in Python. We can

13863
18:12:47.016 --> 18:12:51.373
leverage Python's built-in tools for iterables and dictionaries. And we can use

13864
18:12:51.373 --> 18:12:55.729
projection to slim down these dictionaries to contain only what we need for our

13865
18:12:55.729 --> 18:13:00.306
analysis. Let's practice some projection. We've learned how to project out only the

13866
18:13:00.306 --> 18:13:04.939
fields we need from a query. There are other conditions we can give to MongoDB about

13867
18:13:04.939 --> 18:13:09.350
the data returned. In this lesson, we'll learn how to sort results on the server

13868
18:13:09.350 --> 18:13:11.060
before they get returned to us.

13869
18:13:11.060 --> 18:13:15.915
First, it's useful to review how we can sort a list of retrieved documents with only

13870
18:13:15.915 --> 18:13:20.712
Python. This may be plenty performant, especially for small datasets. After all, we

13871
18:13:20.712 --> 18:13:25.451
can store a local cache of our data and try many different operations on it. Let's

13872
18:13:25.451 --> 18:13:30.075
get a list of all physics prize documents with the year field projected. I'm not

13873
18:13:30.075 --> 18:13:34.756
interested in printing out the full projected documents with the document IDs, so

13874
18:13:34.756 --> 18:13:39.495
I'll use a list comprehension to collect the year values, slice out the first five

13875
18:13:39.495 --> 18:13:39.900
values,

13876
18:13:39.900 --> 18:13:45.182
and print them. It looks like the documents are in reverse chronological order, but

13877
18:13:45.182 --> 18:13:50.528
we get no guarantee of this. To sort the documents in ascending order of year, I use

13878
18:13:50.528 --> 18:13:55.619
the built-in Python sorted function. I also import the item getter function from

13879
18:13:55.619 --> 18:14:00.583
Python's standard library. Given a key, it fetches the value for that key in a

13880
18:14:00.583 --> 18:14:05.484
dictionary. To sort in reverse order, we can pass true as the reverse keyword

13881
18:14:05.484 --> 18:14:07.520
argument to the sorted function.

13882
18:14:07.520 --> 18:14:12.234
We can also ask Mongo to do simple sorting by field values on the server and yield

13883
18:14:12.234 --> 18:14:16.947
results in sorted order. Here we pass a sort argument to the find method, giving a

13884
18:14:16.947 --> 18:14:21.661
list of field direction pairs. In this case, we want to sort on the year field and

13885
18:14:21.661 --> 18:14:26.375
in the ascending direction. I don't need the documents beyond the print statement,

13886
18:14:26.375 --> 18:14:31.203
so I pass the cursor to the list comprehension. To sort by year in descending order,

13887
18:14:31.203 --> 18:14:34.480
we use negative 1 as the second element of the sort pair.

13888
18:14:34.480 --> 18:14:39.440
Why is a list passed to the sort keyword argument? This is because you can sort

13889
18:14:39.440 --> 18:14:44.463
first by one field and then by others. Let's see how. Let's sort prize documents

13890
18:14:44.463 --> 18:14:49.674
first by ascending year and then by descending category. To do this, we provide the

13891
18:14:49.674 --> 18:14:54.571
corresponding pairs in order as a list. Here, we also query for prizes awarded

13892
18:14:54.571 --> 18:14:59.845
between 1966 and 1970, exclusive. We project out only the data we need, the category

13893
18:14:59.845 --> 18:15:00.850
and year values.

13894
18:15:00.850 --> 18:15:05.738
Notice that we could sort by fields that we do not project. In this case, we happen

13895
18:15:05.738 --> 18:15:10.685
to be also projecting the sort fields. For each projection yielded by the cursor, we

13896
18:15:10.685 --> 18:15:15.220
format a string with the year and category value. To do this, we use Python's

13897
18:15:15.220 --> 18:15:20.108
double-star dictionary unpacking syntax. The output shows years increasing and, for

13898
18:15:20.108 --> 18:15:24.938
each year, categories decreasing. In both cases, the order is alphabetical because

13899
18:15:24.938 --> 18:15:26.940
the fields are both string valued.

13900
18:15:26.940 --> 18:15:31.733
For the four-digit year strings, sorting produces the same result as numerical

13901
18:15:31.733 --> 18:15:36.896
sorting. We can see that there was no award for economics in 1967 or 1968, and there

13902
18:15:36.896 --> 18:15:41.935
was no award for peace in 1967. One last thing. The command line shell for MongoDB

13903
18:15:41.935 --> 18:15:46.851
uses JavaScript. You specify a sort using the form of a JavaScript object, which

13904
18:15:46.851 --> 18:15:52.013
looks like a Python dictionary. This works because JavaScript objects in the console

13905
18:15:52.013 --> 18:15:53.980
keep their key order as entered.

13906
18:15:53.980 --> 18:15:59.221
In Python 3.6 and below, though, there is no similar guarantee with dictionaries.

13907
18:15:59.221 --> 18:16:04.461
The order of keys may not be preserved as entered. This is why PyMongo requires a

13908
18:16:04.461 --> 18:16:09.702
list of tuples. Let's get some practice. It's time to speed up our queries. Enter

13909
18:16:09.702 --> 18:16:15.008
indexes. An index in MongoDB is like a books index. Let's say I grab a textbook on

13910
18:16:15.008 --> 18:16:20.378
material science. I want information on eutectic temperatures. I could flip through

13911
18:16:20.378 --> 18:16:20.960
the book.

13912
18:16:20.960 --> 18:16:26.375
I could also try narrowing down to a chapter from skimming the table of contents.

13913
18:16:26.375 --> 18:16:31.790
Instead, I go to the index and back. I see an alphabetical ordering of terms with

13914
18:16:31.790 --> 18:16:37.338
page numbers for each. I find eutectic temperature, which directs me to page 314. I

13915
18:16:37.338 --> 18:16:42.820
go there and voila. With MongoDB, imagine each collection as a book, each document

13916
18:16:42.820 --> 18:16:47.901
as a page, and each field as a type of content. Imagine an ordered index for

13917
18:16:47.901 --> 18:16:48.770
temperatures.

13918
18:16:48.770 --> 18:16:53.472
This means you can find all pages that list temperatures in a range of interest.

13919
18:16:53.472 --> 18:16:57.998
This is hard to do with an actual book. But MongoDB structures documents with

13920
18:16:57.998 --> 18:17:02.523
fields. Thus, you can index fields using the values of those fields. When are

13921
18:17:02.523 --> 18:17:07.343
indexes useful? First, when you expect to get only one or a few documents back. If

13922
18:17:07.343 --> 18:17:12.103
your typical queries fetch most, if not all documents, you might as well scan the

13923
18:17:12.103 --> 18:17:16.100
whole collection. Making Mongo maintain an index is a waste of time.

13924
18:17:16.100 --> 18:17:21.494
Second, when you have very large documents or very large collections. Rather than

13925
18:17:21.494 --> 18:17:27.021
load these into memory from disk, Mongo can use much smaller indexes. How long does

13926
18:17:27.021 --> 18:17:32.548
it take to collect prize documents using certain queries? Let's measure. I use here

13927
18:17:32.548 --> 18:17:38.142
the TimeIt module of Python via the Jupyter Notebook's TimeIt magic. Fetching prizes

13928
18:17:38.142 --> 18:17:43.669
from 1901 takes half a millisecond on my laptop. Fetching all prizes sorted by year

13929
18:17:43.669 --> 18:17:45.600
takes over five milliseconds.

13930
18:17:45.600 --> 18:17:50.446
Let's now create an ascending index on prize years using the create index method.

13931
18:17:50.446 --> 18:17:55.233
Mongo can read a single field index in reverse. For a multi-field index, though,

13932
18:17:55.233 --> 18:18:00.199
direction matters. After creating this index, query performance improves. The first

13933
18:18:00.199 --> 18:18:04.806
query's runtime drops by 30%, the second by 20%. These gains surprise me, not

13934
18:18:04.806 --> 18:18:09.652
because they are low, but because they are high. Our prizes collection is under a

13935
18:18:09.652 --> 18:18:13.900
quarter megabyte, uncompressed. It has fewer than a thousand documents.

13936
18:18:13.900 --> 18:18:18.884
Imagine the performance gain on a much larger collection, especially if the working

13937
18:18:18.884 --> 18:18:23.747
set doesn't fit in RAM. Here we create a compound index on ascending category and

13938
18:18:23.747 --> 18:18:28.371
then ascending year. Thus Mongo maintains an index by ascending year for each

13939
18:18:28.371 --> 18:18:33.294
category. Here we list all years of economics prizes. We involve only the category

13940
18:18:33.294 --> 18:18:38.338
in year fields. Thus Mongo never has to examine the collection itself to execute the

13941
18:18:38.338 --> 18:18:40.800
query. The query is covered by the index.

13942
18:18:40.800 --> 18:18:45.560
We see only a minor speedup, but indexes can take up far less space than their

13943
18:18:45.560 --> 18:18:50.686
collections. Defining indexes that cover common queries can be huge for performance.

13944
18:18:50.686 --> 18:18:55.567
Here is another query, fetching the first award year for the prize in economics.

13945
18:18:55.567 --> 18:19:00.510
Once again, our compound index covers the query. Finally, I want to show you some

13946
18:19:00.510 --> 18:19:05.026
tools to troubleshoot query performance. We won't cover these tools in the

13947
18:19:05.026 --> 18:19:08.260
exercises. The first is the index information method.

13948
18:19:08.260 --> 18:19:13.505
This helps confirm which indexes exist for a collection. The second tool is the

13949
18:19:13.505 --> 18:19:18.949
explain method of a cursor. MongoDB provides output from its query plan, detailing

13950
18:19:18.949 --> 18:19:24.260
how a given query will execute. Filtering, projecting, sorting, and fetching all

13951
18:19:24.260 --> 18:19:29.372
happen in stages. Here we see a full collection scan, or call scan, preceding

13952
18:19:29.372 --> 18:19:34.749
projection. After creating an appropriate index, we see that an index scan, or IX

13953
18:19:34.749 --> 18:19:36.210
scan, happens instead.

13954
18:19:36.210 --> 18:19:40.810
Okay, let's cover some. In this lesson, we will learn about the limit and skip

13955
18:19:40.810 --> 18:19:45.705
parameters of Mongo queries. They can help us inspect a few documents at a time and

13956
18:19:45.705 --> 18:19:50.541
page through a collection. In concert with sorting, they can help us get documents

13957
18:19:50.541 --> 18:19:55.495
with extreme values. Let's say I want to get prize category and year information for

13958
18:19:55.495 --> 18:20:00.036
a few prizes split three ways. First, I check that for all prizes, either all

13959
18:20:00.036 --> 18:20:03.810
laureates have a one-third share or none have a one-third share.

13960
18:20:03.810 --> 18:20:08.570
I verify my assumption with this for loop of assertions. Now, I can print

13961
18:20:08.570 --> 18:20:13.981
information on prizes split three ways. I filter for laureate share equal to 3, and

13962
18:20:13.981 --> 18:20:18.871
I get a long iterator. Tens of lines fill my screen. Can I fetch only a few

13963
18:20:18.871 --> 18:20:24.282
documents to examine before I decide how to proceed next in my analysis? Yes. Mongo

13964
18:20:24.282 --> 18:20:29.629
provides a convenient limit option as an extra parameter to the find method. There

13965
18:20:29.629 --> 18:20:30.020
we go.

13966
18:20:30.020 --> 18:20:35.230
Besides limiting the number of results, we can also skip results server-side. When

13967
18:20:35.230 --> 18:20:40.439
you use the skip parameter in conjunction with limits, you can get pagination with

13968
18:20:40.439 --> 18:20:45.332
the number of results per page set by the limit parameter. You can also chain

13969
18:20:45.332 --> 18:20:50.605
methods to a cursor. This is an alternative to passing extra parameters to the find

13970
18:20:50.605 --> 18:20:55.624
method. Here's what this looks like in the case of setting limits. I don't pass

13971
18:20:55.624 --> 18:20:58.610
limit as a keyword argument to the find method.

13972
18:20:58.610 --> 18:21:04.212
Rather, I chain the limit method with an argument of 3 to the cursor. And here's how

13973
18:21:04.212 --> 18:21:09.814
to amend a cursor by chaining both skip and limit methods to it. Finally, I can even

13974
18:21:09.814 --> 18:21:15.349
alter the sorting on a cursor by chaining a call to the sort method. Here I sort by

13975
18:21:15.349 --> 18:21:20.551
ascending year. One last thing. When sorting using the chained method, PyMongo

13976
18:21:20.551 --> 18:21:26.153
allows a couple of shortcuts. Here we specify sorting as before with a list of field

13977
18:21:26.153 --> 18:21:27.220
direction pairs.

13978
18:21:27.220 --> 18:21:32.485
There is only one pair because we are sorting by only one field. In this case, we

13979
18:21:32.485 --> 18:21:37.620
can destructure that single pair. Here I specify the sort with the field as the

13980
18:21:37.620 --> 18:21:42.950
first argument and the direction as the second argument. Furthermore, PyMongo will

13981
18:21:42.950 --> 18:21:48.410
take the default direction to be ascending. Thus, we can sort by ascending year as a

13982
18:21:48.410 --> 18:21:53.870
chained call with a single argument, year. All these cursors yield the same sequence

13983
18:21:53.870 --> 18:21:55.300
of documents. Finally,

13984
18:21:55.300 --> 18:22:00.216
Note that using the find1 method is different. It's like a call to find with the

13985
18:22:00.216 --> 18:22:05.317
limit set to 1 and with automatic fetching from the cursor. Thus, in this case, you

13986
18:22:05.317 --> 18:22:10.479
cannot use cursor methods. You need to pass skip and sort requirements as arguments.

13987
18:22:10.479 --> 18:22:15.333
Before you skip ahead, let's test some limits, especially after getting some...

13988
18:22:15.333 --> 18:22:20.495
There are cases where you may want to avoid having to fetch and iterate over lots of

13989
18:22:20.495 --> 18:22:21.540
data client-side.

13990
18:22:21.540 --> 18:22:26.575
In this chapter, we'll learn how MongoDB can do a good chunk of your data analysis

13991
18:22:26.575 --> 18:22:31.672
and aggregation for you. In this first lesson, we'll reproduce what we already know

13992
18:22:31.672 --> 18:22:36.461
how to do with the find method of a collection. By doing so, we'll see how the

13993
18:22:36.461 --> 18:22:41.435
implicit stages of a query can map to explicit stages of an aggregation pipeline.

13994
18:22:41.435 --> 18:22:46.409
Here, we iterate over a cursor to yield prize year information for a few USA-born

13995
18:22:46.409 --> 18:22:50.830
laureates. I used indentation in this code to demarcate implicit stages.

13996
18:22:50.830 --> 18:22:55.906
Also, I passed the arguments to find as keyword arguments to name these stages. The

13997
18:22:55.906 --> 18:23:00.554
first stage filters for documents that match an expression. The second stage

13998
18:23:00.554 --> 18:23:05.691
projects out fields I need downstream for output. Finally, the last stage limits the

13999
18:23:05.691 --> 18:23:10.583
number of documents retrieved. With an aggregation pipeline, I make these stages

14000
18:23:10.583 --> 18:23:15.720
explicit. An aggregation pipeline is a list, a sequence of stages, and it looks like

14001
18:23:15.720 --> 18:23:18.350
this. Each stage involves a stage operator.

14002
18:23:18.350 --> 18:23:23.240
Here's an aggregation that produces the same result as our call to find on the left.

14003
18:23:23.240 --> 18:23:28.130
To filter for documents matching an expression, I use the match operator. To project

14004
18:23:28.130 --> 18:23:32.496
fields, I use project. And to limit results, I use limit. This pipeline, in

14005
18:23:32.496 --> 18:23:37.386
particular, has three stages. It matches documents for USA-born laureates. It strips

14006
18:23:37.386 --> 18:23:42.101
the documents of all but prize years. And it yields only the first three. Sorting

14007
18:23:42.101 --> 18:23:45.070
and skipping are also available as pipeline stages.

14008
18:23:45.070 --> 18:23:49.870
Here we project prize years for USA-born laureates. We yield them in chronological

14009
18:23:49.870 --> 18:23:54.671
order. Furthermore, we skip the first year and collect only the second, third, and

14010
18:23:54.671 --> 18:23:59.588
fourth. One quirk of the sort stage in PyMongo is that it requires a dictionary-like

14011
18:23:59.588 --> 18:24:04.389
object. We can use the order-dedict class in Python's included collections module.

14012
18:24:04.389 --> 18:24:09.248
This class yields field-direction pairs in the order they are input. In the case of

14013
18:24:09.248 --> 18:24:13.170
sorting by only one key, we can, of course, use a plain dictionary.

14014
18:24:13.170 --> 18:24:18.002
I use the more general form here so that you know how to ensure compound indices.

14015
18:24:18.002 --> 18:24:22.835
Finally, we can use a count stage to count the number of documents passed in from

14016
18:24:22.835 --> 18:24:27.667
the previous stage. This count gets assigned to a field of your choosing. Here, I

14017
18:24:27.667 --> 18:24:32.619
count the number of USA-born laureates. This aggregation, of course, is the same as

14018
18:24:32.619 --> 18:24:37.392
the count documents method of a collection. The other convenience method we know

14019
18:24:37.392 --> 18:24:39.420
about for aggregation is distinct.

14020
18:24:39.420 --> 18:24:44.065
This method has a counterpart aggregation stage as well, which we'll cover in the

14021
18:24:44.065 --> 18:24:48.596
next lesson. You can now translate collection and cursor methods to aggregation

14022
18:24:48.596 --> 18:24:53.299
pipeline stages. You've seen how to do this for all but the distinct method, which

14023
18:24:53.299 --> 18:24:57.887
we'll cover later. Let's practice doing these translations before we learn about

14024
18:24:57.887 --> 18:25:02.360
more advanced aggregation. In the last lesson, we learned how to translate the

14025
18:25:02.360 --> 18:25:07.120
implicit stages of a query to aggregation stages. Now, let's dip our toes into more

14026
18:25:07.120 --> 18:25:09.070
advanced aggregation capabilities.

14027
18:25:09.070 --> 18:25:14.888
Aggregation stages can use expressions that contain field paths. To see this in

14028
18:25:14.888 --> 18:25:20.706
action, first let's clarify some terminology. An expression object has the form

14029
18:25:20.706 --> 18:25:26.819
field1, expression1, dot dot dot. It's what you pass to an aggregation stage. Here,

14030
18:25:26.819 --> 18:25:32.122
we pass an expression object to a project stage. The object has one key,

14031
18:25:32.122 --> 18:25:36.320
prizes.share, with a corresponding expression value of 1.

14032
18:25:36.320 --> 18:25:41.942
In contrast, here we project a field that we call nPrizes. The field takes the value

14033
18:25:41.942 --> 18:25:47.431
of the expression $SizeMapsTo$Prizes. The string $Prizes is a field path. It takes

14034
18:25:47.431 --> 18:25:52.652
the value of the prizes field for each document processed at that stage by the

14035
18:25:52.652 --> 18:25:57.605
pipeline. Note that you can create new fields or overwrite old ones during

14036
18:25:57.605 --> 18:26:03.161
aggregation. The other new concept here is the operator expression, which treats an

14037
18:26:03.161 --> 18:26:04.700
operator as a function.

14038
18:26:04.700 --> 18:26:09.970
The expression applies the operator to one or more arguments and returns a value.

14039
18:26:09.970 --> 18:26:15.044
Here, the size operator takes the field path $prizes as an argument. Thus, the

14040
18:26:15.044 --> 18:26:20.249
expression object assigns the field n prizes to the size of the prizes array. We

14041
18:26:20.249 --> 18:26:25.714
could also write the operator expression as taking a list of one element, and we get

14042
18:26:25.714 --> 18:26:30.983
the same result. For convenience, when an operator only has one parameter, we can

14043
18:26:30.983 --> 18:26:32.740
omit the brackets as above.

14044
18:26:32.740 --> 18:26:37.632
Many operators available in query filters have counterparts for aggregation. For

14045
18:26:37.632 --> 18:26:42.647
example, here I use the $in operator, which takes two parameters. To get the array

14046
18:26:42.647 --> 18:26:47.478
of prize shares for a laureate, I use a field path. I then project a new field,

14047
18:26:47.478 --> 18:26:52.309
soloWinner, which is true if and only if the array of prize shares contains the

14048
18:26:52.309 --> 18:26:57.446
string value 1. Now we know a bit about expressions and field paths. Let's translate

14049
18:26:57.446 --> 18:27:02.400
the distinct collection method to an aggregation. Here I use a new stage, $group.

14050
18:27:02.400 --> 18:27:07.130
A group stage takes an expression object that must map the underscore ID field. As

14051
18:27:07.130 --> 18:27:11.975
for any MongoDB document, the underscore ID field must be unique. In this case, each

14052
18:27:11.975 --> 18:27:16.704
output document will have as its ID a distinct value of the BornCountry field. All

14053
18:27:16.704 --> 18:27:21.319
BornCountry values get captured because no match stage precedes the group stage.

14054
18:27:21.319 --> 18:27:26.106
Thus, our list comprehension collecting ID values collects all distinct BornCountry

14055
18:27:26.106 --> 18:27:30.951
values. This includes the value None, which happens when a field is not present in a

14056
18:27:30.951 --> 18:27:31.470
document.

14057
18:27:31.470 --> 18:27:36.690
Let's combine a group stage with a project stage. How many prizes has the Nobel

14058
18:27:36.690 --> 18:27:42.109
committee awarded? The project stage is familiar to us from a few slides back, but

14059
18:27:42.109 --> 18:27:47.131
what about this group stage? The underscore ID gets mapped to none for every

14060
18:27:47.131 --> 18:27:52.417
document. This means one and only one document will emerge from the group stage.

14061
18:27:52.417 --> 18:27:57.902
This one document maps a new field, n prizes total, to an operator expression. Some

14062
18:27:57.902 --> 18:27:59.620
operators, like $sum here,

14063
18:27:59.620 --> 18:28:04.576
Act as accumulators in a group stage. This means they don't operate only on one

14064
18:28:04.576 --> 18:28:09.784
document. Rather, they have state and will accumulate a value as one document after

14065
18:28:09.784 --> 18:28:14.803
another of a group gets passed to it. Here, we compute the sum of lengths of all

14066
18:28:14.803 --> 18:28:19.822
prizes arrays across all laureates. We do this without sending a single laureate

14067
18:28:19.822 --> 18:28:25.030
document down the wire. Aggregations like this can save a lot of time and bandwidth

14068
18:28:25.030 --> 18:28:27.100
for very large collections. Okay.

14069
18:28:27.100 --> 18:28:32.051
Time to practice using field paths, operator expressions, and group stages.

14070
18:28:32.051 --> 18:28:37.464
Documents can have array-valued fields, and aggregation stages can introduce them.

14071
18:28:37.464 --> 18:28:42.678
In this lesson, we'll learn a tool to access array elements during aggregation.

14072
18:28:42.678 --> 18:28:48.091
Let's say we want the number of laureates for each prize. One way to do this is to

14073
18:28:48.091 --> 18:28:53.570
project a field using the dollar size operator. We can then add a stage to group by

14074
18:28:53.570 --> 18:28:54.560
prize category,

14075
18:28:54.560 --> 18:29:00.017
producing a count of laureates per category. I remove the projection of year in this

14076
18:29:00.017 --> 18:29:05.474
second pipeline, as there is no need for it. Then, I reset the andLauriates field to

14077
18:29:05.474 --> 18:29:10.866
be the sum of andLauriates values over each category. Finally, I sort by descending

14078
18:29:10.866 --> 18:29:16.063
count. How might we use individual elements of the laureates array? One powerful

14079
18:29:16.063 --> 18:29:21.390
option is the $unwind stage. This outputs one pipeline document per array element.

14080
18:29:21.390 --> 18:29:25.841
Here we unwind the laureates field across three documents. We can use stages

14081
18:29:25.841 --> 18:29:30.584
following an unwind to recompress data. What if we want to normalize our data and

14082
18:29:30.584 --> 18:29:35.269
track only laureate IDs for each prize? After all, we can fetch more information

14083
18:29:35.269 --> 18:29:40.012
from the laureates collection. Here we get a list of laureate IDs for each prize.

14084
18:29:40.012 --> 18:29:44.697
After unwinding the laureates array, we project year, category, and laureate ID.

14085
18:29:44.697 --> 18:29:49.499
Year and category together identify a prize. So we can group by a concatenation of

14086
18:29:49.499 --> 18:29:50.260
those values.

14087
18:29:50.260 --> 18:29:55.034
I use the add to set operator in the group stage to collect laureate IDs for each

14088
18:29:55.034 --> 18:29:59.808
prize, and there you have it. I could also have grouped by underscore ID, but the

14089
18:29:59.808 --> 18:30:04.759
category year combo is more readable, and I introduced you to a new operator. Here's

14090
18:30:04.759 --> 18:30:09.651
another way to understand the unwind operator. Before, we used the size operator to

14091
18:30:09.651 --> 18:30:14.602
project the number of laureates per prize. This projection fed into a group stage to

14092
18:30:14.602 --> 18:30:19.140
output counts by category. Instead of projecting sizes and summing over them,

14093
18:30:19.140 --> 18:30:24.138
We can unwind and count documents. The group stage here counts the documents per

14094
18:30:24.138 --> 18:30:29.261
category fed into it by the unwind stage. The two pipelines shown produce the same

14095
18:30:29.261 --> 18:30:34.447
result. Finally, let's see a stage that often accompanies unwinding, Dollar Lookup.

14096
18:30:34.447 --> 18:30:39.695
This stage pulls in documents from another collection via what's termed a left outer

14097
18:30:39.695 --> 18:30:44.631
join. Let's collect countries of birth for economics laureates. From the prizes

14098
18:30:44.631 --> 18:30:47.630
collection, we first unwind the laureates array.

14099
18:30:47.630 --> 18:30:49.503
Each pipeline document now has a single

14100
18:30:49.503 --> 18:30:54.883
laureates.id. Then we query the laureates collection for documents with the same

14101
18:30:54.883 --> 18:31:00.464
value for ID. For each one we find, we push it into an array we name laureate bios.

14102
18:31:00.464 --> 18:31:05.978
Next, we collect the distinct laureate born country values. We want to feed single

14103
18:31:05.978 --> 18:31:08.130
born country values, not arrays.

14104
18:31:08.130 --> 18:31:13.256
to the add to set operator. Hence, we unwind before the group stage. Is there an

14105
18:31:13.256 --> 18:31:18.574
easier way to do this? Sure! MongoDB doesn't enforce a normalized schema. Thus, you

14106
18:31:18.574 --> 18:31:23.956
can tailor a collection's schema to support query simplicity and efficiency. We know

14107
18:31:23.956 --> 18:31:28.762
the Laureate's collection stores info on prize categories as well. So, this

14108
18:31:28.762 --> 18:31:33.887
one-liner produces the same result as the five-stage aggregation pipeline above.

14109
18:31:33.887 --> 18:31:34.400
Even so,

14110
18:31:34.400 --> 18:31:39.280
It's good to know that you can perform server-side joins in a pinch. Sometimes it

14111
18:31:39.280 --> 18:31:44.160
feels good to unwind. It's time to round out our aggregation know-how and wrap up

14112
18:31:44.160 --> 18:31:48.920
the course. In this lesson, we'll learn how to add fields in a pipeline without

14113
18:31:48.920 --> 18:31:53.740
having to project existing fields. For Nobel laureates that have died, I want to

14114
18:31:53.740 --> 18:31:58.740
know the number of years they were alive. I can start a pipeline to compute this by

14115
18:31:58.740 --> 18:32:01.150
projecting out the died and born fields.

14116
18:32:01.150 --> 18:32:06.745
Skimming the MongoDB documentation, I found a handy operator, date from string. This

14117
18:32:06.745 --> 18:32:11.940
will help us subtract the date of birth from the date of death. But wait, some

14118
18:32:11.940 --> 18:32:17.402
laureate documents have an invalid date of all zeros. Why? This encodes that their

14119
18:32:17.402 --> 18:32:22.863
date of birth is not recorded. To overcome this, let's insert a match stage at the

14120
18:32:22.863 --> 18:32:28.325
start of our pipeline. Now we include only laureates with reasonable years of both

14121
18:32:28.325 --> 18:32:29.790
birth and death. Darn.

14122
18:32:29.790 --> 18:32:34.532
It looks like some laureates have only their year of birth recorded. How can we

14123
18:32:34.532 --> 18:32:39.453
accommodate this? Here's one way we can choose to accommodate a date of birth that

14124
18:32:39.453 --> 18:32:44.135
is only a year. First, we can use a new stage, addFields, to provide new array

14125
18:32:44.135 --> 18:32:49.057
fields split on the hyphen in the date strings. This gives us year, month, and day

14126
18:32:49.057 --> 18:32:54.099
as the array elements. Why use addFields rather than project? Simple. We do not need

14127
18:32:54.099 --> 18:32:58.300
to specify all the other fields we want to pass along in the pipeline.

14128
18:32:58.300 --> 18:33:03.458
This enables us to use the existing born field in this next stage, also an addField

14129
18:33:03.458 --> 18:33:08.616
stage. Here, I rewrite the born field if the string value 0,0 is in the born array.

14130
18:33:08.616 --> 18:33:13.774
I fix it to be a real date by concatenating the year element of born array with the

14131
18:33:13.774 --> 18:33:18.622
string suffix for January 1st. The conditional expression operator, cond, is a

14132
18:33:18.622 --> 18:33:23.718
ternary operator. It evaluates the first expression and, if it's true, returns the

14133
18:33:23.718 --> 18:33:25.520
value of the next expression.

14134
18:33:25.520 --> 18:33:30.417
Otherwise, it returns the value of the third expression. Now, at last, we are able

14135
18:33:30.417 --> 18:33:35.074
to compute the number of years each laureate was alive. Now, let's compute the

14136
18:33:35.074 --> 18:33:39.971
number of years between the died and born dates. I show only the last stage of our

14137
18:33:39.971 --> 18:33:44.211
pipeline so far. First, we subtract the dates. This produces a value in

14138
18:33:44.211 --> 18:33:48.809
milliseconds. Next, we divide by the approximate number of milliseconds in an

14139
18:33:48.809 --> 18:33:52.690
average year. Finally, we floor the value to the nearest integer.

14140
18:33:52.690 --> 18:33:57.907
At this point, I'd verify that this stage works. I may add a limit stage to inspect

14141
18:33:57.907 --> 18:34:03.061
a few output documents. I want to show you one last operator to get a sense of the

14142
18:34:03.061 --> 18:34:07.964
distribution of year values across laureates. MongoDB's bucket operator groups

14143
18:34:07.964 --> 18:34:12.866
values into buckets defined by a sequence of boundaries. Here, we see that one

14144
18:34:12.866 --> 18:34:17.894
laureate died before the age of 40, and two live to be over 100 years old. Let's

14145
18:34:17.894 --> 18:34:21.100
solidify your understanding of the add field stage.

